window.CP_CONTENT = {"publication":{"schema_version":1,"edition":"1.1","repository":"Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract","commit":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","catalog":"publication-catalog.json"},"records":[{"canonical":true,"id":"human-meaning-and-computable-knowledge","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/human-meaning-and-computable-knowledge/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/human-meaning-and-computable-knowledge/","title":"From Human Meaning to Contract-Governed Knowledge","titleRu":"From Human Meaning to Contract-Governed Knowledge","summary":"A bounded foundation for preserving task-relevant distinctions between human expressions, formal representations, knowledge artifacts and authorized agent use.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Status:</strong> research draft for corpus integration</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A system can reproduce a sentence accurately while losing the condition that makes its use appropriate. Conversely, a useful formal representation may omit much of a source while retaining every distinction needed for one declared question. This paper asks how to connect contextual human expressions to knowledge that an agent can inspect, transform and use without confusing interpretation with truth or permission. We distinguish expressions, communicative intentions, referents, propositions, formal interpretations, evidence and current-use authority. We then define a bounded semantic bridge: source selection, interpretation alternatives, an explicit representation profile, protected observations, artifact construction and task-specific admission. An elementary factorization result characterizes when a representation retains enough information for specified observations; it establishes an existence condition, not an automatic procedure for discovering human meaning. An original rule-and-exception example shows how attribution, missing information and operational permission remain separate through the bridge. The framework connects the existing Knowledge Contract accounts of semantic objects, transformations, retrieval, professional artifacts and evidence. Its contribution is an explicit account of their interfaces and failure conditions. It neither reduces all human meaning to computation nor establishes extraction accuracy, empirical superiority, or conformance of a deployed agent.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> meaning; interpretation; abstraction; semantic representation; knowledge contract; applicability; agent knowledge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-the-problem-keeping-the-distinction-that-matters\">1. The problem: keeping the distinction that matters</h2>\n<p>Consider a notice in a fictional community media room:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Members may borrow the camera after their training and the equipment check have been recorded, unless lending is suspended. Borrowing the camera does not authorize copying the files already on it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A short summary, “Members may borrow the camera,” keeps the topic but drops the training condition, the check, the suspension exception and the boundary on copying files. A perfect quotation preserves those words, but still does not establish that a particular person is a member, that a record is current, that the notice was adopted by the room’s operator, or that an agent may release the camera. These are different questions, not different levels of one confidence score.</p>\n<p>The notice is an original explanatory example, not an actual policy or a reported experiment. It provides a concrete problem before notation: which distinctions must survive when an expression becomes a stored artifact and then a recommendation or action? Sections 4–6 formalize that question; Section 7 follows the example through the resulting model.</p>\n<p>A human reader can use shared practice, the surrounding conversation and knowledge of the room to interpret the notice. A computational system needs an explicit account of whichever parts of that background its operation relies on. Writing a field named <code>context</code> does not supply the missing background. Equally, preserving the entire source without interpreting it does not establish that an agent will use the relevant condition.</p>\n<p>Our research question is: <strong>under what declared assumptions does a representation preserve the distinctions needed for a specified use of contextual source material?</strong> We address three subquestions. What is being represented? Which observations determine adequacy for a use? What additional obligations connect a correct representation to an agent’s permitted operation?</p>\n<p>The contribution is a foundation connecting these questions to the existing corpus, rather than a replacement ontology. It introduces a representation-adequacy condition, explains its relation to information loss and computation, and identifies the independent judgments needed at each interface. The method is conceptual analysis, explicit definitions, an elementary deductive result and constructed boundary examples. No experiment on human participants, language models or a production knowledge base is reported here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-related-foundations-and-the-boundary-of-this-contribution\">2. Related foundations and the boundary of this contribution</h2>\n<h3 id=\"21-communication-intention-and-formal-representation\">2.1 Communication, intention and formal representation</h3>\n<p>Grice’s account of nonnatural meaning assigns a role to a communicator’s intention and its recognition by an audience [1]. This motivates keeping an attributed communicative purpose distinct from a string’s recorded form. It does not supply a general algorithm for recovering intentions. We use the distinction, not a claim that all human meaning is exhausted by one theory of speaker intention.</p>\n<p>Shannon’s communication problem concerns reproducing a selected message through a channel; its mathematical treatment deliberately sets aside semantic aspects of that engineering problem [2]. The relevant distinction here is between faithful transmission and appropriate interpretation. A byte-identical copy is useful evidence about a representation, not a proof about what the representation means or permits.</p>\n<p>Bender and Koller argue for separating linguistic form from meaning in evaluating language technology [8]. Their paper is a methodological position, not an empirical result about this framework or a universal verdict on every later multimodal or interactive system. Our claims do not require settling that broader debate: an extraction process still needs a stated interpretation target and evidence appropriate to its intended use.</p>\n<h3 id=\"22-abstraction-and-correctness\">2.2 Abstraction and correctness</h3>\n<p>Abstract interpretation relates a formally specified computation to an abstract account of selected properties [3]. It offers a disciplined analogy: abstraction is judged against declared observations and a relation to the modeled system, not against how compact or fluent the abstract output looks. Natural-language interpretation is an additional problem. A set of structured records is not thereby a sound abstract interpretation, and this paper does not assert that it constructs a Galois connection for unrestricted language.</p>\n<p>Hoare’s axiomatic treatment of programming makes assumptions and inference rules explicit when reasoning about program behavior [4]. The corresponding lesson is to state what an operation requires and preserves. Correctness within a formal model does not establish that the model captures every material feature of the human task. The corpus’s <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">transformation paper</a> develops the separate composition obligations.</p>\n<h3 id=\"23-knowledge-organization-and-provenance\">2.3 Knowledge organization and provenance</h3>\n<p>PROV-DM supplies a model of entities, activities, derivations and responsible agents [5]. SKOS supplies identifiers, labels and relations for organizing concepts [6]. OWL 2 provides formal interpretations and reasoning for explicitly represented domain knowledge [7]. These are relevant foundations with different purposes, not competing spellings of a Knowledge Contract. In particular, the OWL primer distinguishes missing assertions from false assertions under its open-world semantics, and explains that formal knowledge representation does not cover all human knowledge.</p>\n<p>Knowledge Contract adds a task-relative coordination question: which interpretation, requirements, dependencies, assessments and authority conditions must be explicit before a particular use? Its <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">semantic-object model</a> already distinguishes faithful attribution from truth. Its <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">reuse model</a> already separates preparation from current admission. This paper explains the human-to-computational interfaces needed to read those models together.</p>\n<p>The elementary factorization argument below is standard mathematical reasoning, not a claim to invent functions, equivalence classes, provenance or abstraction. The project-specific contribution is the explicit placement of interpretation, representation adequacy, evidence and current authority in one bounded account. Demonstrating that this organization improves practical outcomes remains a separate empirical task.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-is-meant-by-meaning-in-this-framework\">3. What is meant by meaning in this framework</h2>\n<h3 id=\"31-working-distinctions-not-a-complete-psychology\">3.1 Working distinctions, not a complete psychology</h3>\n<p>We use <em>human meaning</em> as an umbrella for what people express, understand or intend in a context. We do not treat it as one directly observable scalar or one necessarily finite object. The system records inspectable representations of selected aspects, not a person’s complete experience. Table 1 gives the working distinctions used in the rest of the paper.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 1. Distinctions required at the human-to-computational boundary.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Working use here</th>\n<th>What the record does not establish</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Expression</td>\n<td>An utterance, passage, sign or other situated communicative material.</td>\n<td>Its complete intended meaning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Communicative intention</td>\n<td>A purpose attributed to the communicator, with the basis for that attribution.</td>\n<td>Direct access to the communicator’s mental state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Referent</td>\n<td>The entity or situation an expression is interpreted as concerning.</td>\n<td>That a matching name identifies the same entity in another context.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Proposition</td>\n<td>A truth-evaluable interpreted content under a stated semantics.</td>\n<td>That the proposition is true merely because it is recorded.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Interpretation</td>\n<td>A reading under explicit assumptions and context.</td>\n<td>That all reasonable readings have been considered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Formal semantics</td>\n<td>A specified relation between a representation and its interpretations or observations.</td>\n<td>That the representation correctly captures the source.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Justification</td>\n<td>Grounds supporting a specified conclusion under assumptions.</td>\n<td>Unconditional truth or permission to act.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Applicability</td>\n<td>Suitability for a named task and context, assessed under declared conditions.</td>\n<td>General suitability for all tasks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Authorization</td>\n<td>Permission supplied by the applicable authority for a concrete operation.</td>\n<td>Availability, competence or success of that operation.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These distinctions refine the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">shared vocabulary</a>; they do not replace its existing definitions. An interpreted instruction is not simply a descriptive proposition. Its content may specify an action and conditions; fulfillment, violation and legitimate adoption are different judgments from the truth of a report that someone issued it.</p>\n<p>A communicative purpose may also differ from a stated request. A person asking for a brief summary may intend to compare options, while another person uses the same summary to execute a procedure. A representation adequate for identifying the topic may be inadequate for either comparison or execution. The requested use therefore belongs in the assessment, rather than being inferred solely from the source’s topic.</p>\n<h3 id=\"32-from-a-human-abstraction-to-an-operational-criterion\">3.2 From a human abstraction to an operational criterion</h3>\n<p>A concept such as <em>qualified member</em>, <em>urgent</em> or <em>similar</em> can organize human practice without carrying one universal numerical definition. To make an operation computable, a profile can specify a criterion: for example, a valid training record for the named member and the current equipment class. This is an operationalization. It becomes an adequate representation only if its relationship to the intended concept is justified in the stated setting.</p>\n<p>Replacing <em>qualified</em> with “score greater than 0.8” does not by itself solve the problem. The scale, measurement, threshold and applicable population would each need a basis. A declaration can make a computation deterministic while leaving its connection to the intended human distinction unsupported.</p>\n<p>Not all useful material must first become a proposition or a number. A procedure can remain an ordered method with checks; an image can remain an essential source representation; a disputed interpretation can remain a set of alternatives. Formalizing only the part needed for a task is legitimate when the unformalized remainder and its consequences are visible.</p>\n<h3 id=\"33-truth-endorsement-and-use\">3.3 Truth, endorsement and use</h3>\n<p>The source can support “the notice states that lending is suspended” without independently establishing the current suspension. Likewise, a quotation of an adopted rule and a quotation of an obsolete draft can have identical words but different operational significance. The <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision and governance model</a> keeps content identity and normative adoption distinct.</p>\n<p>This account also separates epistemic uncertainty from a system’s refusal to proceed. A missing training record does not prove that a member lacks training. It may nevertheless prevent certification under a policy requiring a verified record. The refusal is a decision under that policy, not a new factual claim about the member.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-a-bounded-semantic-bridge\">4. A bounded semantic bridge</h2>\n<p>A <em>semantic bridge</em> is the declared relationship between contextual source material, an interpreted representation and its intended computational use. The term names an analytical interface here, not a new deployed service or wire schema.</p>\n<h3 id=\"41-source-and-interpretation\">4.1 Source and interpretation</h3>\n<p>Fix a source representation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e</annotation></semantics></math>, its revision and locators, and an inspected context <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nI(e,c)\\subseteq X\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>be the set of interpretations retained for the operation, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a declared domain of possible interpreted cases. A case can include relevant referents, conditions and background assumptions. <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a mathematical model of selected possibilities, not a claim to enumerate every human experience.</p>\n<p>Three obligations are separate. Source identity establishes which material was inspected. Interpretive justification supports the retained readings. Coverage addresses whether material alternatives were omitted. A locator can support the first while supplying no evidence for the other two. If the retained set is empty because interpretation failed, the bridge reports that failure; it does not certify every conclusion from an empty set of possibilities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"42-representation-profile-and-protected-observations\">4.2 Representation profile and protected observations</h3>\n<p>A profile <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> declares the representation language, types, interpretation rules, reference conventions, admitted operations and intended observations. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_\\pi\\subseteq X</annotation></semantics></math> be its stated domain, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> its representation space, and</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\alpha_\\pi:X_\\pi\\to A_\\pi\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>its encoding abstraction. A partial encoder can be treated by restricting <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> to its declared domain. Outside that domain, support is not established. Renaming an unsupported construct does not extend the profile.</p>\n<p>For a selected finite family of questions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nf_q:X_\\pi\\to Y_q,\\qquad q\\in Q,\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>for the observation each question requires. An observation need not be a truth value: it may be a quantity with a unit, a set of admissible answers, a preserved exception, or an attribution. Each codomain and interpretation must be specified. These functions describe the intended semantics; their notation does not make them known or computable.</p>\n<p>Call the abstraction <em>adequate for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></em> when there are observation functions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_q</annotation></semantics></math> on the encoded image satisfying</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for every </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nf_q=g_q\\circ\\alpha_\\pi\\quad\\text{for every }q\\in Q.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>In ordinary language, the representation retains enough information to recover the observations those questions require. This is exact adequacy relative to the selected model. It is not total preservation of human meaning and does not include authorization unless an authorization observation is explicitly modeled with its proper current inputs.</p>\n<p>The definition permits <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>⌀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> as a mathematical boundary case. The universal adequacy condition then holds vacuously and supplies no positive evidence that a material distinction was preserved; a declared source domain containing no cases has the same assessment limitation. For a prospective operational adequacy decision, at least one declared material protected observation and a justified nonempty interpretation domain with stated coverage are needed. Without that support, report <code>not_assessed</code> rather than certify adequacy. These are additional assessment conditions, not premises of the factorization proposition or evidence that an implementation checks them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"43-one-bridge-record-several-kinds-of-evidence\">4.3 One bridge record, several kinds of evidence</h3>\n<p>A practical record should identify the source and interpretation assumptions, profile, protected observations, omissions, unresolved alternatives and evidence for the mapping. The existing <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">semantic object</a> supplies content, attribution, context and grounds; the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/\">semantic-types specification</a> supplies existing representation obligations. This paper proposes no incompatible replacement fields.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 2. Interface obligations and appropriate evidence.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Interface</th>\n<th>Required question</th>\n<th>Suitable evidence, within its scope</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Source to inspected representation</td>\n<td>Are these the intended bytes and regions?</td>\n<td>Revision identity, locators and inspected source material.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Representation to interpretations</td>\n<td>Which readings are supported or unresolved?</td>\n<td>Source-bound analysis, alternatives and assessment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Interpretation to formal encoding</td>\n<td>Which distinctions and losses does the profile retain?</td>\n<td>Mapping argument, explicit observations and adverse examples.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Encoding to derived artifact</td>\n<td>Does the transformation preserve the selected properties?</td>\n<td>Preconditions, preservation argument and actual-output checks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Artifact to delivered context</td>\n<td>Were the required dependencies and usable representations delivered?</td>\n<td>Exact output identity and delivery observations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Delivered context to operation</td>\n<td>Is this use justified and currently permitted?</td>\n<td>Applicable assessments, current authorization and operation evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A successful check at one interface does not settle the next. In particular, parsing a formula correctly and proving a consequence of that formula do not validate the interpretation that produced the formula.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-what-preservation-can-establish\">5. What preservation can establish</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-exact-adequacy-is-equivalent-to-preserving-the-required-distinctions\">Proposition 1. Exact adequacy is equivalent to preserving the required distinctions</h3>\n<p>Fix <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_\\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_\\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, the function <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_\\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and the observation family defined in Section 4.2. Functions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_q</annotation></semantics></math> exist on <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_\\pi(X_\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_q=g_q\\circ\\alpha_\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo>∀</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for all </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\alpha_\\pi(x)=\\alpha_\\pi(x&#39;)\n\\ \\Longrightarrow\\\n\\forall q\\in Q,\\ f_q(x)=f_q(x&#39;)\n\\quad\\text{for all }x,x&#39;\\in X_\\pi.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> If the factorization holds, equal representations supply equal arguments to each function <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_q</annotation></semantics></math>, so the observations agree. Conversely, suppose the displayed condition holds. For each represented value <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_q(a)=f_q(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_\\pi(x)=a</annotation></semantics></math>. Such an <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math> exists because <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the image. The condition makes the value independent of the chosen preimage, so <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_q</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined and the factorization follows. No extension outside the image is required. ∎</p>\n<p>This is an elementary factorization argument. It makes the required preservation condition explicit; it supplies neither a smallest representation nor an efficient way to find the functions. Even when the functions exist set-theoretically, evaluating them may require unavailable information or an unsupported computational procedure.</p>\n<p>The contrapositive exposes a useful failure: if two cases are encoded identically but require different answers to one selected question, no deterministic decoder receiving only that encoding can answer both correctly. A randomized decoder receives the same information in both cases; randomness alone does not provide the missing discriminating fact. Additional source access can help, but then the decoder is no longer using only the original encoding.</p>\n<p>Dropping a suspension exception while keeping the same “camera loan” topic is such a collapse for a question about the rule’s applicability. It may be harmless for a different question asking only which item the notice discusses. Thus loss is relative to an observation family, not measured simply by removed words.</p>\n<h3 id=\"51-more-demanding-uses-need-stronger-preservation\">5.1 More demanding uses need stronger preservation</h3>\n<p>If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\subseteq Q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>, adequacy for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> implies adequacy for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math>: retain the observation functions for the smaller family. The converse need not hold. An encoding containing only an item name can answer an identification question but not an eligibility question requiring an exception.</p>\n<p>This statement concerns unchanged models and observation meanings. Changing what <em>eligible</em> means is not merely adding another question to the same family. The <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/#proposition-3-mandatory-strengthening-narrows-certified-applicability\">applicability paper’s strengthening result</a> supplies the corresponding distinction for mandatory requirements.</p>\n<h3 id=\"52-uncertainty-and-conservative-answers\">5.2 Uncertainty and conservative answers</h3>\n<p>For a nonempty retained set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>, a cautious report can offer conclusions common to all its members under a fixed consequence relation. The <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/#proposition-4-additional-readings-narrow-common-consequences\">existing common-consequence result</a> shows why adding possible readings can remove previously common conclusions. It does not show that the retained set includes the correct interpretation.</p>\n<p>A profile can also use an over-approximation: the concrete cases judged possible are included in the cases represented by the abstract value. A property holding in every abstractly represented case then holds in every concrete case included by that premise. The argument is inclusion, not a new theory of abstraction. Sound inclusion itself needs justification; a language-model output labeled <em>conservative</em> does not establish it. Contradictory constraints that leave no possible case must be distinguished from successful certainty.</p>\n<h3 id=\"53-computation-and-termination-are-separate-obligations\">5.3 Computation and termination are separate obligations</h3>\n<p>Once a finite propositional formula and a fixed interpretation of its atoms are supplied, its truth on one valuation can be computed by its stated Boolean operations. Enumerating all valuations of a specified finite set can decide the corresponding bounded entailment question. These operations do not decide whether the atoms correctly interpret the notice or whether the supplied facts are true.</p>\n<p>For richer profiles, the supported decision problem, procedure, termination assumptions and resource limits must be named. The distinctions among OWL sublanguages and profiles illustrate why representation expressiveness and reasoning properties need explicit boundaries [7]. This framework promises no decision procedure for arbitrary natural language, unrestricted logical languages or the entirety of human meaning. A timeout is not a proof of falsity, and an unsupported profile is not an acceptable approximation unless that approximation has its own declared contract.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-algebra-and-geometry-have-different-jobs\">6. Algebra and geometry have different jobs</h2>\n<p>An algebraic profile specifies objects, operations and the laws justified for them. For example, composition of transformations needs compatible intermediate obligations and preservation of the same relevant observations. The <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">transformation paper</a> gives that conditional argument. Applying familiar symbols to source passages does not establish that conjunction, projection or reconstruction has the intended semantics for those passages.</p>\n<p>A geometric representation supplies another mapping, for example from an interpreted object and context to a vector. A distance can support a retrieval or ranking procedure when its meaning and evaluation are specified. It does not follow that nearby vectors are logically equivalent, that one entails another, or that either is suitable for a protected operation.</p>\n<p>Proposition 1 explains the issue without assumptions about a particular embedding model. If the mapping identifies two cases that a required observation distinguishes, exact recovery of that observation from the vector alone is unavailable. Conversely, an injective mapping would not by itself supply an efficient decoder, establish source truth or grant permission.</p>\n<p>The constructive division is to use retrieval to propose candidates and profile-specific checks to justify their intended use. The <a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">retrieval paper</a> separates finding candidates, covering requirements and assembling context under a budget. Neither geometric closeness nor the mere presence of a graph edge replaces those obligations. The formal properties of a particular geometric profile and its empirical usefulness need their own treatment; they are not established by this foundation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-a-worked-rule-from-expression-to-bounded-use\">7. A worked rule, from expression to bounded use</h2>\n<p>This section is an analytic illustration using the invented notice from Section 1. It is not an extraction benchmark or a service execution. Its formalization is a declared reading, not a claim that the original wording uniquely determines it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"71-fix-the-reading-before-evaluating-it\">7.1 Fix the reading before evaluating it</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m,t,k,s</annotation></semantics></math> denote, respectively: membership established for the requester; the required training record established; the equipment check recorded for the relevant camera and lending occasion; and lending suspended in the applicable period. For this illustration the selected rule profile treats its stated conditions as the complete eligibility rule:</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∧</mo><mo>¬</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nE=m\\land t\\land k\\land\\neg s.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The notice alone need not justify that completeness assumption. Its adoption, the meanings of the records and the existence of additional rules need verification before operational use. The example stipulates them only to make the Boolean reading inspectable.</p>\n<p>The separate statement that borrowing does not authorize copying stored files remains separate content. It does not assert that copying is forbidden under every other possible authorization. If the delivered artifact also instructs an executor how to handle the camera, this boundary on the lending permission must be preserved and any independently applicable copying permission checked.</p>\n<h3 id=\"72-preserve-unknown-facts-rather-than-assigning-convenient-values\">7.2 Preserve unknown facts rather than assigning convenient values</h3>\n<p>Suppose <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m,t,k</annotation></semantics></math> are established, but suspension status is not. The retained cases include one with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math> false and one with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math> true. Eligibility is true in the former and false in the latter. A definite positive eligibility answer is therefore unsupported by these observations.</p>\n<p>This does not assert that lending is suspended. It identifies the missing observation needed for certification. If the request is only to explain the rule, both cases can be reported. If the request is to release the camera, an unresolved mandatory condition prevents certification under the stated policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 3. Analytically derived boundary cases, not measured system outputs.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Case</th>\n<th>Established information</th>\n<th>Result within the declared reading</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Positive eligibility</td>\n<td>Membership, training and check hold; suspension is false.</td>\n<td>The eligibility formula is true; current operational authorization is still a separate question.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Active exception</td>\n<td>The first three conditions hold; suspension is true.</td>\n<td>The eligibility formula is false.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Unresolved exception</td>\n<td>The first three conditions hold; suspension is unknown.</td>\n<td>Both eligible and ineligible cases remain possible; no definite eligibility certification.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Changed wording only</td>\n<td>The sentence is restyled while the same atoms, scopes and rule are preserved.</td>\n<td>The formula’s result is unchanged under the stated mapping.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Changed task</td>\n<td>The requester asks to copy stored files rather than borrow the camera.</td>\n<td>Lending eligibility does not answer or authorize the new operation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Unadopted notice</td>\n<td>The text is present but its current authority is not established.</td>\n<td>The system can attribute the statement; it cannot infer a current grant from the text alone.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"73-turn-the-interpretation-into-an-artifact-without-changing-its-status\">7.3 Turn the interpretation into an artifact without changing its status</h3>\n<p>A reusable artifact can contain the rule, its source revision, the selected reading, unresolved facts and the dependencies needed for the intended use. A skill can describe how to inspect the required records. A role can assign responsibility for the check. A competency assessment can support a claim about a particular executor’s ability. A blueprint can arrange the steps and review points. None of these descriptions supplies a missing observation or adopts the rule by itself.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/\">artifact registry</a> defines these distinctions. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">core contract</a> binds requirements and constraints to a concrete Work Item. This is how the formalization becomes operationally useful without treating a document as a self-issued permission.</p>\n<h3 id=\"74-store-retrieve-and-reassess\">7.4 Store, retrieve and reassess</h3>\n<p>A knowledge base can retain the artifact and its historical sources, index representations for discovery, and return candidates. The consumer must still identify the selected revision, obtain the required dependency closure and inspect the actual delivered content. A search hit on an older unrestricted summary cannot substitute for the current conditional rule.</p>\n<p>If suspension changes, the source wording can remain identical while current eligibility changes. If the user changes from explanation to execution, the same record can require a different set of assessments. These are direct applications of the <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled-reuse model</a>, not evidence that the deployed system already enforces it. Preserving this separation also prevents a valid negative result from being hidden behind successful storage or transport.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-integration-with-the-scientific-and-engineering-corpus\">8. Integration with the scientific and engineering corpus</h2>\n<p>This foundation introduces the reading problem and the adequacy interface. Existing canonical documents remain the authority for their own definitions and requirements. Table 4 identifies the relation, rather than implying that every link proves the adjacent claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 4. Corpus relations and their evidential boundaries.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Existing work</th>\n<th>Relation to this paper</th>\n<th>Boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a></td>\n<td>Supplies semantic-object, applicability and alternative-reading definitions.</td>\n<td>Formal records do not establish complete extraction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a></td>\n<td>Supplies conditional preservation, composition and reconstruction results.</td>\n<td>A local preservation argument is not unrestricted language equivalence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">Retrieval and context composition</a></td>\n<td>Applies the distinctions to candidate selection, coverage and delivery.</td>\n<td>Candidate relevance is not certification.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/\">Interaction and memory</a></td>\n<td>Relates interpretation to dialogue, requirements and addressed revisions.</td>\n<td>Memory of a statement is not current adoption or learned competence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a></td>\n<td>Distinguishes proofs, observations, tests and counterexamples.</td>\n<td>A reference model or self-review is not production verification.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">Revision and governance</a></td>\n<td>Separates content revisions, assessments and normative adoption.</td>\n<td>A revised representation is not automatically a newly authorized norm.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">Controlled reuse</a></td>\n<td>States conditions for new use of prepared artifacts.</td>\n<td>Prior preparation does not discharge all current-use obligations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">Knowledge utility</a></td>\n<td>Evaluates useful permitted routes under explicit costs and baselines.</td>\n<td>Representation adequacy does not prove an economic advantage.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>On the engineering side, <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/\">semantic types</a>, <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/contract-family/\">operation contracts</a>, <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/\">context packages</a>, <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/\">compiled artifacts</a> and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">status model</a> operationalize distinct interfaces. Their existence is not evidence that an interpreter or service implements every obligation. A separate <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/reference/foundations/README.md\">integration map</a> records the typed reading relations and proposed vocabulary crosswalk.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">source-transcription study</a> and <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency-closure study</a> provide narrower existing observations. They do not validate this paper’s complete human-to-formal bridge. New bridge, geometry and verification studies must identify their own claims and evidence instead of inheriting a pass from those studies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-what-would-challenge-the-framework\">9. What would challenge the framework</h2>\n<p>Different failures challenge different parts of the account. A faithful transcription paired with a wrong referent challenges interpretation, not transmission. A dropped exception challenges the abstraction’s adequacy for the affected question. A sound representation delivered without its constraint challenges context delivery. A correct eligibility calculation followed by an unauthorized action challenges the use boundary.</p>\n<p>To challenge Proposition 1, a proposed counterexample must satisfy its assumptions about functions, domains and exact equality. Two equal encodings with different required observations are not a counterexample to the proposition: they demonstrate failure of its adequacy condition. A decoder that consults new external data likewise changes the stated information boundary. These distinctions prevent a desired behavior from being smuggled into the premises or a boundary test from being called a refutation of the proof.</p>\n<p>For the wider framework, useful adverse cases include implicit exceptions, ambiguous reference, conflicting sources, norm changes, an incomplete requirements set and a reader unable to use the delivered modality. A reference assessment should record legitimate alternatives and disagreement. It should not be generated solely by the same process whose extraction is being assessed.</p>\n<p>No case count is presented as evidence of universal validity here. The worked cases are analytic consequences of an explicitly chosen reading. A future evaluation needs frozen cases, stated oracles, positive controls, failure categories and actual observed results. The relevant comparison is not merely whether a representation looks clearer, but whether it preserves the distinctions needed for the stated use without unacceptable cost or unjustified refusal.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-limitations-and-unresolved-questions\">10. Limitations and unresolved questions</h2>\n<p>The framework does not determine a speaker’s full intention, settle philosophical theories of meaning, or represent all tacit, embodied, emotional and social aspects of understanding. Those limits do not prevent bounded applications; they require an honest statement of which observations are being preserved.</p>\n<p>The selected domain and interpretation set can omit the true case or a material alternative. The adequacy theorem is conditional on those models. It cannot certify the process that chose them, and no scalar confidence score substitutes for their validation.</p>\n<p>The framework establishes neither a universal representation language nor an optimal granularity. More retained detail can increase cost without resolving a missing assumption. A compact representation can be perfectly adequate for one query and insufficient for another. Deciding which observations matter may itself require subject-matter expertise and interaction.</p>\n<p>Authority, authenticity and institutional legitimacy are treated as explicit dependencies, not solved philosophically or cryptographically here. A source may be authentic and still mistaken; an adopted rule may be faithfully represented without being an acceptable rule in another setting. This paper supplies no legal, safety or domain-specific authorization.</p>\n<p>Finally, the integration is conceptual and documentary. The article does not report model-specific geometry performance, extraction accuracy, production conformance, human comprehension gains or universal savings. A separately reviewed implementation and measured outcomes are needed for those claims. Internal agent review, external peer review and empirical validation remain different assessments.</p>\n<h2 id=\"11-conclusion\">11. Conclusion</h2>\n<p>The useful bridge from human meaning to agent knowledge is not a claim to compress all understanding into a formula. It is an explicit account of what was expressed, how it was interpreted, which distinctions a representation preserves, and what must still be established for a particular use.</p>\n<p>For fixed interpretations and questions, exact representation adequacy has a precise condition: cases merged by the encoding must agree on every required observation. The elementary factorization argument states that condition without promising an automatic interpretation procedure or an efficient decoder. Its practical value is diagnostic: it identifies the missing distinction when an apparently convenient representation cannot support a use.</p>\n<p>Knowledge Contract supplies the surrounding discipline of attributable objects, constrained transformations, dependencies, assessments and current admission. Reading those components through the bridge makes their relationships explicit while preserving their limits. The result is a bounded foundation for further formal profiles and engineering checks, not a completed universal theory of human meaning or a declaration that a deployed agent already satisfies it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Grice, H. P. “Meaning.” <em>The Philosophical Review</em> 66(3), 1957, 377–388. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2307/2182440\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.2307/2182440</a>. <a href=\"https://uh.edu/~garson/Grice_Meaning.pdf\">Primary-text copy</a>.</li>\n<li>Shannon, C. E. “A Mathematical Theory of Communication.” <em>Bell System Technical Journal</em> 27, 1948, 379–423 and 623–656. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-7305.1948.tb01338.x\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-7305.1948.tb01338.x</a>. <a href=\"https://www.nokia.com/bell-labs/publications-and-media/publications/a-mathematical-theory-of-communication/\">Bell Labs publication record</a>.</li>\n<li>Cousot, P., and Cousot, R. “Abstract Interpretation: A Unified Lattice Model for Static Analysis of Programs by Construction or Approximation of Fixpoints.” <em>POPL</em>, 1977, 238–252. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/512950.512973\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1145/512950.512973</a>. <a href=\"https://www.di.ens.fr/~cousot/COUSOTpapers/POPL77.shtml\">Authors’ account</a>.</li>\n<li>Hoare, C. A. R. “An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming.” <em>Communications of the ACM</em> 12(10), 1969, 576–580, 583. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259</a>.</li>\n<li>Moreau, L., and Missier, P. (eds.). <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 30 April 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/2013/REC-prov-dm-20130430/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/2013/REC-prov-dm-20130430/</a>.</li>\n<li>Miles, A., and Bechhofer, S. (eds.). <em>SKOS Simple Knowledge Organization System Reference</em>. W3C Recommendation, 18 August 2009. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/2009/REC-skos-reference-20090818/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/2009/REC-skos-reference-20090818/</a>.</li>\n<li>Hitzler, P., Krötzsch, M., Parsia, B., Patel-Schneider, P. F., and Rudolph, S. (eds.). <em>OWL 2 Web Ontology Language Primer</em>, second edition. W3C Recommendation, 11 December 2012. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/2012/REC-owl2-primer-20121211/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/2012/REC-owl2-primer-20121211/</a>.</li>\n<li>Bender, E. M., and Koller, A. “Climbing towards NLU: On Meaning, Form, and Understanding in the Age of Data.” <em>Proceedings of ACL</em>, 2020, 5185–5198. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2020.acl-main.463\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2020.acl-main.463</a>. <a href=\"https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.463/\">Primary publication</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-the-problem-keeping-the-distinction-that-matters","title":"1. The problem: keeping the distinction that matters"},{"level":2,"id":"2-related-foundations-and-the-boundary-of-this-contribution","title":"2. Related foundations and the boundary of this contribution"},{"level":3,"id":"21-communication-intention-and-formal-representation","title":"2.1 Communication, intention and formal representation"},{"level":3,"id":"22-abstraction-and-correctness","title":"2.2 Abstraction and correctness"},{"level":3,"id":"23-knowledge-organization-and-provenance","title":"2.3 Knowledge organization and provenance"},{"level":2,"id":"3-what-is-meant-by-meaning-in-this-framework","title":"3. What is meant by meaning in this framework"},{"level":3,"id":"31-working-distinctions-not-a-complete-psychology","title":"3.1 Working distinctions, not a complete psychology"},{"level":3,"id":"32-from-a-human-abstraction-to-an-operational-criterion","title":"3.2 From a human abstraction to an operational criterion"},{"level":3,"id":"33-truth-endorsement-and-use","title":"3.3 Truth, endorsement and use"},{"level":2,"id":"4-a-bounded-semantic-bridge","title":"4. A bounded semantic bridge"},{"level":3,"id":"41-source-and-interpretation","title":"4.1 Source and interpretation"},{"level":3,"id":"42-representation-profile-and-protected-observations","title":"4.2 Representation profile and protected observations"},{"level":3,"id":"43-one-bridge-record-several-kinds-of-evidence","title":"4.3 One bridge record, several kinds of evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"5-what-preservation-can-establish","title":"5. What preservation can establish"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-exact-adequacy-is-equivalent-to-preserving-the-required-distinctions","title":"Proposition 1. Exact adequacy is equivalent to preserving the required distinctions"},{"level":3,"id":"51-more-demanding-uses-need-stronger-preservation","title":"5.1 More demanding uses need stronger preservation"},{"level":3,"id":"52-uncertainty-and-conservative-answers","title":"5.2 Uncertainty and conservative answers"},{"level":3,"id":"53-computation-and-termination-are-separate-obligations","title":"5.3 Computation and termination are separate obligations"},{"level":2,"id":"6-algebra-and-geometry-have-different-jobs","title":"6. Algebra and geometry have different jobs"},{"level":2,"id":"7-a-worked-rule-from-expression-to-bounded-use","title":"7. A worked rule, from expression to bounded use"},{"level":3,"id":"71-fix-the-reading-before-evaluating-it","title":"7.1 Fix the reading before evaluating it"},{"level":3,"id":"72-preserve-unknown-facts-rather-than-assigning-convenient-values","title":"7.2 Preserve unknown facts rather than assigning convenient values"},{"level":3,"id":"73-turn-the-interpretation-into-an-artifact-without-changing-its-status","title":"7.3 Turn the interpretation into an artifact without changing its status"},{"level":3,"id":"74-store-retrieve-and-reassess","title":"7.4 Store, retrieve and reassess"},{"level":2,"id":"8-integration-with-the-scientific-and-engineering-corpus","title":"8. Integration with the scientific and engineering corpus"},{"level":2,"id":"9-what-would-challenge-the-framework","title":"9. What would challenge the framework"},{"level":2,"id":"10-limitations-and-unresolved-questions","title":"10. Limitations and unresolved questions"},{"level":2,"id":"11-conclusion","title":"11. Conclusion"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"from-human-meaning-to-contract-governed-knowledge","math_count":40,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/human-meaning-and-computable-knowledge/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/human-meaning-and-computable-knowledge/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"f5cdf5856b230cd8d406fc3aea584d33e81f06ef14a36ebabd238825b3526af7","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"foundations","status":"research-draft"},{"canonical":true,"id":"semantic-objects-and-context","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/semantic-objects-and-context/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/","title":"Semantic Objects, Context, and Conditions of Applicability","titleRu":"Semantic Objects, Context, and Conditions of Applicability","summary":"A formal account of source interpretations, context, mandatory dependencies and conditions of applicability.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Faithful extraction does not by itself establish that source content is true or suitable for a task. This paper asks which distinctions a knowledge system must preserve between source material and its use. We define a versioned semantic object with attributable content, explicit context, and inspectable grounds, and define certified applicability relative to declared requirements and assessments. Five elementary conditional results characterize the model: fidelity alone cannot distinguish truth across observationally identical worlds; a finite dependency graph has a least required closure; mandatory strengthening narrows certified applicability; additional readings narrow common consequences; and complete backward slicing preserves selected deterministic outputs. Counterexamples delimit those results: a computed closure may omit an unrecorded exception, a retained quotation may be false, and a representation may lose the scope of a negation or obligation. The contribution is an integrated account of the obligations between extraction and use, not a new general theory of meaning. The method is deductive; the empirical question of whether an interpreter supplies complete and correct representations remains separate. Published engineering studies provide bounded transcription and dependency evidence, not a demonstration of complete semantic extraction.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> semantic object; provenance; context; applicability; interpretation; dependency closure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question-and-contribution\">1. Research question and contribution</h2>\n<p>An accurately reproduced quotation can contain a false assertion. A true statement can be irrelevant to a task. Identical sentences can concern different objects, dates, or obligations. Text and a single confidence score do not distinguish these cases unless the system assigns incompatible meanings to the same field.</p>\n<p>Our question is: <strong>what must remain explicit when source material becomes knowledge offered for a particular task?</strong> We separate five questions. What representation was inspected? How was it interpreted? What grounds support that interpretation? Which requirements govern its proposed use? What material actually reaches the recipient? A favorable answer to one question does not settle the others.</p>\n<p>The contribution is the organization of these obligations and their failure conditions. The semantic-object record connects attribution and context to inspectable grounds. The applicability definition makes mandatory conditions and justified exemptions explicit. The conditional results identify what follows from those definitions and what still requires independent evidence. They are elementary results, not a claim to a new general theory of meaning or a new extraction algorithm.</p>\n<p>The method is deductive. Sections 2 and 3 define the objects and certification rule. Section 4 states five propositions with their assumptions, proofs, and limits. Sections 5 and 6 examine granularity, delivery, and empirical evaluation; Section 7 identifies the narrower questions addressed by the published engineering studies. The proofs do not establish that an extraction process meets their premises.</p>\n<p>The model complements established work. W3C PROV-DM describes provenance through entities, activities, and agents [1]. SKOS provides concepts, labels, and relations for knowledge organization [2]. A provenance link or concept label does not, by itself, establish a proposition’s truth or suitability for a task. The question addressed here is what additional conditions must hold before an interpretation may support a particular use.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-objects-interpretations-and-grounds\">2. Objects, interpretations, and grounds</h2>\n<p>A <strong>source representation</strong> is a particular version of material: text, image, table, audio, or a combination. A locator identifies a region within that representation. A locator can make inspection repeatable; it does not certify the correctness of the region’s interpretation.</p>\n<p>A <strong>semantic object</strong> is a record</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\no=(i,v,m,A,C,G),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math> is identity, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math> is revision, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the represented content, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math> is attribution, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> is context, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of grounds and dependency relations.</p>\n<p>The represented content may be a proposition, question, instruction, constraint, example, or another declared content role. That role describes what the record represents; it is not an epistemic verdict. Recording an instruction does not establish its legitimacy. Recording an example does not establish a universal rule.</p>\n<p>An <strong>interpretation</strong> maps a source region to a semantic-object record under declared reading assumptions. It may remain uncertain or admit several readings. When a diagram permits two readings, the record can retain both and identify the observations needed to resolve them. Silently choosing one changes the knowledge claim rather than merely simplifying its presentation.</p>\n<p><strong>Grounding</strong> identifies why a particular part of the interpretation is supported. A source may support that an author asserted <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>, while an independent measurement supports <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> itself. These are different grounds. We therefore distinguish</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Says</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">and</mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Says}(a,P) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad P.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The first does not entail the second without additional premises about the source and the relevant domain. Faithful attribution and truth therefore require different justifications.</p>\n<p>Co-occurrence is not an implication either. Two statements appearing in the same paragraph do not acquire a causal or logical relation merely through their proximity.</p>\n<p>Extraction must preserve logical structure when it matters. For a domain with two objects, one satisfying <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> and one not, “not every object satisfies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>” is true while “every object fails <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>” is false. Similarly, absence of an obligation to act is different from an obligation not to act. A semantic representation that merges these distinctions changes the claim even if it preserves most words.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-context-and-applicability\">3. Context and applicability</h2>\n<p>Context serves six distinct functions in this model.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 1. Context roles and the information they identify.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Context role</th>\n<th>Relevant information</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Source</td>\n<td>Terminology, referents, and local legends in the source.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Extraction</td>\n<td>The inspected representation and unresolved details.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Request</td>\n<td>Intent, completeness requirements, and budget.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Recipient</td>\n<td>Usable forms and modalities.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Situation</td>\n<td>Target object, time, and reference frame.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Intended action</td>\n<td>What is to be explained, compared, computed, or executed.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are functional roles, not six required copies of the same value. One linked value may serve several roles. The representation must nevertheless distinguish conditions attached to the source from conditions governing the intended target use.</p>\n<p>Let a use request be <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math>, a relevant state be <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math>, and the finite declared mandatory conditions be <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Each condition has an assessment</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝗌</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝖿</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝗂</mi><mi>𝗅</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗎</mi><mi>𝗇</mi><mi>𝗄</mi><mi>𝗇</mi><mi>𝗈</mi><mi>𝗐</mi><mi>𝗇</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗇</mi><mi>𝗈</mi><mi>𝗍</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">_</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝗅</mi><mi>𝗂</mi><mi>𝖼</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝖻</mi><mi>𝗅</mi><mi>𝖾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\ne_r(o,q,s)\\in\\{\\mathsf{pass},\\mathsf{fail},\\mathsf{unknown},\\mathsf{not\\_applicable}\\}.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>A condition is <strong>discharged</strong> when it passes, or when a justified applicability rule establishes that it imposes no obligation in the exact assessed scope. Write <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_r(o,q,s)</annotation></semantics></math> for the validity of that rule application and its supporting witness. Then</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>Discharged</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇔</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝗌</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo>∨</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo minsize=\"1.2\" maxsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗇</mi><mi>𝗈</mi><mi>𝗍</mi><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">_</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝗉</mi><mi>𝗅</mi><mi>𝗂</mi><mi>𝖼</mi><mi>𝖺</mi><mi>𝖻</mi><mi>𝗅</mi><mi>𝖾</mi></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2\" maxsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Discharged}_r(o,q,s)\n\\iff e_r(o,q,s)=\\mathsf{pass}\n\\ \\lor\\ \\bigl(e_r(o,q,s)=\\mathsf{not\\_applicable}\\land w_r(o,q,s)\\bigr).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The witness identifies the rule, its grounds, and the subject and scope to which it applies. A bare <code>not_applicable</code> label is insufficient. Certified applicability is</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Applicable</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇔</mo><mo>∀</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><msub><mo>Discharged</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Applicable}(o,q,s)\n\\iff \\forall r\\in R(q),\\ \\operatorname{Discharged}_r(o,q,s).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>This definition uses the same four assessment values as the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">status model</a>. It does not treat unknown as pass or allow an unsupported exemption. It also does not equate failure to certify with proof that the content is false. An unanswered question about a relevant date may block a current recommendation while leaving the underlying statement unresolved.</p>\n<p>Certification is only as strong as the completeness and correctness of the declared conditions, assessments, and exemption rules. If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>⌀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(q)=\\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>, the displayed quantification is vacuously true; it does not establish that the request had no material obligations. Using certification to justify a consequential use therefore requires a separate justification that the requirement set covers that use.</p>\n<p>Conditions can be inherited only through a declared rule. A section-level exception may apply to its paragraphs, but arbitrary proximity does not establish inheritance. Conflicting conditions require an explicit conflict decision or a restricted answer. A system must not select whichever condition produces a convenient result.</p>\n<p>The request determines the necessary depth. A short identification question may require fewer dependencies than an instruction to execute a consequential action. Increasing depth can create new obligations; it is not merely a demand for a longer response. Applicability must also be checked for the actual recipient and delivered representation. A condition available to the system but absent from the delivered instructions may not protect the recipient.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-conditional-results\">4. Conditional results</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-fidelity-cannot-discriminate-indistinguishable-worlds\">Proposition 1. Fidelity cannot discriminate indistinguishable worlds</h3>\n<p>Suppose two possible worlds produce the same observed source representation, while <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> is true in one and false in the other. Any deterministic procedure using only that representation produces the same decision in both worlds. It therefore cannot correctly decide the truth of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> in both.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> The procedure receives identical inputs, so its outputs coincide. The correct truth verdicts differ. Hence at least one verdict is incorrect if the procedure returns a definite truth value. A randomized procedure has the same output distribution in both worlds and likewise receives no discriminating evidence. ∎</p>\n<p>This result does not make sources useless. It identifies the extra premise needed to move from faithful attribution to a truth claim: a reliable domain model, independent observation, or another justified discriminating ground.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-least-required-closure-exists-on-a-finite-graph\">Proposition 2. Least required closure exists on a finite graph</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite set of pinned object revisions, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑅</mi><mi>𝑜</mi><mi>𝑜</mi><mi>𝑡</mi><mi>𝑠</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathit{Roots}\\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> selected roots, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_q\\subseteq V\\times V</annotation></semantics></math> the active mandatory dependencies for a fixed request. Define</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑅</mi><mi>𝑜</mi><mi>𝑜</mi><mi>𝑡</mi><mi>𝑠</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>:</mo><mo>∃</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC_0=\\mathit{Roots},\\qquad\nC_{n+1}=C_n\\cup\\{v:\\exists u\\in C_n,(u,v)\\in D_q\\}.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Then the sequence reaches a fixed point <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^*</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the least <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_q</annotation></semantics></math>-closed set containing <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑅</mi><mi>𝑜</mi><mi>𝑜</mi><mi>𝑡</mi><mi>𝑠</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathit{Roots}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> The sequence is increasing and contained in finite <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math>, so only finitely many strict additions are possible. Its fixed point is closed by construction. For any closed set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑅</mi><mi>𝑜</mi><mi>𝑜</mi><mi>𝑡</mi><mi>𝑠</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathit{Roots}</annotation></semantics></math>, induction gives <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_n\\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C^*\\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>\n<p>Cycles do not invalidate this set-theoretic result. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">tested engineering profile</a> rejects cycles; its executable evidence therefore covers the acyclic profile, while the cyclic case here is supported by the proof. Missing edges invalidate an interpretation of computed closure as semantic completeness: a perfectly computed closure can still omit an unrecorded exception.</p>\n<p>“Least” is relative to the declared vertices and edges. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/#counterexample-to-claim-level-minimality\">projection study’s counterexample</a> shows that a least entity closure can still carry unnecessary claims inside a shared qualification. The proposition establishes neither claim-level minimality nor a smallest token budget.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-mandatory-strengthening-narrows-certified-applicability\">Proposition 3. Mandatory strengthening narrows certified applicability</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\subseteq R&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>, and keep the assessments, exemption witnesses, and discharge semantics of shared conditions unchanged. Every object certified under <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> is certified under <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Discharging every condition in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> entails discharging every condition in its subset <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>\n<p>The fixed-semantics condition matters. If adding a requirement also changes the meaning of an earlier one, the comparison is no longer a simple strengthening.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-4-additional-readings-narrow-common-consequences\">Proposition 4. Additional readings narrow common consequences</h3>\n<p>For a nonempty set of possible readings <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>Conseq</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K(I)=\\bigcap_{i\\in I}\\operatorname{Conseq}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>, with a fixed consequence relation. If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\subseteq I&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K(I&#39;)\\subseteq K(I)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Membership in the intersection over <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> requires membership in every set indexed by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as any additional sets. ∎</p>\n<p>This supports conservative reporting under ambiguity. It does not establish that the recorded readings include the correct one, or that the consequence relation is sound for the domain.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-5-a-complete-backward-slice-preserves-selected-deterministic-outputs\">Proposition 5. A complete backward slice preserves selected deterministic outputs</h3>\n<p>Let a finite acyclic computation graph have fixed deterministic node functions and fixed input values. Retain the selected output nodes and every node on which they depend. Evaluating that retained graph gives the same selected outputs as evaluating the full graph.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Order the retained graph topologically. Retained input nodes have unchanged values. Every later retained node has all its predecessors retained and, by induction, receives their unchanged values. Its fixed function therefore returns the same value. This includes the selected outputs. ∎</p>\n<p>The result concerns complete computational dependencies. Hidden inputs, changing functions, omitted edges, or an inadequate model of the original task defeat its premises. It is a restricted acyclic instance of the behavior-preservation obligation studied in program slicing [3], not a new slicing algorithm. Applying that obligation to knowledge additionally requires a justified interpretation and task scope; the result does not prove that an arbitrary textual summary preserves meaning.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-granularity-identity-and-delivery\">5. Granularity, identity, and delivery</h2>\n<p>A semantic object has no task-independent ideal size. A large unit may hide an exception; a tiny unit may detach a predicate from its scope. Decomposition is useful when it permits independent revision and inspection without losing mandatory connections. Its adequacy depends on reconstruction and use obligations, not on a preferred word count.</p>\n<p>Identity, equivalent content, and interchangeable use answer different questions. Records with equivalent claims may have different provenance. Revisions of one record may differ on a critical condition. Objects interchangeable for one question may be distinguishable for another. Deduplication should therefore preserve the provenance that supports or qualifies members of a verified content class.</p>\n<p>Delivery must be checked separately. Keeping an image identifier does not establish that the recipient received a readable image. Alternative text may omit a visual relation needed by the task. The delivered package must include or expose the relevant material at a usable resolution, together with the necessary legend or interpretation. Naming a multimodal field does not establish that these engineering conditions were met.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-falsification-and-scope-of-evidence\">6. Falsification and scope of evidence</h2>\n<p>The propositions concern declared representations under stated premises. A separate empirical question is whether an extraction process supplies the correct representations and dependencies. Testing that process requires independently prepared cases and adjudication rules. The evaluation should distinguish transcription error, attribution error, logical-scope error, omitted context, and unsupported inference. A single aggregate accuracy score can conceal these different failure mechanisms.</p>\n<p>Test cases should include unchanged wording with a changed referent, an exception outside the extracted region, an ambiguous visual mark, and a correct quotation of a false assertion. Independent reference annotations should specify permissible alternative readings. Agreement between two outputs is insufficient when both inherit the same omitted condition.</p>\n<p>An omitted dependency can invalidate a system’s claim to semantic completeness even when its closure computation is correct. Such a case challenges the completeness of the represented dependencies; it does not refute the conditional closure result for the graph actually declared. The same distinction applies when an implementation fails another proposition’s premises. A counterexample to a conditional proposition must satisfy its premises and contradict its conclusion.</p>\n<p>This article establishes the stated conditional properties and makes extraction obligations explicit enough to assess. It reports no measured superiority of a model, annotation process, or user interface. Executable examples can test compliance on finite inputs, but cannot establish complete interpretation of unrestricted language or images. The published observations and their untested boundaries are identified next.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">7. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">source transcription and uncertainty study</a> demonstrates that a reproducible projection can omit recorded uncertainty, and reports a correction that preserves that field. It does not evaluate plural interpretations or the correctness of source locators. Those extraction obligations remain untested by the published studies. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency closure and package delivery study</a> separates fixed-graph closure from historical delivery checks; it does not establish that the graph contains every semantic dependency.</p>\n<p>Definitions shared across the project are indexed in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>. The next scientific steps are <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">constrained transformations</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">retrieval and context composition</a>, which use the applicability and dependency obligations defined here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-conclusion\">8. Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Source fidelity, supported interpretation, certified applicability, and successful delivery are distinct obligations. The semantic-object record makes attribution, context, and grounds inspectable; the applicability rule asks whether each declared mandatory condition has passed or has a justified exemption in the assessed scope. Neither a locator nor a confidence label settles those questions on its own.</p>\n<p>The five propositions describe conditional guarantees, not automatic extraction capabilities. Least closure is relative to a declared graph. Strengthening requires unchanged assessments and discharge semantics. Common consequences depend on the recorded readings and consequence relation. Backward slicing requires complete dependencies, fixed inputs, and deterministic functions on an acyclic graph. Fidelity alone cannot discriminate worlds that present the same source evidence but differ in truth.</p>\n<p>The remaining empirical obligation is to justify the representations, dependencies, assessments, and delivery on which a proposed use relies. Unknown conditions must not be reported as satisfied, and an empty requirement set must not be mistaken for evidence that no obligation exists. The model supplies a disciplined account of those obligations; it does not certify unrestricted interpretation or establish a performance advantage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n<li>W3C. <em>SKOS Simple Knowledge Organization System Reference</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2009. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/</a></li>\n<li>Weiser, M. “Program Slicing.” <em>IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering</em> SE-10(4), 1984, 352–357. <a href=\"https://faculty.cc.gatech.edu/~harrold/6340/cs6340_fall2009/Readings/Weiser.Slicing.TSE.pdf\">Original article</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question-and-contribution","title":"1. Research question and contribution"},{"level":2,"id":"2-objects-interpretations-and-grounds","title":"2. Objects, interpretations, and grounds"},{"level":2,"id":"3-context-and-applicability","title":"3. Context and applicability"},{"level":2,"id":"4-conditional-results","title":"4. Conditional results"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-fidelity-cannot-discriminate-indistinguishable-worlds","title":"Proposition 1. Fidelity cannot discriminate indistinguishable worlds"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-least-required-closure-exists-on-a-finite-graph","title":"Proposition 2. Least required closure exists on a finite graph"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-mandatory-strengthening-narrows-certified-applicability","title":"Proposition 3. Mandatory strengthening narrows certified applicability"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-4-additional-readings-narrow-common-consequences","title":"Proposition 4. Additional readings narrow common consequences"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-5-a-complete-backward-slice-preserves-selected-deterministic-outputs","title":"Proposition 5. A complete backward slice preserves selected deterministic outputs"},{"level":2,"id":"5-granularity-identity-and-delivery","title":"5. Granularity, identity, and delivery"},{"level":2,"id":"6-falsification-and-scope-of-evidence","title":"6. Falsification and scope of evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"7. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"8-conclusion","title":"8. Conclusion"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"semantic-objects-context-and-conditions-of-applicability","math_count":47,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/semantic-objects-and-context/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/semantic-objects-and-context/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"6e466fed142538a5b147568e3a9e3aa017018083af0a1c3ac33afa48758c4c84","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"foundations"},{"canonical":true,"id":"constrained-transformations","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/constrained-transformations/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/constrained-transformations/","title":"Constrained Transformations and the Limits of Knowledge Transfer","titleRu":"Constrained Transformations and the Limits of Knowledge Transfer","summary":"Conditional preservation, composition and reconstruction of knowledge under explicit observations and losses.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A transformation can retain a source’s words while losing a condition needed for their use. Conversely, a change of representation can preserve every observation required by a particular task. This paper asks when such preservation survives a sequence of operations, which distinctions a projection may discard, and what is needed for reconstruction or transfer. We model transformations as partial relations with explicit preconditions, postconditions, protected observations, and loss records. Two conditional propositions establish partial-correctness composition and endpoint preservation when adjacent steps have compatible preconditions and agree on the observation passed between them. A third proposition rules out a left inverse for a noninjective transformation. We distinguish observation equivalence from identity, state coordinate-projection laws with their domains and codomains, and make the retained-information condition for reconstruction explicit. A Boolean construction illustrates shared consequences and residuals without claiming compression or general document similarity. Cross-domain examples explain why matching labels do not justify transferring a rule. The method is deductive: these results do not establish termination, complete semantic annotation, or compliance by a deployed model. Their role is to make the assumptions and limits of a preservation claim inspectable and refutable.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> transformation contract; partial relation; semantic preservation; projection; transfer; reconstruction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question-and-relation-to-prior-work\">1. Research question and relation to prior work</h2>\n<p>An answer may retain the main words of a source while losing a condition that makes the answer usable. A change of notation, by contrast, can preserve everything required for a particular task. The research question is therefore <strong>which observations must remain valid, under which assumptions, for which use</strong>. Byte identity, similar wording, and semantic preservation answer different questions.</p>\n<p>The account is task-relative. It covers representation changes and restricted inference but does not equate them. Converting a value to another unit, summarizing an argument, and deriving a consequence require different premises. A common label such as “processing” supplies no common correctness guarantee.</p>\n<p>Hoare’s account of program correctness separates preconditions, execution, and postconditions [1]. We use that discipline to state obligations at the boundary of knowledge operations. The composition argument is an application of established partial-correctness reasoning, not a claim to have invented sequential composition. Similarly, observation equivalence and the obstruction to inverting a noninjective function use elementary mathematics. The contribution is their explicit, bounded application to knowledge artifacts: a chain must preserve the same task-relevant observation, reconstruction must account for discarded information, and transfer needs a justified bridge between interpretations.</p>\n<p>The method is deductive. We define a transition model, derive consequences from stated premises, and construct cases in which omitting a premise defeats the proposed justification. The proofs concern the declared model. They do not establish that arbitrary natural-language transformations have a complete formal semantics or that a particular implementation follows that model. Section 7 separates the existing engineering evidence from the mathematical argument.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-a-transformation-model\">2. A transformation model</h2>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be declared spaces of input and output states. A transformation is a relation</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>Y</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nT\\subseteq X\\times Y.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>A relation permits more than one admitted output for an input, and it need not supply an output for every input. A transformation contract specifies an input predicate <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math>, an output predicate <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, protected observations, and allowed losses. Its partial-correctness condition is</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\forall x,y:\\ P(x)\\land T(x,y)\\Rightarrow Q(y).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>This implication concerns successful transitions in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>. It neither promises an output for every admitted input nor bounds computation time. In particular, a relation with no transition can satisfy the implication without producing a useful result. A practical implementation must separately distinguish successful output, refusal, ambiguity, insufficient budget, and execution failure.</p>\n<p>A <strong>protected observation</strong> consists of interpretation functions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_X:X\\to O</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_Y:Y\\to O</annotation></semantics></math> into a common observation space. Preservation means</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nP(x)\\land T(x,y)\\Rightarrow p_X(x)=p_Y(y).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The observations are selected for the task. They may include a numerical value, referent, condition, attribution, or unresolved uncertainty. Equality of two unknown observations preserves the recorded uncertainty; it does not establish the unknown proposition. Selecting and justifying the observation functions is itself an obligation: the equations do not discover which semantic features the task requires.</p>\n<p>Not all transformations require equality. A projection may retain only a declared subset of observations; an inference may establish an output proposition from input premises under a rule. The contract must state which relation is required. Calling every relation “equivalence” would erase the distinction between preserving information and deriving a consequence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-composition-and-preservation\">3. Composition and preservation</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-compatible-partial-correctness-contracts-compose\">Proposition 1. Compatible partial-correctness contracts compose</h3>\n<p>Suppose <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_1\\subseteq X\\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{P\\}T_1\\{Q\\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_2\\subseteq Y\\times Z</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{R\\}T_2\\{S\\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(y)\\Rightarrow R(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for every intermediate state, then their relational composition satisfies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{P\\}(T_2\\circ T_1)\\{S\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> For an admitted composed transition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x,z)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_1(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_2(y,z)</annotation></semantics></math>. From <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the first contract yields <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. The compatibility assumption yields <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second contract yields <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(z)</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>\n<p>This proposition does not prove total correctness. A second operation may have no successful transition for an intermediate state even when its partial-correctness implication holds. Existence of a result, termination, and resource sufficiency require additional assumptions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-a-shared-protected-observation-survives-a-finite-chain\">Proposition 2. A shared protected observation survives a finite chain</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>×</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_i\\subseteq X_{i-1}\\times X_i</annotation></semantics></math> for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\leq i\\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. Each step satisfies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{P_i\\}T_i\\{Q_i\\}</annotation></semantics></math> and preserves observations <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i^-:X_{i-1}\\to O</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i^+:X_i\\to O</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_i</annotation></semantics></math> holds. At every junction require</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nQ_i(x)\\Rightarrow P_{i+1}(x),\n\\qquad\nQ_i(x)\\Rightarrow p_i^+(x)=p_{i+1}^-(x),\n\\quad 1\\leq i&lt;n.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>For any successful chain <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_i(x_{i-1},x_i)</annotation></semantics></math> beginning with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_1(x_0)</annotation></semantics></math>, these conditions imply</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\np_1^-(x_0)=p_n^+(x_n).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Partial correctness of the first step yields <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_1(x_1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The first junction implication supplies <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_2(x_1)</annotation></semantics></math>; induction gives every subsequent precondition and postcondition. Each step therefore preserves its observation on the actual transition. The second junction implication equates the preceding output observation with the following input observation on each intermediate state. Substituting those within-step and between-step equalities gives the displayed endpoint equality. ∎</p>\n<p>Both junction requirements are necessary to this argument. On <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{0,1\\}</annotation></semantics></math>, take two identity transitions, with identity observations at both interfaces of the first step and Boolean negation at both interfaces of the second. Each step preserves its own observations, but the chain starts with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math> and ends with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-x</annotation></semantics></math>; its observation junction fails. Separately, let the first transition be identity with an unrestricted precondition and the second map both inputs to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1</annotation></semantics></math>, with precondition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1</annotation></semantics></math>. With identity observations throughout, both local contracts preserve observations on their admitted inputs. Nevertheless the composed path <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\to0\\to1</annotation></semantics></math> changes the observation because the second precondition is not met.</p>\n<p>The common-observation requirement is substantive. If one operation preserves a numerical value but drops its interpretation, and another preserves only the remaining text, the chain has not preserved a quantity with units. Local declarations about different properties do not combine into a stronger end-to-end claim.</p>\n<p>A second counterexample concerns recipients. An operation may omit a condition because its current recipient already knows it. Passing the result to a different recipient changes that precondition. A technically intact record of the earlier check cannot restore a condition that the new recipient lacks.</p>\n<p>The two propositions provide sufficient compositional conditions. Failure of a stated premise prevents using that argument; it does not imply that every particular run violating the premise must lose its endpoint observation. A counterexample to a conditional proposition would have to satisfy all its premises while falsifying its conclusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-equivalence-projection-and-residual-information\">4. Equivalence, projection, and residual information</h2>\n<p>For a fixed observation function <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nx\\sim_p x&#39;\\iff p(x)=p(x&#39;).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>This is an equivalence relation because equality is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive. It is an equivalence of declared observations, not of object identity or all meaning. Enlarging the observation set can split an equivalence class that was adequate for a narrower task.</p>\n<p>A transformation descends to equivalence classes only if it respects the chosen relation. For a deterministic transformation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>, this requires that <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\sim_p x&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> imply <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)\\sim_{p&#39;}f(x&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>. For a deterministic partial transformation, its domain must also contain every equivalent representative or none of them. Otherwise admission or output depends on which representative was selected, and replacing one representative by another is not justified. For a nondeterministic relation, a quotient construction requires its own stated criterion for the reachable output classes; the deterministic-function condition alone does not specify that construction.</p>\n<p>A <strong>projection</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_J</annotation></semantics></math> retains observations indexed by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J</annotation></semantics></math>. The coordinate-projection law must respect the input and output types. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_K</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of records indexed by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>J</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_J^K:X_K\\to X_J</annotation></semantics></math> be restriction to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\subseteq K</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\subseteq J\\subseteq K</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mo>∘</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>J</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>J</mi><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>id</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\pi_I^J\\circ\\pi_J^K=\\pi_I^K,\n\\qquad \\pi_J^J=\\operatorname{id}_{X_J}.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Thus, restricting first to the intermediate coordinates and then to the retained coordinates has the same result as restricting directly to the retained coordinates. Applying the identity restriction on the resulting space makes no further change. This is the typed form of repeated coordinate selection. The expression <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>J</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>∘</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>J</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_J^K\\circ\\pi_J^K</annotation></semantics></math> need not even be defined when the domain and codomain differ. An idempotence equation for a projection on one state space requires a separately defined endomorphism of that space. None of these laws follows merely from calling an arbitrary summarizer a projection.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-a-noninjective-transformation-cannot-have-a-left-inverse\">Proposition 3. A noninjective transformation cannot have a left inverse</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:X\\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> map two distinct inputs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1\\ne x_2</annotation></semantics></math> to the same output. There is no function <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g:Y\\to X</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(f(x))=x</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Such a function would require the same value <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(f(x_1))=g(f(x_2))</annotation></semantics></math> to equal both distinct inputs. ∎</p>\n<p>A retained residual <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(x)</annotation></semantics></math> can distinguish inputs that the transformation alone merges. The pair <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(f(x),\\rho(x))</annotation></semantics></math> is an augmented encoding:</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nh(x)=(f(x),\\rho(x)).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>A reconstruction function on the image of this encoding recovers every input exactly if and only if the augmented encoding is injective on the declared source domain. Necessity follows by the same collision argument as Proposition 3. For sufficiency, assign each attained pair its unique preimage. This is a set-theoretic statement about reconstruction on the image; it supplies neither an algorithm nor a bound on time or storage. An actual consumer also needs a decoder it can execute and access to the correct residual.</p>\n<p>For example, a projection of a two-component record can be reversed when the omitted component is stored as residual information. That restoration uses additional retained information. A residual that repeats only the retained component does not separate the lost alternatives. Neither example shows that compression creates information or that every summary is reversible.</p>\n<p>Bancilhon and Spyratos use a view together with a complement to determine the database state and constrain view-update translation [3]. That established construction motivates the distinction between a projection and the information needed to reconstruct its source. The present model states a reconstruction obligation for knowledge artifacts; it does not establish the view-update laws or a general bidirectional transformation for natural-language summaries.</p>\n<p>Loss records must therefore distinguish intentionally omitted content, unresolved interpretation, and information retained elsewhere. A link to a residual helps only when the recipient can retrieve and interpret it within the permitted scope and resource budget.</p>\n<p>A finite Boolean model gives an exact special case of shared content and residual information. Fix a common finite propositional signature and classical truth-assignment semantics for all formulas in this construction. Write <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(F)</annotation></semantics></math> for the models of formula <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math>. Entailment is inclusion of model sets. The formula <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=F\\lor G</annotation></semantics></math> is a strongest common consequence of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math>: both entail <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and any consequence of both is true throughout <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(F)\\cup M(G)=M(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>¬</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_F=\\neg C\\lor F</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo>¬</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC\\land R_F=C\\land(\\neg C\\lor F)\\equiv C\\land F\\equiv F,\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>because <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math> entails <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>. The reconstruction is logical equivalence in this fixed language, not recovery of the original spelling, syntax tree, or source document. It does not establish economy: <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_F=F</annotation></semantics></math> also reconstructs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math>, and a claim that another residual is smaller requires a separate size measure and comparison. The strongest common consequence is defined by entailment, not by textual similarity or a useful theme between arbitrary documents.</p>\n<p>The residual convention is not unique outside <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>: changing a residual’s truth values there does not change its conjunction with the common consequence. An inconsistent premise set has no models and classically entails every formula. Operational admission must therefore separately require an appropriate consistent, nonempty interpretation rather than turn vacuous entailment into a recommendation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-transfer-between-tasks-and-domains\">5. Transfer between tasks and domains</h2>\n<p>Transfer is a directed operation. A rule applicable under conditions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> can be used for a target only after those conditions have been mapped and checked. Similar vocabulary is a candidate-generation signal, not a proof that the required relation is preserved.</p>\n<p>Consider a source rule</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC(x)\\Rightarrow P(x).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>To infer <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P&#39;(y)</annotation></semantics></math> in a target representation, one needs a mapping between the relevant source and target objects, justification of the corresponding condition, and a bridge showing that the source conclusion supports <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>. Without such a bridge, the source rule remains a statement about its original interpretation.</p>\n<p>An abstract counterexample is sufficient. Two systems can share a scalar variable called “size,” while one has output proportional to size and the other has output proportional to its square. With both proportionality constants set to one, they agree at size one but differ at size two: the outputs are two and four. This is a constructed mathematical example, not an observed experiment. Matching the variable name and one point does not justify transferring the linear rule. A usable transfer contract identifies the governing relation and its domain, rather than relying on surface similarity.</p>\n<p>Units provide a limited positive case. If two units describe the same dimension and their relation is an exact rational factor, exact conversion preserves the represented quantity. The conclusion depends on dimension compatibility, the factor, and exact arithmetic. Same-dimension labels alone do not establish that multiplicative relation. Nor does conversion validate an incorrectly extracted input value, remove measurement uncertainty, or justify an unrelated domain equation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-records-implementation-and-tests\">6. Records, implementation, and tests</h2>\n<p>A transformation record should identify the input and output revisions, declared operation, active preconditions, protected observations, verification result, and any loss or residual. Provenance is useful here because it makes dependencies inspectable [2]. Provenance alone does not establish that the operation satisfied its contract.</p>\n<p>An implementation claiming compliance must connect its actual outputs to the relation used in the argument. A contract written next to a model does not constrain the model’s behavior unless execution is checked or the implementation is independently justified. A finite checker can establish only properties in its supported language and bounded domain. Unsupported rules remain unsupported; they do not become valid because the system produces a plausible explanation.</p>\n<p>Tests should include incompatible intermediate types, a dropped exception, changed recipients, a noninjective projection offered as reversible, an omitted residual, and a transfer with matching labels but incompatible relations. Positive tests should vary irrelevant features while keeping protected observations fixed. Negative tests should change one material assumption at a time and check the declared refusal, qualification, or failure behavior.</p>\n<p>A test report must identify what it exercised: the abstract relation, a finite checker, or the actual implementation. It must also state the carrier, parameter ranges, assumptions, and unit counted. Enumerating a finite model checks that model within those bounds. It neither constitutes an empirical trial on human meanings nor shows that a deployed model enforces the same conditions. A premise-violating example tests the boundary of the justification; a genuine refutation of a proposition requires all its premises and a false conclusion.</p>\n<p>The propositions establish the selected composition, preservation, and reconstruction properties under their explicit assumptions. They are not a completeness claim about all knowledge transformations. No empirical claim is made that a particular extractor or generator reliably supplies those assumptions. The corresponding measurement question is the frequency and consequence of violations on independently constructed transformations, with separate reporting for interpretation, execution, and verification errors.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">7. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">executable projections and revision study</a> reports finite consumer checks for partial extraction and addressed revision. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency closure and package delivery study</a> reports a fixed dependency graph, preserved artifact payloads, and a historical preliminary delivery decision. These controls concern the stated packages and projections; neither study evaluates every transformation relation or cross-domain transfer proposed here.</p>\n<p>The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> defines project terminology. <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a> supplies the interpretation boundary; <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled knowledge reuse</a> applies the preservation argument to previously prepared artifacts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-conclusion\">8. Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Preservation is a claim about declared observations under explicit conditions, not about unchanged words alone. Partial-correctness contracts compose when the first operation’s postcondition supplies the next operation’s precondition. Endpoint preservation additionally requires adjacent operations to agree on the observation they exchange. These conditions justify successful chains; they do not guarantee a result, termination, or compliance by a language model.</p>\n<p>Projection and reconstruction require equally explicit boundaries. Coordinate restriction obeys a typed composition law. A noninjective transformation cannot recover every original input without distinguishing residual information, and an injective augmented encoding establishes only set-theoretic reconstructability until an executable decoder and accessible residual are supplied. In the Boolean illustration, reconstruction means logical equivalence, not restoration of source bytes or a demonstrated reduction in size.</p>\n<p>The resulting method is to identify the property a task needs, state the permitted transformation and its assumptions, and check the actual result at the relevant boundary. Where a condition is unsupported, the preservation claim must be limited rather than inferred from a plausible output or a provenance record. Whether particular extractors and agents reliably meet these obligations remains an empirical question for separately specified studies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Hoare, C. A. R. “An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming.” <em>Communications of the ACM</em> 12(10), 1969, 576–580, 583. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259</a></li>\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n<li>Bancilhon, F., and Spyratos, N. “Update Semantics of Relational Views.” <em>ACM Transactions on Database Systems</em> 6(4), 1981, 557–575. <a href=\"https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~david/cs848s18/constant-complement.pdf\">Original article</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question-and-relation-to-prior-work","title":"1. Research question and relation to prior work"},{"level":2,"id":"2-a-transformation-model","title":"2. A transformation model"},{"level":2,"id":"3-composition-and-preservation","title":"3. Composition and preservation"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-compatible-partial-correctness-contracts-compose","title":"Proposition 1. Compatible partial-correctness contracts compose"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-a-shared-protected-observation-survives-a-finite-chain","title":"Proposition 2. A shared protected observation survives a finite chain"},{"level":2,"id":"4-equivalence-projection-and-residual-information","title":"4. Equivalence, projection, and residual information"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-a-noninjective-transformation-cannot-have-a-left-inverse","title":"Proposition 3. A noninjective transformation cannot have a left inverse"},{"level":2,"id":"5-transfer-between-tasks-and-domains","title":"5. Transfer between tasks and domains"},{"level":2,"id":"6-records-implementation-and-tests","title":"6. Records, implementation, and tests"},{"level":2,"id":"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"7. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"8-conclusion","title":"8. Conclusion"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"constrained-transformations-and-the-limits-of-knowledge-transfer","math_count":84,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/constrained-transformations/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/constrained-transformations/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"6377a15c2d46ba82472342da4d77267c47aae5b1be1d7964f8edd373fa3b0eea","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"foundations"},{"canonical":true,"id":"retrieval-and-context-composition","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/retrieval-and-context-composition/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/","title":"Retrieval, Coverage, and Context Composition under Resource Limits","titleRu":"Retrieval, Coverage, and Context Composition under Resource Limits","summary":"Search coverage, finite context selection, dependency closure and the limits of greedy composition.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A relevant passage is not necessarily a sufficient context, and a complete dependency graph is not evidence that its conditions survived delivery. This paper asks what can be justified about a context assembled from a declared search result under resource limits. We separate search assurance, support for requirements, feasibility of the delivered representation, and verification of the resulting answer. A finite model represents alternative groups of jointly necessary grounds, mandatory dependency closure, transformations, and measured resource budgets. We make explicit that support in the selected closure must be preserved or re-established in the actual representation; a manifest alone does not discharge that obligation. Three conditional results describe a scoped optimum from complete finite enumeration, a set-cover special case, and the classical cardinality-greedy guarantee under submodularity. Counterexamples distinguish joint-ground complementarity, mandatory coverage, shared costs, and loss during rendering. The contribution is a bounded account of selection and delivery obligations, not a new general approximation algorithm. The method combines deductive arguments with separately identified synthetic verification examples. Neither these examples nor exact selection over a finite decision space establish semantic annotation correctness, exhaustive retrieval from an unexamined corpus, recipient understanding, or empirical superiority of a language model.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> retrieval; context composition; coverage; dependency closure; resource budget; submodularity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-the-question-between-retrieval-and-answering\">1. The question between retrieval and answering</h2>\n<p>A retrieval system proposes material that may support an answer. The original retrieval-augmented generation formulation combines external retrieval with a generative model [1]. That architecture does not itself certify that every necessary condition reached the generator or survived its answer.</p>\n<p>Our question is: <strong>what can be certified about a bounded context assembled from a declared search result?</strong> Four judgments are needed. Did the search procedure examine the required scope? Do the available grounds support the requirements? Does the actual delivered representation retain that support while meeting resource and admission conditions? Does the final answer preserve the required content?</p>\n<p>These judgments are related but not interchangeable. Returning ten plausible passages does not prove that an eleventh exception does not exist. Fitting a token limit does not prove that the recipient notices a warning. A completed search does not make contradictory sources consistent. Similarly, computing a dependency closure does not show that a renderer transmitted every material condition.</p>\n<p>The contribution is to separate those obligations within one finite composition model and to identify where a conclusion exceeds its premises. Set-cover difficulty and the submodular-greedy approximation bound are established results [2, 3], not new complexity or approximation theorems claimed here. Their role is to delimit what transfers to this problem and what does not.</p>\n<p>The method is deductive: define the selection and delivery objects, state conditional results, and test omitted assumptions with constructed cases. A separately documented verification package exercises finite abstract instances. It is not a trial of a live retriever, a model-generated summary, or a deployed Knowledge Contract service. The engineering connections and unmeasured questions remain explicit in Section 7.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-query-scope-and-coverage\">2. Query, scope, and coverage</h2>\n<p>A query record fixes an intent, referents, time interpretation, requirements, permitted source scope, and answer form. Its requirements are obligations to establish or communicate, rather than merely words to match. A request for one supporting example has different obligations from a request for all examples, a count, or a statement that none exists.</p>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> be the declared source snapshot and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> the returned candidate set. Search assurance concerns the procedure that produced <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>. An exhaustive finite scan under a specified predicate can support completeness within <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math>; a top-ranked sample generally cannot. Changes to access visibility, scope membership, predicates, or ranking rules can change the answer even if previously returned records are unchanged.</p>\n<p>Coverage is assessed relative to a pinned requirement set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math>. For each requirement we distinguish supported, partial, unsupported, conflicted, and unknown. These are reporting categories with explicit criteria, not a universal many-valued logic. “Unsupported in this package” does not mean false, absent from the whole corpus, or impossible to establish.</p>\n<p>Coverage should be recorded at three boundaries: the registered source base, the retrieved candidates, and the delivered package. A fourth check concerns the actual answer or action proposal. Keeping these reports distinct locates loss: missing from the base, missed by retrieval, omitted during composition, or ignored during generation.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 1. Assessment boundaries and claims they do not establish alone.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Boundary</th>\n<th>What is assessed</th>\n<th>What that assessment does not establish alone</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Source and search scope</td>\n<td>Which snapshot, visibility rules, predicate, and search procedure were examined</td>\n<td>That an unexamined source or hidden record contains no relevant exception</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Candidate support</td>\n<td>Whether a valid group supports each pinned requirement</td>\n<td>That all supporting conditions survive transformation and delivery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Delivered context</td>\n<td>The actual representation, retained obligations, admission conditions, and measured cost</td>\n<td>That the recipient understood or used those obligations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Answer or action proposal</td>\n<td>Whether the actual result satisfies the stated task checks</td>\n<td>General correctness on other tasks or authority to execute an action</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A status applies to its exact boundary and revision. Failure to find support at one boundary must not be relabeled as a proof of absence at another. Unknown validity remains an outstanding assessment, not a passing support group.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-composition-with-joint-grounds-and-dependencies\">3. Composition with joint grounds and dependencies</h2>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite set of candidate object revisions. For a selected subset <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>cl</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{cl}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> denote its mandatory dependency closure under a fixed, declared graph. A requirement <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math> can have alternative supporting groups <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal G_r</annotation></semantics></math>. A group contains jointly necessary grounds, not merely individually relevant fragments.</p>\n<p>The dependency graph ranges over a fixed finite universe containing the candidate revisions and the dependency revisions they may require. Closure includes the selected roots and follows every declared mandatory edge. A reference whose required revision is unavailable is a missing obligation, not a leaf that can silently be ignored. Closure completeness is relative to this declared graph; it does not prove that every material semantic dependency has been discovered.</p>\n<p>Under fixed assessments, define support by</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Supports</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇔</mo><mo>∃</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝒢</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mi>G</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo>cl</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo>∧</mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mo>Valid</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Supports}(r,A)\n\\iff \\exists G\\in\\mathcal G_r:\\ G\\subseteq\\operatorname{cl}(A)\n\\ \\land\\ \\operatorname{Valid}(G,r).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The validity predicate includes the relevant interpretation and applicability checks. Listing a group does not prove that its members support the requirement. In empirical use, that judgment requires independently assessed evidence or a sound supported rule.</p>\n<p>A composition decision <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math> includes selected content, permitted transformations, ordering, modality, and delivery form. Its actual representation is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Render</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Render}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>, with declared cost <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Feasibility requires mandatory support, all other admission conditions, and</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo>Render</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nc(\\operatorname{Render}(z))\\leq B.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>For feasibility, mandatory support must concern the <strong>actual delivered representation</strong>, not only the roots or their abstract closure. Write <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>DeliveredSupports</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{DeliveredSupports}(r,z)</annotation></semantics></math> when a valid supporting group and its task-relevant mandatory conditions are retained in, or correctly transformed into, the representation produced by the decision. That assessment needs a justified preservation argument or checks on the actual output. It is not inferred from membership in a manifest. A separately permitted retrieval route may satisfy an obligation only if the consumer can actually use it within the declared access and resource limits.</p>\n<p>A decision may therefore satisfy the closure-based support formula and still be infeasible: a summarizer can drop an exception, a renderer can truncate a condition, or a linked residual can be unavailable to the recipient. Conversely, an authorized transformation need not preserve every source byte when it demonstrably preserves the required observations. The final answer remains a separate assessment even after delivered support passes.</p>\n<p>The budget may be a vector: tokens, bytes, recipient time, or another measured resource. In that case the budget inequality is interpreted componentwise, with each measured component compared to its corresponding bound. Different units should not be silently added. An unmeasured required component is unresolved. A scalar objective requires a declared conversion or priority rule.</p>\n<p>Costs need not be additive over fragments. Two selected objects can share a dependency, so summing their separately closed sizes double-counts it. Conversely, a delivery format can introduce headings, repeated legends, or fixed overhead. The delivered representation, rather than the unformatted fragments, is the relevant accounting object.</p>\n<p>Least graph closure is also relative to the chosen granularity. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/#counterexample-to-claim-level-minimality\">projection study</a> found extra claims inside a shared qualification even when their dedicated nodes were absent from the selected closure. A least entity set therefore need not be claim-minimal or cheapest to deliver.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-exactness-and-special-case-guarantees\">4. Exactness and special-case guarantees</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-complete-finite-selection-gives-a-scoped-optimum\">Proposition 1. Complete finite selection gives a scoped optimum</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite, explicitly declared decision space. Suppose feasibility and objective value are evaluated correctly for every member. If enumeration finishes and returns a feasible member with maximal objective, that member is optimal in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. If enumeration finishes with no feasible member, no feasible member exists in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Every possible decision in the declared space has been checked. A better feasible decision or any feasible decision in the second case would therefore have appeared in the enumeration, contradicting the stated result. ∎</p>\n<p>This establishes neither completeness of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math> relative to all possible summaries nor correctness of semantic annotations. The declared decisions must include the representation and transformation alternatives to which the optimum claim applies; enumerating only roots is insufficient when unexamined renderings can change feasibility or value. If execution stops early and a feasible candidate has been verified, that candidate is an incumbent. If none has been verified, there is no established incumbent. Early stopping alone establishes neither optimality nor infeasibility; an independent sound bound or certificate would be a separate justification.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-a-restricted-composition-problem-contains-set-cover\">Proposition 2. A restricted composition problem contains set cover</h3>\n<p>Consider unit-cost, independent packages, no transformations or dependencies, and the requirement to cover every obligation using at most <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math> packages. Given a set-cover instance with universe <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math> and subsets <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_1,\\ldots,F_n</annotation></semantics></math>, create one requirement per element of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math> and one package per <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_i</annotation></semantics></math>, supporting exactly those requirements. The construction is polynomial, and selected packages cover all requirements exactly when their corresponding subsets cover <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p>This reduction transfers the known combinatorial difficulty of set cover [2] to this special case. It does not imply that every practical query is difficult, that extraction itself is formally solved, or that a heuristic has a particular approximation ratio.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-the-cardinality-greedy-bound-requires-submodularity\">Proposition 3. The cardinality-greedy bound requires submodularity</h3>\n<p>For this proposition the ground set is finite, each selected element consumes one cardinality unit, and greedy evaluates exact marginal gains. It takes the permitted number of steps unless every element has already been selected. In the latter case, monotonicity makes the full ground set optimal. A rule that stops after seeing zero marginal gains without establishing the proposition’s premises is not covered.</p>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonnegative, monotone, submodular set function with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⌀</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\varnothing)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. With at most <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\geq1</annotation></semantics></math> selected elements and no other constraints, greedy selection by greatest marginal gain achieves at least</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>OPT</mo><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>OPT</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\left(1-\\left(1-\\frac1k\\right)^k\\right)\\operatorname{OPT}\n\\geq (1-1/e)\\operatorname{OPT}.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Argument.</strong> Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O</annotation></semantics></math> be an optimal set. At step <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math>, monotonicity gives <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>OPT</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{OPT}=f(O)\\leq f(A_i\\cup O)</annotation></semantics></math>. Submodularity bounds the gain from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_i\\cup O</annotation></semantics></math> by the sum of at most <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math> current single-element marginal gains. The best next gain is therefore at least <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo>OPT</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\operatorname{OPT}-f(A_i))/k</annotation></semantics></math>. Each step reduces the remaining gap by a factor no greater than <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1/k</annotation></semantics></math>. Iterating from the empty set gives the bound. This is the classical result of Nemhauser, Wolsey, and Fisher [3], not a new theorem of the present model. ∎</p>\n<p>The result does not cover arbitrary size-based greedy selection, nonadditive costs, or arbitrary mandatory closure. Those settings need their own justified algorithms and assumptions. An approximation to an optional-value objective is not permission to miss a mandatory requirement. Before applying the bound to a derived objective, prove its stated properties on the actual ground set and show that its feasible family is the one in the proposition.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-counterexamples-to-convenient-assumptions\">5. Counterexamples to convenient assumptions</h2>\n<p>Joint grounds need not be submodular. Suppose a requirement needs both a rule <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> and a condition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⌀</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\varnothing)=f(\\{a\\})=f(\\{b\\})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(\\{a,b\\})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Adding <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math> to the empty set yields no gain; adding it after <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> yields one. This violates diminishing returns. Individual ranking can therefore discard both members of the only useful pair.</p>\n<p>Feasible root selections need not be downward-closed because coverage is mandatory. Consider one requirement whose only supporting group is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{a\\}</annotation></semantics></math>, no dependencies, and a budget large enough for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math>. The selection <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\{a\\}</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible, but its subset <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>⌀</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> does not cover the requirement. This counterexample uses the same closure-based support definition as §3.</p>\n<p>Selected roots and delivered objects must not be confused. Removing a required condition from a root set can leave its closure unchanged because another selected root still requires it. Removing that condition from the actual delivered set is different: the resulting delivery may fail the closure obligation. A method justified for downward-closed feasible selections cannot be imported without checking which of these objects its guarantee concerns.</p>\n<p>Budget pressure does not remove these obligations. If two mandatory grounds and an exception cost seven units in total and the budget is five, deleting the exception produces a smaller but inadmissible package. A feasible reformulation might exist, but it must be constructed and checked. Failure of one heuristic to find it is not proof of impossibility.</p>\n<p>Shared dependencies illustrate a separate accounting issue. In a constructed instance, two roots each cost one unit and both require a condition costing three units. The sum of their separately closed costs is eight, but their union costs five when the shared condition is transmitted once and there is no other overhead. A format that repeats that condition has a different cost. This example refutes modular root-cost accounting for the union representation; it does not by itself refute submodularity of an objective. Joint grounds, shared costs, and mandatory feasibility are distinct reasons to check an algorithm’s hypotheses.</p>\n<p>The delivery boundary has its own counterexample. A root and its required exception may both belong to the computed closure, while the renderer transmits only the root. A closure-only checker can pass the selection even though the delivered context fails the requirement. The correct repair is to check retained support or construct a valid transformed representation, not to relabel the smaller delivery as complete.</p>\n<p>Finally, declared coverage can be invalidated by new information. Support is monotone only under explicitly fixed grounds, applicability judgments, and conflict rules. An added contradictory source or a changed requirement can require reassessment even when old passages remain available.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-stopping-and-measurement\">6. Stopping and measurement</h2>\n<p>A useful result reports its scope and stopping reason. Distinct outcomes include a verified complete package, an explicitly partial package, a need for clarification, insufficient resources, an exhausted search limit, and stale or conflicting evidence. Stopping after a limit does not establish absence. A useful partial answer is possible when the answer form permits it and its uncovered obligations remain visible.</p>\n<p>A complete-package claim should identify the selected supporting group for each mandatory requirement, the exact input and output revisions, the relevant preservation or output checks, and the resource measurements. A hash identifies bytes; it does not establish semantic validity. A solver that explored only part of its declared decision space reports that limit rather than treating an empty result as an infeasibility certificate.</p>\n<p>Evaluation should compare methods under the same requirement snapshot, source scope, delivery constraints, and outcome rubric. Relevant baselines include ranked selection with dependency repair, selection of complete supporting groups, and exact enumeration on small instances. Removing dependencies or truncating delivery supplies ablations, not necessarily competitive baselines.</p>\n<p>Measures should distinguish retrieval misses, invalid support groups, omitted dependencies, budget violations, and final-answer failures. Report denominators and uncertainty, including the number of cases that could not be adjudicated. Counting selected fragments or citing a larger source list does not measure sufficiency.</p>\n<p>The mathematical results here hold for the declared finite model. They establish neither universal end-to-end answer correctness nor measured superiority on people, models, or production workloads. Recipient understanding and attention require separate observation, even when representation cost is measured exactly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">7. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency closure and package delivery study</a> supplies exhaustive fixed-graph closure checks for all-required dependencies, plus historical layout and preliminary delivery checks. Its root-subset enumeration is not a budgeted composition optimizer. Those historical studies do not evaluate alternative supporting groups or resource-constrained selection. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/reviews/retrieval-context/REVIEW.md\">finite verification note</a> accompanying this revision adds newly authored abstract cases and a reproducible checker for explicit selection and delivery boundaries. It is not a historical replay, a semantic extraction evaluation, or a production selection study. It supplies no measured comparison of language models or end-to-end retrieval methods. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection study</a> supplies the distinct counterexample to claim-level minimality. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">contract profile conformance study</a> concerns structural validation, which must remain distinct from semantic sufficiency.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> gives common terminology. <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a> specifies evaluation obligations; <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">knowledge utility</a> explains why a composition objective must state its baseline, horizon, and resource accounting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-conclusion\">8. Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A retrieval result, an abstractly supported selection, a feasible delivered context, and a satisfactory final answer are different objects of assessment. Their checks must remain tied to the declared requirements, source snapshot, interpretation, representation, and recipient. In particular, a computed dependency closure cannot certify a delivery that loses a material condition.</p>\n<p>Complete enumeration provides an optimum only within a finite declared decision space and only with correct feasibility and value assessments. The set-cover reduction explains a restricted source of combinatorial difficulty. The classical greedy bound applies under its cardinality and submodularity assumptions, not to every budgeted selection procedure. Joint grounds, mandatory coverage, and actual representation costs must be examined separately.</p>\n<p>The practical result is a set of explicit proof and measurement obligations: justify support, verify its preservation into delivery, account for resources in their stated units, and report what a stopping condition leaves unresolved. The finite examples help detect particular mistakes in those obligations. They do not replace semantic assessment, independent review, or measurement of actual agent outcomes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Lewis, P., et al. “Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks.” <em>Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems</em> 33, 2020. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401\" class=\"uri\">https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401</a></li>\n<li>Karp, R. M. “Reducibility Among Combinatorial Problems.” In <em>Complexity of Computer Computations</em>, 1972, 85–103. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9</a></li>\n<li>Nemhauser, G. L., Wolsey, L. A., and Fisher, M. L. “An Analysis of Approximations for Maximizing Submodular Set Functions—I.” <em>Mathematical Programming</em> 14, 1978, 265–294. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01588971\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01588971</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-the-question-between-retrieval-and-answering","title":"1. The question between retrieval and answering"},{"level":2,"id":"2-query-scope-and-coverage","title":"2. Query, scope, and coverage"},{"level":2,"id":"3-composition-with-joint-grounds-and-dependencies","title":"3. Composition with joint grounds and dependencies"},{"level":2,"id":"4-exactness-and-special-case-guarantees","title":"4. Exactness and special-case guarantees"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-complete-finite-selection-gives-a-scoped-optimum","title":"Proposition 1. Complete finite selection gives a scoped optimum"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-a-restricted-composition-problem-contains-set-cover","title":"Proposition 2. A restricted composition problem contains set cover"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-the-cardinality-greedy-bound-requires-submodularity","title":"Proposition 3. The cardinality-greedy bound requires submodularity"},{"level":2,"id":"5-counterexamples-to-convenient-assumptions","title":"5. Counterexamples to convenient assumptions"},{"level":2,"id":"6-stopping-and-measurement","title":"6. Stopping and measurement"},{"level":2,"id":"7-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"7. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"8-conclusion","title":"8. Conclusion"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"retrieval-coverage-and-context-composition-under-resource-limits","math_count":48,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/retrieval-and-context-composition/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/retrieval-and-context-composition/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"e457fadcb17424e15083504a2babb2646aedc8aec7370048cd77854a8c7af64f","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"systems"},{"canonical":true,"id":"interaction-and-memory","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/interaction-and-memory/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/","title":"Interaction, Documents, and Memory with Explicit Revision Boundaries","titleRu":"Interaction, Documents, and Memory with Explicit Revision Boundaries","summary":"Versioned requirements, dialogue branches, memory and evidence of learning in agent interaction.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A conversation record does not by itself identify which requirements a result satisfies, authorize a correction, or justify persistent memory. This paper asks how an assistant can keep those boundaries inspectable while messages, tasks, documents, and memory change. We separate message content from interpreted communicative acts, Work Items from attempts, and temporary context from admitted memory. A versioned work state records requirements and their generation; a delivered document is assessed as a particular output rather than inferred correct from its sources. Three conditional propositions address stale-result admission, duplicate internal effects, and observations preserved by a narrow update. Their premises include non-reissued generations, an acceptance-time comparison, atomic event records, and correctly scoped state components. Constructed boundary cases show why matching requirement bytes, a prior check, or a plausible event log is insufficient. We also distinguish memory retention from current applicability and observed performance from learning. The method is deductive and scope-limited. It neither supplies a complete interpreter of human intent nor demonstrates concurrent production enforcement, improved recall, or learning. Its contribution is an explicit account of what must be interpreted, authorized, versioned, and checked before a conversational result can become current guidance.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> dialogue; Work Item; document revision; memory; requirements; learning assessment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question\">1. Research question</h2>\n<p>A conversation can contain instructions, quotations, corrections, tentative ideas, and reports of previous actions. It can also receive a delayed result after the user has changed the goal. If every new message simply overwrites one mutable state, the system cannot reliably explain which requirements a result satisfies or why a memory applies.</p>\n<p>We ask: <strong>how should interaction state change so that the scope of a statement, correction, or result remains inspectable?</strong> The proposed answer separates interpretation from authorized transition, and separates immediate dialogue context from persistent knowledge.</p>\n<p>The model builds on the distinctions among entities, activities, and agents in PROV-DM [1]. That source supplies a provenance vocabulary, not a guarantee that an assistant correctly interpreted an instruction or executed a permitted transition. This paper applies explicit identity, scope, and assessment boundaries to interaction. It does not claim that versioning or transaction atomicity originate here.</p>\n<p>The contribution has three parts: a separation of the objects that change during interaction; conditional arguments about three state transitions; and boundary cases that identify what those arguments leave open. The method is deductive: define the state, state the premises, derive the bounded consequence, and test the effect of omitting a premise. Practical adequacy still requires independently interpreted conversations and checks on the actual implementation. A structured event log alone establishes neither.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-messages-acts-and-work\">2. Messages, acts, and work</h2>\n<p>A <strong>message</strong> is a received or generated representation with sender attribution, time, and content. A <strong>communicative act</strong> is an interpreted function of some content: a request, correction, question, proposal, acknowledgment, or report. One message can contain several acts. An act record should identify its target, scope, and uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Quoted content is not automatically an instruction from the present speaker. Likewise, a statement that an action occurred is evidence of that report, not proof of execution. Interpretation assigns a candidate role; authorization determines whether the resulting transition is permitted.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Work Item</strong> is a bounded objective with requirements, constraints, expected outputs, and acceptance conditions. <code>WorkItem</code> is a serialization spelling of that same entity, not a second concept. An <strong>attempt</strong> is one execution toward the objective. A document or other <strong>output</strong> is a result of an attempt. A message can create or modify a Work Item without itself constituting an execution attempt.</p>\n<p>Let the active work state be</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>O</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nW=(i,g,R,C,O,A),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math> is identity, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math> is a requirement generation, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R</annotation></semantics></math> requirements, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> constraints, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O</annotation></semantics></math> output expectations, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math> acceptance conditions. A material change creates a new generation whose identifier is never reissued within that Work Item, including after rollback or restoration of earlier requirements. A strictly increasing counter is one implementation. A requirements-content hash alone does not distinguish a later return to earlier content.</p>\n<p>The identity is part of the comparison. Equal generation numbers in different Work Items do not identify the same assignment. A branch or restored task must preserve a distinguishable task-and-generation scope rather than silently reuse an old identity pair. This is an identity obligation, not a requirement that every implementation use one global counter.</p>\n<p>Earlier work need not be discarded. An earlier output can be re-evaluated for the active generation while its original provenance remains intact. The new assessment must concern that exact output, target generation, and intended use; a passing assessment for another output is not transferable merely because their labels match. The active generation must still be checked at acceptance. Matching a generation also does not decide which of several attempts for that same generation should become current. That requires a separately stated selection rule and any applicable output checks.</p>\n<p>Understanding, agreement, evidence, and authority remain separate. A user can understand an answer and reject its conclusion. An authorized owner can accept a document despite an acknowledged evidential limitation. Neither act changes the truth of its factual propositions by definition.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-context-and-memory-layers\">3. Context and memory layers</h2>\n<p>We distinguish four layers by their use and admission rules, not by their physical storage location.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 1. Interaction layers and the distinction each must preserve.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>What it records</th>\n<th>What its presence does not establish</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Immediate conversation context</td>\n<td>Messages and candidate interpretations needed for the current exchange</td>\n<td>A standing instruction or persistent preference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Current Work Item state</td>\n<td>The active objective, generation, requirements, and constraints</td>\n<td>Completion of an attempt or correctness of an output</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Durable user or project memory</td>\n<td>Retained content with provenance, scope, and assessment</td>\n<td>Applicability to every later task or recipient</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Externally sourced knowledge</td>\n<td>Attributed material and its interpretation</td>\n<td>Truth, present authority, or permission to retain and reuse it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Each layer therefore needs its own admission, revision, and retrieval rules. Keeping all four in one database does not remove their logical differences.</p>\n<p>A memory record contains content, provenance, applicability scope, assessment state, and a revision or revocation relation. A statement intended for one task does not become a permanent preference merely because it is repeated. Widening its scope requires a justified transition, such as an explicit instruction or a separately supported generalization.</p>\n<p>Memory admission is therefore a decision with conditions. Relevant conditions can include attribution, intended persistence, consistency with current instructions, permission to retain, and a defined use scope. Unknown mandatory conditions do not count as satisfied. Retrieval later must check whether the record remains applicable to the present task and recipient.</p>\n<p>Memory has dependencies. A derived preference, summary, or plan may rely on earlier records whose revision changes its basis. Current use, archival availability, and storage retention are separate decisions. A historical record may be retained for a permitted audit purpose while being excluded from current guidance. This distinction does not itself authorize retention, override an erasure obligation, or give a later recipient access. Those conditions remain subject to the applicable policy.</p>\n<p>Repeated retrieval is not a new assessment. A record can be returned faithfully yet be unsuitable for the present task because its scope changed, its basis was revoked, or its intended persistence was never established. When a mandatory condition is unknown, neither repetition nor storage duration changes that condition into a pass.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-documents-as-evaluated-outputs\">4. Documents as evaluated outputs</h2>\n<p>A document is a versioned representation, not merely a string emitted during conversation. Its assessment has several axes: source fidelity, factual support, satisfaction of requirements, internal consistency, readability, accessibility, and acceptance for a specific use. A fluent document can fail a mandatory requirement; a faithful transcription can be poorly organized.</p>\n<p>Generation and editing therefore have a final-output obligation. The actual document must be inspected after assembly, including headings, references, tables, images, and conditions that rendering can omit or detach from their scope. Checking only the retrieved sources or an intermediate outline leaves the delivered output unevaluated.</p>\n<p>An assessment should name the exact document revision and representation it inspected. A Markdown-source check is not automatically a PDF-layout check; a valid intermediate file is not evidence that the recipient received that file. The required checks depend on the intended use, but their subject cannot silently change between assessment and delivery. A content identifier can bind a check to bytes; it does not by itself establish the document’s meaning or accessibility.</p>\n<p>A local edit also has both local and global effects. Replacing one section can change cross-references, definitions, totals, or the force of a conclusion elsewhere. The edit record should state its intended scope, while dependency information identifies the additional checks needed. A claim that “only one paragraph changed” is not evidence that every unaffected-looking paragraph remains valid.</p>\n<p>Document summaries become a separate memory object when reused. A summary may omit details acceptable for its original audience but necessary for a later task. Reuse should apply the same applicability and dependency discipline as any other prepared knowledge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-conditional-transition-properties\">5. Conditional transition properties</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-generation-admission-prevents-a-specified-stale-update\">Proposition 1. Generation admission prevents a specified stale update</h3>\n<p>Suppose a result carries the Work Item identity and generation for which it was produced, and generation identifiers are unique and never reissued within that Work Item. Suppose the acceptance transition checks those identifiers against the active state at the same serialization point at which it makes the result current. Direct acceptance requires an exact match; otherwise a separate successful re-evaluation bound to the active generation is required. Then a result from an earlier generation cannot be accepted as current solely through its original completion event.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Non-reuse of generation identifiers makes an earlier generation different from the active one, even if the requirements later return to earlier content. Its completion event therefore fails the direct-admission comparison at acceptance. The only remaining path requires a new evaluation, so original completion alone is insufficient. ∎</p>\n<p>This property depends on correct identifiers and enforcement at the transition boundary. It does not guarantee that the interpreter recognized a material requirement change or that the re-evaluation is semantically correct.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-recorded-duplicate-events-need-not-duplicate-effects\">Proposition 2. Recorded duplicate events need not duplicate effects</h3>\n<p>Let an event handler serialize the check and creation of a stable event identifier so that at most one first commit for that identifier can succeed. The identifier, payload fingerprint, result or stable result reference, and internal effect commit in one atomic state change. A concurrent contender that loses the first-commit race performs no committed effect and reads the winning record. Subsequent handling of the same identifier and payload returns the stored result without applying the effect again; a different payload is rejected as a conflict.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Serialization permits at most one successful first commit. Atomicity binds that commit’s effect to its identifier, fingerprint, and result, and prevents an aborted contender from retaining an internal effect. Every later successful lookup takes the duplicate or conflict branch, neither of which applies a new effect. The duplicate branch can return the result because that result or its stable reference was committed with the identifier. ∎</p>\n<p>A unique identifier constraint can enforce the first-commit rule only when a conflicting transaction rolls back its whole internal effect and then reads the committed record. Independent atomic transactions without that uniqueness or serialization condition are insufficient: two first deliveries can both observe absence. The result establishes idempotent handling within the stated state boundary, not exactly-once execution of an external action whose system lies outside it. Fingerprint equality also presupposes the declared canonical payload comparison and collision assumptions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"dependency-directed-reassessment\">Dependency-directed reassessment</h3>\n<p>The general reachability result is stated once in <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/#proposition-3-complete-dependencies-identify-candidates-for-reassessment\">Revision and governance, Proposition 3</a>. Applied to memory and documents, it identifies output assessments that may need reconsideration after a represented dependency changes. The premise is a complete finite dependency graph; the conclusion is candidate identification, not a verdict that the affected output is now false. An omitted dependency defeats that premise even if traversal is correct.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-a-narrow-update-preserves-independent-state-components\">Proposition 3. A narrow update preserves independent state components</h3>\n<p>Let a transition write only a declared component set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J</annotation></semantics></math> of a product state and leave all other components unchanged. Then every observation depending solely on components outside <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Such an observation receives identical component values before and after the transition. ∎</p>\n<p>This syntactic preservation result requires correct scope. If two components are semantically coupled but that coupling is omitted, unchanged bytes do not guarantee an unchanged valid interpretation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-learning-correction-and-counterexamples\">6. Learning, correction, and counterexamples</h2>\n<p>A useful tutoring conversation distinguishes the learner’s own attempt, supplied assistance, observed performance, and the assessment being made. Repeating an explanation or accepting an answer does not establish learning. A learning claim requires a declared skill, assessment conditions, a time horizon, and evidence that discriminates retained ability from immediate assistance or memorized responses.</p>\n<p>This is a measurement obligation rather than a theory of human learning. Different studies may examine transfer, delayed retention, error correction, or independent task completion. Their outcomes should not be collapsed into one unqualified “learned” flag. The same separation applies when assessing an agent: a successful assisted attempt, a newly stored record, and improved performance on an independently specified task are different observations. This paper reports none of those as a measured improvement.</p>\n<p>Counterexamples test the boundaries of dialogue interpretation. A correction to one branch must not silently rewrite another branch. An ambiguous “use that version” should not become a verified referent without resolution. A delayed answer must not reinstate a withdrawn requirement. A quoted instruction must not acquire the authority of its surrounding message merely because it uses imperative wording.</p>\n<p>Further counterexamples concern memory and documents. A task-specific choice should not become an unrestricted preference; a revoked premise should not remain an unnoticed basis for a recommendation; and a successful local edit should not waive whole-document consistency checks. These cases can be specified without assuming any particular domain or model.</p>\n<h3 id=\"constructed-transition-boundary-cases\">Constructed transition-boundary cases</h3>\n<p>Consider requirements that change from A to B and later return to A. An old output and the active requirements can have matching content fingerprints while belonging to different generations. The direct-admission comparison still rejects the old generation. A new assessment may justify reuse, but changing the output label to the active generation is not such an assessment. Likewise, checking a matching generation and then accepting after an intervening change violates the shared-serialization-point premise of Proposition 1. Neither case refutes the proposition: each removes a stated premise or substitutes a weaker rule.</p>\n<p>For Proposition 2, the event identifier must be interpreted in its declared namespace and the recorded outcome must remain available throughout the claimed deduplication scope. Deleting that record and treating a replay as unseen can apply the effect again. A retention period can bound a claim; it cannot support unqualified deduplication of later replays after the identifying state is gone. Distinct identifiers for the same business action are another case: this proposition deduplicates identifiers and matching payloads, not all semantically equivalent intentions.</p>\n<p>A further boundary is an external action. Suppose an external service applies an action, but the local handler fails before recording the outcome. Retrying without a shared atomic boundary or an effective external idempotency mechanism can repeat the action. Recording local intent first does not alone prove that the action occurred. The two systems’ evidence and recovery protocol require their own argument; the internal atomicity proposition supplies no exactly-once guarantee for them.</p>\n<p>Finally, an observation in Proposition 3 must really depend only on unchanged components under the same interpretation. Updating a shared referent, an aliased object, or an external basis can defeat a claim of independence even if a nearby field’s bytes did not change. A declared write set is not proof of the actual write set. These are constructed boundary cases, not reported defects or measured frequencies in a deployed assistant.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-evidence-and-limits\">7. Evidence and limits</h2>\n<p>The model supports finite transition tests and independently annotated dialogue cases. A useful evaluation reports interpretation mistakes separately from transition-handling mistakes. Otherwise a correct event processor can hide a poor interpreter, or an accurate interpreter can be blamed for an unrelated state-management defect.</p>\n<p>This paper establishes only its conditional state properties. It reports no measured improvement in learning, long-term recall, or agent reliability. Structural validation cannot authenticate a speaker by itself, and a requirements map cannot prove that the final document satisfies its intended meaning. Those dependencies require their own evidence and must remain visible in a claim of end-to-end performance.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">executable projections and revision study</a> evaluates reader boundaries, selection of records for addressed revision, and finite partial projections. It does not test Work Item generation admission or concurrent event idempotence; no engineering study in this corpus supplies implementation evidence for Propositions 1–2. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial skill pilot</a> compares two produced versions of one text under disclosed editorial requirements. The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision and governance</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence and falsification</a> supply related definitions and assessment boundaries.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-conclusion\">8. Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Interaction requires more than retaining the transcript. A system must distinguish what was said, how it was interpreted, which transition was authorized, which requirement generation an output addresses, and whether retained information applies now. These distinctions make disagreement and uncertainty inspectable rather than silently converting them into accepted state.</p>\n<p>The three propositions establish conditional safeguards, not a complete theory of dialogue. Non-reissued generations and an acceptance-time comparison prevent the specified stale update. A serialized, atomic event record prevents duplicate internal effects within its stated identity and retention boundary. A narrow write preserves only observations that genuinely depend on unchanged components. None of these results establishes semantic interpretation, external exactly-once execution, or an automatic learning outcome.</p>\n<p>The practical consequence is to bind each assessment to its actual subject and scope, preserve historical provenance without treating it as current authority, and test the delivered representation rather than infer its quality from intermediate success. Independent semantic evaluation and implementation evidence remain separate requirements. When those observations are missing, the limitation must remain visible instead of being hidden behind a fluent answer, an event identifier, or a positive receipt.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question","title":"1. Research question"},{"level":2,"id":"2-messages-acts-and-work","title":"2. Messages, acts, and work"},{"level":2,"id":"3-context-and-memory-layers","title":"3. Context and memory layers"},{"level":2,"id":"4-documents-as-evaluated-outputs","title":"4. Documents as evaluated outputs"},{"level":2,"id":"5-conditional-transition-properties","title":"5. Conditional transition properties"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-generation-admission-prevents-a-specified-stale-update","title":"Proposition 1. Generation admission prevents a specified stale update"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-recorded-duplicate-events-need-not-duplicate-effects","title":"Proposition 2. Recorded duplicate events need not duplicate effects"},{"level":3,"id":"dependency-directed-reassessment","title":"Dependency-directed reassessment"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-a-narrow-update-preserves-independent-state-components","title":"Proposition 3. A narrow update preserves independent state components"},{"level":2,"id":"6-learning-correction-and-counterexamples","title":"6. Learning, correction, and counterexamples"},{"level":3,"id":"constructed-transition-boundary-cases","title":"Constructed transition-boundary cases"},{"level":2,"id":"7-evidence-and-limits","title":"7. Evidence and limits"},{"level":2,"id":"8-conclusion","title":"8. Conclusion"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"interaction-documents-and-memory-with-explicit-revision-boundaries","math_count":9,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/interaction-and-memory/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/interaction-and-memory/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"6bf13bff5230129c4f077515015f2926c65f6fb2d97f3e1d61e5eaa6b584a2c8","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"systems"},{"canonical":true,"id":"evidence-and-falsification","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/evidence-and-falsification/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/","title":"Evidence, Reasoning, and Falsification in Knowledge Systems","titleRu":"Evidence, Reasoning, and Falsification in Knowledge Systems","summary":"How claims, proofs, finite tests, counterexamples and comparative evaluations support different conclusions.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A validation report, formal proof, review, and deployment log support different claims. Treating them as interchangeable can turn a correct structural check into an unsupported assertion about meaning or practical benefit. This paper asks how a knowledge system can keep a claim accountable to the subject, scope, and limitations of its actual grounds. We distinguish five assessment axes and represent offered justifications as finite graphs of premises and inference applications. Two conditional propositions state when sound rules preserve true premises and when an in-scope countermodel refutes a universal claim. We then specify a falsification protocol that separates failures of the model, implementation, interpretation, and evaluation. The protocol binds observations to exact subjects, retains unresolved outcomes, distinguishes boundary tests from theorem refutations, and requires appropriate comparators and stopping rules. Constructed cases expose shared-oracle errors, vacuous exclusion tests, and changes of scope after a failure. The method is deductive and methodological, not a new empirical benchmark or an automatic semantic verifier. Its contribution is a precise account of what may be concluded from a result, including what remains unmeasured or unsupported.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> evidence; inference; falsification; claim scope; evaluation; counterexample.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question-and-evidential-distinctions\">1. Research question and evidential distinctions</h2>\n<p>Knowledge systems produce citations, validation reports, explanations, benchmark scores, approvals, and execution logs. These objects can be relevant evidence without answering the same question. A citation can identify a source without establishing the truth of its content. Approval can establish permission without establishing correctness. A passing test can establish an observed behavior without covering every possible behavior.</p>\n<p>We ask: <strong>how can a scientific and engineering claim remain accountable to the kind, scope, and limitations of its actual grounds?</strong> The proposed method connects each assertion to its subject, premises, assessment procedure, potential refuters, and revision conditions. It does not replace those grounds with one overall success label.</p>\n<p>Program-logic reasoning already makes correctness conditional on stated preconditions and semantics [1]. PROV-DM provides a vocabulary for the origins and production of entities [2]. We use these established distinctions without claiming that conditional proof or provenance originate here. The additional operational concern is that evidence about a source, model, checker, implementation, and deployment must not silently migrate between those subjects.</p>\n<p>The contribution has three parts: a separation of assessment questions; explicit boundaries for deductive and finite-test claims; and a falsification procedure that preserves both failures and uncertainty. The method is deductive and methodological. Section 3 proves two elementary conditional results, while the remaining sections specify obligations and constructed failure cases. The paper introduces neither a general algorithm for deciding natural-language truth nor measured evidence that the proposed organization improves agent performance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-claims-and-independent-assessment-axes\">2. Claims and independent assessment axes</h2>\n<p>A <strong>claim</strong> is a proposition or quantified assertion with an identified subject, scope, and interpretation. A useful record specifies its content, revision, claim kind, assumptions, supporting grounds, potential refuters, assessment method, and unresolved dependencies. Relevant versions, task distribution, population, time interval, and operating conditions belong to its scope when they can affect the conclusion.</p>\n<p>Claim kinds include definitional, deductive, empirical, comparative, causal, and normative. They are not mutually exclusive labels for an entire document. A paper can define a model, prove a conditional property, and propose an empirical hypothesis; each assertion still needs the appropriate grounds. A definition fixes terminology within the model. Naming a desired property in that definition does not show that a real system has it.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 1. Assessment axes that must not be silently substituted for one another.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Axis</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Insufficient substitute</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Authenticity and attribution</td>\n<td>Is the source or record authentic and correctly attributed?</td>\n<td>A plausible author label alone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Source fidelity</td>\n<td>Is the represented content faithful to that source?</td>\n<td>Valid fields or fluent wording alone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Evidential support</td>\n<td>Do the grounds support the proposition under its stated interpretation?</td>\n<td>A citation or positive receipt alone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Applicability</td>\n<td>Does the proposition apply to the present use?</td>\n<td>Correctness under a different date, task, or scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Authorization</td>\n<td>Is the proposed use permitted?</td>\n<td>Truth, usefulness, or technical capability alone</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These assessments need not agree. An accurately attributed false statement can be useful as an object of refutation. A true proposition can be inapplicable to a different date. An authorized decision can be poorly supported. A single confidence score preserves these distinctions only if its interpretation and aggregation rule actually represent them; the presence of a number is not such an explanation.</p>\n<p>Unknown is not false, and absence of a recorded refutation is not confirmation. An assessment can remain unresolved while a restricted informational output is permitted. A mandatory action precondition, however, is not satisfied merely because the system failed to find contrary evidence.</p>\n<p>An assessment also needs a precise subject. Identify the claim revision, assessed artifact or implementation revision, relevant configuration, input, representation, and assessment method as needed by that claim. A check of one file cannot silently become a check of another rendering or deployed build. A digest helps bind a record to bytes under a declared serialization and hash assumption; it does not authenticate the author, establish semantic equivalence, or show that those bytes were delivered to a recipient. Evidence may be reused only when its relationship to the new subject and scope is justified.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-justification-graphs-and-conditional-soundness\">3. Justification graphs and conditional soundness</h2>\n<p>A <strong>justification graph</strong> contains claim nodes, premise nodes, and explicit inference applications. An inference application identifies its rule, input claims, output claim, and relevant side conditions. It may also carry a computation or verification witness. The graph is an explanation of the offered justification, not a complete record of every internal mental or computational event.</p>\n<p>For the following result, take a finite directed acyclic derivation graph. Each leaf is a premise, and every other claim is obtained by a supported inference rule whose inputs appear earlier in a topological order. Interpret truth in one fixed domain model.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-sound-rules-preserve-true-premises-in-a-finite-derivation\">Proposition 1. Sound rules preserve true premises in a finite derivation</h3>\n<p>If every premise is true in the model, every used rule is sound for that model when its side conditions hold, and every side condition of every inference application is satisfied in the model, every derived claim is true in that model.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Order the graph topologically. The initial premise nodes are true by assumption. At each subsequent node, all input claims are true by the induction hypothesis and all side conditions hold by assumption. Soundness of that rule under those conditions makes the output true. Induction covers every node. ∎</p>\n<p>The result is conditional on premises, rule semantics, and discharged side conditions. For example, a rule that cancels a divisor cannot be used when its nonzero-divisor condition is false. The proposition does not show that the premises were correctly extracted, that the domain model is adequate, or that the implementation actually followed the rule. A computation with correctly applied arithmetic and an incorrectly read input remains an incorrect domain result.</p>\n<p>Circular support is outside this proof. A cycle that repeats a conclusion as its own premise supplies no independent foundation. Some systems admit explicitly defined fixed-point reasoning, but they require a separate semantics and correctness argument. Finite serialization of a cycle is not that argument.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-a-countermodel-refutes-a-universal-claim-within-its-scope\">Proposition 2. A countermodel refutes a universal claim within its scope</h3>\n<p>For a claim <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall x\\in D, P(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, an admissible <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0\\in D</annotation></semantics></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg P(x_0)</annotation></semantics></math> refutes the claim.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Universal quantification requires <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math> for every element of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>, including <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>\n<p>The domain condition matters. A failure outside the claimed domain can motivate a broader study but does not refute the narrower theorem. Conversely, narrowing the domain after seeing a failure produces a revised claim; it does not retroactively turn the original claim into a successful one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-different-checks-establish\">4. What different checks establish</h2>\n<p><strong>Table 2. Evidence types and the boundary of their immediate conclusions.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Evidence type</th>\n<th>What it can support within its stated conditions</th>\n<th>What does not follow from it alone</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Structural validation</td>\n<td>Compliance with the representation rules implemented by a correct validator</td>\n<td>Correct interpretation of natural-language fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Formal proof</td>\n<td>A consequence of stated assumptions under specified semantics</td>\n<td>Satisfaction of those assumptions by an implementation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Executable test</td>\n<td>An observed outcome for the tested input, configuration, and oracle</td>\n<td>Correctness on untested cases or other revisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Exhaustive finite check</td>\n<td>The checked property on a completely enumerated finite model</td>\n<td>Coverage of an unbounded domain or a different deployment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Substantive review</td>\n<td>A scoped assessment under the reviewer’s sources, expertise, and rubric</td>\n<td>Automatic independence, external peer review, or runtime conformance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Production observation</td>\n<td>An actual observed behavior, failure, or cost at the recorded boundary</td>\n<td>Causal superiority over an unmeasured alternative</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>For a finite check, the carrier, enumerated values, supported operations, and tested predicate must be stated. Complete enumeration is a substantive premise, not a synonym for a large iteration count. Coverage may fail because a generator omits an input, an execution outcome, or a configuration. A correct checker applied to an incomplete enumeration cannot establish the advertised larger universal claim.</p>\n<p>An empty domain makes a universal formula true vacuously. A test set containing no relevant negative case can likewise fail to challenge an exclusion rule. Neither observation demonstrates successful useful behavior. Record the vacuous or unexercised case separately, and include applicable positive controls when usefulness is part of the claim. Repeated copies of one test do not enlarge the distinct tested domain.</p>\n<p>Evidence types are complementary, not a ladder on which more records always mean stronger support. Ten tests sharing an incorrect oracle can repeat one mistake. A second reading by the producer is self-review. A separate reviewer who sees the proposed answer or earlier verdict performs a non-blind review; this is not automatically the independent assessment intended by a blinded design. Describe the actual separation of information, source access, rubric, and adjudication rather than infer independence from different agent names.</p>\n<p>For example, suppose an extracted value is wrong and both a calculation and its reference answer use that same extracted value. Their agreement can validate the calculation relative to its input while missing the source-reading error. A reference independently established from the source can expose the discrepancy. This is a constructed example of shared dependence, not a measured extraction-failure rate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-a-falsification-protocol\">5. A falsification protocol</h2>\n<p>An evaluation begins by freezing the claim and requirements before examining the decisive cases. Record subject versions, admissible inputs, expected behavior, failure criteria, scope of generalization, and the treatment of uncertain cases. Also identify the reference procedure and the evidence needed to decide that a proposed counterexample belongs to the claimed domain. A later change creates a new claim or evaluation revision; it does not overwrite the earlier result.</p>\n<p>Adversarial cases should probe both the required conditions and the system’s enforcement of them. For extraction, change a referent, logical scope, or source revision. For composition, omit a jointly required ground or add a conflicting condition. For reuse, change a material dependency or current permission. For resource accounting, repeat a telemetry event or expose a shared cost counted twice. The objective is to distinguish enforcement from success on familiar positive examples.</p>\n<p>Two uses of such cases must remain separate. A case outside a theorem’s premises can show why those premises matter, but does not refute that conditional theorem. An admissible case in which an implementation wrongly asserts that a condition has passed can refute the implementation’s enforcement claim. To refute the theorem itself, the case must satisfy all its premises and falsify its conclusion under the same interpretation. Failure of a premise does not prove that every result outside the premise is wrong either; it removes that particular justification.</p>\n<p>An independent impact oracle is especially important for dependency completeness. Traversing an annotated graph can verify traversal; it cannot expose an omitted dependency when the reference answer comes from that same graph. A useful test changes a relevant source feature and separately establishes whether the output should change. Shared errors in annotation and evaluation must not be counted as independent confirmation.</p>\n<p>Separate at least four failure classes: the model’s claim is false; the implementation violates the model; the input interpretation or annotation is wrong; or the evaluation lacks enough information to decide. These classes identify different repairs. One incident can involve several defects, so assigning a single convenient label must not erase the other supported findings. Uncertain domain membership or an unverified oracle leaves the alleged refutation unresolved rather than making it a confirmed failure or success.</p>\n<p>A refutation record should retain the exact claim revision, in-scope witness, conditions, actual output, expected relation, and enough execution or derivation information to inspect the contradiction. For a stochastic or nondeterministic implementation, state whether the claim quantifies over all permitted executions or over a distribution. One verified prohibited success contradicts a claim that such success never occurs. It does not by itself refute a different claim that permits a specified nonzero failure probability. Testing only one convenient outcome does not cover an all-executions claim.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-comparators-analysis-and-stopping\">6. Comparators, analysis, and stopping</h2>\n<p>A comparative claim needs a relevant baseline with matched tasks, resource accounting, and acceptance conditions. A method that produces more complete outputs at higher cost must not be described as unconditionally cheaper. Skipping required checks cannot yield an apparent speed advantage without disclosing the lost assurance. Report whether comparisons share source material, evaluator information, execution budgets, and the same meaning of an acceptable output.</p>\n<p>The analysis plan should state the primary outcome, denominators, missing and unresolved cases, uncertainty estimates, and stopping conditions. A report must distinguish planned cases, attempted cases, interpretable outcomes, and accepted outputs. An infrastructure failure is not automatically a semantic error; excluding it without explanation can nevertheless change the practical-success denominator.</p>\n<p>As an arithmetic illustration, two passes, one failure, and one unresolved outcome yield two passes among three resolved outcomes, but only two confirmed passes among four planned cases. If the unresolved outcome may eventually pass or fail, the final fraction on those four cases lies between one half and three quarters. This is an identification bound for that fixed ledger, not a confidence interval or an estimate for a population. Reporting the resolved-only fraction as the overall success rate changes the denominator and conceals the unresolved case.</p>\n<p>If evaluation stops at a budget limit, identify the covered and uncovered cases and whether the planned stopping policy was followed. Selecting only successful attempts or revising the success rule after inspection creates a different evidential object from the planned evaluation. Exploratory analysis can be useful, but should be identified as exploratory rather than relabeled as a predeclared test.</p>\n<p>Data separation also matters. Cases used to design a rule, tune a threshold, or repair an implementation are development evidence. They may later be reused as regression tests, but not called unseen evaluation. Shared sources, near-duplicates, and leaked reference annotations can make nominally separate cases dependent. Count distinct inputs, execution attempts, graph configurations, and assertions separately; their totals are not interchangeable units of evidence.</p>\n<p>For causal or practical-effect claims, the required design is stronger than a catalog of passing controls. The <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">knowledge utility paper</a> states the intervention and attribution conditions. Observed use, user approval, or a plausible demonstration alone does not establish improved outcomes relative to a meaningful alternative.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-the-evidential-status-of-this-research-program\">7. The evidential status of this research program</h2>\n<p>The scientific results presented here are definitions, explicit conditional arguments, and countermodels. They specify what a compliant knowledge process would have to preserve. Published engineering studies provide the separate place for reproducible executions, actual observations, and their limits. A link to a study is not a declaration that every proposed experiment has been performed; the study’s own methods and evidence record determine its scope.</p>\n<p>This distinction allows a formal or methodological paper to have a complete argument while practical hypotheses remain open. Completion means that the paper states and supports its actual contribution, not that every imaginable deployment question has been settled. Conversely, removing a provisional label cannot substitute for missing evidence.</p>\n<p>A falsifiable engineering claim must identify an implementation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>, its revision <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math>, a well-formed input domain <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and a mandatory condition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>. The claim to test is</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>:</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mo>¬</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mo>Admit</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>𝗌</mi><mi>𝗎</mi><mi>𝖼</mi><mi>𝖼</mi><mi>𝖾</mi><mi>𝗌</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\forall x\\in D:\\quad \\neg C(x)\n\\Rightarrow \\operatorname{Admit}(I_v,x)\\ne\\mathsf{success}.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Here <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> includes validly formed negative cases; it is not prefiltered to inputs already satisfying <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>. A single input in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> that violates <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math> and nevertheless receives successful admission refutes the claim about that implementation revision. No premise assumes that the implementation already enforces the condition. Positive controls separately test whether permitted cases can succeed, because refusal of every case would satisfy this exclusion property without showing usefulness. Semantic and practical predictions require independently interpreted or measured outcomes in addition to structural controls.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">contract profile conformance study</a>, <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">source transcription and uncertainty study</a>, and <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse admission and cost controls study</a> address distinct subjects. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial skill pilot</a> addresses document production. Their results should remain separate until a justified analysis explains how they combine.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-scope-and-shared-definitions\">8. Scope and shared definitions</h2>\n<p>The model does not automate the discovery of every relevant premise, guarantee the trustworthiness of a reviewer, or establish universal usefulness of structured knowledge. It makes these dependencies inspectable and supplies conditions under which a particular claim can be rejected, retained, or narrowed.</p>\n<p>The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> defines evidence, verification, authority, and related terms. <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">Revision and governance</a> specifies how new evidence changes assessments without erasing their history.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-conclusion\">9. Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A result supports a claim only through an explicit relationship among its subject, interpretation, assumptions, assessment procedure, and scope. Provenance, fidelity, support, applicability, and authority are distinct questions. A positive structural receipt cannot stand in for all of them.</p>\n<p>The two propositions give conditional deductive boundaries. A finite acyclic derivation preserves truth when its premises, rules, and side conditions satisfy the stated assumptions. An admissible countermodel refutes a universal claim within its domain. Neither result discovers missing premises, authenticates observations, or establishes that a deployed system follows the model. Finite checks, substantive reviews, and practical comparisons therefore require their own precisely bounded records.</p>\n<p>The practical method is to preserve exact subjects and adverse results, separate missing evidence from negative evidence, test the relevant boundary with a defensible oracle, and retain revisions when a claim changes. Completion of a bounded argument does not require every future experiment to finish. It does require that unmeasured benefits, unchecked implementations, and unresolved cases remain visible rather than being converted into an overall success label.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Hoare, C. A. R. “An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming.” <em>Communications of the ACM</em> 12(10), 1969, 576–580, 583. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259</a></li>\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question-and-evidential-distinctions","title":"1. Research question and evidential distinctions"},{"level":2,"id":"2-claims-and-independent-assessment-axes","title":"2. Claims and independent assessment axes"},{"level":2,"id":"3-justification-graphs-and-conditional-soundness","title":"3. Justification graphs and conditional soundness"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-sound-rules-preserve-true-premises-in-a-finite-derivation","title":"Proposition 1. Sound rules preserve true premises in a finite derivation"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-a-countermodel-refutes-a-universal-claim-within-its-scope","title":"Proposition 2. A countermodel refutes a universal claim within its scope"},{"level":2,"id":"4-what-different-checks-establish","title":"4. What different checks establish"},{"level":2,"id":"5-a-falsification-protocol","title":"5. A falsification protocol"},{"level":2,"id":"6-comparators-analysis-and-stopping","title":"6. Comparators, analysis, and stopping"},{"level":2,"id":"7-the-evidential-status-of-this-research-program","title":"7. The evidential status of this research program"},{"level":2,"id":"8-scope-and-shared-definitions","title":"8. Scope and shared definitions"},{"level":2,"id":"9-conclusion","title":"9. Conclusion"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"evidence-reasoning-and-falsification-in-knowledge-systems","math_count":15,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/evidence-and-falsification/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/evidence-and-falsification/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"926b29d227f9c576bc69834ae6f8a72d514fc3b5916bf66c966df5aee5a2ce28","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"evaluation"},{"canonical":true,"id":"revision-and-governance","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/revision-and-governance/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/revision-and-governance/","title":"Knowledge Revision, Version Identity, and the Adoption of Norms","titleRu":"Knowledge Revision, Version Identity, and the Adoption of Norms","summary":"Separate identities for content, assessment and normative adoption, with explicit revision and publication decisions.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A knowledge record can be corrected, reassessed, rendered differently, or adopted as a rule. Treating these events as one change of status obscures which claims and permissions remain justified. This paper asks how knowledge revision can preserve that distinction. We separate object identity, content revision, representation, assessment, and normative adoption. Four conditional propositions concern exact historical selection, equal results from deterministic checks with equal complete inputs, dependency-based identification of reassessment candidates, and the non-entailment of contingent factual claims by adoption alone. The method is deductive, supplemented by constructed boundary cases rather than deployment measurements. The contribution is a bounded account of the obligations for transferring checks and admitting current use, not a new truth-maintenance algorithm or a realization of AGM belief revision. In particular, reproducing a check result does not establish the check’s soundness, and identifying an affected assessment does not establish its failure. Complete dependency discovery, reliable authentication, retention in a deployed system, and the comparative effectiveness of a governance policy remain separate obligations.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> knowledge revision; provenance; versioning; governance; normative adoption; historical reproducibility.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question\">1. Research question</h2>\n<p>A knowledge repository often uses “current,” “approved,” or “valid” as if each described one property. In practice, a record can be the latest revision while being disputed, an approved policy can contain a factual error, and a historical version can remain necessary for reproducing an old result.</p>\n<p>Our question is: <strong>how can a system revise knowledge and adopt norms without confusing content, evidence, and authority?</strong> We propose distinct objects and transition rules for each. The purpose is not to freeze knowledge, but to preserve a clear explanation of why a particular revision or assessment was used at a particular time.</p>\n<p>PROV-DM offers the general distinction between entities, activities, and responsible agents [1]. We use that structure to connect revisions and decisions. The additional contribution is a set of obligations for transferring checks, following current versions, retaining historical grounds, and admitting normative decisions.</p>\n<p>Dependency-directed revision has an established basis in Doyle’s truth-maintenance work, which records reasons for beliefs and revises them when assumptions change [2]. The reachability result below isolates an elementary prerequisite for identifying affected assessments; it does not implement a truth-maintenance system. Alchourrón, Gärdenfors, and Makinson study contraction and revision of logical theories under explicit postulates [3]. Our use of “revision” concerns versioned records, assessments, and authority decisions, and claims neither an AGM revision operator nor satisfaction of those postulates.</p>\n<h3 id=\"11-method-and-contribution-boundary\">1.1 Method and contribution boundary</h3>\n<p>The argument proceeds by definitions, explicit premises, and their consequences. The four propositions isolate elementary obligations; their short proofs should not be read as novel results about arbitrary belief systems. Constructed cases expose what stops following when a premise is removed. Neither those cases nor a mechanically checked document establish an implemented governance mechanism.</p>\n<p>The references perform different jobs. PROV-DM specifies provenance relations; its revision relation is a specialized derivation, not a proof that a revision is correct [1, Section 5.2.2]. Doyle supplies the prior problem of maintaining reasons for beliefs [2]. AGM supplies formal contraction and revision of theories with a consequence operation and stated postulates [3, Sections 1–2]. A versioned collection of documents is not, merely by being versioned, a deductively closed theory equipped with those operations. Connecting this account to an AGM operator would require an explicit representation, operation correspondence, and proof of the relevant postulates. None is assumed here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-five-different-objects-of-change\">2. Five different objects of change</h2>\n<p>An <strong>identity</strong> groups revisions treated as versions of one object under a declared identity policy. The policy matters: a correction, a translation, a derived summary, and a competing interpretation need not all be revisions of the same object.</p>\n<p>A <strong>content revision</strong> is a pinned content state. Material content changes create a new revision with a relation to its predecessor. Immutability here is a property of the repository’s version discipline, not a guarantee supplied by writing a revision label.</p>\n<p>A <strong>representation</strong> is a delivery or serialization of content: a document rendering, machine format, or other declared form. Two representations may preserve selected content while differing in accessibility or layout. Equivalence requires an appropriate preservation check; a shared title is insufficient.</p>\n<p>An <strong>assessment</strong> concerns a proposition about a revision: whether it is supported, applicable, conflicted, current for a purpose, or compliant with a rule. New evidence can change an assessment without changing the assessed text. Overwriting the text to reflect every assessment change would confuse the object with judgments about it.</p>\n<p>A <strong>normative adoption</strong> is a decision to use a rule within a named community, process, or jurisdiction. It identifies the decision maker, authority basis, scope, effective period, adopted revision, and review or withdrawal conditions. Adoption makes the rule operative under that policy; it does not make every factual premise of the rule true.</p>\n<p>These distinctions permit a corrected revision and a withdrawn assessment to coexist with an earlier historical record. They also prevent unrelated properties—such as popularity, technical availability, or a successful build—from acting as substitutes for adoption or evidential support.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-revision-operations-and-their-grounds\">3. Revision operations and their grounds</h2>\n<p>A revision operation records the affected object, motivating evidence or request, proposed change, relevant dependencies, decision, and resulting revisions or assessments. A useful operation can correct a transcription, replace a false premise, narrow an applicability condition, add a competing interpretation, or change a normative decision.</p>\n<p>Not every conflict should be resolved by replacing one statement. The conflict may concern different dates, referents, scopes, or interpretations. Preserving those distinctions can dissolve an apparent contradiction without declaring either source universally correct. If the conflict remains unresolved, the system should retain that assessment and avoid presenting one branch as established merely because it is newer.</p>\n<p>Checks attach to a revision and an assessment scope. A successful check of an old text is not automatically a successful check of a materially changed text. Transfer requires a reason: the checked property is unaffected by the change, the transformation preserves it, or the check has been repeated. The reason should be inspectable rather than inferred from a similar filename.</p>\n<p>Duplicate detection is likewise scoped. Equivalent content can be consolidated while retaining multiple provenance relations. Merging identities without a justified identity policy can destroy distinctions between independent sources, conflicting readings, or separate normative authorities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"31-a-scoped-transfer-obligation\">3.1 A scoped transfer obligation</h3>\n<p>A transfer record should identify the old and new subjects, the selected property, the evaluator and its interpretation, the relevant context, and the grounds for treating the inputs as complete. This is an explanatory obligation, not a new machine schema. Content equality can discharge only those comparisons for which content is the entire relevant input. Changes of policy, recipient, time interpretation, or evaluator can leave the text unchanged while invalidating that justification.</p>\n<p>Proposition 2 concerns equality of check results. A deterministic checker that always returns success also repeats its answer on equal inputs. That fact does not make the answer sound. Using the result as evidence for a property requires a separate justification of the checker for that property and scope. Permission to use or disclose the result requires its own current decision.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-conditional-properties\">4. Conditional properties</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-pinned-references-preserve-historical-selection\">Proposition 1. Pinned references preserve historical selection</h3>\n<p>Suppose a reference names an immutable revision, and retrieval either returns that exact revision or reports failure. Changing the repository’s current-revision pointer does not change the content selected by that reference.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Selection depends on the named revision, not on the current pointer. The pointer change therefore cannot alter the selected identifier or its immutable content. ∎</p>\n<p>This property requires storage integrity and exact reference resolution. A readable label or hash field alone does not ensure either. It also guarantees selection, not perpetual availability, current applicability, or permission to disclose the historical content.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-checks-transfer-through-preserved-complete-inputs\">Proposition 2. Checks transfer through preserved complete inputs</h3>\n<p>Let a deterministic check <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math> depend on a complete input tuple <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math>. If two revisions and their evaluation contexts induce the same tuple, then the check returns the same result for both.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Determinism gives equal outputs for equal complete inputs. ∎</p>\n<p>The word “complete” is decisive. A check that depends on a policy version, current authorization, or a source interpretation cannot be transferred using only equality of visible text. If the evaluator itself changes, the equality-of-function assumption must also be reconsidered.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-3-complete-dependencies-identify-candidates-for-reassessment\">Proposition 3. Complete dependencies identify candidates for reassessment</h3>\n<p>If every dependency relevant to an assessment is represented in a finite graph, complete reverse reachability from a changed dependency identifies all assessments potentially affected by that change.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> A relevant dependency gives a finite path from the assessment to the changed object. Reversal makes the assessment reachable from the change. Complete traversal includes every such path. ∎</p>\n<p>This result identifies candidates for reassessment, not automatic failures. An assessment may remain valid because an alternative ground still suffices. Conversely, a missing edge can hide an affected assessment despite a correct traversal algorithm.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-4-adoption-is-not-a-truth-preserving-inference-rule\">Proposition 4. Adoption is not a truth-preserving inference rule</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an adoption record and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math> a factual proposition in the adopted rule. Suppose <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\land F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∧</mo><mo>¬</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\land\\neg F</annotation></semantics></math> are both satisfiable under the admitted interpretation. Then the adoption record alone does not entail <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<p><strong>Argument.</strong> The satisfying interpretation of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∧</mo><mo>¬</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\land\\neg F</annotation></semantics></math> is a countermodel to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\Rightarrow F</annotation></semantics></math>. The other interpretation shows that the same adoption record is also compatible with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus adoption alone leaves this contingent factual content undecided. ∎</p>\n<p>The independence premise excludes logical validities and facts already entailed by the adoption record, such as a correctly represented fact about the adoption event itself. An additional reliable procedure could supply evidence for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math>, but its reliability would be a separate premise. This does not deny the practical force of adopted rules; it locates that force in the declared governance policy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-authority-and-normative-admission\">5. Authority and normative admission</h2>\n<p>A governance policy defines who may propose, review, adopt, amend, or withdraw a norm, under which conditions. Those are different permissions. A role description can explain responsibility without granting every operation associated with the role.</p>\n<p>Admission should evaluate current authority, the applicable procedure, the exact proposed revision, required reviews, unresolved conflicts, and any effective-date conditions. When a condition is mandatory, unknown is not pass. A record that says a reviewer approved something must be connected to the correct subject and a trustworthy attribution mechanism; a field named “reviewer” cannot authenticate itself.</p>\n<p>The model does not prescribe one decision rule for every community. A maintainer decision, a quorum, or an external mandate can each be represented, provided the rule and its scope are explicit. The research obligation is to state what the procedure establishes and what it leaves unproven.</p>\n<p>An appeal or correction creates a new event rather than erasing the earlier decision. This permits analysis of why an earlier output was produced and how the current basis differs. Accountability requires that history remain inspectable under the applicable retention and access rules; it does not require unrestricted public disclosure of every underlying record.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-historical-reproduction-and-current-use\">6. Historical reproduction and current use</h2>\n<p>Historical reproduction asks what followed from a pinned state and policy. Current use asks whether the result is admissible now. These can have different answers. A historical computation may remain reproducible even when its source has been superseded. A current recommendation may require reassessment despite exact reproduction of the earlier computation.</p>\n<p>Time therefore has several meanings: when an event occurred, when it was recorded, when a rule became effective, and when a use is being admitted. Collapsing them into one timestamp makes delayed information and retrospective correction difficult to represent correctly.</p>\n<p>Likewise, storage retention, publication status, and use eligibility are distinct. An artifact can remain archived while current use is withdrawn. Removing it from search results does not erase all copies. Retaining an original for reproducibility does not grant permission to use it in a new context. Each transition should identify its intended effect.</p>\n<p>Migration between representations introduces another preservation question. A migration report should state what is mapped, what is transformed with checked preservation, what is intentionally omitted, and what cannot be represented. A successful import is not evidence of semantic equivalence if critical distinctions were collapsed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"61-constructed-revision-sequence\">6.1 Constructed revision sequence</h3>\n<p>Consider a fictional procedure whose first revision permits automated processing only for validated inputs. An assessment records that this revision preserves the condition, and an authorized decision adopts that exact revision for a named process. The following changes have different effects; the example asserts no observed deployment result.</p>\n<p><strong>Table 1. Changes that must not collapse into a single status.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Change</th>\n<th>What changes</th>\n<th>What does not follow</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A new counterexample shows that the input checker accepts an invalid case.</td>\n<td>The assessment of that checker and dependent assurances needs revision.</td>\n<td>The stored procedure text has changed, or every dependent conclusion is false.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A proposed second revision removes the validation condition.</td>\n<td>The procedure has new content requiring its own assessment.</td>\n<td>An adoption of the first revision also adopts the second.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>An authorized withdrawal ends current use of the first revision.</td>\n<td>Its present normative status changes.</td>\n<td>Its historical content or the earlier adoption event disappears.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A rendered copy omits the condition while retaining the title.</td>\n<td>The delivered representation fails the stated preservation obligation.</td>\n<td>Matching titles establish equivalent instructions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>The earlier revision is unavailable from storage.</td>\n<td>Exact retrieval must report failure under Proposition 1’s premise.</td>\n<td>A current replacement can be silently returned as the pinned historical revision.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Suppose two permitted grounds independently suffice for one assessment. Losing one ground makes that assessment a reassessment candidate, but the other may still justify it. Conversely, an omitted dependency can prevent a traversal from finding an assessment that has actually lost its only ground. These cases separate dependency completeness, candidate identification, and the subsequent support judgment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-falsification-and-evidence-boundary\">7. Falsification and evidence boundary</h2>\n<p>The model can be tested with cases that change one material feature: a source revision, authority grant, effective date, interpretation rule, or mandatory dependency. It should also include unchanged content with a changed assessment, and changed layout with preserved content but impaired delivery.</p>\n<p>Counterexamples are particularly useful where identifiers conceal assumptions. Reusing a prior receipt after a policy change tests whether the receipt is wrongly treated as permanent permission. Updating a current pointer while resolving a pinned reference tests historical selection. Merging two textually equal records with different provenance tests whether deduplication destroys relevant history.</p>\n<p>These controls can establish behavior on finite represented cases. They do not prove complete dependency discovery, correct interpretation of all governance rules, or trustworthy authentication in an unexamined deployment. The formal propositions remain conditional on those boundaries.</p>\n<p>The contribution is an explicit account of the stated distinctions between content, assessment, historical selection, and normative adoption, together with checkable consequences and counterexamples. It does not claim that this list exhausts every governance model. Nor does it make an empirical claim that one governance procedure is universally superior or that the model resolves substantive disagreement automatically.</p>\n<h3 id=\"71-what-a-useful-test-must-distinguish\">7.1 What a useful test must distinguish</h3>\n<p>A finite test suite should pair each intended behavior with a deliberately weaker alternative: resolve a pin versus silently follow the current pointer; compare complete inputs versus compare text alone; reassess reachable dependents versus mark them all false; and inspect present authority versus reuse an earlier approval. Failure to distinguish those alternatives is a limitation of the test, not evidence that the extra obligation is unnecessary.</p>\n<p>The independence premise of Proposition 4 also needs a control. When the factual proposition is already entailed by the adoption record, the stated non-entailment conclusion is not licensed by that proposition. An adoption event can establish a correctly recorded fact about that event while leaving another empirical claim unsupported. No general rejection of institutional facts follows.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">8. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">executable projections and revision study</a> concerns pinned revisions and bounded update behavior. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse admission and cost controls study</a> examines current eligibility of previously prepared artifacts.</p>\n<p>The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/\">interaction and memory</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled knowledge reuse</a> define the connected work-state and admission obligations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-conclusion\">9. Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Knowledge revision requires an account of which object changed and which justification remains applicable. Under the stated premises, a pin fixes historical selection, equal complete inputs preserve a deterministic check’s result, and complete represented dependencies permit identification of reassessment candidates. Adoption alone does not settle a contingent factual claim when the same adoption record is compatible with both its truth and falsity.</p>\n<p>These results are deliberately limited. A repeated result is not a soundness proof; a dependency path is not a finding of invalidity; and a historical adoption is not permanent current authority. The account specifies distinctions and conditional obligations that an implementation can be tested against. It does not establish a universal revision policy, an automatic method for discovering all semantic dependencies, or measured advantages over other systems. Each stronger claim requires its own model, evidence, and review.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>W3C. <em>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model</em>. W3C Recommendation, 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/\" class=\"uri\">https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-dm/</a></li>\n<li>Doyle, J. “A Truth Maintenance System.” <em>Artificial Intelligence</em> 12(3), 1979, 231–272. <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0004370279900080\">Publisher record</a>. <a href=\"https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/5377b306-4ecc-4687-b1f5-78cbb4a0543a\">MIT report version, AI Memo 521</a>.</li>\n<li>Alchourrón, C. E., Gärdenfors, P., and Makinson, D. “On the Logic of Theory Change: Partial Meet Contraction and Revision Functions.” <em>The Journal of Symbolic Logic</em> 50(2), 1985, 510–530. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2307/2274239\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.2307/2274239</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question","title":"1. Research question"},{"level":3,"id":"11-method-and-contribution-boundary","title":"1.1 Method and contribution boundary"},{"level":2,"id":"2-five-different-objects-of-change","title":"2. Five different objects of change"},{"level":2,"id":"3-revision-operations-and-their-grounds","title":"3. Revision operations and their grounds"},{"level":3,"id":"31-a-scoped-transfer-obligation","title":"3.1 A scoped transfer obligation"},{"level":2,"id":"4-conditional-properties","title":"4. Conditional properties"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-pinned-references-preserve-historical-selection","title":"Proposition 1. Pinned references preserve historical selection"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-checks-transfer-through-preserved-complete-inputs","title":"Proposition 2. Checks transfer through preserved complete inputs"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-3-complete-dependencies-identify-candidates-for-reassessment","title":"Proposition 3. Complete dependencies identify candidates for reassessment"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-4-adoption-is-not-a-truth-preserving-inference-rule","title":"Proposition 4. Adoption is not a truth-preserving inference rule"},{"level":2,"id":"5-authority-and-normative-admission","title":"5. Authority and normative admission"},{"level":2,"id":"6-historical-reproduction-and-current-use","title":"6. Historical reproduction and current use"},{"level":3,"id":"61-constructed-revision-sequence","title":"6.1 Constructed revision sequence"},{"level":2,"id":"7-falsification-and-evidence-boundary","title":"7. Falsification and evidence boundary"},{"level":3,"id":"71-what-a-useful-test-must-distinguish","title":"7.1 What a useful test must distinguish"},{"level":2,"id":"8-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"8. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"9-conclusion","title":"9. Conclusion"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"knowledge-revision-version-identity-and-the-adoption-of-norms","math_count":11,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/revision-and-governance/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/revision-and-governance/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"20079132357dc2e4e651cde78d528b37712ecdef030af3934ebd2e8048e3f78a","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"systems"},{"canonical":true,"id":"controlled-knowledge-reuse","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/controlled-knowledge-reuse/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/","title":"Knowledge Contracts and the Admission of Prepared Knowledge","titleRu":"Knowledge Contracts and the Admission of Prepared Knowledge","summary":"The conditions under which saved knowledge may support a new task, recipient or execution.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Preparing knowledge in advance can reduce repeated work, but prior verification does not by itself justify a new use. This paper asks when a prepared artifact may support a different request under current conditions. We define a knowledge contract as an explicit account of requirements, constraints, grounds, permitted transformations, and verification obligations. Reuse is a directed admission relation for a particular artifact, request, policy, and state. A conditional preservation theorem gives sufficient conditions for reuse: sound prior verification; complete and unchanged relevant state and request observations; preservation by every permitted transformation output; coverage of the new artifact requirements; and discharge of current-use requirements. Counterexamples show why unchanged bytes, previously returned search results, or an unexpired time-to-live are insufficient. The contribution is a precise separation of reusable preparation from current authorization and delivery, with explicit obligations for revalidation, rebuilding, or rejection. The argument is deductive: it does not provide an algorithm for discovering every semantic dependency, establish a production coordination guarantee, or demonstrate a universal economic advantage from caching.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> knowledge contract; compilation; reuse admission; constraints; dependencies; current authorization.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question-and-relation-to-caching\">1. Research question and relation to caching</h2>\n<p>A summary, context package, workflow, or professional instruction may be prepared once and used repeatedly. Repetition avoids some preparation work only while the prepared result remains suitable. Even identical request text can refer to a changed recipient, policy, source revision, or time interpretation.</p>\n<p>We ask: <strong>when does an earlier verification justify a new use of a prepared artifact?</strong> The question has two parts. Which previously established properties remain valid? Which conditions must be checked again for the requested use? Treating these as one question can make a valid preparation appear to authorize an invalid delivery.</p>\n<p>HTTP caching provides explicit freshness and reuse rules, including restrictions for authenticated requests [1]. Extensions permit specified forms of stale serving [2]. These protocols govern HTTP response reuse; they do not, by themselves, establish that a prepared knowledge artifact meets a new task’s semantic requirements and current-use conditions. Incremental build correctness likewise depends on consistency between a result and its complete task dependencies [3]. A successful build alone does not establish coverage of new requirements or permission to deliver the result to a new recipient.</p>\n<p>This paper brings those obligations into one bounded admission argument. Its contribution has three parts: a distinction between a contract and the artifact prepared under it; sufficient conditions for preserving verified properties while covering a new request; and counterexamples that identify what fails when a condition is omitted. The contribution is not a new caching algorithm or a claim that dependency tracking and authorization originate here.</p>\n<p>The method is deductive. We define the relevant objects and admission relation, state explicit premises, prove their consequence, and examine boundary cases. The theorem is a composition of those premises, not an automatic decision procedure for checking them. Implementation-specific observations belong to the engineering studies linked in Section 8; they are not substituted for the proof.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-contracts-and-their-professional-specialization\">2. Contracts and their professional specialization</h2>\n<p>A <strong>knowledge contract</strong> specifies a bounded use: its objective, requirements, mandatory constraints, admissible grounds, permitted transformations, expected result, and verification or stopping conditions. Its content can be represented in different formats. The scientific concept does not depend on one schema version or transport envelope.</p>\n<p>Constraints are first-class obligations. They may restrict scope, resources, time, recipient, authority, interpretation, or the allowed operation. A preference may guide selection among admissible options. A high utility score cannot compensate for failure to satisfy a mandatory constraint. If a mandatory condition is unknown, the corresponding admission has not passed.</p>\n<p>The contract does not itself establish truth, grant authority, or supply a missing capability. It states what must be supported and checked. A structurally valid contract can still contain a false premise or an unimplementable demand.</p>\n<p>In a professional profile, the following terms have distinct functions:</p>\n<p><strong>Table 1. Operational distinctions in the professional profile.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>role</strong></td>\n<td>Specifies expected responsibilities.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>competency</strong></td>\n<td>Specifies an ability under stated conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Competency assessment</td>\n<td>Binds supporting evidence to a particular executor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>skill</strong></td>\n<td>Is a reusable procedure with conditions, steps, and checks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>blueprint</strong></td>\n<td>Organizes roles, procedures, and decision points for a class of work.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are the project’s operational definitions, not a claim of one universal external taxonomy.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Work Item</strong> instantiates an objective with actual requirements and outputs. A <strong>capability</strong> concerns an available operation. <strong>Authorization</strong> concerns permission to perform it in the current context.</p>\n<p>A role can be responsible for an outcome without possessing every capability or permission needed to produce it. A skill description does not establish that an executor meets a competency requirement. Likewise, a successful attempt does not establish that ability across every task in the described class.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-preparation-and-the-reuse-relation\">3. Preparation and the reuse relation</h2>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> include the task, requirements, referents, recipient, isolation scope, resource budget, and time interpretation. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a pinned source state and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math> a preparation recipe. A preparation attempt produces, when successful,</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Compile</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Compile}(b,q,S)\\to(a,D,E,L),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> is an artifact revision, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> its declared dependencies, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math> its verification evidence, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> its coverage, losses, and unresolved conditions.</p>\n<p>Preparation can fail or produce an explicitly partial result. A content hash checks bytes under a declared serialization; it does not establish truth or deterministic regeneration.</p>\n<p>For a new request <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>, policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and current state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mo>⇝</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\na\\rightsquigarrow_{p,s}q&#39;\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>when the artifact is admitted for that use. The relation is directed: a complete artifact may support an authorized narrower projection, while a partial artifact does not support a broader task solely because both concern the same topic.</p>\n<p>The admission decision may be to reuse unchanged, instantiate with checking, revalidate, rebuild, bypass reuse, or reject the requested use. A decision to revalidate leaves a check outstanding; it does not permit delivery before that check. An instantiated result has its own content identity and requires checks on the actual output.</p>\n<p>For the theorem, the complete requirement set includes every mandatory condition imposed by the request and the fixed admission policy. Discharging that set is the policy’s admission criterion within the declared scope. Any additional mandatory veto must be included; the theorem does not authorize a use under an unmodeled condition. Current-use obligations are independent of prior preparation in the sense that they require their own discharge, not in the sense of statistical independence.</p>\n<p>Build and admission have different dependencies. A generator version can matter for provenance or a known defect, while current permission can matter at every delivery. Neither invalidating everything after every tool update nor ignoring all interpretation changes is a justified general policy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-conditional-preservation-theorem\">4. Conditional preservation theorem</h2>\n<p>Fix an admission policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_1(x,q,s),\\ldots,P_m(x,q,s)</annotation></semantics></math> be properties of an artifact revision <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math>, request <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math>, interpreted under fixed semantics. For each property, let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta_j(s)</annotation></semantics></math> be its relevant state observation and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa_j(q)</annotation></semantics></math> its relevant request observation. Let the complete mandatory requirements of the new request be partitioned as</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nR(q&#39;)=R_{\\mathrm{artifact}}(q&#39;)\\cup R_{\\mathrm{use}}(q&#39;).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The first group concerns the artifact properties justified by preparation; the second contains independent conditions to be discharged at current use, including authorization and delivery. Requirement discharge has the <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/#3-context-and-applicability\">applicability paper’s meaning</a>: a passing assessment or a justified exemption for the exact subject and scope, never an unsupported or unknown condition. Assume:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Sound prior verification.</strong> <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_j(a,q,s_0)</annotation></semantics></math> actually holds for every <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math>. The presence of a positive receipt is not itself this premise.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Complete and unchanged observations.</strong> The manifest <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> contains every state input material to the selected properties and their interpretation. For every relevant <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x,q,s</annotation></semantics></math>, each property factors as <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover><mi>P</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_j(x,q,s)=\\widehat P_j(x,\\kappa_j(q),\\delta_j(s))</annotation></semantics></math> for a fixed predicate <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>P</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat P_j</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta_j(s_0)=\\delta_j(s_1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa_j(q)=\\kappa_j(q&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>. This expresses completeness for request components as well as state components.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Preservation for every permitted transformation output.</strong> The permitted transformation is a relation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>, possibly the identity. For every <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(a,a&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>, and every <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_j(a,q&#39;,s_1)\\Rightarrow P_j(a&#39;,q&#39;,s_1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Coverage of the new artifact requirements.</strong> Under the fixed assessment semantics, the selected properties suffice to discharge every requirement in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\mathrm{artifact}}(q&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>. Explicitly, for every relevant output <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo>⋀</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><munder><mo>⋀</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mo>Discharged</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\bigwedge_{j=1}^{m} P_j(x,q&#39;,s_1)\n\\Rightarrow\n\\bigwedge_{r\\in R_{\\mathrm{artifact}}(q&#39;)}\n\\operatorname{Discharged}_r(x,q&#39;,s_1).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Current independent admission.</strong> For every proposed output <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(a,a&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>, every requirement in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\mathrm{use}}(q&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math> is discharged for that output at the declared current admission point. A check for another recipient, representation, or generation does not satisfy this premise.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then every such output <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> retains all selected properties for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(q&#39;,s_1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and all requirements in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(q&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math> are discharged. It is therefore admitted under the specified policy and scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> By assumptions 1–2, for each <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover><mi>P</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover><mi>P</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nP_j(a,q,s_0)\n=\\widehat P_j(a,\\kappa_j(q),\\delta_j(s_0))\n=\\widehat P_j(a,\\kappa_j(q&#39;),\\delta_j(s_1))\n=P_j(a,q&#39;,s_1).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Thus <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_j(a,q&#39;,s_1)</annotation></semantics></math> holds. Assumption 3 transfers it to every permitted transformation output <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>. Assumption 4 discharges the new artifact requirements, and assumption 5 discharges the independent current-use requirements. Their union is the complete declared requirement set, so no mandatory requirement remains outside the argument. ∎</p>\n<p>Two counterexamples explain why unchanged state alone is insufficient. An artifact can establish Boolean condition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math> while a new request also requires <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>, which is false; preserving <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math> does not cover <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Separately, the property “the artifact’s referent equals the requested referent” can hold for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> and fail for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> without any source-state change. The first case violates requirement coverage; the second violates request-observation agreement.</p>\n<p>The theorem is deliberately conditional. A manifest labeled “complete” does not prove its completeness, a checker can be unsound, and an unjustified coverage implication cannot authorize a new task. The result quantifies over successful permitted outputs; it does not promise that <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math> has any output or terminates. Natural-language interpretation, hidden dependencies, and current authority need their own justification. Preservation also cannot make a false premise true: it can maintain a faithful record of a mistaken source. For a genuinely narrower requirement set with unchanged interpretations and assessments, <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/#proposition-3-mandatory-strengthening-narrows-certified-applicability\">mandatory strengthening</a> supplies the corresponding subset implication; a change of request is not automatically such a narrowing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-dependencies-and-scope-counterexamples\">5. Dependencies and scope counterexamples</h2>\n<p>A dependency can concern a content field, relation, alternative ground, interpretation rule, normative basis, representation, or search scope. Recording dependencies at finer granularity can avoid unnecessary rebuilding, but only if the manifest remains complete for the selected properties.</p>\n<p>Negative, exhaustive, counted, and ranked answers expose a common dependency error. For a previously sound negative answer over a fixed visible search scope and predicate, inserting a newly visible matching record into that scope makes the old answer false, even if all previously returned records are unchanged. A manifest containing only those returned identifiers therefore cannot justify reuse of that answer.</p>\n<p>Here, a fixed scope means the same search domain and visibility rule, not a frozen set of record contents. The insertion changes the state within that domain. A record outside the domain, a record invisible to the requester, or a record that does not satisfy the predicate does not supply this counterexample.</p>\n<p>The dependency therefore includes the searched scope, visibility, predicate, and completeness assumptions. Exact top-ranked results also depend on ranking and tie rules. Caching the output of an approximate search does not make that search exhaustive.</p>\n<p>Multimodal dependencies can include an image revision, region, legend, or transformation. A corrected symbol can invalidate a textual interpretation even when the text itself is unchanged. Moving identical bytes, however, may require a locator or delivery check without changing semantic content. The required response depends on which property changed.</p>\n<p>Losing one source need not invalidate a conclusion if an independently sufficient permitted ground remains. A traversal of dependencies can identify the affected artifacts. Deciding whether they remain supported requires the declared support rule and current assessments.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-state-time-and-delivery\">6. State, time, and delivery</h2>\n<p>Lifecycle, residency, freshness, and reuse eligibility answer different questions. An artifact may be ready and resident yet unauthorized for a particular recipient. Eviction can change latency without changing its content. A recent cache hit records use, not renewed validation.</p>\n<p>Time policies must be explicit:</p>\n<p><strong>Table 2. Time policies and their conditions.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Policy</th>\n<th>Conditions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Strict current use</strong></td>\n<td>Requires the relevant current conditions at a declared admission point.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><strong>Historical pinned use</strong></td>\n<td>Preserves a past state for the requested purpose while still respecting present access and revocation conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><strong>Bounded stale use</strong></td>\n<td>Permits specified age or divergence only where the contract allows it.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Known revocation, corruption, or incompatibility is not repaired by an unexpired time-to-live.</p>\n<p>A check immediately before delivery does not establish a strong current-state guarantee if a material condition can change before the protected operation. A permission check may pass, the permission may then be withdrawn, and the artifact may still be sent. Each step can behave as specified while the intended current-admission claim fails.</p>\n<p>A stronger guarantee requires a declared admission point and a mechanism that binds the relevant evaluation to that point. A coherent snapshot, suitable lock, valid lease, or equivalent protocol can provide this coordination under stated assumptions. The guarantee applies to that point and scope. It does not promise that the world remains unchanged afterward.</p>\n<p>Publication has a related race: a slow preparation attempt must not replace a newer artifact merely because it finishes later. Generation checks and protected publication can prevent this overwrite under their storage assumptions.</p>\n<p>Repeated or reordered invalidation events can be handled idempotently. Lost events require reconciliation or independent validation; event deduplication alone does not recover missing information.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-economic-and-practical-limits\">7. Economic and practical limits</h2>\n<p>Admission determines which routes are permitted. Cost or predicted utility can then rank those routes. A cheap but inadmissible result is not a valid optimization candidate. Reuse may reduce repeated preparation while adding costs for storage, validation, invalidation, reconstruction, and coordination.</p>\n<p>Comparisons should include a valid cold path, with all compared routes held to the same required quality and authority conditions. Provider-level caching, retrieval caching, prepared context, and professional artifacts reuse different units. Savings from the same avoided operation must not be counted more than once across these layers.</p>\n<p>The result established here is a conditional admission argument with counterexamples. It establishes no universal speedup, cost reduction, or production concurrency guarantee. Those workload- and implementation-specific claims require their own measurements and failure models. Repeated successful reuse does not by itself raise the truth or completeness status of the underlying knowledge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work\">8. Connections to the published engineering work</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse admission and cost controls study</a> examines finite admission decisions and accounting controls. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">contract profile conformance study</a> addresses supported representations. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency closure and package delivery study</a> addresses closure and actual delivery.</p>\n<p>Machine interfaces and their compatibility versions belong in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">engineering core specification</a>. The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">constrained transformations</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">knowledge utility</a> complete the conceptual links.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-conclusion\">9. Conclusion</h2>\n<p>The reuse route established here relies on sound prior verification, complete and unchanged relevant state and request observations, preservation by every permitted transformation output, coverage of the new artifact requirements, and discharge of current-use requirements for the actual output. Together, these premises are sufficient for admission within the declared policy and scope; they are not presented as necessary conditions for every possible way of establishing suitability. A stored receipt, matching hash, familiar topic, or recent cache hit does not establish those premises by itself.</p>\n<p>The practical implication is to preserve the distinction between preparing an artifact and authorizing its use. When an obligation cannot be discharged, the system must follow the applicable revalidation, rebuilding, bypass, or rejection route rather than infer permission from prior success. Whether that discipline improves cost, latency, or outcomes remains a separate empirical question. The present result supplies a precise conditional argument and its limits, not a claim that every dependency can be discovered or every implementation enforces the required checks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Fielding, R., Nottingham, M., and Reschke, J. <em>HTTP Caching</em>. RFC 9111, 2022. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111\" class=\"uri\">https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111</a></li>\n<li>Nottingham, M. <em>HTTP Cache-Control Extensions for Stale Content</em>. RFC 5861, 2010. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5861\" class=\"uri\">https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5861</a></li>\n<li>Mokhov, A., Mitchell, N., and Peyton Jones, S. “Build Systems à la Carte.” <em>Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages</em> 2(ICFP), article 79, 2018, 1–29. <a href=\"https://simon.peytonjones.org/assets/pdfs/build-systems-original.pdf\">Author’s copy</a>. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/3236774\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1145/3236774</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question-and-relation-to-caching","title":"1. Research question and relation to caching"},{"level":2,"id":"2-contracts-and-their-professional-specialization","title":"2. Contracts and their professional specialization"},{"level":2,"id":"3-preparation-and-the-reuse-relation","title":"3. Preparation and the reuse relation"},{"level":2,"id":"4-conditional-preservation-theorem","title":"4. Conditional preservation theorem"},{"level":2,"id":"5-dependencies-and-scope-counterexamples","title":"5. Dependencies and scope counterexamples"},{"level":2,"id":"6-state-time-and-delivery","title":"6. State, time, and delivery"},{"level":2,"id":"7-economic-and-practical-limits","title":"7. Economic and practical limits"},{"level":2,"id":"8-connections-to-the-published-engineering-work","title":"8. Connections to the published engineering work"},{"level":2,"id":"9-conclusion","title":"9. Conclusion"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"knowledge-contracts-and-the-admission-of-prepared-knowledge","math_count":53,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/controlled-knowledge-reuse/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/controlled-knowledge-reuse/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"aadd6e7ecfb43e5fa912ec81adb5f367ee167397b5ce603d2fd0848d89d21ea4","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"evaluation"},{"canonical":true,"id":"knowledge-utility","kind":"paper","type":"paper","path":"papers/knowledge-utility/PAPER.md","route":"/en/science/knowledge-utility/","title":"Knowledge Utility, Resource Accounting, and Lifecycle Decisions","titleRu":"Knowledge Utility, Resource Accounting, and Lifecycle Decisions","summary":"Task-relative benefit, complete resource accounting, comparison baselines and limits of causal inference.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Knowledge is useful relative to a task, recipient, policy, time horizon, and alternative. Truth, evidence quality, popularity, and utility therefore cannot be represented as one interchangeable score. This paper defines conditional utility and separates measured outcomes, predicted benefit, resource costs, and causal attribution. It gives conservation conditions for shared-cost allocation, shows why complementary contributions are generally nonadditive, and derives a restricted break-even condition for prepared knowledge. Lifecycle decisions are framed in terms of future marginal value subject to mandatory constraints, while storage retention remains distinct from residency and current eligibility. The contribution is an accounting and evaluation model that prevents common forms of double counting and unsupported attribution. It does not supply universal weights, prices, retention thresholds, or empirical claims that structured knowledge is always beneficial. Those quantities require explicit measurement under a stated intervention and comparison policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> knowledge utility; causal attribution; resource accounting; marginal value; retention; reuse economics.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-research-question\">1. Research question</h2>\n<p>A fact can be true and irrelevant to a task. A false claim can be useful as an object of investigation without becoming true. An expensive artifact can be worth retaining, while a frequently retrieved one can add little to outcomes. These cases show why “knowledge value” is not a permanent intrinsic score.</p>\n<p>We ask: <strong>how should a system assess the contribution and cost of knowledge without confusing truth, exposure, attribution, and usefulness?</strong> The model below treats value as conditional and preserves the units and assumptions of each assessment.</p>\n<p>The causal distinction is central. An observed association between access to an artifact and a good outcome does not alone identify the artifact’s effect. Causal inference requires assumptions connecting observed data to the intervention being assessed [1]. Our contribution is to carry that discipline into knowledge selection, prepared-artifact reuse, and retention accounting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-conditional-utility-and-outcome-vectors\">2. Conditional utility and outcome vectors</h2>\n<p>For knowledge artifact <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nV(k\\mid q,c,\\pi,h,b,r),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the task or task distribution, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math> the consumer and context, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi</annotation></semantics></math> the use and evaluation policy, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math> the observation horizon, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math> the baseline, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math> the pinned artifact and policy revisions. These conditions are part of the claim. Changing them can change the value being estimated.</p>\n<p>An outcome can be a vector: task quality, consumer effort, latency, uncertainty reduction, prevented harm, introduced harm, and future usefulness. Each component needs a definition, unit, observation method, and treatment of missing data. Predicted benefit, measured outcome, and unresolved assessment must remain distinct. Unknown is not zero.</p>\n<p>A scalar score is legitimate only with an explicit mapping <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math> from the outcome vector to a declared scalar scale. That mapping includes weights, trade-offs, and any monetary conversions. Different stakeholders can legitimately choose different mappings. Where no justified common scale exists, a partial comparison such as Pareto dominance is more informative than an unexplained total ranking. Subsequent comparisons keep <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math> and the conditioning arguments fixed unless a policy change is explicitly being assessed.</p>\n<p>Costs must be included once. One can define a net utility that already contains resource penalties, or subtract a separate cost measure from a gross benefit measure. Subtracting the same cost again produces an accounting error. A new weight or tariff policy creates a new assessment, rather than silently rewriting the meaning of an earlier score.</p>\n<p>Truth, evidential support, source quality, applicability, and utility remain separate axes. Utility does not authorize a false factual claim, and low utility does not refute a proposition. These distinctions permit honest evaluation of investigation, correction, and rarely needed knowledge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-resource-accounting\">3. Resource accounting</h2>\n<p>A resource ledger records the operations that actually consume resources. Depending on the system, these can include input and output tokens, processor time, elapsed time, human effort, storage over time, network transfer, index updates, checks, retries, and reconstruction after eviction. The measurement unit and scope must be explicit.</p>\n<p>Elapsed latency is not generally the sum of parallel operation durations. Logical artifacts are not physical replicas. A node count is not a monetary cost without a model connecting it to resources. Monetary amounts require a tariff revision, currency, date, and allocation policy. Billed, estimated, and predicted costs should not be merged into one unlabeled total.</p>\n<p>A retry can incur real additional cost; a duplicated telemetry event does not. Stable event identity with payload-conflict handling separates these cases. Tokens, their priced estimate, and the corresponding invoice line are different views of a cost, not three independent charges.</p>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-1-conservative-shared-cost-allocation-prevents-creation-of-cost\">Proposition 1. Conservative shared-cost allocation prevents creation of cost</h3>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℰ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite set of measured operations in one accounting scope and unit. For each operation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e</annotation></semantics></math>, let its nonnegative cost <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_e</annotation></semantics></math>, nonnegative consumer allocations <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{e,1},\\ldots,a_{e,n_e}</annotation></semantics></math>, and nonnegative unallocated residual <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_e</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n_e}a_{e,i}+u_e=c_e.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>If every underlying operation and each of its allocated and residual parts are included exactly once, then</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℰ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℰ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal E}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{n_e}a_{e,i}+u_e\\right)\n=\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal E}c_e.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Sum the per-operation equalities over <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℰ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal E</annotation></semantics></math>. Nonnegativity also gives <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{e,i}\\leq c_e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_e\\leq c_e</annotation></semantics></math> for every component, so an allocation cannot hide an excessive charge behind a negative share. ∎</p>\n<p>The result is an accounting identity, not a uniquely fair allocation rule. It requires a closed accounting scope. Omitting shared infrastructure or counting the same event in two ledgers defeats the premises.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-contribution-and-causal-attribution\">4. Contribution and causal attribution</h2>\n<p>Events such as found, selected, delivered, consumed, cited, and accepted describe different stages. A citation does not show that the cited artifact caused the outcome. Acceptance of a Work Item does not establish the separate usefulness of every attached source.</p>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_w(\\pi+k)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the outcome for task <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math> under a policy that offers artifact <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_w(\\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> the outcome under the same policy without that intervention. With the scalar mapping <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math> from §2 and a fixed task distribution <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒟</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal D</annotation></semantics></math>, the artifact-specific contribution target is</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\Delta V(k)=\\mathbb E_{w\\sim\\mathcal D}\n\\left[U(Y_w(\\pi+k))-U(Y_w(\\pi))\\right].\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>The policies differ only in access to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math> and its predeclared consequences. A comparison against a different baseline policy <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_b</annotation></semantics></math> is also meaningful, but estimates the whole policy contrast. If the new condition changes the model, review budget, interface, or task selection as well, attributing that contrast specifically to <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math> requires additional identification. The conditioning arguments of §2 remain fixed in the displayed shorthand.</p>\n<p>The expression defines a target; it does not identify it from arbitrary logs. Randomized assignment, paired task designs, or observational adjustment each require their own assumptions about assignment, interference, support, measurement, and the relevant population. Unrecorded selection effects can make a frequently used artifact appear beneficial because it is offered on easier tasks.</p>\n<p>Observation, association, prediction, and causal estimate must therefore be labeled separately. A model’s predicted gain can guide exploration but cannot be reported as an observed effect. The observation horizon also matters: immediate completion, delayed retention, and later reuse are different outcomes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-complementarity-duplication-and-exposure\">5. Complementarity, duplication, and exposure</h2>\n<p>Write <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\mathrm{set}}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> for the utility assessment of offering an artifact set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math>, with all conditions from §2 fixed. This set function is distinct from the single-artifact notation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(k\\mid q,c,\\pi,h,b,r)</annotation></semantics></math>; its singleton values refer to the same assessed single-artifact interventions. Set utility need not equal the sum of its members’ utility. Consider a rule and its necessary condition. Neither alone is useful for the permitted task, but the pair is. If</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>⌀</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nV_{\\mathrm{set}}(\\varnothing)=V_{\\mathrm{set}}(\\{a\\})=V_{\\mathrm{set}}(\\{b\\})=0,\n\\qquad V_{\\mathrm{set}}(\\{a,b\\})=1,\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>then each leave-one-out contribution within the pair is one. Summing those contributions yields two, although the total gain is one. Marginal contribution therefore depends on the reference set, and summing several margins can double-count complementary value.</p>\n<p>Redundant copies create a different problem. Splitting one informational artifact into multiple identifiers should not automatically create multiple independent contributions. Attribution requires a declared identity and coalition policy. An allocation rule can distribute a defined total; it cannot discover unobserved outcomes or establish a uniquely fair reward by itself.</p>\n<p>Use frequency needs exposure and opportunity denominators. Low use can reflect narrow applicability, limited demand, poor retrieval, restricted access, or an immature observation period. If a ranking system suppresses artifacts with few uses, new artifacts may receive no opportunity to demonstrate value. This is a possible feedback mechanism, not proof that every ranking system exhibits it.</p>\n<p>Evaluation can address the problem through a declared exploration budget, independent task samples, and separate reporting for new and mature artifacts. Such choices are policy decisions with costs and assumptions, not universal constants.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-reuse-economics-and-lifecycle-decisions\">6. Reuse economics and lifecycle decisions</h2>\n<h3 id=\"proposition-2-a-simple-reuse-policy-has-a-conditional-break-even-point\">Proposition 2. A simple reuse policy has a conditional break-even point</h3>\n<p>Suppose a valid cold execution costs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math> per request. Preparing an equally acceptable reusable artifact costs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math> once, and each admitted reuse costs <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_{\\mathrm{hit}}</annotation></semantics></math>, including its required checks. These costs are nonnegative and measured in the same unit over a fixed horizon <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math>. With <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\geq1</annotation></semantics></math> requests, no intervening invalidation, and no other costs, reuse is cheaper exactly when</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">equivalently</mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"></mspace><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nB+Nc_{\\mathrm{hit}}&lt;NG,\n\\quad\\text{equivalently}\\quad\nN(G-c_{\\mathrm{hit}})&gt;B.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong> Compare the total costs and rearrange the inequality. If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G\\leq c_{\\mathrm{hit}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a positive preparation cost cannot be recovered under these assumptions. ∎</p>\n<p>This result is deliberately narrow. In the <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">companion study’s accounting model</a>, it is the special case with hit fraction <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1</annotation></semantics></math>, no rebuilds or additional invalidation and storage costs (<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=I=S=0</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_{\\mathrm{hit}}=c_v+c_r</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_v</annotation></semantics></math> is per-request validation and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_r</annotation></semantics></math> is additional hit-path cost. Both formulas use the same horizon and cost scope. Storage, invalidation, rebuilding, coordination, failed attempts, and changing demand can alter the calculation. The two routes must satisfy the same relevant acceptance conditions; omitting required checks is not an economic improvement under that contract.</p>\n<p>Lifecycle choices concern future marginal benefits, future costs, switching costs, and mandatory constraints. Past acquisition cost belongs in the historical ledger; a large historical expenditure does not by itself justify continued expensive residency.</p>\n<p><strong>Retention</strong> is the storage policy governing preservation or deletion of bytes. <strong>Residency</strong> concerns where those retained bytes are available, including whether reconstruction is needed. Search visibility, background preparation, and current-use eligibility are separate lifecycle decisions. Archiving a retained original, removing it from search, and withdrawing permission to use it therefore have different effects. A rarely used artifact may be necessary for reproducibility or a critical deadline. A negative utility estimate does not alone authorize destroying the only retained original.</p>\n<p>Residency policies can use measured demand and rehydration cost, with hysteresis to avoid repeated switching. Thresholds must be calibrated to the workload and constraints. Popularity cannot override revocation, known corruption, or a mandatory scope restriction. The <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled reuse paper</a> defines admission before economic ranking.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-evaluation-and-falsification\">7. Evaluation and falsification</h2>\n<p>A utility study should predeclare the task distribution, baseline, intervention, outcome rubric, horizon, resource scope, and treatment of unknown outcomes. It should record opportunity to use the knowledge, actual exposure, and downstream consumption separately. An ablation must remove the intended contribution without silently changing unrelated parts of the system.</p>\n<p>Useful negative controls include repeated telemetry with one real operation, complementary artifacts whose summed margins overstate total gain, identical knowledge under multiple identifiers, and a policy change bundled with artifact access. Each reveals a specific attribution or accounting error.</p>\n<p>For lifecycle policies, evaluation should include rare mandatory tasks, bursts of demand, changes of source state, and reconstruction after eviction, while measuring storage retention, residency, and eligibility separately. Results should state whether costs are observed or modeled and whether the policy was tuned on the same workload. A successful finite control does not establish optimal retention or residency for every future demand pattern.</p>\n<p>The results of this paper are a conditional evaluation model, conservation and break-even properties, and counterexamples to additive or popularity-based shortcuts. No universal utility score or measured economic superiority is asserted. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse admission and cost controls study</a> contains finite admission counterexamples and exact cost arithmetic on synthetic inputs; it reports no measured model savings or production workload. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial skill pilot</a> supplies a separate exploratory comparison of document outputs, without a cost estimate.</p>\n<p>The shared <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence and falsification paper</a> define the corresponding claim and measurement boundaries.</p>\n<h2 id=\"references\">References</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Pearl, J. “Causal Inference in Statistics: An Overview.” <em>Statistics Surveys</em> 3, 2009, 96–146. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1214/09-SS057\" class=\"uri\">https://doi.org/10.1214/09-SS057</a></li>\n</ol>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"1-research-question","title":"1. Research question"},{"level":2,"id":"2-conditional-utility-and-outcome-vectors","title":"2. Conditional utility and outcome vectors"},{"level":2,"id":"3-resource-accounting","title":"3. Resource accounting"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-1-conservative-shared-cost-allocation-prevents-creation-of-cost","title":"Proposition 1. Conservative shared-cost allocation prevents creation of cost"},{"level":2,"id":"4-contribution-and-causal-attribution","title":"4. Contribution and causal attribution"},{"level":2,"id":"5-complementarity-duplication-and-exposure","title":"5. Complementarity, duplication, and exposure"},{"level":2,"id":"6-reuse-economics-and-lifecycle-decisions","title":"6. Reuse economics and lifecycle decisions"},{"level":3,"id":"proposition-2-a-simple-reuse-policy-has-a-conditional-break-even-point","title":"Proposition 2. A simple reuse policy has a conditional break-even point"},{"level":2,"id":"7-evaluation-and-falsification","title":"7. Evaluation and falsification"},{"level":2,"id":"references","title":"References"}],"title_id":"knowledge-utility-resource-accounting-and-lifecycle-decisions","math_count":46,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/knowledge-utility/PAPER.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/papers/knowledge-utility/PAPER.md","source_sha256":"e807eacfe0c69620457232e00a1f09edb1e9753493e5f5478f8bdfa7e05c73e1","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"evaluation"},{"canonical":true,"id":"knowledge-contract-core","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/","title":"Knowledge Contract: Core Model and Admission Requirements","titleRu":"Knowledge Contract: Core Model and Admission Requirements","summary":"The nine core groups, first-class constraints, profiles, admission and execution requirements.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p>A Knowledge Contract binds a body of knowledge to its intended use, grounds, dependencies, constraints, and assessment. It lets a recipient inspect what is offered, which conditions matter, and what has actually been checked. It does not turn a source assertion into a fact or an instruction into permission to act.</p>\n<p>This specification defines the architecture of a conforming implementation. Capitalized requirement words follow <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R001.</strong> Conformance MUST identify the implemented profile, its version, the reader, and the assessed scope.</p>\n<p>A claim of full conformance cannot rest on a schema check alone. Publication edition 1.1 is the edition of this document; serialized profiles retain their own versions. The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/#4-protocol-compatibility\">compatibility record</a> identifies the existing bounded implementation without renaming it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-core-model\">2. Core model</h2>\n<p>The contract has nine logical groups.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R002.</strong> A serializer MAY organize them differently if its mapping preserves their obligations.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Required meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Identity</td>\n<td>Stable object identity, immutable revision, contract definition and representation profile versions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>Intended use, task and requirements revision, recipient, required profiles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Content</td>\n<td>Addressable units, their semantic roles, source references and protected conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>Conditions of applicability and explicit unknown, inapplicable or withheld values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Grounds</td>\n<td>The source revision, locator, derivation and evidence supporting each relevant claim</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Dependencies</td>\n<td>Required relationships distinguished from optional reading links</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Obligations</td>\n<td>Preconditions, invariants, preservation duties, coverage requirements and acceptance obligations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Projection</td>\n<td>Delivered representation, included and omitted units, declared losses and transformations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Observations</td>\n<td>Assessments bound to a subject revision, method, observer, time and limitations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>KCC-R003.</strong> An empty list MUST mean an established empty set within a declared scope.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R004.</strong> It MUST NOT stand for an unknown set.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R005.</strong> A profile MAY represent a genuinely irrelevant group as not applicable with a reason.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R006.</strong> It MUST NOT silently omit a required obligation because no evidence is available.</p>\n<p>The contract’s immutable content and the current decision to use it are distinct records. Revoking a grant changes admissibility for a use; it does not rewrite what the earlier contract said.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-constraints-are-first-class-obligations\">3. Constraints are first-class obligations</h2>\n<p>A <strong>constraint</strong> restricts an admissible interpretation, transformation, delivery or action.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R007.</strong> Each constraint MUST have an identity, subject, scope, predicate or human-readable rule, source of authority when relevant, severity, and a checking method or an explicit unresolved status.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R008.</strong> An implementation MUST retain its applicability conditions and dependencies.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/#4-constraints-and-professional-artifacts\">artifact registry</a> defines the readable <code>CONSTRAINT.md</code> artifact.</p>\n<p>Four distinctions are mandatory:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Example of an abstract rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Applicability condition</td>\n<td>Under what circumstances does the knowledge apply?</td>\n<td>The input belongs to the stated domain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Action constraint</td>\n<td>What conduct is forbidden or bounded?</td>\n<td>The operation may write only the named resource</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Capability requirement</td>\n<td>Can this executor perform the required method?</td>\n<td>An assessment covers this skill and environment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Acceptance criterion</td>\n<td>What demonstrates the requested result?</td>\n<td>The delivered output satisfies the pinned criterion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>KCC-R009.</strong> A constraint can also be an acceptance obligation, but the relationship MUST be explicit.</p>\n<p>Success at an output criterion does not excuse a prohibited action used to obtain it. A skill description does not establish capability. A role assignment does not by itself supply a grant.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R010.</strong> When profiles compose, all applicable mandatory constraints MUST remain in force unless an identified authority, authenticated at the same declared trust boundary as evidence, has explicitly resolved a conflict within its verified authority.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R011.</strong> Load order, a later paragraph, or a more convenient result MUST NOT implicitly override a restriction.</p>\n<p>A contradiction blocks the affected use or produces a narrowed, explicitly permitted alternative.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-profiles-and-compatibility\">4. Profiles and compatibility</h2>\n<p>The supported profile families are professional work, dialogue, documents, reasoning, visual representations, and reuse. Each profile declares its version, why it is present, whether it is required, and its payload. The need for a profile follows from the declared purpose and requirements; a parser is not assumed to infer it correctly from arbitrary natural language.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R012.</strong> A reader MUST advertise supported representations and required extensions.</p>\n<p>An unknown required profile makes the contract unusable for that request. A profile carrying any mandatory constraint is required regardless of a producer-supplied optional flag.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R013.</strong> A reader MUST derive requiredness from the trusted request/policy and known constraint references as well as profile declarations, and MUST block an unknown constraint-bearing profile.</p>\n<p>An opaque profile whose constraint relevance cannot be established is unknown for admission, not harmless.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R014.</strong> Only an extension established as irrelevant to every mandatory obligation MAY be retained as optional opaque data, with the limitation reported.</p>\n<p>Preserving its bytes does not verify its semantics. Compatibility is directional: a new reader accepting an old record does not prove that an old reader can safely rewrite the new record.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-admission-and-execution\">5. Admission and execution</h2>\n<p><strong>KCC-R015.</strong> Admission MUST evaluate the exact contract revision, requirements, recipient, environment, applicable constraints, mandatory dependencies, and current authority.</p>\n<p>Each mandatory check yields pass, fail, unknown, or justified not applicable.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R016.</strong> Unknown MUST NOT be promoted to pass.</p>\n<p>An execution additionally binds the Work Item revision, requirements revision, contract revision or digest, attempt, executor, relevant grants and capabilities, output identity, and criterion-specific evidence. Receipt of a plan is not execution. A successful process exit is not acceptance. If a remote effect is uncertain after a timeout, the attempt remains unresolved until reconciliation; a retry requires an explicit repeat policy.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R017.</strong> A conforming execution adapter MUST validate evidence provenance at its declared trust boundary.</p>\n<p>A boolean supplied by an untrusted caller saying that evidence is trusted is insufficient. The existing finite model assumes a supplied context and does not provide that production trust boundary; this limitation is measured in the linked study.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-migration-and-failure-handling\">6. Migration and failure handling</h2>\n<p>Migration creates a new representation and retains the source revision and transformation record. Missing conditions, grants or capabilities remain unknown. A free-text blueprint is not automatically converted into a process graph.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R018.</strong> Unmapped fields and semantic losses MUST be reported.</p>\n<p>Schema validity alone cannot justify <code>semantic_equivalence</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R019.</strong> Refusal reports SHOULD identify the failed obligation and an actionable reason without exposing protected grounds to an unauthorized recipient.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R020.</strong> An implementation MUST NOT create a success-shaped output when assembly, authorization, or evidence checking fails.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases-and-evidence\">7. Acceptance cases and evidence</h2>\n<p><strong>KCC-R021.</strong> A conformance suite MUST include: a supported positive request; a missing required dependency; an unknown required profile; a conflicting constraint; an outdated requirement revision; an unauthorized executor; mismatched output evidence; a migration with unknown values; and a false claim of trusted evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>KCC-R022.</strong> Schema, internal consistency, applicability, and execution acceptance MUST be reported separately.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">contract-profile study</a> reports 35/35 and 26/26 bounded scenario results, alongside false trusted-input acceptances and a 9/9 versus 9/9 baseline tie. These observations support finite conformance claims and expose the trust-boundary gap. They do not establish general task superiority or production authorization.</p>\n<p>The scientific justification is developed in <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled knowledge reuse</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence and falsification</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">shared glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-core-model","title":"2. Core model"},{"level":2,"id":"3-constraints-are-first-class-obligations","title":"3. Constraints are first-class obligations"},{"level":2,"id":"4-profiles-and-compatibility","title":"4. Profiles and compatibility"},{"level":2,"id":"5-admission-and-execution","title":"5. Admission and execution"},{"level":2,"id":"6-migration-and-failure-handling","title":"6. Migration and failure handling"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases-and-evidence","title":"7. Acceptance cases and evidence"}],"title_id":"knowledge-contract-core-model-and-admission-requirements","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"81cb01a8326eaeb0c14e929609c52cc85e5a344c61f3744d2e0def228cdc60d5","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"contract-family","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/contract-family/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/contract-family/","title":"Contracts for Knowledge Operations","titleRu":"Contracts for Knowledge Operations","summary":"Common preconditions, invariants, postconditions, effects and evidence for knowledge operations.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p>An operation contract states when an operation may run, what it preserves or changes, which outcomes are permitted, and what evidence supports the reported outcome. Its promise is local to a declared input, requirements revision and environment. Completing one operation does not necessarily satisfy the entire Work Item.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R001.</strong> This document specifies operation semantics; a conforming adapter MUST also identify its representation profile and executable checks.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">transformation paper</a> supplies the conditional formal model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-common-contract-record\">2. Common contract record</h2>\n<p><strong>KOP-R002.</strong> Every operation definition MUST identify the following:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Field group</th>\n<th>Required content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Definition</td>\n<td>Operation name, immutable definition version and supported input/output profiles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>The local goal and its relationship to a Work Item and requirements revision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Inputs</td>\n<td>Exact artifact revisions, cardinalities, relevant environment and missing-input behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Preconditions</td>\n<td>Conditions that authorize and enable the attempt; checking methods and trust boundaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Invariants</td>\n<td>Properties preserved throughout the operation’s declared observable scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Postconditions</td>\n<td>Conditions attached to each allowed outcome, including partial and refused outcomes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Effects</td>\n<td>Read/write scope, external effects, repeat policy, reconciliation and compensation behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Resources</td>\n<td>Budget, deadline, maximum attempts and stop rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>Subject bindings, observation method and the limits of the evaluator</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>KOP-R003.</strong> Preconditions, invariants and postconditions MUST remain separate.</p>\n<p>A later successful observation does not establish that an invariant held at every earlier step. Evidence of an output’s shape does not establish its semantic correctness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-operation-families\">3. Operation families</h2>\n<p>The following functional families organize implementations. They are responsibilities, not a requirement to deploy separate services.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>Preservation obligation</th>\n<th>Principal failure mode</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Acquire and address</td>\n<td>Source identity, version, locator and acquisition provenance</td>\n<td>An unavailable or substituted source is treated as observed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Interpret and extract</td>\n<td>Attribution, negation, quantifiers, exceptions and unresolved readings</td>\n<td>A source claim becomes an unqualified fact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Search and compare</td>\n<td>Query semantics, scope, candidate provenance and comparison criterion</td>\n<td>Similarity becomes equivalence or completeness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Select and compose</td>\n<td>Mandatory dependency closure, joint identity, coverage and total budget</td>\n<td>Necessary conditions disappear during shortening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Transform and render</td>\n<td>Declared protected observations, representation requirements and losses</td>\n<td>A readable description is substituted for essential media</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Deliver and acknowledge</td>\n<td>Recipient, representation, revision and delivery observation</td>\n<td>A locator is mistaken for delivered content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Plan and execute</td>\n<td>Work bindings, capability, authority, attempt identity and effects</td>\n<td>A plan or timeout is mistaken for a known execution outcome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assess and revise</td>\n<td>Exact subject, method, new evidence and preserved history</td>\n<td>A verdict is transferred to another revision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Store and reuse</td>\n<td>Dependency pins, current applicability, authorization and invalidation</td>\n<td>A cache hit bypasses the admission decision</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>KOP-R004.</strong> An implementation MAY expose narrower operations within these families.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R005.</strong> Every exposed operation MUST state which of these responsibilities it owns and which it delegates.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R006.</strong> It MUST NOT hide a necessary responsibility between adapters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-composition\">4. Composition</h2>\n<p><strong>KOP-R007.</strong> For operations A then B, every allowed successful output of A used by B MUST satisfy B’s preconditions.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R008.</strong> A shared invariant MUST survive both transitions.</p>\n<p>Failure to establish that relation leaves composition unresolved even when each operation passes its isolated tests.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R009.</strong> A semantic transformation MUST declare its protected observations: for example the truth conditions of a proposition, the order of procedural steps, or the labels and connections of a diagram.</p>\n<p>Equality of those observations is a narrower claim than equality of all meanings.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R010.</strong> Lossy transformations MUST retain a loss account.</p>\n<p>An inverse can be claimed only within a declared domain for which reconstruction has been justified.</p>\n<p>Operations with effects require a separate composition policy. A local transaction around a record does not make an external service atomic.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R011.</strong> A timeout after dispatch MUST preserve an unknown-effect state until a domain-specific reconciliation establishes what occurred.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-outcomes-and-evidence\">5. Outcomes and evidence</h2>\n<p>The outcome vocabulary is <code>success</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>refused</code>, <code>failed</code> and <code>unresolved</code>, as defined in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#3-canonical-dimension-vocabularies\">status model</a>. <code>ready</code> is a lifecycle or run phase, not an operation outcome. Stop reasons explain why control ended; effect observations describe what is known about external changes.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R012.</strong> These dimensions MUST NOT be collapsed into a single completion flag.</p>\n<p>A comparison operation can succeed by reporting non-equivalence. A refusal can correctly enforce a constraint without satisfying the user’s original goal.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R013.</strong> Evidence MUST identify the definition version, input and output revisions, attempt, relevant requirements, checker and check result.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R014.</strong> An inherited observation MUST name the premise that permits inheritance.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R015.</strong> Checks of supplied claims and checks of independently observed conditions MUST be distinguishable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-acceptance-cases\">6. Acceptance cases</h2>\n<p><strong>KOP-R016.</strong> A conforming adapter MUST exercise valid input; invalid input; unknown mandatory preconditions; a protected-property violation; partial output; an exhausted budget; a stale requirement revision; and, for effectful operations, a timeout with uncertain effect.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R017.</strong> A composition test MUST include a valid A output that violates B’s precondition.</p>\n<p>The expected result is refusal or unresolved composition, not automatic continuation.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency and delivery study</a> supplies a finite closure replay. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">executable projection study</a> supplies bounded projection and revision evidence. Their finite results do not certify every family in this specification.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-representation-and-evolution\">7. Representation and evolution</h2>\n<p>The architecture does not require a new global artifact type for each operation. A versioned definition can be represented as an addressed record linked from attempts and evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>KOP-R018.</strong> Legacy example envelopes remain interpretable under their original profiles; an adapter MUST disclose any loss when mapping them to this family.</p>\n<p>An operation definition change creates a new version. Attempts keep the definition they used. Reassessment under the new definition is a new observation, not a retrospective claim that the earlier attempt followed it. See <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/\">knowledge evolution</a> and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">status model</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-common-contract-record","title":"2. Common contract record"},{"level":2,"id":"3-operation-families","title":"3. Operation families"},{"level":2,"id":"4-composition","title":"4. Composition"},{"level":2,"id":"5-outcomes-and-evidence","title":"5. Outcomes and evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"6-acceptance-cases","title":"6. Acceptance cases"},{"level":2,"id":"7-representation-and-evolution","title":"7. Representation and evolution"}],"title_id":"contracts-for-knowledge-operations","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/contract-family/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/contract-family/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"310cd3f151275739a2391d2c8d8b7c23ad64b46f4c1a56717f7f0975866f60c8","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"semantic-types","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/semantic-types/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/","title":"Semantic Types, Attribution, and Representations","titleRu":"Semantic Types, Attribution, and Representations","summary":"Semantic facets, attribution, scoped operators, ambiguous readings and multimodal representations.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R001.</strong> This specification defines the distinctions an implementation MUST preserve when interpreting and representing knowledge.</p>\n<p>Its facets organize content without claiming an exhaustive taxonomy of all human meaning. A procedure, a quoted procedure, and a deliberately incorrect procedural example may use similar words but support different uses.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R002.</strong> A conforming profile MUST state its vocabulary, cardinalities and representation mapping.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">scientific model</a> defines the separation between a source representation, interpretation, grounds and applicability.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-independent-semantic-dimensions\">2. Independent semantic dimensions</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Information to retain</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Representation</td>\n<td>Text, image, audio, video, structured data or an explicit combination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Content kind</td>\n<td>Definition, observation, description, model, procedure, structure, constraint, goal, argument, question or another declared kind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Discourse function</td>\n<td>Explanation, instruction, quotation, illustration, counterexample, question or another contextual role</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Aboutness</td>\n<td>The entities, concepts or domains to which the content refers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Attribution</td>\n<td>Who authored, reported, interpreted or assessed the addressed content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Source position</td>\n<td>Whether a source asserts, hypothesizes, denies, asks or reports another position</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Operators</td>\n<td>Negation, quantification, modality, condition, exception, order and their exact scopes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assessments</td>\n<td>References to observations about fidelity, support or another stated property</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Governance</td>\n<td>Adoption and authority records, where relevant to the intended use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Applicability</td>\n<td>Domain, time, recipient and other conditions under which the content may be used</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>SEM-R003.</strong> A profile MAY store several dimensions in one structured record, but MUST NOT conflate their meanings.</p>\n<p>A source position is not a truth value. An author’s approval of a method is not current permission for a recipient to perform it. A generated image description is not the source image.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R004.</strong> For each required facet, the profile MUST distinguish known value, unknown value, justified not applicable, and withheld value.</p>\n<p>Unknown is not an empty known list.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R005.</strong> Withheld indicates a disclosure boundary and MUST NOT be represented as evidence that no value exists.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-identity-granularity-and-relations\">3. Identity, granularity and relations</h2>\n<p>One source region can support several semantic objects; one semantic object can require several source regions.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R006.</strong> Extraction boundaries MUST follow the obligations of the intended interpretation.</p>\n<p>A condition in a heading, a table unit or a diagram legend can be mandatory even if it falls outside a retrieved chunk.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R007.</strong> Source identity, semantic object identity, representation identity and retrieval chunk identity MUST remain distinguishable.</p>\n<p>Rechunking an index need not change the interpreted semantic object. Correcting an interpretation does require a new semantic revision. A different rendering can preserve a semantic revision only when the claimed equivalence has a stated basis.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R008.</strong> Relations MUST identify their endpoints and kind.</p>\n<p>A navigation link suggests further reading; a mandatory dependency says that a declared use requires the linked information.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R009.</strong> A vague association MUST NOT be silently upgraded to a proof premise, causal link or authority relation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-scope-and-ambiguity\">4. Scope and ambiguity</h2>\n<p><strong>SEM-R010.</strong> Operators MUST bind to their actual scope.</p>\n<p>“Not all members satisfy P” cannot be rewritten as “all members do not satisfy P.” An absence of permission is not an explicit prohibition, although either may prevent a particular action under its policy. A reported hypothesis is not an accepted conclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R011.</strong> When several interpretations remain possible, an implementation MUST retain the alternatives or mark the interpretation unresolved.</p>\n<p>Choosing one is permitted only with a recorded basis and a declaration of remaining uncertainty. A confidence score alone does not explain which interpretation was chosen or which distinction could change the answer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-multimodal-content-and-delivery\">5. Multimodal content and delivery</h2>\n<p><strong>SEM-R012.</strong> A media reference MUST identify the representation revision and selector semantics.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R013.</strong> Required legends, ordering, coordinate conventions, units and joint components MUST be retained.</p>\n<p>Delivery of a textual substitute is sufficient only when the task’s protected observations can be established from that substitute.</p>\n<p>OCR, a model-generated description, a human transcription and an original visual are separately attributable derivatives.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R014.</strong> A profile MUST record which was inspected.</p>\n<p>Claims about transcription fidelity require a comparison method and a reference; merely preserving all generated descriptions does not measure their accuracy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-validation-obligations\">6. Validation obligations</h2>\n<p><strong>SEM-R015.</strong> The conformance suite MUST include an attributed false assertion, a scoped negation, a quotation inside another quotation, a counterexample with tempting instructional wording, an unresolved alternative reading, and a required media dependency omitted from delivery.</p>\n<p>The expected behavior is preservation or explicit refusal; fluent reconstruction is not a substitute for those checks.</p>\n<p>Structural validation can check facet presence, vocabulary and references. It does not establish that the extractor found every relevant condition. Semantic fidelity therefore requires content-specific assessment and a stated inspection method.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-evidence-and-interoperability\">7. Evidence and interoperability</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">transcription and uncertainty study</a> reports how generated descriptions and uncertainty were retained in readable projections. It explicitly separates that result from unmeasured OCR or interpretation accuracy. Domain examples belong to that engineering study.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#skos\">SKOS</a> provides useful concepts, labels and relations; <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#provenance\">PROV-DM</a> supplies provenance distinctions. This profile does not claim to replace either standard or to make their graphs prove content truth.</p>\n<p><strong>SEM-R016.</strong> Vocabulary mappings MUST state whether they are exact, broader, narrower or merely related.</p>\n<p>See the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> for the project’s preferred terms.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-independent-semantic-dimensions","title":"2. Independent semantic dimensions"},{"level":2,"id":"3-identity-granularity-and-relations","title":"3. Identity, granularity and relations"},{"level":2,"id":"4-scope-and-ambiguity","title":"4. Scope and ambiguity"},{"level":2,"id":"5-multimodal-content-and-delivery","title":"5. Multimodal content and delivery"},{"level":2,"id":"6-validation-obligations","title":"6. Validation obligations"},{"level":2,"id":"7-evidence-and-interoperability","title":"7. Evidence and interoperability"}],"title_id":"semantic-types-attribution-and-representations","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"4428c3cdacfe5e8bcf00b8d9100a79f5ce9691edecd914063698bfb3f97e8f6f","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"context-package","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/context-package/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/","title":"Context Packages: Coverage, Dependencies, and Delivery","titleRu":"Context Packages: Coverage, Dependencies, and Delivery","summary":"Requirements for selected context, coverage, mandatory dependencies, budgets and observed delivery.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p>A context package is a versioned selection of knowledge, conditions and grounds prepared for one declared use under a resource budget. It allows inspection of what was selected, why it is sufficient or incomplete, which dependencies were included, and what the recipient actually receives.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. The package supports an answer, plan or execution stage. It is not itself a grant to act or evidence that the intended result occurred. See <a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">retrieval and context composition</a> for the formal distinction between coverage and search completeness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-required-package-description\">2. Required package description</h2>\n<p><strong>CTX-R001.</strong> A conforming package MUST contain or reference its identity and revision; task and requirements revision; purpose and recipient; source and corpus snapshots; selected unit revisions; mandatory dependency closure; slot-level coverage; declared search scope and assurance; applicable constraints; representation and delivery requirements; resource account; and the observations supporting its status.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R002.</strong> Each requirement slot MUST identify the subject, quantifier, required conditions and supporting units.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R003.</strong> Joint requirements MUST be supported for the same subject and revision.</p>\n<p>Evidence for a property of one object and a different property of another cannot satisfy a conjunction about one object.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R004.</strong> The package MUST identify omitted requirements and unresolved dependencies.</p>\n<p>If a necessary ground is restricted, an authorized summary or substitute requires its own justification. Silently removing the ground while retaining a claim of complete support is prohibited.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-selection-and-closure\">3. Selection and closure</h2>\n<p>Selection starts from candidate units and computes the closure of the mandatory relations active for this use. Navigation links do not automatically join the closure. Unknown or missing required endpoints prevent a complete-package claim.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R005.</strong> The finite set-theoretic closure exists even with cycles, but a particular executable profile MAY require an acyclic dependency graph.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R006.</strong> That restriction MUST be stated and checked.</p>\n<p>Rejection of a cycle by such a profile is not a claim that the abstract closure is undefined.</p>\n<p>Adding units can introduce new dependencies and costs.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R007.</strong> A composer MUST recalculate closure and budget after a change.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R008.</strong> It MUST NOT infer that a larger text is always safer or more useful: additional content may conflict, distract, disclose protected information or exceed the recipient’s usable capacity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-coverage-and-assurance\">4. Coverage and assurance</h2>\n<p>Coverage is assessed separately for candidate set, assembled package and final output.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R009.</strong> Each report MUST bind to its own stage and subject revision.</p>\n<p>A supported candidate set does not make a truncated package supported; a supported package does not prove that an answer used it correctly.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Coverage value</th>\n<th>Meaning within the declared requirements and scope</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>supported</td>\n<td>Every mandatory slot has the required established support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>partial</td>\n<td>A supported part exists, with named missing or unresolved obligations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>unsupported</td>\n<td>The evaluated scope provides no required support under the chosen procedure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>conflicted</td>\n<td>Material incompatible grounds prevent a single admitted conclusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>unknown</td>\n<td>The available observations cannot establish the coverage state</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Search assurance is a separate dimension: <code>candidate_only</code>, <code>exhaustive_in_scope</code>, or <code>incomplete_procedure</code>, using the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#3-canonical-dimension-vocabularies\">canonical token definitions</a>. A request for two examples may be supported without exhaustive search. A request for all matching objects requires an additional completeness obligation.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R010.</strong> An empty top-k result MUST NOT be reported as proof of global absence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-budget-and-projection\">5. Budget and projection</h2>\n<p><strong>CTX-R011.</strong> The budget MUST account for the actual delivery context: system and task instructions, relevant history, selected material, tool descriptions, anticipated tool results, output allowance and margin where applicable.</p>\n<p>A character count is not an exact token measurement.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R012.</strong> Estimated and measured quantities MUST be distinguishable and identify the tokenizer or measurement method.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R013.</strong> A projection MUST retain protected units, their relations and task-relevant uncertainty.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R014.</strong> It MUST identify excluded units and losses.</p>\n<p>An output that reconstructs the selected bytes exactly can still be semantically incomplete if selection omitted an exception. Byte equality and task sufficiency therefore require different checks.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R015.</strong> When the mandatory closure cannot fit, the composer MUST return a partial result, negotiate an allowed narrower scope, change representation with justified preservation, or refuse.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R016.</strong> It MUST NOT silently discard a mandatory condition to meet the limit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-delivery-and-receipt\">6. Delivery and receipt</h2>\n<p>The manifest describes what should be delivered; a receipt records what was observed at the recipient boundary. A media URL does not establish byte availability, display, legibility or understanding.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R017.</strong> The delivery check MUST match the task’s representation requirements and the declared reader capabilities.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R018.</strong> Any output assessment MUST name the exact delivered package and requirements revision.</p>\n<p>An acknowledgement of receipt or comprehension is not a capability assessment, agreement to execute, or authorization.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-and-evidence\">7. Acceptance and evidence</h2>\n<p><strong>CTX-R019.</strong> Acceptance cases MUST include an unresolved dependency, cross-object conjunction, missing quantifier coverage, a budget overflow caused by closure, omitted mandatory media, an outdated requirement revision, and a final output that drops a supported condition.</p>\n<p><strong>CTX-R020.</strong> For each, the check MUST distinguish package construction from downstream use.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency-closure and delivery study</a> includes an executable finite replay of all 4,096 subsets of the 12 skill roots and 24,576 single-element inclusion relations on a 110-node dependency graph. This verifies the specified graph behavior, not the completeness of dependencies extracted from arbitrary sources. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection study</a> examines downstream representations and reports the limits of measured minimality.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-compatibility\">8. Compatibility</h2>\n<p>Legacy coverage fields can be imported only with their subject and semantics. Missing source snapshots, budget measurements or delivery observations remain unknown. A new document edition does not retrospectively upgrade earlier illustrative examples into executed packages. Terms are defined in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-required-package-description","title":"2. Required package description"},{"level":2,"id":"3-selection-and-closure","title":"3. Selection and closure"},{"level":2,"id":"4-coverage-and-assurance","title":"4. Coverage and assurance"},{"level":2,"id":"5-budget-and-projection","title":"5. Budget and projection"},{"level":2,"id":"6-delivery-and-receipt","title":"6. Delivery and receipt"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-and-evidence","title":"7. Acceptance and evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"8-compatibility","title":"8. Compatibility"}],"title_id":"context-packages-coverage-dependencies-and-delivery","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/context-package/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/context-package/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"5bba82777b44dc0cf6b1b0c218292c1476c0271806acef195db6c3b8a9448e41","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"compiled-artifact-contract","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/","title":"Compiled Knowledge Artifacts and Reproducible Derivation","titleRu":"Compiled Knowledge Artifacts and Reproducible Derivation","summary":"Saved derivatives with exact inputs, recipes, dependency manifests, templates and output evidence.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p>A compiled knowledge artifact is a saved derivative produced by a declared extraction, selection, transformation or composition. It connects a result with its inputs, recipe, dependencies, requirements, output representation and evidence. Its availability can avoid repeated work, but availability alone does not justify reuse.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R001.</strong> A conformance claim MUST identify the artifact kind, representation profile and checks performed. <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">Controlled knowledge reuse</a> provides the model; <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/\">cache identity</a> and <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/\">reuse policy</a> govern later applications.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-result-kinds-and-minimum-information\">2. Result kinds and minimum information</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Result kind</th>\n<th>Additional minimum obligation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Retrieval result</td>\n<td>Query semantics, searched snapshot, procedure limits and candidate identities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Selected context</td>\n<td>Requirement coverage, mandatory closure, projection and resource account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Derived explanation or answer</td>\n<td>Supported claims, intended audience, losses and required uncertainty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Dependency closure</td>\n<td>Roots, relation policy, exact graph snapshot and unresolved edges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Parameterized template</td>\n<td>Allowed substitutions, domain restrictions and instance validation rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Scoped negative result</td>\n<td>The exact negative claim, finite scope, completeness premise and invalidators</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These kinds do not exhaust all possible derivatives.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R002.</strong> An extension MUST state its additional obligations and compatibility rules.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-artifact-and-build-records\">3. Artifact and build records</h2>\n<p><strong>CKA-R003.</strong> The artifact MUST identify immutable output bytes or a precisely canonicalized structured value, its hash domain, representation profile, content identity, source and rule-set revisions, recipe identity, and the producing attempt.</p>\n<p>A content digest establishes equality within its encoding assumptions; it does not authenticate the author or establish source truth.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R004.</strong> The build recipe MUST include exact input references, transformation definition versions, relevant parameters, and environment dependencies that can change the claimed result.</p>\n<p>A build identity records this declaration. Reproducibility additionally requires actual successful reruns under a stated equivalence criterion. A matching recipe hash does not prove that the recipe was executed or that nondeterministic outputs are equal.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R005.</strong> An attempt record MUST distinguish the <code>planned</code>, <code>ready</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>paused</code> and <code>finished</code> execution phases from the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#3-canonical-dimension-vocabularies\">status model</a>; outcome; stop reason; output references if any; and observed effects.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R006.</strong> A failed attempt MUST NOT create a fictional successful artifact.</p>\n<p>Partial outputs can be retained as partial with their limitations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-dependency-manifest\">4. Dependency manifest</h2>\n<p><strong>CKA-R007.</strong> The manifest MUST include each declared dependency’s identity, revision, role, requirement status, relevant validity conditions and change sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Dependencies can include sources, selectors, profiles, rules, compiler versions, authority epochs, queries and requirements. Omitting a relevant dependency invalidates any claim that the manifest is complete for that use.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R008.</strong> A result based on absence or exhaustive enumeration MUST bind the universe or index snapshot and completeness procedure.</p>\n<p>Adding a new matching item can invalidate a negative result even if none of its previously read positive inputs changed.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R009.</strong> The manifest MUST distinguish a navigational citation from an operational dependency.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R010.</strong> It SHOULD explain why each operational dependency is material, so a reviewer can identify both missing dependencies and unjustified over-invalidation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-templates-and-instances\">5. Templates and instances</h2>\n<p>A template defines allowed substitutions, protected structure and the conditions under which an instance may be produced. Every instance receives its own identity, bound parameters, requirements and admission decision. The template’s age or prior acceptance does not automatically transfer to an instance.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R011.</strong> Parameter substitution MUST NOT remove scope, change a protected quantifier or carry authority from one recipient to another without an explicit rule.</p>\n<p>Unvalidated parameters yield an unresolved or rejected instance. If instantiation changes the meaning outside the declared template domain, the result is a new derivation rather than a valid instance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-evidence-and-publication-boundary\">6. Evidence and publication boundary</h2>\n<p><strong>CKA-R012.</strong> Evidence MUST bind to the exact artifact and check.</p>\n<p>Structural validity, reconstruction equality, task sufficiency, current applicability and execution success remain separate observations. An artifact can be immutable and byte-correct while ineligible for a new request.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R013.</strong> Publishing a mutable current pointer MUST follow the complete atomic predicate in <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/#5-guarded-publication-and-serving\">cache identity §5</a>, including dependency and authority epochs in the same decision as the head update.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R014.</strong> A build that finished successfully under an old generation MUST NOT overwrite a newer or revoked head.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R015.</strong> The final admission decision MUST be valid at the actual serve boundary, not only when the build began.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases-and-limits\">7. Acceptance cases and limits</h2>\n<p><strong>CKA-R016.</strong> A conformance suite MUST cover an altered input with an unchanged lookup key, a recipe change, an unsuccessful build, a template parameter outside its domain, a stale publication generation, a negative result invalidated by corpus growth, and evidence for the wrong output.</p>\n<p><strong>CKA-R017.</strong> The expected result MUST be an explicit new build, refusal, unresolved state or valid guarded reuse under a named policy.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse-admission study</a> separates analytical policies, bounded checks and measured outcomes. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection study</a> reports finite transformations and revision behavior. Neither establishes a deployed distributed cache or a general semantic equivalence solver.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-compatibility-and-migration\">8. Compatibility and migration</h2>\n<p><strong>CKA-R018.</strong> A migration MUST retain the old artifact and hash domain, identify the mapping and its losses, and create a new representation record.</p>\n<p>A new document edition does not change an existing binary or wire protocol. Earlier bounded profiles are named in the <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/#4-protocol-compatibility\">compatibility appendix</a>; implementations claiming additional behavior require corresponding evidence.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-result-kinds-and-minimum-information","title":"2. Result kinds and minimum information"},{"level":2,"id":"3-artifact-and-build-records","title":"3. Artifact and build records"},{"level":2,"id":"4-dependency-manifest","title":"4. Dependency manifest"},{"level":2,"id":"5-templates-and-instances","title":"5. Templates and instances"},{"level":2,"id":"6-evidence-and-publication-boundary","title":"6. Evidence and publication boundary"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases-and-limits","title":"7. Acceptance cases and limits"},{"level":2,"id":"8-compatibility-and-migration","title":"8. Compatibility and migration"}],"title_id":"compiled-knowledge-artifacts-and-reproducible-derivation","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"2cc5e781565136b5ad9fdbb2facae47d58564b1e300173d0a91a2c6cf16d5a82","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"artifact-registry","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/","title":"Artifact Registry, Readable Names, and Document Formats","titleRu":"Artifact Registry, Readable Names, and Document Formats","summary":"Readable artifact names, canonical Markdown formats, identity rules and professional artifact distinctions.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p>The registry gives humans and agents a predictable way to find, interpret and revise artifacts. It separates the identity of an object from its title, revision, file representation and current assessment. A new technical term does not automatically require a new database table or service.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. The formats below are project conventions except where an external format is explicitly named. They do not claim to be universal standards for competencies, roles or blueprints.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-names-identity-and-revisions\">2. Names, identity and revisions</h2>\n<p><strong>REG-R001.</strong> Every current artifact MUST live in a uniquely named directory within its type collection.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R002.</strong> Directory names use meaningful lowercase words separated by single hyphens; a reader MUST be able to infer the topic without looking up an internal number.</p>\n<p>Names such as <code>r1</code>, <code>r10</code> or <code>p09</code> are not valid titles or current canonical slugs.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R003.</strong> Historical identifiers MAY remain in provenance and migration maps.</p>\n<p>The main file has a predictable name determined by type. A rename preserves provenance and provides a compatibility map when an old path was published. An immutable revision identifies a particular content state. A mutable “current” pointer is a selection governed by a separate decision, not a new identity for every request.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-publication-formats\">3. Publication formats</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Collection</th>\n<th>Main file</th>\n<th>Required content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>papers/&lt;topic&gt;/</code></td>\n<td><code>PAPER.md</code></td>\n<td>Abstract, question and contribution, definitions, argument or results, counterexamples, limitations, references to engineering evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>engineering/specifications/&lt;topic&gt;/</code></td>\n<td><code>SPECIFICATION.md</code></td>\n<td>Purpose, conformance, model, requirements, failure handling, acceptance cases, compatibility, evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>engineering/studies/&lt;topic&gt;/</code></td>\n<td><code>STUDY.md</code></td>\n<td>Question, materials and rights, method, actual results, adverse cases, reproducibility, limitations, links to claims</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>reference/glossary/</code></td>\n<td><code>GLOSSARY.md</code></td>\n<td>Preferred terms, definitions, distinctions, aliases and language mapping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>reference/bibliography/</code></td>\n<td><code>BIBLIOGRAPHY.md</code></td>\n<td>Identified works, primary URLs or DOIs, and the role of each source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>appendices/&lt;topic&gt;/</code></td>\n<td><code>APPENDIX.md</code></td>\n<td>A named supporting subject, its scope, references and relationship to the main documents</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>REG-R004.</strong> Current publications MUST appear in <code>publication-catalog.json</code>.</p>\n<p>The catalogue records type, slug, path, title, summary, language and edition. It is a reading manifest, not an authorization to index or execute the content.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R005.</strong> Each main Markdown file MUST have YAML front matter with <code>name</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>artifact</code>, <code>edition</code>, <code>author</code>, <code>language</code>, <code>license</code>, and a nonempty <code>description</code>.</p>\n<p>Sources are registered through the repository source register.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R006.</strong> It MUST contain one descriptive H1.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R007.</strong> Version and machine identifiers belong in metadata, compatibility notes or provenance; they MUST NOT dominate the reading title.</p>\n<p>Relative links connect the current edition. Historical claims link to pinned revisions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-constraints-and-professional-artifacts\">4. Constraints and professional artifacts</h2>\n<p>The professional profile extends the same folder convention. Collections are created when actual artifacts exist; empty speculative directories are unnecessary.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Artifact</th>\n<th>Main file</th>\n<th>Meaning and required sections</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Role</td>\n<td><code>ROLE.md</code></td>\n<td>Responsibility; scope; permitted decisions; required skills or competencies; interfaces; accountability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Skill</td>\n<td><code>SKILL.md</code></td>\n<td>A reusable procedure; when to use it; inputs; steps; checks; constraints; outputs; supporting resources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Competency</td>\n<td><code>COMPETENCY.md</code></td>\n<td>An ability requirement or assessed claim; subject; performance criteria; assessed conditions; evidence; validity limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Blueprint</td>\n<td><code>BLUEPRINT.md</code></td>\n<td>A solution structure or process; intended result; components or steps; dependencies; choices; constraints; acceptance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Constraint</td>\n<td><code>CONSTRAINT.md</code></td>\n<td>Subject; scope; normative rule or condition; origin/authority; enforcement/check; exceptions; failure behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Work Item</td>\n<td><code>WORK_ITEM.md</code></td>\n<td>Goal; requirements revision; inputs; assignments; constraints; plan; result criteria; attempts and outcomes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A competency definition says what ability would count. A competency assessment binds evidence to a particular executor and conditions. Neither a role nor a skill file certifies that ability. A blueprint organizes work; it does not supply authorization. Constraints are linked directly from all artifacts to which they apply, not buried in a general note or mistaken for optional advice.</p>\n<p>For skills, the external <a href=\"https://agentskills.io/specification\">Agent Skills specification</a> governs the <code>SKILL.md</code> filename and required <code>name</code> and <code>description</code> front matter. This project uses its supported <code>metadata</code> map for extra project fields rather than inventing incompatible required top-level keys. The skill name matches its directory. Supporting code, references and assets remain inside that skill’s directory. The document-body headings above are a project convention, not mandatory headings imposed by Agent Skills.</p>\n<p>Repository instructions use <a href=\"https://agents.md/\">AGENTS.md</a>, a plain Markdown instruction file. It is not a universal serialization format for a role or an agent identity.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R008.</strong> Provider-specific executable agent configurations MUST have an explicit adapter and remain distinct from the human-readable role definition.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-registry-obligations\">5. Registry obligations</h2>\n<p><strong>REG-R009.</strong> Each registered revision MUST identify its kind, representation profile, identity, revision, license or disclosure restriction, provenance, required dependencies and applicable observations.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R010.</strong> A reader MUST distinguish known, unknown, not applicable and withheld information.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R011.</strong> Required references MUST resolve to the intended revision or report an unresolved dependency.</p>\n<p>Historical registration of 34 artifact types describes one example registry; it does not establish 34 exhaustive kinds of knowledge. Its common envelope is a carrier for those examples, not a restriction on all future contracts. The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/\">source map</a> preserves that compatibility context outside the scientific argument.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-acceptance-and-migration\">6. Acceptance and migration</h2>\n<p><strong>REG-R012.</strong> Registry checks MUST reject duplicate canonical names, missing required main files, incompatible front matter, broken current links and silent identity reuse.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R013.</strong> A rename test MUST show where the previous path leads and how a cited immutable revision remains recoverable.</p>\n<p>Unknown required extensions block use; preserving unknown optional data does not establish that it was interpreted.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R014.</strong> Migration MUST preserve source identity and original revisions, record the mapping, and disclose fields that could not be translated.</p>\n<p><strong>REG-R015.</strong> It MUST NOT infer a capability, grant or successful execution from the presence of an artifact.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision paper</a> explains these distinctions; the <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial study</a> records an actual bounded use of skills and a blueprint without claiming superiority from their existence.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-names-identity-and-revisions","title":"2. Names, identity and revisions"},{"level":2,"id":"3-publication-formats","title":"3. Publication formats"},{"level":2,"id":"4-constraints-and-professional-artifacts","title":"4. Constraints and professional artifacts"},{"level":2,"id":"5-registry-obligations","title":"5. Registry obligations"},{"level":2,"id":"6-acceptance-and-migration","title":"6. Acceptance and migration"}],"title_id":"artifact-registry-readable-names-and-document-formats","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"ec6584f97c5160fd3c8b17a5036aaeb8d302bf86dd61e567f2c5d858e9159470","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"status-model","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/status-model/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/","title":"Assessment, Execution, and Publication States","titleRu":"Assessment, Execution, and Publication States","summary":"Independent assessment, execution, effect, freshness, publication and review states.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p>A status is meaningful only for a named subject, property, revision and scope. One “verified” flag cannot faithfully represent schema validity, source fidelity, truth, coverage, authorization, execution outcome and publication review. This specification separates those questions and defines their transition obligations.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R001.</strong> A profile MUST select the dimensions needed for its purpose and declare any aggregation rule.</p>\n<p>It need not attach every possible dimension to every record. The <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence paper</a> explains why stronger conclusions need different evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-observation-record\">2. Observation record</h2>\n<p><strong>STA-R002.</strong> Each observation MUST identify its subject and immutable revision, assessed property, requirements or criterion revision, method, observer, relevant time, result, grounds and limits.</p>\n<p>A result without a subject is not transferable evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R003.</strong> If the method trusts supplied input rather than independently authenticating it, that assumption MUST be stated.</p>\n<p>The check result vocabulary is <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>unknown</code>, and <code>not_applicable</code>. The last value requires a demonstrated inapplicability rule, not missing data. For a conjunctive set of mandatory checks, any applicable failure prevents acceptance; any remaining applicable unknown prevents an all-pass conclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R004.</strong> A positive result MUST NOT overwrite a negative or unresolved result on another mandatory property.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-canonical-dimension-vocabularies\">3. Canonical dimension vocabularies</h2>\n<p><strong>STA-R005.</strong> A profile using the current tokens MUST use the snake_case values below without overloading one dimension with another.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R006.</strong> An older profile MAY retain its original lexical values only through an explicit, loss-reporting adapter.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Tokens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td><code>known</code>, <code>unknown</code>, <code>not_applicable</code>, <code>withheld</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Coverage</td>\n<td><code>supported</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>unsupported</code>, <code>conflicted</code>, <code>unknown</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Search assurance</td>\n<td><code>candidate_only</code>, <code>exhaustive_in_scope</code>, <code>incomplete_procedure</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Local run phase</td>\n<td><code>planned</code>, <code>ready</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>paused</code>, <code>finished</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Operation outcome</td>\n<td><code>success</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>refused</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>unresolved</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Stop reason</td>\n<td><code>completed</code>, <code>clarification_needed</code>, <code>budget_insufficient</code>, <code>retrieval_limit</code>, <code>stale_evidence</code>, <code>precondition_not_met</code>, <code>cancelled</code>, <code>execution_error</code>, <code>reconciliation_pending</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>External effect</td>\n<td><code>none_established</code>, <code>known_effect</code>, <code>unknown_effect</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Artifact lifecycle</td>\n<td><code>building</code>, <code>ready</code>, <code>quarantined</code>, <code>retired</code>, <code>failed</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Residency</td>\n<td><code>resident</code>, <code>evicted</code>, <code>absent</code>, <code>unknown</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Freshness</td>\n<td><code>fresh</code>, <code>stale</code>, <code>expired</code>, <code>unknown</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Reuse eligibility</td>\n<td><code>eligible</code>, <code>ineligible</code>, <code>unknown</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Evolution impact</td>\n<td><code>compatible</code>, <code>restricted</code>, <code>requires_review</code>, <code>blocked</code>, <code>unknown_impact</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Reuse decision</td>\n<td><code>exact_reuse</code>, <code>revalidate</code>, <code>rebuild</code>, <code>instantiate</code>, <code>refuse</code>, <code>unresolved</code>, <code>serve_stale</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Case evidence</td>\n<td><code>planned</code>, <code>illustrated</code>, <code>executed</code>, <code>evaluated</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Review kind</td>\n<td><code>editorial</code>, <code>internal_technical</code>, <code>independent_agent</code>, <code>external_peer</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>unsupported</code> always refers to the declared evaluated scope. <code>ready</code> describes run or artifact availability and is not a successful operation outcome. <code>none_established</code> reports no established effect within the observed scope; it does not assert that no remote effect exists. Review kind describes the actual protocol, not the name of the reviewer. An independently tasked agent review is not external human peer review.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R007.</strong> Legacy labels MUST map explicitly: <code>terminated</code> → run phase <code>finished</code>; <code>unsupported in scope</code> → coverage <code>unsupported</code>; “incomplete execution of planned search” → <code>incomplete_procedure</code>; <code>not_measured</code> → assessment <code>unknown</code> with reason “no observation.” Profile-specific quality states such as book-package <code>reviewed</code> retain their own declared scope; they are not aliases for <code>pass</code>.</p>\n<p>Evolution impact is an input to admission, not a shortcut to its conclusion:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Impact</th>\n<th>Eligibility and permitted next decision</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>compatible</code></td>\n<td>Still <code>unknown</code> until every other mandatory gate passes; then eligible for the policy’s permitted reuse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>restricted</code></td>\n<td>Original broader request is ineligible; a separately identified narrowed request is assessed anew</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>requires_review</code> / <code>unknown_impact</code></td>\n<td>Unknown eligibility; <code>revalidate</code> or <code>unresolved</code>, with no admitted serve yet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>blocked</code></td>\n<td>Ineligible for that use; <code>refuse</code>, or a distinct permissible rebuild/instance evaluated from its own grounds</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"4-transition-rules\">4. Transition rules</h2>\n<p>Transitions apply to the same identified attempt, lifecycle instance, policy and use scope.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R008.</strong> Implementations MUST follow the tables; transitions not listed are rejected.</p>\n<p>Creating a new subject or attempt starts a new history rather than changing the meaning of an old record.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R009.</strong> Every transition MUST record the triggering observation, actor, prior state and guard result.</p>\n<h3 id=\"execution-phase\">Execution phase</h3>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>From</th>\n<th>Event and guard</th>\n<th>To</th>\n<th>Terminal?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>no attempt</td>\n<td>Allocate a unique attempt with pinned Work Item and requirements</td>\n<td><code>planned</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>planned</code></td>\n<td>Establish inputs, capability and preconditions for dispatch</td>\n<td><code>ready</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>ready</code></td>\n<td>Recheck current authority and preconditions at actual dispatch</td>\n<td><code>running</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>running</code></td>\n<td>Record a resumable checkpoint under a declared pause policy</td>\n<td><code>paused</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>paused</code></td>\n<td>Validate checkpoint, current bindings and renewed dispatch guards</td>\n<td><code>running</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>planned</code> / <code>ready</code></td>\n<td>Cancel, refuse or fail before dispatch; record reason and actual effect observations</td>\n<td><code>finished</code></td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>running</code> / <code>paused</code></td>\n<td>Terminate with explicit outcome, reason and effect observations</td>\n<td><code>finished</code></td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>STA-R010.</strong> A finished attempt MUST NOT return to running; retry uses a new attempt with a repeat policy.</p>\n<p>Later reconciliation adds effect observations to the same historical attempt without pretending another execution occurred. A successful process exit does not supply missing acceptance evidence.</p>\n<h3 id=\"artifact-lifecycle\">Artifact lifecycle</h3>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>From</th>\n<th>Event and guard</th>\n<th>To</th>\n<th>Terminal?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>no lifecycle</td>\n<td>Allocate a build record</td>\n<td><code>building</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>building</code></td>\n<td>Successful build, required checks and the atomic publication guard all pass</td>\n<td><code>ready</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>building</code></td>\n<td>Build fails; retain cause and any explicitly partial output</td>\n<td><code>failed</code></td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>building</code> / <code>ready</code></td>\n<td>Authorized integrity, safety or provenance investigation suppresses use</td>\n<td><code>quarantined</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>quarantined</code></td>\n<td>Authorized release after cause resolution, complete reassessment and current publication guard</td>\n<td><code>ready</code></td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>building</code> / <code>ready</code> / <code>quarantined</code></td>\n<td>Authorized withdrawal or cancellation retires this lifecycle</td>\n<td><code>retired</code></td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>quarantined</code> means use is suspended while a named issue is investigated; only the policy’s authorized actor can release it. <code>retired</code> means this lifecycle is withdrawn and cannot become ready again. <code>failed</code> is also terminal for this build. A replacement or reinstatement creates a new lifecycle/adoption record and retains prior revocations and restrictions. Identical bytes do not erase those decisions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"freshness-and-eligibility-projections\">Freshness and eligibility projections</h3>\n<p>These are projections of immutable observations, not edits to old verdicts.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R011.</strong> New projection records MAY replace the current display only while preserving the observations on which earlier displays were based.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Dimension: from</th>\n<th>Trigger and required evidence</th>\n<th>To</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Freshness: any</td>\n<td>A new applicable check, valid clock bounds and current policy establish a new window</td>\n<td><code>fresh</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Freshness: <code>fresh</code></td>\n<td>Soft deadline reached while no hard deadline has expired</td>\n<td><code>stale</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Freshness: <code>fresh</code> / <code>stale</code></td>\n<td>A hard deadline or permitted age bound is reached</td>\n<td><code>expired</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Freshness: any</td>\n<td>Mandatory time, policy or chronology cannot be established</td>\n<td><code>unknown</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Eligibility: any</td>\n<td>Every current mandatory gate for this exact request is discharged</td>\n<td><code>eligible</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Eligibility: any</td>\n<td>A known mandatory restriction fails</td>\n<td><code>ineligible</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Eligibility: any</td>\n<td>A material dependency, authority or assessment becomes unknown</td>\n<td><code>unknown</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>STA-R012.</strong> An expired or ineligible observation MUST NOT be overwritten merely because another record has a later timestamp.</p>\n<p>Eligibility can become positive only after every known blocking cause is resolved under the current policy. Rechecking a digest alone cannot renew source-event age. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/\">reuse policy</a> defines deadline and decision semantics.</p>\n<p>Residency changes only on storage observations: storing and reading back the named bytes establishes <code>resident</code>; an eviction receipt establishes <code>evicted</code>; a scoped absence check establishes <code>absent</code>; lack of trustworthy storage observation gives <code>unknown</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R013.</strong> These transitions MUST NOT change truth, review, freshness or eligibility by themselves.</p>\n<p>Assessment, coverage, assurance, external effect, review and case-evidence records are append-only observations. They have no automatic success progression: an illustration never becomes an executed trial without a new execution record, and an unknown effect is resolved only by evidence from the relevant effect boundary. Conflicting observations remain addressable. The monotone properties are observation history, terminal attempt/lifecycle histories and non-reused <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/#5-guarded-publication-and-serving\">publication generations</a>, not steadily improving truth or eligibility.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-scientific-and-editorial-status\">5. Scientific and editorial status</h2>\n<p>A completed publication can state conditional propositions and report bounded results while acknowledging unanswered empirical questions. Its edition number identifies a coherent text, not proof of all future applications.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R014.</strong> The vocabulary MUST NOT equate “published,” “reviewed,” “implemented,” and “effective.”</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R015.</strong> An internal agent review MUST identify the reviewer, input revision, method and conclusions, and MUST be labelled as such.</p>\n<p>It does not constitute independent external peer review. A finite test report requires actual outputs; an illustrated scenario is not an executed test.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R016.</strong> An executed failed trial remains useful evidence and MUST retain its negative result.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-user-facing-projections\">6. User-facing projections</h2>\n<p><strong>STA-R017.</strong> Interfaces MAY summarize these dimensions, but the summary MUST preserve the distinctions relevant to the user’s decision.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R018.</strong> “Indexed revision X” is more precise than an unqualified “current.” “Checks passed for the supplied record” MUST NOT become “the action is authorized” when current authorization was outside the checked boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R019.</strong> An unknown effect after a timeout MUST remain visible when a user decides whether to retry.</p>\n<p>Technical detail can be expanded on demand, but critical uncertainty cannot be hidden behind a positive badge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-and-evidence\">7. Acceptance and evidence</h2>\n<p><strong>STA-R020.</strong> Tests MUST cover a passed schema with false supplied grounds, successful execution with a failed result criterion, a finished run with unknown effect, a resident but revoked artifact, a correct historical result ineligible for a current task, and an internal review that does not establish empirical validity.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">conformance study</a> demonstrates why a trusted-input premise matters. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial pilot</a> reports an equal observed score for the structured and baseline conditions. Neither result permits a general effectiveness label. See <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision and governance</a> for the distinction between an observation and adoption of a rule.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-bounded-exchange-profile\">8. Bounded exchange profile</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/schemas/contract-observation.schema.json\">JSON Schema</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/tools/check_contract_profile.py\">validator</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/schemas/fixtures/contract-observation/valid\">examples</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/tools/test_contract_profile.py\">tests</a> define a concrete exchange subset: <code>kc-observation/1</code>, <code>kc-dependency-change/1</code> and <code>kc-profile-advertisement/1</code>. This is an original project profile using <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259\">JSON</a> and <a href=\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/json-schema-core\">JSON Schema 2020-12</a>, not a claim that a full Knowledge Contract, executor or API is interoperable.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R021.</strong> An exchange record MUST be a single strict UTF-8 JSON object without a BOM, duplicate member names at any depth, non-finite number tokens or isolated Unicode surrogates.</p>\n<p>Unknown fields, profiles and record versions are rejected.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R022.</strong> Integers used as epochs or sequence numbers MUST be in 0 through 9,007,199,254,740,991.</p>\n<p>Timestamp strings use UTC seconds <code>YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ</code> with a valid calendar date; other clock models require another profile.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Record</th>\n<th>Required fields and meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>KCObservation/v1</code></td>\n<td><code>observation_id</code>, <code>subject</code>, assessed <code>property</code>, pinned <code>criterion</code>, method <code>id</code>/<code>version</code>, <code>observer</code>, <code>observed_at</code>, check <code>result</code>, textual <code>scope</code>, <code>evidence_refs</code>, <code>trust_assumptions</code>, <code>limitations</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Subject/criterion/evidence reference</td>\n<td><code>artifact_id</code>, immutable <code>revision</code>, <code>hash_domain</code>, exact <code>sha256</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>not_applicable</code> observation</td>\n<td>Additionally, a pinned <code>inapplicability_rule</code>; presence is structural evidence only, not proof that the rule applies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>KCDependencyChange/v1</code></td>\n<td><code>event_id</code>, ordering <code>namespace</code>, <code>sequence</code>, <code>dependency_id</code>, old/new epochs and revisions, event <code>kind</code>, impact, observer and time; see the cache specification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>KCProfileAdvertisement/v1</code></td>\n<td><code>reader_id</code>, explicit <code>read_profiles</code>, <code>write_profiles</code>, supported <code>hash_domains</code>; lists are capability declarations, not authorization grants</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>STA-R023.</strong> Implementations MUST compare schema/profile support before exchanging records.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R024.</strong> They MUST NOT infer support for an entire contract family from support for this subset.</p>\n<p>Scope strings are exact descriptive bindings; automated semantic equivalence of two scope strings is outside this profile.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Registered hash domain</th>\n<th>Exact input and equality</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>sha256/raw-bytes-v1</code></td>\n<td>SHA-256 of the exact referenced file bytes; no decoding or rewriting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>sha256/raw-utf8-json-v1</code></td>\n<td>SHA-256 of the exact valid exchange JSON bytes, including whitespace; no canonicalization or Unicode normalization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>STA-R025.</strong> Identity comparisons MUST include both domain and digest.</p>\n<p>Reserializing an equal JSON value can change byte identity and therefore creates a new representation.</p>\n<p><strong>STA-R026.</strong> This profile deliberately does not define value-level canonical JSON hashing; a profile that needs it MUST name a separate procedure and domain.</p>\n<p>Structural validation does not authenticate an observer, prove an observation or admit an action.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-observation-record","title":"2. Observation record"},{"level":2,"id":"3-canonical-dimension-vocabularies","title":"3. Canonical dimension vocabularies"},{"level":2,"id":"4-transition-rules","title":"4. Transition rules"},{"level":3,"id":"execution-phase","title":"Execution phase"},{"level":3,"id":"artifact-lifecycle","title":"Artifact lifecycle"},{"level":3,"id":"freshness-and-eligibility-projections","title":"Freshness and eligibility projections"},{"level":2,"id":"5-scientific-and-editorial-status","title":"5. Scientific and editorial status"},{"level":2,"id":"6-user-facing-projections","title":"6. User-facing projections"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-and-evidence","title":"7. Acceptance and evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"8-bounded-exchange-profile","title":"8. Bounded exchange profile"}],"title_id":"assessment-execution-and-publication-states","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/status-model/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/status-model/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"2f66342472fc4198eba573d7d1eda5d58d5bae9de904f901f5094037dd628d0a","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"cache-identity-and-invalidation","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/","title":"Cache Identity, Dependency Tracking, and Invalidation","titleRu":"Cache Identity, Dependency Tracking, and Invalidation","summary":"Content, build and lookup identity, dependency changes and guarded publication and serving.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p>This specification governs how a saved result is identified, found as a candidate, invalidated after change and admitted at a serve boundary. Equal bytes, equal recipes, semantic suitability and current authorization are separate relations.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. The architecture is supported by conditional arguments and bounded models, not by a claim of a deployed distributed coherence protocol.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R001.</strong> Every implementation MUST state its consistency assumptions and failure behavior.</p>\n<p>See <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled reuse</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-identity-layers\">2. Identity layers</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Identity</th>\n<th>What it fixes</th>\n<th>What it does not establish</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Content identity</td>\n<td>Exact bytes or canonical structured value in a named hash domain</td>\n<td>Meaning, authorship, truth or permission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Build identity</td>\n<td>Declared inputs, recipe, parameters and relevant environment</td>\n<td>Actual execution or deterministic output</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Lookup identity</td>\n<td>A candidate class for a query, recipient and policy</td>\n<td>Eligibility of every retrieved candidate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Current head</td>\n<td>The selected revision within a namespace and generation</td>\n<td>Timeless suitability for all consumers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>CCH-R002.</strong> A lookup MUST be followed by request/artifact descriptor comparison and the required admission checks.</p>\n<p>Negation, quantifiers, recipient, purpose and scope are not dispensable variations of a similar query.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R003.</strong> A digest collision assumption and the selected byte or canonical-value domain MUST be stated when digest equality is used as identity evidence.</p>\n<p>The implemented byte domains are registered in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">status exchange profile</a>; an unnamed canonicalization is unsupported.</p>\n<p>A request descriptor fixes the fields below. The artifact descriptor records the corresponding values for which the saved artifact was prepared and assessed.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Descriptor component</th>\n<th>Comparison rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Purpose and recipient identifiers</td>\n<td>Exact identities; aliases need an independently established identity mapping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Requirements/criterion references</td>\n<td>Exact immutable revisions and digests, or a separately checked entailment of every new mandatory requirement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Representation and required profile references</td>\n<td>Exact profile/version support or an explicit checked adapter with loss account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Subject, scope, quantifier and protected operators</td>\n<td>Exact structure under the named representation profile, or an explicit scoped preservation argument; similarity is insufficient</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Constraint and authority references</td>\n<td>Complete current mandatory set, with no ignored constraint-bearing profile; current grants are rechecked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Required output/delivery form and budget</td>\n<td>Actual delivered form satisfies the requested profile and actual resource bounds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Temporal mode and dependency snapshot</td>\n<td>Requested historical/current/bounded-stale policy plus complete current read-set checks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>CCH-R004.</strong> The descriptor comparison MUST return an itemized <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>unknown</code> or justified <code>not_applicable</code> for the relevant components.</p>\n<p>Missing fields, unknown profile semantics or an unproved implication remain unknown.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R005.</strong> The algorithm MUST NOT equate two requests merely because their lookup key, text embedding or source bytes agree.</p>\n<p>Descriptor equality is a preliminary gate, not a grant of current authority. This architecture does not serialize full descriptors; a complete descriptor wire mapping remains a profile-specific obligation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-dependency-tracking\">3. Dependency tracking</h2>\n<p><strong>CCH-R006.</strong> The read set MUST contain every declared dependency material to the claimed reuse guarantee, including source revisions, required selectors, profiles, rules, request requirements, compiler behavior and authorization state.</p>\n<p>Dependency kind determines the impact rule. Relocation of identical bytes can preserve content identity while changing accessibility; a changed interpretation can invalidate applicability without changing a source file.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R007.</strong> Negative results and exhaustive claims MUST also track their universe or index generation and completeness assumptions.</p>\n<p>New content can invalidate “no match” or “all matches” without modifying any previously selected object. Top-k retrieval does not supply that completeness premise.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R008.</strong> Unknown impact MUST produce re-evaluation or a conservative unresolved result under the policy.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R009.</strong> It MUST NOT be inferred harmless simply because the old key still matches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-change-events-and-ordering\">4. Change events and ordering</h2>\n<p>A change event identifies the affected dependency, previous and new revisions or epochs, event identity, ordering namespace and impact class.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R010.</strong> Epochs are nonnegative integers and a relevant change MUST strictly advance the affected dependency epoch even when its content bytes are unchanged.</p>\n<p>An authority revocation changes admissibility without necessarily changing content.</p>\n<p>The implemented <code>KCDependencyChange/v1</code> record in the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/schemas/contract-observation.schema.json\">exchange schema</a> supplies <code>event_id</code>, <code>namespace</code>, <code>sequence</code>, <code>dependency_id</code>, <code>old_epoch</code>, <code>new_epoch</code>, old/new revision references, kind, impact, observer and time.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R011.</strong> <code>new_epoch</code> MUST exceed <code>old_epoch</code>; <code>revoked</code> events MUST carry <code>impact: blocked</code>.</p>\n<p>Null revision references denote absence of an addressable revision, not established content equality. Event kinds are <code>revision_changed</code>, <code>revoked</code>, <code>availability_changed</code> and <code>unknown_change</code>. Impact uses the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#3-canonical-dimension-vocabularies\">status model</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R012.</strong> A receiver MUST deduplicate an identical event identifier only when the complete event bytes agree, detect conflicting reuse of that identifier, and treat a missing or reordered sequence in its declared ordering namespace as a reconciliation requirement.</p>\n<p>The profile serializes these facts; it does not provide event transport or certify complete event delivery. No inference of harmlessness follows from a missed event.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R013.</strong> If event loss is possible, reconciliation MUST compare observed dependency state with authoritative state before renewing affected admission claims.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R014.</strong> A disconnected system MAY serve under an explicitly bounded policy, but MUST NOT claim immediate global revocation when its consistency protocol cannot establish it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-guarded-publication-and-serving\">5. Guarded publication and serving</h2>\n<p>A <strong>head namespace</strong> identifies one ordered stream of current-head decisions. Its <strong>generation</strong> is a nonnegative counter that strictly increases on every head change, including rollback, and every revocation applying to that head.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R015.</strong> Generation values MUST NOT be reused after rollback, restart, restore or counter exhaustion.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R016.</strong> A runtime unable to recover the counter safely MUST establish a fresh namespace and invalidate old read sets.</p>\n<p>The exchange subset bounds integers at 2^53−1; exhaustion fails closed rather than wrapping.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R017.</strong> A worker’s read set MUST contain the namespace and generation, every material dependency’s exact revision and epoch, and relevant authority/policy epochs.</p>\n<p>Completeness of that dependency set is an explicit premise of the guarantee, not a fact inferred from a cache key.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R018.</strong> Publication MUST make one linearizable decision over the following predicate: the current namespace and head generation equal the worker’s expected values; every declared material dependency still has the observed epoch/revision; current authority and policy permit this exact output/use; and all required build checks remain satisfied.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R019.</strong> The head update and increment of generation MUST occur atomically with that decision.</p>\n<p>A separate dependency check followed by a compare-and-set on the head alone is insufficient: revocation can occur between them.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R020.</strong> Every relevant revocation MUST advance its authority/dependency epoch and the affected head generation within the declared decision boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R021.</strong> A late worker MUST NOT restore an older head, undo a revocation or publish against an unchanged-looking dependency after an intervening change.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R022.</strong> A deployment lacking an atomic boundary over these conditions MUST fail closed or explicitly offer a weaker named consistency profile; it cannot claim this strict guarantee.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R023.</strong> The serve guard MUST validate the exact output, relevant current dependency epochs and current authority at the actual serve consistency point.</p>\n<p>Build-time validation does not authorize a later unguarded serve. Locks, transactions or a justified validation-and-commit protocol are mechanisms only when their scope covers the whole predicate. Remote effects outside that boundary retain their own reconciliation semantics.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R024.</strong> A lease MUST define scope, expiry, revocation behavior, clock assumptions and partition behavior.</p>\n<p>A strict revocation claim cannot treat an unobservable revocation as harmless unless its explicitly adopted authorization semantics permit the stated delay.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/tools/test_contract_profile.py\">reference model and regression tests</a> serialize the namespace, generation, dependency-epoch and revocation part of this predicate under one in-memory lock. They do not evaluate output-specific grants, semantic validity or build checks. They exhibit a late publisher, a changed dependency under a current head token, revocation between read and publish, concurrent publishers, exhaustion of either a head or dependency counter, and rollback without generation reuse. They are finite local checks, not evidence of distributed deployment or durable crash recovery.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-storage-recovery-and-observability\">6. Storage, recovery and observability</h2>\n<p>Residency and eligibility are independent. Eviction can remove a valid copy; retention can preserve an invalid one.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R025.</strong> Recovery MUST verify content identity and rebuild the current eligibility decision rather than restoring a historical positive verdict as current fact.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R026.</strong> Logs SHOULD record lookup candidate, compared descriptors, dependency observations, admission decision, publication generation and actual serve result.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R027.</strong> Logging MUST respect the recipient’s disclosure rights.</p>\n<p>Required audit retention is a separate policy from cache residence time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases\">7. Acceptance cases</h2>\n<p>Required tests include: identical query text with a changed recipient; changed source under a matching lookup key; a new object invalidating a negative result; a lost invalidation event found by reconciliation; a late build attempting to overwrite a newer head; expiry exactly at a hard deadline; and revocation between preliminary check and serve.</p>\n<p><strong>CCH-R028.</strong> Each test MUST identify the simulated or real consistency boundary.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse study</a> reports the available evidence and its deployment limits. <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#http-caching\">RFC 9111</a> is a reference for HTTP cache semantics, not proof that the semantic or authority checks defined here have been implemented. Existing bounded profiles and retained results are identified in the <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/#4-protocol-compatibility\">compatibility appendix</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-identity-layers","title":"2. Identity layers"},{"level":2,"id":"3-dependency-tracking","title":"3. Dependency tracking"},{"level":2,"id":"4-change-events-and-ordering","title":"4. Change events and ordering"},{"level":2,"id":"5-guarded-publication-and-serving","title":"5. Guarded publication and serving"},{"level":2,"id":"6-storage-recovery-and-observability","title":"6. Storage, recovery and observability"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases","title":"7. Acceptance cases"}],"title_id":"cache-identity-dependency-tracking-and-invalidation","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"742982eabd0d797ccc5bba4c8472d25a4236c84baed21281f13a7601a4ce2f39","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"reuse-policy","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/","title":"Reuse Policy: Time, Authorization, and Decision Outcomes","titleRu":"Reuse Policy: Time, Authorization, and Decision Outcomes","summary":"Temporal modes, conservative deadlines, authorization, decision outcomes and retention boundaries.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R014.</strong> A reuse policy determines whether a saved result may satisfy a new request, what MUST be rechecked, and which time and authority constraints apply.</p>\n<p>It operates on a specific artifact, recipient, purpose, requirements revision and observation time.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. A cache hit is only a candidate. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/\">compiled-artifact specification</a> defines the saved result; <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/\">identity and invalidation</a> defines its dependency and serve guards.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-admission-before-timing\">2. Admission before timing</h2>\n<p><strong>RUS-R001.</strong> Before deciding freshness, the policy MUST establish semantic compatibility with the request, required dependency availability, applicable constraints, current disclosure authorization and any action-specific grant.</p>\n<p>A fresh but inapplicable result is ineligible. A byte-identical object does not inherit the authority of another recipient.</p>\n<p>A sufficient existing authorization is used within its scope; this specification does not require asking a user for the same permission at every pipeline step. It does require checking that the authorization still covers the actual action, subject and time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-temporal-modes\">3. Temporal modes</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>Commitment</th>\n<th>Continuing obligations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Historical pinned</td>\n<td>Describe the identified historical revision as historical</td>\n<td>Current disclosure authorization and applicable hard restrictions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Current</td>\n<td>Establish the relevant current source and policy state at the serve boundary</td>\n<td>Dependency, scope and authority checks under the declared consistency model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Bounded staleness</td>\n<td>Permit a named maximum age under an explicit policy</td>\n<td>Hard restrictions, provenance, uncertainty and any mandatory revalidation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>RUS-R002.</strong> Service availability MUST NOT silently choose a weaker temporal mode.</p>\n<p>Historical correctness does not authorize disclosure of a withdrawn or restricted record. A background refresh does not by itself make the old content eligible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-time-model-and-deadlines\">4. Time model and deadlines</h2>\n<p><strong>RUS-R003.</strong> The policy MUST distinguish source-event time, source-observation time, artifact-build time, check time, authorization interval and storage time.</p>\n<p>Updating a digest check does not make an old fact new. The relevant clock and uncertainty bounds belong to the observation.</p>\n<p>For the bounded interval model, a check lies within <code>[checked.lower, checked.upper]</code> and the request time within <code>[now.lower, now.upper]</code>. The check is the policy-relevant observation, not a storage touch or unrelated digest check.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R004.</strong> Times MUST use one declared clock and unit.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R005.</strong> Bounds and durations MUST be finite; intervals require lower ≤ upper, and TTL and permitted maximum age require nonnegative values.</p>\n<p>A TTL yields a <strong>soft</strong> deadline <code>checked.lower + ttl</code> unless the policy explicitly classifies that TTL as hard.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R006.</strong> A policy MUST identify each deadline’s class and basis.</p>\n<p>The effective hard deadline is the minimum of every applicable finite hard deadline, including hard TTL, grant expiry and a mandatory maximum-age bound. Reuse requires <code>now.upper &lt; effective_hard_deadline</code>; equality closes the window. An explicit absence of all hard limits is not an unknown limit and does not waive other gates.</p>\n<p>A soft deadline is reached at <code>now.upper &gt;= soft_deadline</code>. Reaching it changes freshness to stale and requires <code>revalidate</code> before serving unless the request explicitly permits bounded stale serving. Under bounded staleness the conservative age is <code>now.upper - checked.lower</code>, anchored to that same policy-relevant check. <code>serve_stale</code> requires age strictly below the declared maximum age, no hard expiry or revocation, all other gates discharged, and a recorded refresh duty.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R007.</strong> A failed refresh MUST NOT reset either deadline.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R008.</strong> Invalid intervals (lower &gt; upper), a definitely future check (<code>checked.lower &gt; now.upper</code>), negative durations, incompatible clocks or unknown mandatory bounds MUST yield <code>unresolved</code> without an admitted serve.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R009.</strong> Where order itself is mandatory, overlapping intervals that cannot establish <code>checked.upper &lt;= now.lower</code> also remain unresolved; an implementation MUST NOT invent a chronology from point estimates.</p>\n<p>These interval rules do not claim distributed causal ordering.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-decision-outcomes\">5. Decision outcomes</h2>\n<p>The following tokens are <strong>reuse decisions</strong>, distinct from operation outcomes in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#3-canonical-dimension-vocabularies\">status model</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Decision</th>\n<th>Required behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>exact_reuse</code></td>\n<td>Serve only after every required gate and final serve guard pass within the chosen temporal mode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>revalidate</code></td>\n<td>Obtain current observations; no admitted serve before they discharge the gates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>rebuild</code></td>\n<td>Produce a new artifact under the current inputs/rules and assess it separately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>instantiate</code></td>\n<td>Produce and assess a new template instance with bound parameters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>refuse</code></td>\n<td>Report a known blocking condition at the permitted disclosure level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>unresolved</code></td>\n<td>Report missing, invalid or inconclusive mandatory observations; do not serve as admitted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>serve_stale</code></td>\n<td>Serve only under explicit bounded-staleness authorization, recording stale freshness, bound, observation basis and refresh duty</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>RUS-R010.</strong> A stale fallback MUST NOT conceal known semantic invalidity, a hard expiry or revocation.</p>\n<p><code>serve_stale</code> differs from <code>revalidate</code>: the former permits delivery under a stated weaker temporal promise, the latter does not. Choosing a rebuild or refresh plan does not establish successful completion of that plan.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-cost-retention-and-measurement\">6. Cost, retention and measurement</h2>\n<p>Cost can influence an eligible choice, but cannot excuse a mandatory violation.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R011.</strong> A utility estimate MUST state its task, baseline, horizon, units and included costs.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R012.</strong> Build, retrieval, validation, maintenance and integration costs MUST NOT be counted twice.</p>\n<p>Planned savings are estimates until measured.</p>\n<p>Retention is a storage policy, not an eligibility policy. Popularity can justify keeping bytes longer; it does not extend the validity of their grounds. Negative results require especially explicit corpus scope and invalidation conditions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-and-evidence\">7. Acceptance and evidence</h2>\n<p>Required cases include a current request with unavailable source-head evidence; a historical request after disclosure revocation; unknown clock bounds; equality at hard expiry; a soft TTL versus a hard TTL; invalid and overlapping clock bounds; a bounded-age limit measured from the relevant check; stale serving explicitly allowed and explicitly forbidden; refresh failure; a template instance with changed authority; and a low-cost option that violates a mandatory constraint.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">reuse-admission and cost study</a> distinguishes formal checks from measured operational savings. The <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">utility paper</a> states conditional cost results and the assumptions needed for causal comparison. <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#stale-http-responses\">RFC 5861</a> provides an HTTP analogy for stale handling; it is not an authorization policy for knowledge or actions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-compatibility\">8. Compatibility</h2>\n<p>No illustrative TTL is a universal default.</p>\n<p><strong>RUS-R013.</strong> A deployment MUST choose and justify its policy for the task and risk.</p>\n<p>Importing an older record preserves its original observations and adds unknown values where the new policy needs information not present in the source. A prior all-purpose validity flag cannot be losslessly decomposed into freshness, eligibility and authority without additional evidence.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-admission-before-timing","title":"2. Admission before timing"},{"level":2,"id":"3-temporal-modes","title":"3. Temporal modes"},{"level":2,"id":"4-time-model-and-deadlines","title":"4. Time model and deadlines"},{"level":2,"id":"5-decision-outcomes","title":"5. Decision outcomes"},{"level":2,"id":"6-cost-retention-and-measurement","title":"6. Cost, retention and measurement"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-and-evidence","title":"7. Acceptance and evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"8-compatibility","title":"8. Compatibility"}],"title_id":"reuse-policy-time-authorization-and-decision-outcomes","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"be75f0191414c07a113dafb0c983cd1d1e73834be0b670e0d610b4354f37ecd2","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"knowledge-evolution","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/","title":"Knowledge Evolution and Versioned Rules","titleRu":"Knowledge Evolution and Versioned Rules","summary":"Versioned creation rules, adoption, impact analysis, reassessment, migration and coherent publication.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-conformance\">1. Purpose and conformance</h2>\n<p>This is an architectural requirement set. Interoperability requires a named representation profile; the implemented exchange subset is limited to <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#8-bounded-exchange-profile\">observations, change events and profile advertisements</a>, and the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package profile</a> has its own schema. No full-contract wire format or deployed runtime is implied.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R001.</strong> Every new knowledge or decision artifact MUST record the exact contracts, profiles and rules that materially governed its creation.</p>\n<p>Reassessment under later rules is a new observation. The original derivation and revision remain traceable.</p>\n<p>Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. This specification governs changes to interpretation and admission rules as well as software versions. A named research direction does not by itself establish an implemented schema or executable validator. The <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision paper</a> supplies the underlying model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-creation-basis\">2. Creation basis</h2>\n<p><strong>EVO-R002.</strong> A creation-basis record MUST identify the artifact revision, material source revisions, contract and profile definitions, relevant rule-set versions, transformation recipe, responsible actor, creation time or interval, and known gaps.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R003.</strong> References MUST resolve to immutable revisions or record the inability to resolve them.</p>\n<p>Only actually used rules are included as creation inputs.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R004.</strong> A later adopted rule MUST NOT be inserted into the old basis as though it governed the original work.</p>\n<p>Broad labels such as “latest contract” are insufficient when the exact revision is material.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R005.</strong> The implementation MUST distinguish content change from representation change, assessment change and governance change.</p>\n<p>An updated assessment can change whether a use is admitted without modifying source bytes. A corrected interpretation produces a new semantic revision even if the original source is unchanged.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-change-proposal-and-adoption\">3. Change proposal and adoption</h2>\n<p><strong>EVO-R006.</strong> Each proposal MUST identify the previous and proposed rule revisions, motivation, affected scope, expected compatibility, migration implications, evidence, objections and known unresolved questions.</p>\n<p>Adoption requires the decision maker and authority specified by the governance policy. A contributor, editor or automated validator does not acquire that authority merely by producing a proposal.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R007.</strong> Conflicting adoption decisions MUST block the affected use until a decision maker with authenticated authority resolves the conflict or explicitly adopts a permissible narrower scope.</p>\n<p>The unresolved conflict is <code>unknown_impact</code> with unknown eligibility and an <code>unresolved</code> operation outcome; a separate known prohibition yields <code>blocked</code> and refusal. The expected test result is no publication or admitted serve while the conflict is unresolved.</p>\n<p>An adoption decision is evidence that a norm was accepted within its scope. It is not proof that every factual claim motivating that norm is true.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R008.</strong> The decision MUST retain links to the assessed proposal and any restrictions or effective date.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-impact-analysis-and-reassessment\">4. Impact analysis and reassessment</h2>\n<p>Impact analysis starts from declared dependency relations.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R009.</strong> Affected artifacts MUST be classified with the relevant new rule set and use scope.</p>\n<p>The impact tokens are <code>compatible</code>, <code>restricted</code>, <code>requires_review</code>, <code>blocked</code> and <code>unknown_impact</code>, with the eligibility/decision mapping in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#3-canonical-dimension-vocabularies\">status model</a>. These are projections over individual observations, not permanent global truth labels.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R010.</strong> A compatibility result MUST state the preservation argument or tests.</p>\n<p>Unknown dependency coverage cannot support a claim that every affected artifact was found. Conservative re-evaluation is required when an untracked dependency could change a mandatory obligation.</p>\n<p>Reassessment records the old artifact, new rule set, relevant current state, method, results and limitations. A passing reassessment under a narrower test does not establish compatibility with a broader profile. Multiple assessments can coexist when their scopes differ.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-migration\">5. Migration</h2>\n<p>A migration creates a new representation or revision with an explicit source link, mapping, validator results, losses and unresolved fields.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R011.</strong> It MUST preserve negation, conditions, attribution and applicable constraints or declare their loss.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R012.</strong> Missing capability or authority MUST remain unknown.</p>\n<p>A migration cannot manufacture an execution attempt or successful result.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R013.</strong> If semantic equivalence has not been established, the migration MUST state that limit even when the new structure is valid.</p>\n<p>Reversible byte transformation establishes only the declared representation property. Lossless semantic migration needs an interpretation-preservation argument within a defined domain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-visibility-and-rollback\">6. Visibility and rollback</h2>\n<p><strong>EVO-R014.</strong> Publication MUST select a coherent set of artifact revisions, catalogue entries and rendered representations.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R015.</strong> A pointer update MUST have an identified adoption decision and the complete atomic <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/#5-guarded-publication-and-serving\">publication guard</a>, including current authority and dependency epochs.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R016.</strong> The website, downloadable corpus and API index MUST expose their actual source revisions so a consumer can detect differences.</p>\n<p>Rollback changes the selected current head; it does not erase the later revision or its observations.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R017.</strong> If a rule was withdrawn because of a known defect or disclosure restriction, a rollback MUST preserve that knowledge and MUST NOT silently reauthorize a previously blocked use.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases\">7. Acceptance cases</h2>\n<p>Required cases include a rule revision with no content change; an affected artifact discovered through an indirect dependency; an unresolved impact due to incomplete dependencies; an invalid migration that loses a constraint; conflicting adoption decisions; a late publisher; and rollback after revocation.</p>\n<p><strong>EVO-R018.</strong> Each case MUST retain the historical creation basis and the separate current decision.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection and revision study</a> supplies bounded evidence about addressed changes and finite projections. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">conformance study</a> records limited migration checks. Their results do not establish automatic migration of arbitrary knowledge graphs or a deployed community governance service.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-publication-and-implementation-versions\">8. Publication and implementation versions</h2>\n<p><strong>EVO-R019.</strong> The document edition, serialized profile, validator implementation, evidence protocol, service build and index snapshot MUST have separately identified versions.</p>\n<p>Removing a development label from a manuscript does not change a deployed protocol. Conversely, an unfinished service feature does not prevent publication of a complete formal argument with explicit assumptions.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/\">source map and compatibility record</a> identifies what this edition replaces and what historical wire versions remain unchanged. The <a href=\"/en/appendices/api-integration/\">API integration appendix</a> defines the observable publication boundaries without claiming an unverified contract-composition endpoint.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-conformance","title":"1. Purpose and conformance"},{"level":2,"id":"2-creation-basis","title":"2. Creation basis"},{"level":2,"id":"3-change-proposal-and-adoption","title":"3. Change proposal and adoption"},{"level":2,"id":"4-impact-analysis-and-reassessment","title":"4. Impact analysis and reassessment"},{"level":2,"id":"5-migration","title":"5. Migration"},{"level":2,"id":"6-visibility-and-rollback","title":"6. Visibility and rollback"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases","title":"7. Acceptance cases"},{"level":2,"id":"8-publication-and-implementation-versions","title":"8. Publication and implementation versions"}],"title_id":"knowledge-evolution-and-versioned-rules","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"4662bdd76c5716cb7d9b55c1b99e00dda626b34c3565e2f03ceecb61fe15685a","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"book-package","kind":"spec","type":"specification","path":"engineering/specifications/book-package/SPECIFICATION.md","route":"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/","title":"Book Packages: Source Identity, Semantic Layers, and Validation","titleRu":"Book Packages: Source Identity, Semantic Layers, and Validation","summary":"A concrete book-package schema, reversible conversion ledger, semantic layers, rights boundaries and synthetic conformance checks.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Author:</strong> Pavel Valentov · <strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Implementation component</th>\n<th>Scope</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/schemas/book-package.schema.json\">Schema</a></td>\n<td><code>book-package/2</code>, five <code>KCBook*/v2</code> record families</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/tools/check_book_package.py\">Validator and projection</a></td>\n<td>Structural and cross-file checks, safe Markdown rendering, candidate rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/tools/build_book_package.py\">Builder</a></td>\n<td>Wraps source bytes and supplied conversion output; does not interpret a book</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/schemas/fixtures/book-package\">Synthetic fixtures</a></td>\n<td>Invented source and negative records; no third-party book text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/tools/test_book_package.py\">Conformance tests</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/tools/test_book_package_review.py\">review regressions</a></td>\n<td>Validation, reversible edits, rights, scoped review, rendering and status derivation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose-and-validation-boundary\">1. Purpose and validation boundary</h2>\n<p>A book package binds one source representation to its conversion, semantic records and review observations. This specification defines the implemented <code>book-package/2</code> profile. Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. Record validation does not establish conversion fidelity, semantic truth, actual reviewer independence, publication rights or task-specific admission.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R001.</strong> The manifest MUST declare <code>validation_profile: book-package/2</code>, and every record MUST use its <code>KCBookPackageManifest/v2</code>, <code>KCBookObject/v2</code>, <code>KCBookRelation/v2</code>, <code>KCBookEdit/v2</code> or <code>KCBookQuality/v2</code> tag.</p>\n<p>Unknown versions are unsupported.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R002.</strong> A consumer MUST NOT silently reinterpret a version 1 record as version 2.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Knowledge Contract admission runtime</strong> decides use under the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">core contract</a>. The inherited literal field <code>kc2_admission</code> is not a protocol version.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R003.</strong> Its value in producer records MUST remain <code>not_measured</code>; the producer cannot grant admission.</p>\n<p><code>not_measured</code> maps to assessment <code>unknown</code> with reason “no observation,” never to <code>pass</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-layout-and-reversible-edits\">2. Layout and reversible edits</h2>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>&lt;package_id&gt;/\n  manifest.json\n  document.md\n  readable.md\n  assets/\n  edits.jsonl\n  objects.jsonl\n  relations.jsonl\n  quality.json</code></pre>\n<p><strong>BPK-R004.</strong> The package identifier MUST be a readable lowercase hyphenated slug, 3–96 characters; a single letter followed by digits is forbidden.</p>\n<p><code>release_id</code> identifies the particular release separately.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R005.</strong> Component paths MUST remain relative and inside the package, with no parent traversal or symlinks.</p>\n<p><code>document.md</code> is the unchanged converter output.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R006.</strong> Accepted edits MUST target <code>readable_md</code>; <code>asset_text</code> has no supported digest-chain semantics and is rejected.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R007.</strong> A package without <code>document_md</code> and <code>readable_md</code> MUST NOT contain accepted edits or a built semantic layer.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R008.</strong> Each accepted edit MUST have a nonempty original and replacement, an anchor, basis, reason, actor, input digest and output digest.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R009.</strong> At its step, the original MUST occur exactly once, and the replacement MUST occur exactly once in the resulting text.</p>\n<p>Occurrences include overlaps: <code>aa</code> occurs twice in <code>xaaay</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R010.</strong> Applying the accepted edits in ledger order MUST produce <code>readable.md</code>; undoing them in reverse order MUST reproduce <code>document.md</code> exactly.</p>\n<p>Insertions, deletions and ambiguous repeated-fragment edits are unsupported by this search-reversible profile.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R011.</strong> JSON readers MUST reject duplicate object member names at every depth, non-finite number tokens and a UTF-8 BOM, so independent readers cannot choose different rights or status values from the same record.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R012.</strong> All comparison and hashing MUST use exact bytes with strict UTF-8 decoding and no newline translation.</p>\n<p><code>\\r\\n</code>, <code>\\r</code> and <code>\\n</code> remain distinct. JSON Lines, <code>SHA256SUMS</code> and check lists split only on <code>\\n</code>; one final newline is allowed, blank JSON Lines and lines ending in <code>\\r</code> are rejected. U+2028, U+0085 and other control characters do not become additional record separators.</p>\n<p>The edit lifecycle is <code>pending</code> → <code>accepted</code> or <code>rejected</code>; <code>stale</code> means an obsolete proposal whose base no longer matches, and is never replayed. Accepted ledger entries are immutable: a later correction creates another entry or a new package release. A stale proposal’s declared or overlapping dependents remain unresolved until reassessed in a new valid record.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-identity-and-integrity-observations\">3. Identity and integrity observations</h2>\n<p><code>work_id</code> identifies the work, <code>edition_id</code> the edition and language, and <code>representation_id</code> one source file. Translations and editions retain separate identities and anchors, with explicit <code>related_works</code> relations. A representation records its source digest, size, format, integrity (<code>verified</code>, <code>mismatch</code>, <code>unverified</code>) and completeness (<code>full</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>bibliography_only</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R013.</strong> A <code>verified</code> or <code>mismatch</code> integrity declaration MUST include <code>integrity_check</code> with <code>method</code>, <code>observer</code>, <code>checked_at</code>, <code>expected_sha256</code> and <code>observed_sha256</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R014.</strong> The observed digest MUST equal the representation digest.</p>\n<p>Verified integrity requires equal expected and observed digests; mismatch requires different digests.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R015.</strong> The expected digest MUST NOT be replaced to conceal a mismatch.</p>\n<p>This is an attributed producer observation. The validator checks its shape and internal consistency; it cannot authenticate the expected digest’s authority or reconstruct an unavailable source. The builder records whether its expected digest was supplied by the caller.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R016.</strong> A production consumer MUST authenticate the relevant observation at its declared trust boundary before treating it as evidence of source integrity.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R017.</strong> <code>document_md.input_sha256</code> MUST equal the representation digest and its component record MUST name the converter and version.</p>\n<p>With no supported converter output, the package remains <code>adapter_required</code>; no empty or invented document substitutes for conversion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-semantic-objects-and-facets\">4. Semantic objects and facets</h2>\n<p>An object identifies a concept, claim, procedure, figure, table, formula, code block, example or other structure.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R018.</strong> Every object MUST retain its text, language, origin, at least one source anchor, status and all eight facet fields: <code>representation_modes</code>, <code>content_kinds</code>, <code>discourse_functions</code>, <code>aboutness</code>, <code>source_position</code>, <code>semantic_operators</code>, <code>assessment_refs</code>, and <code>governance_refs</code>.</p>\n<p>Qualifiers carry conditions and numeric units when present.</p>\n<p>The facets implement a bounded profile of <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/\">semantic types</a>. Representation modes include <code>text</code>, <code>image</code>, <code>audio</code>, <code>video</code>, <code>structured_data</code> and their explicit combinations. Source positions are <code>asserts</code>, <code>hypothesizes</code>, <code>presumes</code>, <code>denies</code>, <code>asks</code>, <code>reports_other_position</code>: <code>presumes</code> denotes a presupposition and is not a synonym of hypothesis.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R019.</strong> Operators include epistemic and deontic modality, quantifier, negation, condition, exception and order; each operator MUST name its scope.</p>\n<p>A free-text qualifier condition applies to the whole object unless a scoped operator supplies the more precise binding.</p>\n<p>The nineteen content-kind tokens are <code>definition</code>, <code>state_description</code>, <code>observation_measurement</code>, <code>generalization</code>, <code>explanatory_model</code>, <code>formal_model</code>, <code>procedure</code>, <code>structure_configuration</code>, <code>constraint_norm</code>, <code>goal_intent</code>, <code>preference_evaluation</code>, <code>argument_evidence</code>, <code>example</code>, <code>counterexample</code>, <code>question_problem_gap</code>, <code>narrative_episode</code>, <code>analogy_metaphor</code>, <code>hypothesis_interpretation</code>, and <code>commitment_speech_act</code>. This vocabulary is not an exhaustive taxonomy.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R020.</strong> Each facet MUST be <code>known</code> with a value, or <code>unknown</code>, <code>not_applicable</code> or <code>withheld</code> with an explicit reason and no value.</p>\n<p>Known representation modes, content kinds, discourse functions and aboutness are nonempty by this profile’s cardinality rule. Known-empty operators, assessment references or governance references assert an established empty set and are allowed only with that meaning.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R021.</strong> An object named by a package review MUST include that review’s identifier in known <code>assessment_refs</code>; known-empty cannot deny the very review that cites it.</p>\n<p>Unknown references remain unknown rather than empty.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R022.</strong> A <code>benchmark</code> claim MUST additionally record corpus, device, tool version, settings, units and mode.</p>\n<p>Structural presence does not verify those declarations or establish task applicability.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-relations\">5. Relations</h2>\n<p>A relation connects two existing, different objects of the same package with one of <code>defines</code>, <code>uses</code>, <code>variant_of</code>, <code>requires</code>, <code>compared_with</code>, <code>contradicts</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R023.</strong> Every relation MUST retain its conditions, origin, evidence, endpoints and status.</p>\n<p>Every relation has at least one evidence anchor tagged <code>from</code>, <code>to</code> or <code>both</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R024.</strong> A <code>contradicts</code> relation MUST include separate <code>from</code> and <code>to</code> evidence, so one-sided attribution cannot masquerade as a two-sided finding.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R025.</strong> A relation touching a stale or rejected endpoint MUST itself be stale or rejected.</p>\n<p>Review of a relation does not silently rehabilitate its endpoint.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-evidence-anchors-and-source-formats\">6. Evidence anchors and source formats</h2>\n<p><strong>BPK-R026.</strong> Every anchor MUST bind the same representation identifier and source digest as its manifest.</p>\n<p>The implemented format-to-anchor mapping is exact:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Anchor kind</th>\n<th>Exact format tokens</th>\n<th>Coordinates</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>page_bbox</code></td>\n<td><code>pdf</code>, <code>djvu</code>, <code>ps</code></td>\n<td>page from 1; bounding box with positive area; origin and unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>spine_offset</code></td>\n<td><code>epub</code></td>\n<td>member <code>spine_item</code>, optional element path, start/end offsets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>section_block</code></td>\n<td><code>html</code>, <code>md</code>, <code>txt</code>, <code>tex</code></td>\n<td>section and block identifiers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>sheet_range</code></td>\n<td><code>xlsx</code></td>\n<td>sheet, cell range, <code>formula</code> / <code>cached_value</code> / <code>literal</code> layer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>json_pointer</code></td>\n<td><code>json</code></td>\n<td>RFC 6901 pointer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>slide_block</code></td>\n<td><code>ppt</code>, <code>pptx</code></td>\n<td>slide number and block identifier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>none</td>\n<td><code>zip</code>, <code>tar_gz</code>, <code>sevenz</code></td>\n<td>opaque source identity only; archive member addressing is unsupported</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>BPK-R027.</strong> Archives MAY be retained as an unavailable source requiring an adapter, but MUST NOT carry converter output, edits or semantic records under this profile.</p>\n<p>A member-path anchor requires a separately versioned extension. DocC is not a format token; a DocC JSON file uses <code>json</code>.</p>\n<p>The validator checks geometry, ordering and the declared mapping, not whether a cited source location exists or supports the text. Extracting a different representation creates a new binding; it does not justify reusing coordinates from the previous one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-attribution-and-safe-rendering\">7. Attribution and safe rendering</h2>\n<p>Origins are <code>author_statement</code>, <code>editor_correction</code>, <code>generated_meaning</code>, <code>generated_description</code>, <code>our_experiment</code> and <code>model_inference</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R028.</strong> A non-author origin MUST have a generation record and <code>presented_as_author: false</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R029.</strong> An author statement MUST NOT carry a generation record.</p>\n<p>On an author statement, <code>presented_as_author: true</code> means an asserted verbatim quotation; <code>false</code> means an explicitly attributed paraphrase. This flag never establishes quotation fidelity by itself.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R030.</strong> The public Markdown renderer MUST preserve every object and relation field, including qualifiers, operator scopes, source position, conditions, anchors, generation and assessment references.</p>\n<p>It emits each complete record in a JSON literal block, with a fence longer than any backtick run in the payload. Origin labels and record headings are emitted outside that container from controlled fields.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R031.</strong> Payload headings, HTML or apparent origin labels MUST NOT create new rendered record structure.</p>\n<p>Every non-author block is visibly labelled “GENERATED, not the author’s statement.”</p>\n<p><code>document_md.contains_generated_text</code> is always false; generated meanings and image descriptions remain separate derivatives. A missing asset records <code>missing_asset</code> without a fabricated path or digest. The public renderer refuses non-<code>author_open</code> packages.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-quality-observations-and-readiness\">8. Quality observations and readiness</h2>\n<p>Extraction and semantic observations remain separate; there is no combined score. Extraction statuses are <code>pass</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>adapter_required</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>not_measured</code>. Check results use the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">status model</a>: <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>unknown</code>, <code>not_applicable</code>. Each check has a unique identifier and method.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R032.</strong> A <code>not_applicable</code> check MUST state the inapplicability reason in <code>detail</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R033.</strong> Extraction <code>pass</code> MUST contain at least one applicable passing check, no failed or unknown check, and matching seen/expected unit counts when those counts are supplied.</p>\n<p>An empty checklist or an all-inapplicable checklist cannot prove successful extraction. <code>adapter_required</code> contradicts available converter output; <code>pass</code> or <code>partial</code> contradicts absent converter output.</p>\n<p>CER is either measured with a value, reference class and sample size, or <code>not_measured</code> with null. A null is not a measured zero. Semantic status is <code>not_built</code>, <code>not_measured</code>, <code>built_unreviewed</code> or <code>reviewed</code>; the first two require empty object and relation files.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R034.</strong> Every semantic review MUST identify <code>review_id</code>, <code>reviewer_id</code>, <code>producer_id</code>, reviewer type, independence basis, time, result, the exact object and relation identifiers examined, and the current object-file, relation-file and readable-file digests.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R035.</strong> <code>sample_size</code> counts distinct reviewed objects, not relations or checklist items, and MUST equal the object-ID list length.</p>\n<p>Reviews cannot name nonexistent records. A review of earlier bytes cannot be transferred to new bytes by changing only the timestamp.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R036.</strong> The <code>knowledge_ready</code> profile uses <strong>complete coverage</strong>, not a statistical sample: every object and relation MUST be included, every included record MUST be <code>reviewed</code>, the result MUST be <code>supported</code>, and the declared reviewer MUST be a human or blind agent distinct from the producer.</p>\n<p>Same-session review, partial support, unsupported results and omitted records cannot earn this status.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R052.</strong> There MUST be at least one object.</p>\n<p>A narrower review is retained honestly under <code>partial_pilot</code>.</p>\n<p>These checks verify declared scope, separation and byte bindings. They do not prove that a human review occurred or that identities are independent. The synthetic fixture uses explicitly invented reviewer identities for testing. Authenticating observations belongs to the consuming trust boundary.</p>\n<h3 id=\"total-package-status-decision-table\">Total package-status decision table</h3>\n<p><strong>BPK-R037.</strong> The validator MUST compute the first matching row below and require equality with the submitted <code>package_status</code>.</p>\n<p>All well-formed combinations receive one result; contradictory component/check declarations are additionally rejected rather than assigned a success status.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Priority</th>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Derived status</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Source integrity is <code>mismatch</code>, even if completeness is bibliographic</td>\n<td><code>blocked_integrity</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Completeness is <code>bibliography_only</code></td>\n<td><code>bibliography_only</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Converter output is absent</td>\n<td><code>adapter_required</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Semantic layer is <code>not_built</code> or <code>not_measured</code>, with zero objects and relations</td>\n<td><code>structure_only</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Full, verified source; extraction passes; complete independent declared review supports all reviewed records</td>\n<td><code>knowledge_ready</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Any remaining well-formed converted package</td>\n<td><code>partial_pilot</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>BPK-R038.</strong> <code>blocked_integrity</code>, <code>bibliography_only</code> and <code>adapter_required</code> MUST omit both document and readable components.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R039.</strong> <code>structure_only</code>, <code>partial_pilot</code> and <code>knowledge_ready</code> MUST contain both.</p>\n<p>A reviewed package with unverified integrity or partial source coverage is therefore representable as <code>partial_pilot</code>. Extraction failure remains visible even when files are structurally present.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-correction-impact-and-reassessment\">9. Correction impact and reassessment</h2>\n<p><strong>BPK-R040.</strong> An accepted correction MUST invalidate every object explicitly named in <code>depends_on_objects</code> <strong>or</strong> whose source anchor overlaps the edit.</p>\n<p>The producer’s dependency list cannot suppress the overlap rule. Section/block and slide/block matches are exact; intervals and comparable page boxes use intersection; JSON pointers use equality or ancestor relationships. Sheet ranges conservatively invalidate the whole same-sheet scope; incomparable geometry is treated as potentially overlapping.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R041.</strong> An affected object MUST be stale or rejected until its <code>reviewed_edit_ids</code> explicitly names the accepted corrections and a supported, independent declared review covers that object and the current readable and semantic file digests.</p>\n<p>References to unknown or unaccepted edit identifiers are rejected. A stale proposal cannot be cleared by this accepted-edit mechanism. Dependent relations follow section 5. This is a declared reassessment record, not evidence that a producer’s semantic reasoning was correct.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-rights-and-publication-layers\">10. Rights and publication layers</h2>\n<p><code>rights.license_class</code> is <code>author_open</code>, <code>third_party_restricted</code> or <code>unknown</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R042.</strong> Only <code>author_open</code> MAY declare the package publication <code>license</code> (<code>CC-BY-4.0</code> or <code>Apache-2.0</code>) and nonempty <code>publishable_layers</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R043.</strong> Restricted or unknown works MUST omit that licence field and leave the publication layer list empty.</p>\n<p>This blocks book text carried through semantic chunks as well as through document files. No quotation budget or right to republish is inferred from extraction, review or a producer-supplied licence string.</p>\n<p>Private processing of an otherwise valid restricted package is outside public projection.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R044.</strong> The projection function MUST return no rows for such a package.</p>\n<p>This conservative producer rule implements the established exclusion of third-party book text from the repository; it is not a legal finding about possible uses elsewhere. Any separately authored derivative requires its own source/rights assessment and publication decision.</p>\n<h2 id=\"11-candidate-export-and-admission-runtime\">11. Candidate export and admission runtime</h2>\n<p><strong>BPK-R045.</strong> <code>candidate_projection(package_directory)</code> MUST validate a private immutable byte snapshot and compute the rendering from that snapshot itself.</p>\n<p>It does not accept a raw manifest plus caller-selected Markdown.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R046.</strong> It MUST return no rows on validation failure or for <code>blocked_integrity</code>, <code>bibliography_only</code> or <code>adapter_required</code>, or without an allowed open publication licence.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Package element</th>\n<th>Candidate projection</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Allowed document/readable Markdown</td>\n<td>One proposed row for its exact bytes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Allowed knowledge layer</td>\n<td>One proposed <code>knowledge.md</code> row for the full protected rendering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>JSON records, assets and quality file</td>\n<td>Retained in the package; not separate Markdown export rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Unlisted layer or prohibited status/rights class</td>\n<td>No row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Runtime admission</td>\n<td>Remains <code>not_measured</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Rows identify <code>articles/book-packages/&lt;package_id&gt;/&lt;file&gt;</code>, digest, holder, licence, limits and <code>status: proposed</code>. These paths fit the exporter path syntax but are not automatically allowlisted. The existing <code>KnowledgeContractCandidateExport/v2</code> exporter still requires its own admitted scope, exact merged revision and reviewed publication decision.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R047.</strong> The producer MUST NOT widen that scope or treat a candidate row as publication or runtime admission.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/appendices/api-integration/\">API integration appendix</a> distinguishes repository publication, index admission and actual service results. No live consumer acceptance of this new profile is claimed by local conformance tests.</p>\n<h2 id=\"12-validation-and-evidence-limits\">12. Validation and evidence limits</h2>\n<p>The JSON Schema checks record shape, fixed vocabularies and conditional fields. The package validator adds digest and count checks; identity and endpoint consistency; anchor mapping and geometry; byte-exact reversible replay; correction impact; extraction conjunction; review scope and file bindings; complete status derivation; and publication rights boundaries.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R048.</strong> The conformance suite MUST retain each regression’s expected cause, including negative review, unknown extraction, restricted licence bypass, arbitrary rendering, Markdown structure injection, stale correction, rejected endpoint and obsolete profile.</p>\n<p>Its status-table test enumerates 3,240 input combinations; enumeration tests the decision table, not 3,240 historical book experiments. Structural incoherence is tested separately.</p>\n<p>The package does not establish source fidelity, truth, real reviewer independence, unseen source geometry, authentic licence ownership or downstream admission. Generated descriptions are separate from the media they describe. The linked <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">transcription study</a> reports historical evidence; it is not validation of the new wire profile.</p>\n<h2 id=\"13-compatibility-and-migration\">13. Compatibility and migration</h2>\n<p>Version 1 is preserved at immutable <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e\">source revision ed2e188c</a>. Version 2 is intentionally stricter and adds observations, scoped review, the readable package identifier, explicit validation profile and semantic vocabularies.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R049.</strong> Existing record tags MUST NOT simply be rewritten: missing observations remain missing, unsupported edits remain unsupported, and review of old bytes does not establish review of migrated bytes.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R050.</strong> A migration MUST create a new package release, retain the original revision, identify every mapping or dropped unsupported field, revalidate digests and edits, and obtain any newly required review.</p>\n<p><strong>BPK-R051.</strong> It MUST NOT invent integrity observations, reviewer identities or author attribution.</p>\n<p>V2 builders emit empty semantic layers until interpretation is actually performed. The publication edition, package profile, validator build and admission-runtime version remain distinct identities.</p>\n<p>Credit and licence: Knowledge Contract Arcana, <a href=\"https://contract.place\" class=\"uri\">https://contract.place</a> (CC BY 4.0). See <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/ATTRIBUTION.md\">attribution</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose-and-validation-boundary","title":"1. Purpose and validation boundary"},{"level":2,"id":"2-layout-and-reversible-edits","title":"2. Layout and reversible edits"},{"level":2,"id":"3-identity-and-integrity-observations","title":"3. Identity and integrity observations"},{"level":2,"id":"4-semantic-objects-and-facets","title":"4. Semantic objects and facets"},{"level":2,"id":"5-relations","title":"5. Relations"},{"level":2,"id":"6-evidence-anchors-and-source-formats","title":"6. Evidence anchors and source formats"},{"level":2,"id":"7-attribution-and-safe-rendering","title":"7. Attribution and safe rendering"},{"level":2,"id":"8-quality-observations-and-readiness","title":"8. Quality observations and readiness"},{"level":3,"id":"total-package-status-decision-table","title":"Total package-status decision table"},{"level":2,"id":"9-correction-impact-and-reassessment","title":"9. Correction impact and reassessment"},{"level":2,"id":"10-rights-and-publication-layers","title":"10. Rights and publication layers"},{"level":2,"id":"11-candidate-export-and-admission-runtime","title":"11. Candidate export and admission runtime"},{"level":2,"id":"12-validation-and-evidence-limits","title":"12. Validation and evidence limits"},{"level":2,"id":"13-compatibility-and-migration","title":"13. Compatibility and migration"}],"title_id":"book-packages-source-identity-semantic-layers-and-validation","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/book-package/SPECIFICATION.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/specifications/book-package/SPECIFICATION.md","source_sha256":"1ce2ce4fa9e0e2647b3472f1da0a250fa3abae00c78b7518d3497f106c09722c","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"contract-profile-conformance","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/","title":"Contract profiles: finite conformance, trust boundaries, and a strong simple comparator","titleRu":"Contract profiles: finite conformance, trust boundaries, and a strong simple comparator","summary":"Finite contract checks, false trusted-input counterexamples and an equal strong-comparator result.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>This study examines whether a finite implementation of a Knowledge Contract follows its declared structural and application rules. The implementer’s development fixtures matched 35 of 35 expected ordinary outcomes; this is regression evidence. A separately composed control suite, prepared without inspecting the implementation but within the same research session, matched 26 of 26. These results concern already interpreted JSON instances. The implementation also accepted two author and four control inputs whose trusted annotations were deliberately false. Those acceptances expose a trust boundary and are excluded from the ordinary totals. A subsequent strong plain-form checker matched the recorded Knowledge Contract decisions on all nine shared scenarios after its author knew their inputs and expected outcomes. This shows that a direct checker can encode the tested obligations; it establishes neither generalization nor an advantage of the larger representation. The complete retained result records accompany this report.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-model\">Question and model</h2>\n<p>The practical question is whether a consumer can distinguish an adequately bound informational package from one that omits a required condition, uses evidence for another task, or lacks a current supplied permission. The tested carrier is <code>kc-synthesis/0.1</code>. Its nine logical groups represent identity, purpose, content, scope, grounds, dependencies, obligations, projection, and observations. Optional versioned profiles add professional work, dialogue, documents, reasoning, visual delivery, and reuse.</p>\n<p>A profile extends the obligations of the core. It does not override them by appearing later in a file. If admission requires the core condition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_0</annotation></semantics></math> and applicable profile conditions <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_1,\\ldots,C_n</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Admit</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><munderover><mo>⋀</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Admit}(x) \\Rightarrow \\bigwedge_{i=0}^{n} C_i(x)\n\\Rightarrow C_0(x).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>This implication follows from the admission definition. Whether software implements that definition correctly is a separate question. Whether an external annotation is true is another. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/conditional-properties-source.json\">conditional-properties record</a> states these distinctions and supplies counterexamples. Their theoretical setting is developed in <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a>.</p>\n<p>The implementation assumes that objects and relationships have already been interpreted. A supplied capability annotation, source availability, and a current authority decision are inputs. The checker does not independently interview an actor, authenticate an external grant, or establish a source’s factual accuracy. An informational contract may omit the professional profile; an execution request has additional binding requirements. Unknown required profiles block use. Unknown optional profiles can be retained as opaque data with a diagnostic, which does not count as interpreting them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"design-and-unit-of-observation\">Design and unit of observation</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/evaluation-protocol-source.json\">evaluation protocol</a> distinguishes a scenario from assertions nested inside it. The author prepared the interface and its own suite. A different agent in the same session prepared control intents before inspecting the implementation. After receiving the interface, that agent fixed concrete cases, expected outcomes, and a runner before its first import of the validator implementation. The retained <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/control-review-source.json\">control review</a> describes this sequence.</p>\n<p>There were 28 initial control intents: 24 ordinary and four trust-boundary stress intents. Interface mapping produced 26 ordinary concrete scenarios and four stress scenarios. The 26 comprise six expected positive outcomes, seventeen expected rejections, and three migration-preservation cases. Only four of the six positive outcomes are application admissions. One intent could not be mapped and five were only partially represented. These omissions remain part of the scope assessment; they are not additional passed cases.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-15\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-21\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-21\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-21\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-21\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Operation in the retained control report</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Ordinary cases</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Expected positive outcomes</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Expected rejections</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Preservation-only cases</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Application assessment</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">13</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">4</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">9</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Execution verification</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">6</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">5</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Profile compatibility</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Contract validation</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">0</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Legacy migration</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">0</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">0</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Total</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">26</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">6</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">17</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are different predicates: a valid schema, an accepted execution, a compatible profile and permission for an application are not interchangeable decisions. The table is recomputed from the report’s <code>operation</code> and expected-check fields. The four application positives are C01, C02, C06B and C18; C02B concerns execution, and C12 concerns compatibility.</p>\n<p>The control composition is organizational separation within one session and agent family. It is not an external replication or independent domain review. Cases were deliberately selected to exercise mechanisms, so their proportions do not estimate error rates in a population of real tasks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"results\">Results</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-21\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-28\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-28\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-21\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Evaluation</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Ordinary expected outcomes matched</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">False trusted inputs accepted</th>\n<th>Meaning of the result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Author development fixtures</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">35/35: 13 expected positives, 22 expected rejections</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2/2</td>\n<td>Regression evidence; separately disclosed trust-boundary stress</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Separate control suite</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">26/26</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">4/4</td>\n<td>Exact outcomes on the frozen concrete control cases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Strong plain-form comparison</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">9/9 for each representation</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">Outside this comparison</td>\n<td>Equal decisions on three allowed and six forbidden admissions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These counts come from the exact <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/author-conformance-report.json\">author report</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/separate-control-report.json\">control report</a>, and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/strong-plain-form-comparison.json\">strong-comparator report</a>. The reports retain input hashes, case rows, expected outcomes, and observations. Their bytes were recovered from the retained publication reader and checked against its source index; <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a> records the identities and hashes.</p>\n<p>Positive controls matter. A checker that rejects every input would avoid forbidden admissions but fail useful cases. The six positive control outcomes include a compact informational core, correctly bound work, exact synthetic execution evidence, a current grant despite a different revoked grant, an opaque optional profile, and an eligible reuse instance. Negative cases include missing capability, a mismatched or expired grant, changed requirements, absent declared imagery, unknown required profiles, lost conditions, and evidence bound to another attempt or output.</p>\n<p>The migration cases concern a declared six-field legacy subset. They check retained original values, explicit gaps, and the absence of invented capability or authority. They do not demonstrate lossless migration of every previous contract implementation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"counterexamples-and-the-comparator\">Counterexamples and the comparator</h2>\n<p>The control stress cases deliberately supplied four internally consistent falsehoods: a positive capability annotation, a fabricated grant, false subject content, and an incorrect claim that a source was available. All four were admitted. This is a counterexample to the proposition that passing structural and binding checks establishes real competence, authorization, factual truth, or production access enforcement. It is not a successful semantic test merely because the interface’s expected answer was acceptance.</p>\n<p>Two comparators must also remain distinct. The initially registered diagnostic ablation checked field presence and simple form but omitted the cross-object conditions under investigation. It admitted all nine shared inputs, including all six forbidden ones; it therefore matched only the three expected positives, whereas the contract matched all nine. Its separate calibration rejected an empty task, but that input is outside the shared nine-case denominator. This establishes the effect of removing relational checks in that code. It is not a fair estimate of an advantage over a strong task form.</p>\n<p>The subsequent strong checker received the same material information and directly checked current sources, work and requirements revisions, actor assignment, conditions, grants, and required capabilities. It used fixed fields rather than an extensible ontology and profile registry. It matched all nine expected outcomes, exactly as the stored contract run did. The comparator author knew the nine inputs and their expected outcomes from the first results. Equality is therefore an existence demonstration that a direct checker can encode these obligations, not a held-out test of generalization. Only the comparator was newly executed in this comparison; the contract side used its preserved first result. A later comparative evaluation needs unseen cases for both implementations.</p>\n<p>The equality directly challenges a claim that the larger representation is necessary for these nine decisions. It leaves open different questions about exchange, maintenance, additional profiles, preparation effort, and scale. None of those outcomes was measured here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"claim-test-counterexample-and-limit\">Claim, test, counterexample, and limit</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Claim</th>\n<th>Model or test</th>\n<th>Counterexample or rival explanation</th>\n<th>Supported conclusion and limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Profile composition preserves core obligations</td>\n<td>Conjunctive admission and required-profile cases</td>\n<td>An implementation silently overrides a core condition</td>\n<td>The defined rule preserves the core; the finite suite checks selected implementations of it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Evidence must refer to the attempted work</td>\n<td>Exact task, requirements, actor, attempt, output, and criterion bindings</td>\n<td>Old evidence has a similar description but a different target</td>\n<td>Tested mismatches are rejected; forged trusted bindings remain outside authentication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A skill description establishes capability</td>\n<td>Separate description and capability inputs</td>\n<td>A false capability annotation is accepted</td>\n<td>The description alone is insufficient; input truth still needs an external basis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Contract conformance implies source truth</td>\n<td>Schema and consistency checks</td>\n<td>The admitted false-source stress case</td>\n<td>This implication is refuted within the test design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>The contract outperforms a strong simple form</td>\n<td>Nine shared decisions</td>\n<td>The direct checker makes the same decisions</td>\n<td>No advantage is established on this shared domain</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"reproduction-and-evidence-boundaries\">Reproduction and evidence boundaries</h2>\n<p>The accompanying files are sufficient to inspect all recorded results, separate the stress denominator, inspect the comparator decisions, and verify the provenance of retained JSON. The exact original runners, the thirty concrete control inputs and their oracle, the strong comparator code, and the frozen implementation are not included. This is an unresolved recovery and publication gap for project-authored synthetic material, not a third-party-book rights restriction. The locally available later implementation has a different hash from the one bound to this report and cannot substitute for it. Consequently this folder supplies inspectable retained reports rather than a complete historical rerun. Hashes identify artifacts; they do not replace missing code, authenticate an observer, or certify a result’s meaning. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/historical-recovery-gaps.json\">recovery inventory</a> lists the exact missing identities and the evidence used to identify them.</p>\n<p>The review did recover the exact <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/historical-recovery/contract-synthesis-0.1.schema.json\">historical <code>kc-synthesis/0.1</code> schema</a>, whose SHA-256 agrees with the author report. It is published separately, without changing the retained evidence inventory. Recovering this one input does not recover the missing implementation, concrete fixtures or runners and does not imply conformance to the current architecture.</p>\n<p>Rendered prose sources are explicitly identified as text views recovered from the retained HTML. Their original Markdown source hashes are recorded separately from the hashes of those new views. They are not misrepresented as byte-identical Markdown reconstructions.</p>\n<p>The retained conditional-properties, protocol and review narratives are in Russian; JSON keys are mainly English, with some Russian descriptions. The model, design and boundary paragraphs above are English summaries of the cited passages, including the same-session review scope and unmapped controls. They are summaries, not a certified translation of every source line. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/evidence-language-index.json\">evidence language index</a> identifies the language and preserved hash of each distributed file.</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>The tested profile performed its declared finite structural and relational checks, including useful positive controls and prohibited admissions. The accepted false trusted inputs demonstrate why source admission, current authority, and semantic assessment need their own evidence. The strong-comparator tie further narrows the contribution: this study supplies a working representation and inspectable finite results, while leaving comparative practical benefit unestablished. These distinctions are prerequisites for a usable contract service, not unfinished text that should be concealed by an undifferentiated success label. Terminology is consolidated in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-model","title":"Question and model"},{"level":2,"id":"design-and-unit-of-observation","title":"Design and unit of observation"},{"level":2,"id":"results","title":"Results"},{"level":2,"id":"counterexamples-and-the-comparator","title":"Counterexamples and the comparator"},{"level":2,"id":"claim-test-counterexample-and-limit","title":"Claim, test, counterexample, and limit"},{"level":2,"id":"reproduction-and-evidence-boundaries","title":"Reproduction and evidence boundaries"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"contract-profiles-finite-conformance-trust-boundaries-and-a-strong-simple-comparator","math_count":3,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"788c09269946b294420024f28656ef616e2df056e4d8246bfdf67c69ca4434a9","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"dependency-closure-and-package-delivery","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/","title":"Dependency closure, readable artifact layouts, and a pinned package replay","titleRu":"Dependency closure, readable artifact layouts, and a pinned package replay","summary":"Historical package evidence and a public exhaustive replay of a finite dependency graph.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>A task package is useful only if selecting an artifact also delivers its required conditions and dependencies. This study examines that obligation in a frozen editorial knowledge package with 110 records and twelve skill roots. Its retained selftest matched 44 ordinary checks: 21 expected acceptances and 23 expected rejections. Two additional structural-boundary counterexamples were accepted as designed and are reported separately. The dependency portion exhaustively examined all 4,096 skill subsets and 24,576 one-step inclusion pairs, with no disagreement against a separate fixed-point implementation. A public replay reproduces that graph portion without distributing the source book. A later metadata calculation shows that the actual editorial request delivered 108 of 110 records, so the observed package does not demonstrate a small context. A separate migration report records preservation of all 110 payloads, and a pinned historical replay reproduced an unresolved preliminary decision. The results establish bounded graph, layout, and integration behavior, not semantic completeness or agent competence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-delivery-problem\">The delivery problem</h2>\n<p>An editorial skill can depend on a rule, a condition under which that rule applies, an acceptance criterion, and a source fragment. Selecting only the top-level skill description can omit the qualification that makes its use appropriate. The consumer follows explicitly required dependencies and treats navigation separately. Avoiding arbitrary navigation is a design motivation; it is not evidence that this package is selective or economical. The retained package was derived from the supplied OCR representation of Ilyakhov and Sarycheva’s <em>Пиши, сокращай</em>, identified in the <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#editorial-input\">editorial-source note</a>.</p>\n<p>This study distinguishes three identities: the artifact’s stable identity, the immutable content revision, and its human-readable path. A renamed folder need not create different knowledge. A changed condition cannot be dismissed as a cosmetic rename. The scientific obligations are developed in <a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">Retrieval and context composition</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">Revision and governance</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"model-and-algorithms\">Model and algorithms</h2>\n<p>Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math> be the fixed finite set of artifact records and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(v)</annotation></semantics></math> the required dependencies of record <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math>. For selected roots <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math>, the intended closure is the least set <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(S)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"></mspace><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nS\\subseteq C(S), \\qquad v\\in C(S)\\Rightarrow D(v)\\subseteq C(S).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Only declared <code>required</code> groups with the <code>all</code> operator participate in this tested profile. An alternative-choice dependency would need another selection policy and is not silently interpreted as <code>all</code>. A dependency’s identity, revision, expected type, and payload hash are checked when loading the original package. Unknown roots and cycles cause explicit rejection in the consumer.</p>\n<p>The production-side algorithm performs depth-first traversal with separate active and completed sets. The reference implementation repeatedly computes</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><munder><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nS_{k+1}=S_k\\cup\\bigcup_{v\\in S_k}D(v)\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>until the set stops growing. On a finite graph, the latter terminates after at most <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> strict increases. The comparison examines agreement, idempotence, monotonicity, and the union equation for this declared dependency relation. These are graph properties; they do not state that the natural-language content has a complete semantic dependency model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exhaustive-finite-checks\">Exhaustive finite checks</h2>\n<p>Twelve selectable skill roots yield <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{12}=4,096</annotation></semantics></math> subsets. The selftest evaluates every subset, compares consumer closure with the separate reference, checks <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(C(S))=C(S)</annotation></semantics></math>, and checks that the closure equals the union of the individual root closures. It also examines every pair formed by adding one absent skill. There are <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>11</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>576</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\cdot2^{11}=24,576</annotation></semantics></math> such pairs; each must satisfy inclusion of the smaller closure in the larger one.</p>\n<p>The retained <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/finite-package-selftest.json\">finite-package selftest</a> records no reference disagreements, idempotence failures, union failures, or monotonicity failures. Union, idempotence and monotonicity are consequences of least closure; the subset sweep mainly checks their implementation on this fixed adjacency relation, rather than supplying thousands of distinct semantic observations. These counts are not independent samples of tasks and cannot be converted into a probability that arbitrary knowledge is complete.</p>\n<p>The historical file contains 46 rows: 44 ordinary checks (21 expected acceptances and 23 expected rejections) and two accepted trust-boundary counterexamples. The latter are excluded from ordinary conformance, following the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/README.md#counting-and-interpretation\">common counting convention</a>. Additional controls include stale review bindings, a changed protected number, missing change grounds, a wrong source relation, an omitted critical qualification, another task’s context, stale competency dependencies, attempted execution without supplied capability and authority, package tampering, and immutable receipt behavior. A synthetic positive review fixture is explicitly labeled as synthetic; it is not evidence that a real reviewer judged the text.</p>\n<p>Two intentionally passing boundaries are especially informative. A structurally valid rule can state a false proposition. An output can contain the exact protected numeric string while negating its meaning. The checker accepted those inputs in the respective structural and literal-preservation checks. Therefore neither structural conformance nor exact string presence is a substitute for semantic review.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-public-reproduction-of-the-graph-result\">A public reproduction of the graph result</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/dependency-graph.json\">dependency graph</a> publishes required adjacency data and opaque vertex identities, with no artifact bodies, source passages, OCR, or book images. It was extracted after the original loader checked the frozen package’s referenced records. Its source graph, index, and loader hashes are recorded in the file. The supplied <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/replay_closure.py\">replay program</a> uses the original traversal rule and a separately written fixed-point comparison in the same publication workflow. This is algorithmic separation, not independent external replication.</p>\n<p>From this study directory, run:</p>\n<pre class=\"bash\"><code>python3 evidence/replay_closure.py</code></pre>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/public-closure-replay.json\">saved public replay</a> reproduces 4,096 subset comparisons and 24,576 inclusion checks, and rejects an unknown root and a constructed cycle. This is a new replay of the extracted historical graph. It does not pretend to rerun all 46 historical checks, validate the source book, or observe a new model response. No additional Python packages or network calls are required.</p>\n<h2 id=\"delivered-record-counts-and-fault-controls\">Delivered record counts and fault controls</h2>\n<p>The least-fixed-point background and the limited role of program mutation are identified in the <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#evaluation-methods\">methodological references</a>. The measurements below concern this retained finite graph and these declared faults.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/checks/check_closure_size_and_mutations.py\">post-review graph check</a> calculates closure sizes from the same 110-vertex, 405-edge graph. Its <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/checks/closure-size-and-mutation-check.json\">saved output</a> includes every skill, both roles, both blueprints and the top-level agent root. Counts describe records, not bytes, tokens or attention cost. Human-readable names come from the retained path migration; the historical IDs remain in the machine result for traceability.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Selected roots</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Delivered records</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Individual skill roots</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">45–57 out of 110 (40.9–51.8%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>All twelve skills together</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">102</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Main editor role</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">105</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Review role</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">57</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Editing blueprint</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">73</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Top-level assistant</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">110</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Editorial pilot: editing blueprint plus both roles</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">108 (98.2%)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The twelve single-skill counts in historical selector order are 45, 57, 45, 49, 53, 51, 51, 47, 49, 55, 53 and 45; the output maps each count to a readable name. The pilot declared five applied skills out of twelve delivered. This is no demonstration that dependency closure produces a compact task context. It establishes delivery of the declared closure; whether dependency granularity and role-level roots can reduce package size remains unmeasured.</p>\n<p>The same new check tests three explicit implementation mutations: omitting cycle rejection, skipping a required child, and treating an unknown root as a leaf. A two-node cycle, a three-node chain and an unknown-root control distinguish all three mutants from the unchanged traversal. These are post-review synthetic fault controls, separate from the historical 44 ordinary checks and two boundary counterexamples. They show sensitivity to those three defects, not general mutation adequacy.</p>\n<pre class=\"bash\"><code>python3 checks/check_closure_size_and_mutations.py</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"readable-layout-migration\">Readable layout migration</h2>\n<p>The later editorial toolkit layout replaced code-like filenames with descriptive paths while retaining the underlying artifact identities and payload revisions. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/layout-preservation-report.json\">preservation report</a> records the migration tool’s assertion of byte equality for all 110 records and unchanged source bytes. Each row stores one hash and <code>bytes_equal: true</code>, not two separately observed old/new hashes. Thus the report alone cannot independently re-establish both byte sequences. Old and new paths are listed explicitly, so a reader can distinguish reported relocation from revision; the original report is preserved without adding invented observations.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/layout-conformance-report.json\">layout conformance report</a> records nine successful tests. Its positive inventory includes 110 document passports, 110 records, twenty operational closures, four constraints, 163 Markdown files, 1,294 local links, and 24 adapters. These are different inventory units and are not added into a single success rate. Other checks examine alias equivalence, forty source locators, and delivery of constraints as critical content.</p>\n<p>Negative controls remove a constraint passport, remove a required constraint from delivery, introduce an invalid slug, break a link while updating its file hash, and create disagreement between the index and registry. Each is rejected by the expected diagnostic family. The updated-hash broken-link control is a useful counterexample: internally consistent checksums do not imply usable navigation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"historical-package-replay\">Historical package replay</h2>\n<p>An integration gate was pinned to the earlier package, not to the later layout. Reusing that gate against changed folder membership would compare different targets. The retained <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/historical-package-replay.json\">historical replay</a> instead used the exact earlier target manifest and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/historical-replay-environment.json\">declared environment</a>. The five command exits matched their expected sequence: 0, 0, 0, 3, 0. The source and temporary replay copy each retained all 239 checked payload files unchanged.</p>\n<p>The exit of 3 belonged to preliminary admission. Its expected decision was <code>needs_resolution</code>, with <code>R_EXPECTED_HEAD</code> as the resolution code. Reproducing that decision means the gate reproduced the specified unresolved condition. It does not mean the package was accepted by a server. The record explicitly reports no new model runs, no new text outcomes, no external semantic review, server admission not evaluated, and production readiness false.</p>\n<p>The recorded environment was Python 3.12.14 on Linux with five exact dependency versions. That environment claim belongs to the pinned historical replay. The later layout also has dependencies with version ranges; matching the earlier environment does not establish an exact later environment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"claim-evidence-and-boundary\">Claim, evidence, and boundary</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-25\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Claim</th>\n<th>Evidence</th>\n<th>Counterexample or failure control</th>\n<th>Limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Required closure follows the declared graph</td>\n<td>Exhaustive subset replay and separately implemented fixed point</td>\n<td>Unknown root, cycle and three new implementation mutants</td>\n<td>Completeness of the declared graph is an external premise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>The migration tool reports unchanged content</td>\n<td>110 equality assertions, with one hash per row</td>\n<td>Layout/index disagreement</td>\n<td>Both old and new byte sequences are not separately pinned in that report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Constraints are actually delivered</td>\n<td>Explicit critical-constraint checks</td>\n<td>Missing passport and missing delivery</td>\n<td>Only the four declared constraints are covered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A matching package hash means the package is usable</td>\n<td>Navigation and preliminary-admission checks</td>\n<td>Broken link with updated hash; unresolved expected head</td>\n<td>Integrity, navigation, and admission remain separate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Exact strings imply preserved meaning</td>\n<td>Deliberately negative meaning with the same numeric string</td>\n<td>The literal check accepts it</td>\n<td>A semantic reviewer remains necessary</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>This study provides a reproducible finite dependency result and inspectable layout and package-replay records. It shows how readable artifact names can coexist with stable identity, and how constraints can remain visible in the delivered closure. It also preserves the failure boundaries that make these results interpretable: a complete graph traversal may follow an incomplete annotation; almost the whole package may be delivered; an intact package may require resolution; a string-preserving edit may change meaning. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial pilot</a> evaluates actual text separately. Definitions are collected in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n<p>The original report identities remain in <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a>. The evidence is mostly English-keyed JSON, with retained Russian descriptions where present; its <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/evidence-language-index.json\">language index</a> identifies individual files. The prose above supplies English summaries of the false-rule and reversed-meaning controls. The newly generated graph measurements and mutation outcomes are identified separately in the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/review-corrections.json\">correction record</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"the-delivery-problem","title":"The delivery problem"},{"level":2,"id":"model-and-algorithms","title":"Model and algorithms"},{"level":2,"id":"exhaustive-finite-checks","title":"Exhaustive finite checks"},{"level":2,"id":"a-public-reproduction-of-the-graph-result","title":"A public reproduction of the graph result"},{"level":2,"id":"delivered-record-counts-and-fault-controls","title":"Delivered record counts and fault controls"},{"level":2,"id":"readable-layout-migration","title":"Readable layout migration"},{"level":2,"id":"historical-package-replay","title":"Historical package replay"},{"level":2,"id":"claim-evidence-and-boundary","title":"Claim, evidence, and boundary"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"dependency-closure-readable-artifact-layouts-and-a-pinned-package-replay","math_count":11,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"0012f1506d1c9e95b29e7f8916952d2278e791bc2a7f7153e8c3c5980928f66a","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"source-transcription-and-uncertainty","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/","title":"Reproducible transcription is not preservation of uncertainty","titleRu":"Reproducible transcription is not preservation of uncertainty","summary":"Retained generated descriptions, lost uncertainty and a reproducible synthetic projection counterexample.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>An audit of three book-export packages found that their readable Markdown could be reconstructed byte for byte while omitting every one of 153 explicitly recorded uncertainty fields attached to generated illustration descriptions. The underlying structured records remained intact. A separate derivative projection preserved all 153 fields and labeled all 917 descriptions as generated and unreviewed; removing only the recorded insertions restored the original readable bytes. This is a completed finite demonstration of a representation defect and its local correction. It does not measure OCR accuracy, validate the descriptions, or establish a benefit of graph storage over a serializer implementing the same rule. This report separates the corpus observations, later export integration, unresolved trace defects, and a small public synthetic illustration of the projection rule.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-source-scope\">Question and source scope</h2>\n<p>The question is whether a transformation delivers the qualifications attached to the content a reader actually receives. Preserving the source archive is necessary for audit, but it does not make an omitted qualification visible in a derived document. The scientific distinction is developed in <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a>.</p>\n<p>The studied corpus covered cooking, marine navigation, and electrical automation. The retained source audit identifies a 1952 edition of <em>Книга о вкусной и здоровой пище</em>, G. I. Fain’s 1977 <em>Навигация, лоция и мореходная астрономия</em>, and V. A. Timofeev’s 1964 <em>Сто схемных задач по автоматике</em>. Dates and page counts describe the observed editions. They do not establish present applicability of advice or authorize practical use.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-20\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-26\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-26\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-26\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Export family</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Page anchors</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Imported generated descriptions</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Nonempty uncertainty fields</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Cooking</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">456</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">374</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">111</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Navigation</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">289</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">319</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Electrical automation</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">154</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">224</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Total</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">899</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">917</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">153</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/structural-corpus-report.json\">structural corpus report</a> supports the counts. Pages, descriptions, and uncertainty fields are different units. They are not 1,969 independently verified observations, and a page anchor is not an expert judgment on a page.</p>\n<p>The structural builder and its validator shared a lexical table detector. Agreement on its table counts is therefore not independent validation of table recognition. The report also records zero image-byte checks in this structural pass; consistency of declared image hashes is a metadata observation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"audit-and-transformation-method\">Audit and transformation method</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/corpus-audit-source.json\">corpus audit</a> examined 39 files, thirteen per export. For 33, the export receipt contained expected sizes and hashes, all of which matched. The remaining README and receipt files were pinned by the observer; no receipt was treated as authenticating its own bytes.</p>\n<p>The observer reconstructed the readable output from the source document, editing ledger, and structured illustration records. The target readable text was used for comparison rather than inserted into the generated output. The reconstruction covered 174 recorded edits, seven italic-wrapper mappings in the navigation export, three caption-to-image-alt copies, and the generated descriptions. All three readable files matched the retained targets byte for byte.</p>\n<p>The mapping was developed after inspecting the available examples. It is a known-data reconstruction, not a blind qualification of the producer. Every replayed editing operation retained its unreviewed status. Reversibility established what the operation did to the representation; it did not establish that the editorial change was linguistically correct.</p>\n<p>The important counterexample appeared in the illustration projection. The readable template emitted description, purpose where present, and recognized image text. It did not emit the separately stored <code>uncertain</code> field. All 153 nonempty values were absent from the corresponding readable descriptions. This finding concerns that exact field and projection. It does not assert that every description lacked all cautious wording, or that the JSON source had been destroyed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"preserving-uncertainty-as-a-testable-obligation\">Preserving uncertainty as a testable obligation</h2>\n<p>For a record <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math> with an explicit qualification <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, the relevant requirement is that the delivered projection contain the qualification, or explicitly declare the permitted loss. Byte identity of a different object does not satisfy that requirement. A projection can be perfectly reproducible and consistently omit the same field.</p>\n<p>The corrective derivative therefore introduced a narrow rule. After each generated description, it inserted the declared generated/unreviewed status. When <code>uncertain</code> was nonempty, it inserted the exact field value with an explicit label. Every insertion carried an input row, record hash, asset identity, anchor, and output range. The source text and original readable files were preserved.</p>\n<p>The transformation profile and expected counts were fixed before generation, after the audit had revealed the defect. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/uncertainty-projection-source.json\">derivative report</a> records the outcome:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Obligation</th>\n<th>Observed result</th>\n<th>Boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Preserve generated/unreviewed status</td>\n<td>917/917 descriptions labeled</td>\n<td>The labels do not independently assess the description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Preserve each nonempty uncertainty value</td>\n<td>153/153 exact values delivered</td>\n<td>These are unreviewed model statements, not calibrated probabilities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Preserve the previous readable representation</td>\n<td>Removing only declared insertions restores all three original files</td>\n<td>The experiment concerns this specified transformation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Keep source inputs unchanged</td>\n<td>All 39 retained source-file hashes unchanged</td>\n<td>Integrity is distinct from source correctness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>No new OCR, subject model call, or human domain review occurred in this transformation. Imported model descriptions were already present in the exports. The zero new-call count must not be read as a claim that the corpus contains no model-produced material.</p>\n<h2 id=\"domain-specific-consequences\">Domain-specific consequences</h2>\n<p>The three domains exposed different dependencies. In a cooking instruction, a qualification can belong to one ingredient branch or a later serving operation. Merging branches or moving an action can alter the procedure even if familiar words survive. In navigation, a table or diagram can depend on a legend, a sign convention, a preceding page, and applicability to a particular instrument. Exact numerical transcription alone does not deliver those conditions. In an electrical diagram, visible connections can support a qualitative description while numerical outputs remain unknown without input values and operating context.</p>\n<p>These examples motivated engineering obligations; they are not new cooking, navigation, or circuit-design recommendations. The later <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection study</a> evaluates selected tasks involving such dependencies. Neither study treats a historical book’s age as an automatic proof of error, or as an adequate reason to authorize contemporary action.</p>\n<h2 id=\"trace-defects-and-later-integration\">Trace defects and later integration</h2>\n<p>The audit also found unresolved addressability. Of 5,538 quality flags, the observed mapping addressed 5,518; sixteen table or formula offsets and four orphan flags remained unresolved. A disappearing flag was not counted as repaired OCR. It could reflect changed text, changed diagnostics, or missing provenance.</p>\n<p>A separate raw-to-document analysis reproduced all recorded correction rows in the cooking and automation exports. For navigation, 53 of 58 rows were reproduced, leaving five rows in two blocks without a unique raw-serialization binding. Those rows were not labeled five OCR errors. The evidence was insufficient to reconstruct the producer’s transformation, which is a different finding.</p>\n<p>The twenty unresolved quality flags plus these five correction rows are the 25 unresolved coordinate traces named in the later integration summary. This reconciles its inventory; it does not turn two different kinds of trace defect into a single OCR error rate.</p>\n<p>Later local integration processed the same 917 description records in two runs. Each recorded 19,305 satisfied field checks and no violated field checks, preserving 153 nonempty and 764 empty uncertainty values. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/local-export-integration-report.json\">integration summary</a> also preserves the initial control result of fifteen expected outcomes out of sixteen. One authored control supplied pretty JSON where JSONL was required, causing an earlier-than-intended rejection. Its separately retained correction exercised the intended missing-uncertainty case. A post-replacement control distinguished visibility of a validated new snapshot from unconfirmed durability.</p>\n<p>This integration occurred after export. It was not installed in the upstream OCR producer. Physical power-loss durability was not tested. Seven truncated descriptions formed a targeted review queue; the other nonempty uncertainty fields were not automatically equated with defective or truncated outputs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"public-illustration-and-retained-evidence\">Public illustration and retained evidence</h2>\n<p>The source books, OCR transcriptions, extracted illustrations, and full generated book descriptions are not redistributed here. Project-authored reports and aggregate metadata are provided under readable filenames, with exact JSON identities and separate rendered-prose provenance in <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a>. Full replay of the original corpus audit requires the specified source packages and original runner closure; the reports alone do not supply them.</p>\n<p>A <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/checks/demonstrate_uncertainty_projection.py\">corrected public illustration</a> uses the four invented records from the preserved edition-1.0 program, two with nonempty uncertainty. It compares the base serializer against a <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/checks/synthetic-base.txt\">separately stored literal target</a>, rather than comparing an expression with itself. A changed-description mutation fails that comparison. The projection preserves both fields, marks all four records, records insertion ranges and rejects an absent uncertainty field; removing exactly those insertions restores the stored target, while damaging an insertion range breaks restoration. These are finite checks of the invented projection rule, not a reconstruction of the historical source-plus-editing-ledger pipeline. Run:</p>\n<pre class=\"bash\"><code>python3 checks/demonstrate_uncertainty_projection.py</code></pre>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/checks/uncertainty-projection-check.json\">saved corrected demonstration</a> is a new synthetic illustration. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/replay_uncertainty_projection.py\">earlier program</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/public-projection-demonstration.json\">earlier result</a> are retained unchanged; their <code>base_reconstruction_exact</code> predicate compared the same construction with itself and supplied no independent reconstruction evidence. The corrected harness removes that inference. Its four records are not substituted for the 917 historical descriptions, and its checks are not added to historical corpus totals. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/review-corrections.json\">correction record</a> binds the new fixture, code and result separately.</p>\n<p>The corpus audit and derivative report are Russian prose views. The methods, missing-field finding, coordinate counts and qualification limits above are English summaries of their relevant passages; the raw sources have not been translated or rewritten in place. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/evidence-language-index.json\">evidence language index</a> records the language of every file.</p>\n<h2 id=\"limits-and-conclusion\">Limits and conclusion</h2>\n<p>The corpus’s character error rate (CER) and word error rate (WER) remained unmeasured. With a declared alignment and reference unitization, each counts substitutions, deletions and insertions divided by reference characters or words; the historical audit supplied neither a complete ground-truth transcript nor that measurement protocol. <code>null</code> therefore does not mean zero error. The <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#evaluation-methods\">evaluation-method note</a> identifies the scoring background.</p>\n<p>Image checks belong to different stages and denominators. The earlier reader source reports three selected PNGs checked out of 918 described images. The later 917-description corpus/structural audit checked no image bytes. A separate addressability experiment checked three selected JPEGs, as its <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/addressability-study-source.json\">retained source</a> states. None is a byte audit of the whole image collection, and the earlier three-of-918 observation cannot be reassigned to the later 917-record pass. Neither every OCR passage nor the calibration of model uncertainty was assessed. Structural graph construction created addressable source structure; it did not thereby extract a verified semantic knowledge graph.</p>\n<p>The established engineering result is precise: the chosen readable template lost an explicitly represented qualification field; a declared and reversible projection preserved it on the inspected finite corpus. A simple serializer can implement that rule. The evidence supports an explicit preservation obligation and supplies a concrete counterexample to equating hashes with meaning preservation. It does not establish superiority of the larger architecture. Related terms are defined in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-source-scope","title":"Question and source scope"},{"level":2,"id":"audit-and-transformation-method","title":"Audit and transformation method"},{"level":2,"id":"preserving-uncertainty-as-a-testable-obligation","title":"Preserving uncertainty as a testable obligation"},{"level":2,"id":"domain-specific-consequences","title":"Domain-specific consequences"},{"level":2,"id":"trace-defects-and-later-integration","title":"Trace defects and later integration"},{"level":2,"id":"public-illustration-and-retained-evidence","title":"Public illustration and retained evidence"},{"level":2,"id":"limits-and-conclusion","title":"Limits and conclusion"}],"title_id":"reproducible-transcription-is-not-preservation-of-uncertainty","math_count":2,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"6119790ada666b76782c0952e12e47799c51893a85f71a7e36f72d9ac44f3fe0","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"executable-projections-and-revision","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/","title":"Executable projections, addressable revisions, and the limits of graph minimality","titleRu":"Executable projections, addressable revisions, and the limits of graph minimality","summary":"Finite consumer checks, paired agent tasks, addressed revision and the limits of graph minimality.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>This report synthesizes three completed development stages: packet reading, targeted revision, and executable partial extraction. The first reader stage recorded twelve criterion violations and a reconstruction-status distribution of ten complete, 38 partial, three rejected and six unsupported main-layer results. A later development and regression boundary suite satisfied 772 declared checks across 38 calls under a revised reader and rubric. A targeted revision comparison produced equal outcomes on ten case templates instantiated for three books, yielding thirty graph-versus-row pairs. A consumer checked on 102 finite controls then extracted 24 prescribed projections from ten fresh agent responses organized as five related case pairs. Both representations succeeded on all five primary cases under a coarse rubric that reached its ceiling on the rated projections. These bounded results do not establish graph superiority or general semantic completeness. A post-hoc finding shows that a minimal set of reachable entities can still contain unnecessary claims inside entity text.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-design-progression\">Question and design progression</h2>\n<p>The engineering question is whether a consumer can retrieve a useful partial result without silently dropping its conditions, treating a new assessment as original provenance, or resolving an ambiguous identity by arbitrary order. The scientific arguments are developed in <a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">Retrieval and context composition</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">Revision and governance</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a>.</p>\n<p>The stages changed interfaces, mappings, cases, and sometimes evaluation rules. They are therefore reported as related development studies. Their successive scores do not constitute a controlled estimate of improvement caused by one isolated intervention. Historical results remain unchanged.</p>\n<h2 id=\"initial-reader-four-distinct-failure-mechanisms\">Initial reader: four distinct failure mechanisms</h2>\n<p>The retained <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/historical-reader-study-source.json\">initial reader study</a> records 57 main-layer calls with 786 satisfied and twelve violated criteria. Their reconstruction statuses were ten complete, 38 partial, three rejected and six unsupported. These status labels are not interchangeable with satisfaction of all obligations. The public summary does not bind every main-layer status to a predeclared expected status, so it cannot establish which of the 38 partial and nine rejected/unsupported outcomes were planned rather than unexpected. That distinction remains unavailable at the case level.</p>\n<p>The separate <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/historical-ocr-reader-report.json\">book-layer report</a> records eighteen calls with 252 satisfied and zero violated criteria: six base cases were complete and twelve deliberately damaged or incomplete controls were partial by design. Calls, reconstruction statuses and nested criteria are separate denominators; the criterion counts are not 1,050 independent tasks or an accuracy estimate. There were no native-model subject trials or human-subject trials in that stage.</p>\n<p>The twelve violations fell into four groups. Five concerned the difference between an address resolving to a placeholder and content actually being available. Three concerned inconsistent derivation of revision identity between records and relation endpoints. Two concerned diagnostic pointers into a packet versus its transport envelope. Two concerned choosing the first candidate despite conflicting records.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-30\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-40\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-30\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Original failure group</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Recorded violations</th>\n<th>Subsequent correction scope</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Placeholder address versus available content</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">5</td>\n<td>Contract/rubric distinction and reader change: separate address resolution from content state and availability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Inconsistent inferred revisions</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3</td>\n<td>Reader normalization and an explicit inference rule applied consistently to records and endpoints</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ambiguous pointer coordinate space</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td>Diagnostic output and contract clarification: identify packet/envelope space and representation hash</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>First-candidate choice under conflict</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td>Resolver behavior and output contract: retain the candidate set and explicit ambiguity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is an English summary of the original report’s four-group analysis. It does not allocate each later success causally to code or rubric alone; both changed, particularly for the five placeholder cases.</p>\n<p>This analysis matters because the violations do not all mean lost content. In the pointer cases, the invalid outer hash was detected, but the diagnostic coordinate system was ambiguous. In the placeholder cases, relationships were retained while the meaning of <code>resolved</code> differed between reader and oracle. Revising the explanation of these mechanisms does not erase the recorded violations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"revised-reader-boundaries\">Revised reader boundaries</h2>\n<p>The later reader separated address resolution, content state, content availability, applicability, and authority. An empty supplied string, zero, or <code>false</code> was not treated as an absent field. A source-declared revision remained distinct from an inferred revision and its inference rule. Diagnostic pointers named their coordinate space and representation hash. Conflicting candidates remained an explicit set, so record order could not silently create unique identity.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/reader-boundary-report.json\">boundary report</a> contains 38 development calls: 26 counterexamples prepared alongside the revision and twelve regression cases from the earlier failures. All satisfied the declared checks, totaling 772 satisfied and zero violated criteria. A paired permutation control preserved ambiguity and the candidate-value hash set. The revised mapping and rubric differed from the earlier ones, so this was neither a held-out acceptance test nor an instrument-only retest. “Qualified” in the historical record means that these finite declared boundaries were checked; it does not establish correctness on arbitrary future packets.</p>\n<h2 id=\"targeted-revision-and-a-strong-alternative\">Targeted revision and a strong alternative</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/targeted-revision-comparison.json\">targeted revision report</a> compares an explicit dependency graph with flat rows that contain the same material dependencies and conditions. Both use a shared evidence validator and serializer. The experimental difference is how affected records are selected. This is a deliberately strong simple alternative, not a form deprived of relevant information.</p>\n<p>Ten distinct templates were instantiated for three books, producing thirty paired cases and sixty executions. All sixty produced the expected outcome; both representations agreed on all three book instances of every template. The shared validator and serializer restrict this comparison to the record-selection step and help explain equality once both selectors choose the same records. Thirty pairs are not thirty independently designed mechanisms.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Template family</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Paired book instances with equal outcomes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>No source or contract change</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Valid representation map</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Incorrect representation map</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Missing representation map</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Empty representation map</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Changed source text</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Changed image bytes</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Required uncertainty preserved</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Required uncertainty omitted</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Required uncertainty unknown</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">3/3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The outputs included 36 unresolved-mapping assessments and twelve unknown-contract assessments because those were declared outcomes of particular controls. These states are visible results, not failures concealed behind a pass total. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/addressability-study-source.json\">addressability study source</a> describes the design and shared machinery; the machine comparison retains the per-template and per-book identities.</p>\n<p>Source changes created separate candidate revisions and retained prior records and history. New assessments did not invent current-use authority. Timing was a single local invocation including shared decoding, not model latency or product cost. This comparison establishes no advantage for graph representation on the tested scope.</p>\n<h2 id=\"executable-projection-contract\">Executable projection contract</h2>\n<p>The subsequent finite consumer accepts two representations of the same contract: nodes and typed edges, or rows with typed dependencies. It normalizes both and computes required closure from named roots. Relations distinguish content requirements, qualifications, and assessment requirements. Source attachments also distinguish content support from assessment basis. Evidence used to evaluate a card must not silently become the source from which the card’s content was derived.</p>\n<p>Typed identities carry record and revision identifiers, the contract of creation, and prior-version references. Assessments separately identify their exact target, assessment contract, and verdict. A request can include historical revisions in a separate history layer. Historical payloads and outgoing relations are preserved rather than edited to make an old record satisfy new rules.</p>\n<p>Missing addresses, invalid sources, malformed input, or a closure exceeding the declared entity budget yield a blocked result with no usable partial output under this profile. An explicitly represented semantic unknown can remain useful content; it is not itself a schema failure. Minimality is relative to the declared relationships. The consumer cannot infer an omitted qualification merely because the author should have included it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"consumer-qualification-before-the-new-responses\">Consumer qualification before the new responses</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/consumer-qualification-report.json\">qualification report</a> records 51 control families in two forms: 102 executions, comprising 24 expected acceptances and 78 expected blocks. All matched, and normalized results agreed in all 51 pairs. A separately written verifier, which did not import the consumer, computed expected structural closure and rejected twelve deliberately damaged outputs. The verifier and consumer were developed within the same session and agent family. Separation of code paths is useful, but does not establish external independence or exclude shared assumptions.</p>\n<p>Three pre-freeze corrections are disclosed. Invalid JSON types gained explicit guards; Python’s equality between booleans and numbers could no longer mask a change in preserved JSON type; and normalized outputs explicitly retained relations and typed links. These changes preceded the new subject responses. The finite controls do not prove the consumer for every possible input or arbitrary non-JSON Python object.</p>\n<h2 id=\"five-agent-case-pairs\">Five agent case pairs</h2>\n<p>The next stage fixed inputs, requests, reference material, rubric, implementations, and run order before obtaining ten responses in fresh agent contexts. There was one response per case and representation, with no corrective subject retry. The source-based tasks were deliberately related and comprise four source families, not ten independent samples.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Case</th>\n<th>Required preservation in a partial extraction</th>\n<th>Observed result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Unreviewed navigation description</td>\n<td>Useful description together with uncertain notation and missing context</td>\n<td>Both main projections retained the uncertainty and source limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Experimental cooking revision</td>\n<td>A changed branch, its prior value, and a distinct independent branch</td>\n<td>Prescribed current, historical, and separate-branch extractions retained their respective bindings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Changed assessment contract</td>\n<td>Original creation contract and prior verdict, new assessment, and a new qualified revision</td>\n<td>Old provenance remained intact; new assessment and revision were separate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Navigation table</td>\n<td>Selected values, interpolation basis, sign, and unknown applicability</td>\n<td>Both forms retained the specified values and grounds in all prescribed views</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Electrical diagram image</td>\n<td>Visible block and connection structure without invented numerical signals</td>\n<td>Both forms retained qualitative observations and limits of numerical inference</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/agent-projection-results.json\">measured-results record</a> reports 24 available projections checked by the separately written verifier described above. Each received the rubric’s highest fidelity and task-correctness score: both axes were saturated across all 24 projections. Differences smaller than one score step could not be resolved by that instrument. All prescribed views succeeded in five of five cases for each form, with equal primary outcomes in all five pairs. The ten raw responses were different; equal outcome scores do not imply identical representations or equal quality under a more discriminating assessment.</p>\n<p>The semantic reviewer saw the representation, participated in design review, and belonged to the same agent family. No human expert review occurred. There were 84 nested critical-obligation judgments; these are not 84 independent experiments. Exact runtime model revision, decoding, seed, token use, latency, and compute cost were unavailable. Saved outputs were copied from visible final answers rather than captured as native API streams. Therefore the study supplies an observed agent case series, not a reproducible native-model benchmark.</p>\n<h2 id=\"counterexample-to-claim-level-minimality\">Counterexample to claim-level minimality</h2>\n<p>After evaluation, two row-form projections were found to include extra numerical statements inside a shared applicability qualification. An extraction of one selected table value consequently carried another value even though the latter’s dedicated calculation node was not selected. The extra values were correct, and the frozen rubric allowed additional correct information. The original scores were retained; the observation was recorded separately.</p>\n<p>This is a counterexample to inferring claim-level minimality from entity-level closure. A minimal reachable node set does not imply that every sentence inside those nodes is necessary, or that every incidental claim has all of its own grounds in the projection. A later contract could give individual claims addresses or constrain the scope of shared qualifications. That refinement is an implication of the counterexample, not a property already demonstrated by this run.</p>\n<h2 id=\"evidence-and-reproduction-boundaries\">Evidence and reproduction boundaries</h2>\n<p>The exact machine summaries and retained source views accompany this study; <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a> distinguishes original JSON bytes from reconstructed text views. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/offline-projection-replay-report.json\">offline replay report</a> records one historical extracted-bundle replay in which all 24 projections matched and passed a separate verifier. It explicitly reports no new subject-model calls, OCR, native trials, or semantic assessments. That replay is deterministic processing of saved outputs.</p>\n<p>This public evidence folder does not contain the complete original source packages, candidate responses and frozen requests, or the complete native runtime. In particular, the frozen synthetic control bundle, original consumer, separate verifier and mutation artifacts are not supplied. Their absence is an unresolved recovery and publication gap for project-authored material, not a claim that book rights prohibit publishing synthetic controls or software. Their bound hashes and byte lengths are listed in the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/historical-recovery-gaps.json\">recovery inventory</a>; recreating similar code would produce a new experiment, not restore the historical one. Readers can inspect retained counts and limitations, but cannot infer a complete replay environment from hashes. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">public closure study</a> provides a separately executable graph mechanism using metadata-only inputs.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/evidence/projection-study-source.json\">projection study source</a>, addressability source and initial reader source are Russian prose views. The design, four failure mechanisms, status distribution and limitations above are English summaries of their relevant passages; they are not a full independently reviewed translation. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/evidence-language-index.json\">evidence language index</a> identifies every file. Agent-judging limitations are related to the <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#evaluation-methods\">evaluation-method references</a>; this local rubric does not reproduce their benchmarks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>The studies demonstrate finite preservation of explicitly declared content, qualifications, assessment roles, and history through selected retrieval and revision operations. They also identify useful counterarguments: simple representations can reach the same outcomes, a corrected evaluator changes the interpretation of scores, and graph minimality does not bound the number of claims inside text. These results support the explicit obligations of the contract while keeping comparative effectiveness, arbitrary-root completeness, domain correctness, and product authorization outside the established conclusion. Definitions are consolidated in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-design-progression","title":"Question and design progression"},{"level":2,"id":"initial-reader-four-distinct-failure-mechanisms","title":"Initial reader: four distinct failure mechanisms"},{"level":2,"id":"revised-reader-boundaries","title":"Revised reader boundaries"},{"level":2,"id":"targeted-revision-and-a-strong-alternative","title":"Targeted revision and a strong alternative"},{"level":2,"id":"executable-projection-contract","title":"Executable projection contract"},{"level":2,"id":"consumer-qualification-before-the-new-responses","title":"Consumer qualification before the new responses"},{"level":2,"id":"five-agent-case-pairs","title":"Five agent case pairs"},{"level":2,"id":"counterexample-to-claim-level-minimality","title":"Counterexample to claim-level minimality"},{"level":2,"id":"evidence-and-reproduction-boundaries","title":"Evidence and reproduction boundaries"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"executable-projections-addressable-revisions-and-the-limits-of-graph-minimality","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"6242027572df695e1907e77d55588ea3b3a64741407b6922b87f0612de956ed8","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"reuse-admission-and-cost-controls","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/","title":"Controlled reuse: admission counterexamples and the cost of a cache hit","titleRu":"Controlled reuse: admission counterexamples and the cost of a cache hit","summary":"Executable admission counterexamples and an analytical break-even calculation with explicit costs.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Reusing a prepared knowledge artifact can avoid work, but a matching identifier or a high hit rate does not establish that reuse is appropriate or economical. This analytical study develops explicit admission conditions and counterexamples involving missing dependencies, changed search scope, current authority, time boundaries, and overlapping cost claims. A homogeneous cost model gives a conditional break-even identity. Public code supplies worked arithmetic examples and a finite synthetic checker with all nine named gates, including missing-gate counterexamples. It does not report measured model savings, production throughput, or a deployed authorization system. The contribution is an inspectable set of obligations and counterexamples, with explicit boundaries between analytical consequences and empirical claims requiring observations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-objects\">Question and objects</h2>\n<p>The question is when a retained artifact can be used for a new request without losing its requirements, overstating its evidence, or hiding the cost of validation. The scientific models are developed in <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">Controlled knowledge reuse</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">Knowledge utility</a>. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/evidence/reuse-properties-source.json\">retained conditional-properties source</a> records the original assumptions, arguments, and counterexamples behind this analysis.</p>\n<p>A build can be represented as</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>Compile</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\n\\operatorname{Compile}(b,q,S)\\rightarrow(a,D,E,L),\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math> is a recipe, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> the request, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> the declared source snapshot, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> the prepared revision, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math> its material dependencies, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math> evidence, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> coverage, loss, and stopping information. A later request <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> requires a separate reuse decision under a current policy and context. Similarity may retrieve a candidate; it does not establish suitability.</p>\n<p>Build dependencies explain how an artifact was obtained. Admission dependencies govern whether it can now be supplied or applied. A changed access decision can prohibit delivery without changing the artifact’s text. A changed generator version need not require rebuilding every unaffected artifact. These decisions require explicit relevance rules rather than one undifferentiated freshness flag.</p>\n<h2 id=\"admission-before-optimization\">Admission before optimization</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">status model</a> distinguishes <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>unknown</code> and <code>not_applicable</code>. A mandatory obligation is discharged by <code>pass</code>, or by <code>not_applicable</code> together with an identified rule and evidence showing that exclusion is valid for the exact scope. Unsupported exclusion, missing observations and applicable unknowns do not discharge it. The nine gate classes used here are integrity, result class, task requirements, material dependency equivalence, current access, temporal policy, permitted transformations, modality delivery, and budget. The finite public checker deliberately makes all nine applicable; it therefore requires an explicit <code>pass</code> for every one and rejects <code>not_applicable</code> without a witness. It does not infer applicability from unrestricted text.</p>\n<p>Partial content can be admissible when the request explicitly permits it and the gaps remain visible. That does not make the conditions attached to the selected content optional. High predicted utility also cannot turn a failed mandatory condition into permission. Economic optimization occurs within the set of admissible alternatives.</p>\n<p>Reuse strategies include exact reuse, instantiation of a template, reuse after revalidation, rebuilding, bypassing the cache, and refusing reuse. These strategies are not a second operation-outcome vocabulary: the operation still reports <code>success</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>refused</code>, <code>failed</code> or <code>unresolved</code> under its contract. A decision to revalidate is not itself permission to serve before the necessary check completes. An instantiated result has its own identity and must be checked after substitution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"counterexamples-that-determine-the-design\">Counterexamples that determine the design</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Proposed shortcut</th>\n<th>Counterexample</th>\n<th>Required distinction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Listed dependencies are unchanged, so the result is preserved</td>\n<td>An omitted legend changes the meaning of an unchanged number or symbol</td>\n<td>Dependency completeness is a substantive premise, not a manifest label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A negative answer has an empty dependency set</td>\n<td>A new matching item is added after an exhaustive search returned none</td>\n<td>Absence, count, and exact top-k depend on search-domain membership and policy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A previously accepted payload can be sent to another requester</td>\n<td>The requester, tenant, resource scope, or permission has changed</td>\n<td>Content identity and current delivery authority are separate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>A live cache lifetime overrides an unavailable authority service</td>\n<td>A permission was revoked while the authority result became unknown</td>\n<td>Unknown current authority does not inherit an old success by default</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>A cached completion message completes another task</td>\n<td>The message describes an earlier attempt and external effect</td>\n<td>Retained content is not a new execution event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Frequent reads prove continuing validity</td>\n<td>Repeated hits add no source assessment</td>\n<td>Access statistics and validation time are separate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The preservation claim is necessarily conditional. Suppose a sound prior assessment established properties <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_1,\\ldots,P_m</annotation></semantics></math> of artifact <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> for request <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> in state <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0</annotation></semantics></math>. Their complete dependency and interpretation interface must remain equivalent in <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_1</annotation></semantics></math>, and each property’s request dependence must factor through declared components on which <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math> and the new request <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> agree. For every output <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> allowed by the transformation relation <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(a,a&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>, the transformation must preserve those properties. These premises establish their retention, but admission for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math> additionally requires that the retained properties entail discharge of every mandatory requirement of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q&#39;</annotation></semantics></math>, with valid inapplicability witnesses where used, and that current authorization and delivery obligations hold. An old verified property set cannot justify an uncovered new requirement. Omitting an actual dependency or assuming that the old assessment was sound invalidates a premise; a Boolean field called <code>complete_manifest</code> establishes neither premise. The <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled-reuse theorem</a> states the corresponding conditional argument.</p>\n<p>Suitability is also directional. A complete nonempty set may support an existential answer through an allowed projection. One known member does not establish the whole set, its count, or absence of other members. A symmetric similarity score cannot supply this missing implication.</p>\n<h2 id=\"time-concurrency-and-state\">Time, concurrency, and state</h2>\n<p>Artifact lifecycle, residency, freshness, and current eligibility are different assessments. A valid retained revision may be cold or temporarily unavailable. A resident fresh artifact may be inaccessible to this requester. A historical answer can deliberately use a pinned old revision while still respecting current access restrictions.</p>\n<p>A strict-current policy needs a declared point at which relevant source heads and authority are considered current. A bounded-stale policy needs an explicit allowed scope and age. Neither policy may silently ignore known withdrawal, corruption, incompatibility, or a material unresolved gap. A cache hit updates usage statistics; it does not reset the time when an underlying source was checked. <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#http-caching\">HTTP cache freshness</a> and <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#stale-http-responses\">stale-response extensions</a> supply limited technical analogies, not permission to bypass knowledge or authority checks.</p>\n<p>There is also a gap between checking and delivery. A permission or dependency can change after validation but before the result is supplied. An implementation must specify its consistency boundary, for example through a checked epoch, lease, or transactional publication protocol. Those mechanisms need their own concurrency tests. The analytical obligation here does not claim that a distributed service has already implemented them correctly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"an-exact-cost-model\">An exact cost model</h2>\n<p>Consider homogeneous requests with equal admissible quality and non-overlapping accounting categories over a fixed accounting horizon. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of requests, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G</annotation></semantics></math> the cost of the cold admissible path, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_v</annotation></semantics></math> additional candidate-search and admission cost per request, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_r</annotation></semantics></math> the remaining cost of a usable reuse, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho</annotation></semantics></math> the fraction of usable reuses. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">MG</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math> be preparation, additional background builds, invalidation, and storage costs. All costs use one declared unit and are nonnegative, with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq\\rho\\leq1</annotation></semantics></math>. The retained edition-1.0 examples used <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V,R,h</annotation></semantics></math> for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_v,c_r,\\rho</annotation></semantics></math>; their original data and code remain unchanged. Then</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC_0=NG,\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC_1=B+Nc_v+N\\rho c_r+N(1-\\rho)G+MG+I+S.\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Writing <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(N)=B+MG+I+S</annotation></semantics></math> for overhead, which may depend on request count over the declared horizon, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=\\rho(G-c_r)-c_v</annotation></semantics></math>, subtraction gives</p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\nC_0-C_1=Nd-F(N).\n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n<p>Strict savings require <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Nd&gt;F(N)</annotation></semantics></math>. The closed threshold <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌊</mo><mi>F</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lfloor F/d\\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math> applies only when overhead <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math>, expected hit fraction <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, and unit costs are independent of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math> over that horizon, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\leq0</annotation></semantics></math>, nonnegative overhead cannot be recovered through more requests in this homogeneous model. For actual observations, use the integer hit count <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_N</annotation></semantics></math>: savings are <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_N(G-c_r)-Nc_v-F(N)</annotation></semantics></math>. A fractional expected hit count is not an observed event count. If storage grows as <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(N)=sN</annotation></semantics></math>, fixed other overhead <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_0</annotation></semantics></math> is recovered only when <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d&gt;s</annotation></semantics></math>, with threshold <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌊</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lfloor F_0/(d-s)\\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math>; there is no such threshold when <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\leq s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Invented scenario</th>\n<th>Parameters</th>\n<th>Algebraic result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>High hit rate with expensive validation</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100,\\rho=0.9,G=10,c_r=1,c_v=9,F=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Cold cost 1,000; reuse cost 1,090; loss 90</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Positive margin with preparation cost</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100,\\rho=0.9,G=10,c_r=1,c_v=1,F=100</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Cold cost 1,000; reuse cost 390; savings 610</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Zero margin</td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100,\\rho=0.5,G=10,c_r=2,c_v=4,F=100</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>The fixed loss of 100 remains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Storage increases with request count</td>\n<td>Same positive-margin parameters, but <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(N)=100+8N</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>At <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100</annotation></semantics></math>, reuse cost 1,190; loss 190; no profitable count under these constant rates</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are exact arithmetic examples in invented cost units. They are not prices, measured token bills, or estimates of the project’s performance. Heterogeneous requests require an appropriately stratified sum rather than substitution of incompatible averages.</p>\n<h2 id=\"utility-and-double-counting\">Utility and double counting</h2>\n<p>Compute savings alone are not net task value. Quality loss, additional review, delayed outcomes, and downstream harm require an explicit utility policy and evidence. If their values are unknown, assigning zero would create an unsupported benefit. If a route violates a hard constraint, a favorable monetary estimate does not admit it.</p>\n<p>Costs also need a shared baseline. Suppose a cold operation costs ten units, provider caching reduces it to six, and using a prepared artifact reduces it to one. The two separately calculated savings, four and nine, cannot be added into thirteen units saved from a ten-unit operation. If the artifact hit eliminated the model call, a provider-cache saving on that nonexistent call is not another observed benefit. Resource events, retries, storage, and shared infrastructure need non-overlapping accounting or an explicit allocation rule.</p>\n<p>No inference about causal contribution follows merely from counting citations, delivered artifacts, or completed tasks. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial pilot</a>, for example, observed usable outputs under both conditions and a tie. That result cannot be turned into measured economic benefit without resource and outcome observations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"public-examples-and-claim-limits\">Public examples and claim limits</h2>\n<p>The corrected <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/checks/check_admission_and_cost.py\">counterexample program</a> names all nine mandatory gate classes. It checks a fully passing positive control and 27 separate missing, failed or unknown gate mutations, plus malformed inputs including a one-gate-only map and an unjustified <code>not_applicable</code>. The supplied states remain local inputs; this does not authenticate an authority service or establish real-world applicability. The exact rational cost calculations are worked examples of the identity, including linearly growing storage overhead, not empirical validation of economic assumptions. The small dependency and absence models illustrate counterexamples on invented inputs.</p>\n<pre class=\"bash\"><code>python3 checks/check_admission_and_cost.py</code></pre>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/checks/admission-and-cost-check.json\">saved corrected result</a> is a new deterministic demonstration. The earlier <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/evidence/replay_reuse_counterexamples.py\">edition-1.0 program</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/evidence/public-reuse-counterexamples.json\">result</a> remain byte-identical as historical evidence: that program tested only three supplied gates and erroneously accepted a nonempty subset when a required gate was omitted. Its six fail/unknown controls do not establish the missing-gate obligation. The corrected program supersedes that local gate demonstration without changing any historical model outcome. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/review-corrections.json\">correction record</a> identifies the new files separately.</p>\n<p>The original property source is a Russian rendered-text view with separately recorded source and representation hashes in <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a>. The admission conditions, dependency counterexamples and temporal limits above are English summaries and the current argument; the strengthened new-request-coverage premise is an explicit correction, not a claim that the old source already proved it. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/evidence-language-index.json\">evidence language index</a> records individual file languages. None of the public programs reruns an earlier production system.</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Controlled reuse requires two decisions: whether a route satisfies its obligations, and whether it is preferable among admissible routes. The logical counterexamples identify inputs that cannot be omitted from the first decision. The cost identity specifies what must be measured for the second. The completed analytical result is useful precisely because it permits a negative answer: a cache can be correct but uneconomical, economical under invented assumptions but unmeasured in practice, or superficially efficient while inadmissible. Terminology is consolidated in the <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-objects","title":"Question and objects"},{"level":2,"id":"admission-before-optimization","title":"Admission before optimization"},{"level":2,"id":"counterexamples-that-determine-the-design","title":"Counterexamples that determine the design"},{"level":2,"id":"time-concurrency-and-state","title":"Time, concurrency, and state"},{"level":2,"id":"an-exact-cost-model","title":"An exact cost model"},{"level":2,"id":"utility-and-double-counting","title":"Utility and double counting"},{"level":2,"id":"public-examples-and-claim-limits","title":"Public examples and claim limits"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"controlled-reuse-admission-counterexamples-and-the-cost-of-a-cache-hit","math_count":56,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"fa5494d6609d15a61ce071eb39c3a87fa0b982c61f756f3308276c127ae8484a","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"editorial-skill-pilot","kind":"study","type":"study","path":"engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/STUDY.md","route":"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/","title":"Editing a scientific section with a knowledge toolkit: a masked single-section comparison","titleRu":"Editing a scientific section with a knowledge toolkit: a masked single-section comparison","summary":"One preregistered editorial comparison, exact initial outputs, masked review and an observed tie.","language":"en","html":"<h2 id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h2>\n<p>Two agents edited the same 804-word Russian scientific section. Both received the common brief, shared context, source section, protected-claim registry and scoring rubric. The assisted condition additionally used an editorial toolkit containing skills and a process blueprint. The shared checklist contained nineteen protected claims, including eight conceptual distinctions, and exact literals. A separate agent reviewed anonymized outputs before their conditions were revealed. Both complied with the supplied claim checklist, passed the editorial acceptance gate, and received eight points out of ten; preference was a tie. A technical audit found a mechanically consistent trace from assisted changes to declared rules and source ranges, with limitations in how specifically some rules supported the edit. The pilot demonstrates an operational editing and traceability process on one real section. It does not establish unaided meaning preservation, better writing than the shared structured brief, a causal effect of individual skills, or scientific validity of the text.</p>\n<h2 id=\"question-and-scope\">Question and scope</h2>\n<p>The user’s practical question was whether newly created writing skills and a blueprint could guide an agent through a real publication task while preserving meaning. The selected unit was the complete working-definitions section of the project introduction: 804 words by whitespace counting, four subsections, and a table with eight distinctions. The input was Russian, so this pilot does not evaluate English translation quality. The toolkit was derived from Maxim Ilyakhov and Lyudmila Sarycheva’s <em>Пиши, сокращай</em>; the <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#editorial-input\">bibliographic and input-identity note</a> identifies the work and the supplied representation without redistributing it.</p>\n<p>The trial separated editorial quality from truth of the underlying scientific content. An editor could improve references, paragraph structure, and sentence clarity without establishing the correctness of a formal claim. The relevant scientific framing appears in <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a>, and <a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">Knowledge utility</a>.</p>\n<p>The exact <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/source-section.txt\">source section</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/source-context.txt\">shared context</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/common-editorial-brief.txt\">common brief</a>, and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/original-pilot-protocol.txt\">original protocol</a> are retained as historical text records. Their internal paths and terminology describe the original experiment; the present publication’s navigation is provided by this report.</p>\n<h2 id=\"design-and-preregistration\">Design and preregistration</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/editorial-preregistration.json\">preregistration</a> predates the editor agents. It pins the inputs, identifies two conditions, specifies one first completed response per condition, and requires review with condition labels hidden until the written evaluation. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/protected-claims.json\">protected-claim registry</a> contains nineteen claims, protected headings and anchors, link targets, numbers, versions, and 37 exact literals. First-response status is the editor’s declaration: the final bytes are hash-pinned, but no independent complete event log verifies that status, rereading before registration, or the absence of edits after self-review.</p>\n<p>Both agents received the exact section, common brief, shared context and protected-claim registry in fresh task contexts. The brief disclosed the full scoring procedure, including five axes and the meanings of scores 0, 1 and 2. The baseline edited without reading the toolkit; the assisted condition additionally used the readable-layout package, five declared applied skills, and the process blueprint for editing existing text. The registry itself is a structured statement of obligations. The comparison therefore isolates the added toolkit and compiler route relative to a common structured brief, not a brief-only condition against a contract. Preservation counts measure compliance with a supplied checklist rather than discovery or unaided preservation of unstated meaning.</p>\n<p>After the first responses were saved, another agent received the source and variants labeled alpha and beta without the editing logs or condition mapping. It issued the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/masked-editorial-review.json\">masked review</a>. The subsequent <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/condition-label-reveal.json\">label reveal</a> records alpha as baseline and beta as toolkit-assisted and states that review was completed before reveal. This is masking of condition labels, not proof that the reviewer could not infer a method from stylistic differences. All roles operated within the same agent environment; there was no independent human editorial panel.</p>\n<h2 id=\"results-on-the-retained-responses\">Results on the retained responses</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-27\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-36\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-36\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Observation</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Common-brief baseline</th>\n<th class=\"table-align-right\">Toolkit-assisted editing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Protected claims preserved</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">19/19</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">19/19</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Conceptual table distinctions preserved, nested within protected claim 11</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">8/8</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">8/8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Exact protected literals present</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">37/37</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">37/37</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Headings / anchors / link destinations</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">5 / 5 / 3</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">5 / 5 / 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Sentence clarity, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>References and terminology, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Coherence, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Economy without loss, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Scientific genre and reader, 0–2</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Total assigned by the reviewer</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">8/10</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">8/10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Words by whitespace</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">830</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">820</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Editorial decision</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">Accepted within the declared scope</td>\n<td class=\"table-align-right\">Accepted within the declared scope</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/editorial-comparison-results.json\">comparison result</a>, exact <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/baseline-edit.txt\">baseline response</a>, and exact <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/skill-assisted-edit.txt\">assisted response</a> accompany the report. The original <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/editorial-mechanical-checks.json\">mechanical check</a> verifies hashes, literal presence, and document structure. The semantic preservation counts and stylistic scores come from the recorded agent review, not the mechanical checker.</p>\n<p>The eight distinctions are included in the nineteen claims, not eight additional independent obligations. Both score vectors were <code>(2, 2, 2, 1, 1)</code>: the first three axes were at their ceiling, and the 0–2 scale was coarse. A difference smaller than one score step on an axis would not be resolved by this instrument. Equality of totals is a result under this rubric, not evidence of identical writing quality.</p>\n<p>The review used identical <code>gate_reasoning</code> text for both variants, so that paragraph is templated reasoning rather than two independently developed arguments. The record also contains three examined issues per variant. Their English summaries are:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Variant and issue</th>\n<th>What the reviewer examined</th>\n<th>Recorded disposition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Baseline: ALPHA-STYLE-01</td>\n<td>A list of four distinctions is repeated when each is explained</td>\n<td>Non-blocking redundancy; reflected in the economy score</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Baseline: ALPHA-WORDING-01</td>\n<td>A phrase about non-identity became a phrase about lack of implication</td>\n<td>Accepted within the supplied checklist’s local meaning; no new formal precision claimed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Baseline: ALPHA-SOURCE-01</td>\n<td>Both historical Work Item spellings remain</td>\n<td>Inherited ambiguity, not charged as a new defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assisted: BETA-SCOPE-01</td>\n<td>The working-profile qualifier moved before role, skill and blueprint together</td>\n<td>No protected claim of unconditional scope was identified; no loss established within the brief</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assisted: BETA-STYLE-01</td>\n<td>A dense contract definition remains long</td>\n<td>Non-blocking retained limitation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assisted: BETA-SOURCE-01</td>\n<td>Both historical Work Item spellings remain</td>\n<td>Inherited ambiguity, not charged as a new defect</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These dispositions expose the local judgment behind “preserved”; they do not settle a broader scientific interpretation that the checklist did not specify.</p>\n<p>Both responses were slightly longer than the original. Reduction in word count was not the declared target. Replacing compressed references with explicit nouns can increase length while reducing the reader’s effort to reconstruct a reference. Consequently this trial supplies no general rule that successful editing must make a text shorter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-edits-changed\">What the edits changed</h2>\n<p>The assisted response made some list references explicit. Instead of asking the reader to connect the first, second, and third items to an earlier list, it named source context, interaction context, and application context directly. It also replaced a vague plural referent with the already defined object being described. These changes clarified existing relationships without adding another theoretical claim.</p>\n<p>The section discussing a contract was divided into thematic paragraphs addressing the contract and package, version history, work objects, and the boundary of the research. Some technically dense material was preserved because its distinctions were protected. The masked reviewer identified local strengths in both variants rather than a consistent overall advantage. A repeated list remained in the baseline; a long contract definition remained in the assisted version. Neither was repaired after comparison to manufacture a better first-response result.</p>\n<h2 id=\"skill-use-and-technical-trace\">Skill use and technical trace</h2>\n<p>The assisted route went through a brief, request, compiled Knowledge Contract, proposed edit, review, and registration. Five skills were declared applied: defining the editing task, precise rewording, preserving numbers and conditions, structuring sentences and paragraphs, and reviewing the result with its source trace. Required dependencies caused twelve skills and 108 records to be delivered. The other seven skills were not counted as applied merely because they were loaded.</p>\n<p>The separate <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/editorial-trace-audit.json\">technical audit</a> records twelve groups of material changes, twenty change grounds, and seven process grounds. It checked 49 unique input/output locators and 24 unique source ranges. Quotes, character and UTF-8 offsets, and line bindings matched, and all changed non-whitespace characters were covered by the recorded trace. It also checked agreement among request, input, contract, selected context, output, assessment, and receipt bindings. The audit read fifteen source fragments covering nine cited rules. For the precision rule K10, making pronoun references explicit was an editorial application of a general principle, not a separately stated method in the book. The trace is mechanically consistent, while some changes have only general rule support. No visual PDF verification occurred, and the source OCR questions K02/F02 and K03/F03 remained open.</p>\n<p>These observations establish consistency and inspectability of the particular trace. They do not reveal every internal action of the editor or prove that a cited rule caused a wording choice. The edited scientific section is the project’s own text and is distributed here in full. The private third-party source is the writing guide underlying the toolkit’s rules; its cited ranges and the private source-derived toolkit are not redistributed. The published audit reports those rule-source checks with a pinned source identity, rather than supplying the guide itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"counterarguments-and-boundaries\">Counterarguments and boundaries</h2>\n<p>One rival explanation is that the common brief, context, explicit checklist and disclosed rubric already supplied what this section needed. Another is that the baseline model may already have known the general editing method or similar principles through prior training; the experiment did not measure or exclude such exposure. The original protocol expressly avoided assuming that the model lacked general editing knowledge. Both explanations are compatible with the observed tie. A useful subsequent test would include project-specific rules unavailable in the common brief, a held-out task set, and a more discriminating or pairwise-preference assessment. The present result does not prove that the toolkit is useless, but cannot support a superiority claim.</p>\n<p>A second counterargument is that a complete-looking trace may accompany an incorrect edit. Mechanical equality, literal presence, and a consistent receipt do not establish meaning preservation. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">package-delivery study</a> supplies an explicit negative example in which the protected numeric string remains while the sentence’s meaning is negated. The editorial review addresses that distinct question here, within one section and one reviewer’s judgment.</p>\n<p>A third boundary concerns terminology. At the time of the pilot, the editors recorded an ambiguity between two spellings of the work-item entity rather than silently deciding that they denoted different things. The author subsequently clarified that the spellings refer to one entity. The present <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> uses that decision. Frozen pilot inputs and scores retain their historical bytes; they are not retroactively rewritten as if the clarification preceded the experiment.</p>\n<p>There was one section and one retained response per condition, each declared first-completed by its editor. Scores are qualitative rubric judgments, not calibrated population measurements. The trial did not record token usage, cost, or latency. It did not isolate the contribution of each skill, the blueprint, or the surrounding compiler. Native command-line clients were not used. The underlying scientific claims and all referenced literature were not revalidated by this editorial exercise. The methodological distinctions between preregistered and post-hoc work, and between masked labels and a validated judge, are discussed in the <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#evaluation-methods\">methodological references</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"public-record-checks\">Public record checks</h2>\n<p>The supplied <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/replay_editorial_checks.py\">replay checker</a> rechecks the public retained texts against the frozen source hash, original response hashes, exact literals, headings, anchor order, and link destinations:</p>\n<pre class=\"bash\"><code>python3 evidence/replay_editorial_checks.py</code></pre>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/public-editorial-record-check.json\">saved check</a> confirms those mechanical properties. It deliberately does not compute semantic fidelity or reproduce the eight-point ratings. Complete model-runtime identity and the full private toolkit source closure are not supplied, so executing this checker is not a new agent trial. <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/evidence/origin.json\">origin.json</a> pins the distributed records and distinguishes original data from newly generated check results.</p>\n<p>The source section, context, brief, protocol, edits and substantive review prose are Russian; JSON field names are mainly English. The descriptions of the two conditions, the six examined issues and the audit qualifications above are English summaries of the cited Russian passages, not a full independently reviewed translation. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/evidence-language-index.json\">evidence language index</a> identifies every file without rewriting its frozen bytes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"conclusion\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p>The assisted editorial process worked on the selected real section: it produced an accepted text and an inspectable chain from task to changes and review. The baseline also worked, with the same protected-claim result and score. This completed pilot supports a narrow operational claim about usable process and traceability. A claim of better writing, lower cost, or improved scientific correctness requires a different and adequately observed comparison. The present result remains valuable without changing the tie into an advantage.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract"},{"level":2,"id":"question-and-scope","title":"Question and scope"},{"level":2,"id":"design-and-preregistration","title":"Design and preregistration"},{"level":2,"id":"results-on-the-retained-responses","title":"Results on the retained responses"},{"level":2,"id":"what-the-edits-changed","title":"What the edits changed"},{"level":2,"id":"skill-use-and-technical-trace","title":"Skill use and technical trace"},{"level":2,"id":"counterarguments-and-boundaries","title":"Counterarguments and boundaries"},{"level":2,"id":"public-record-checks","title":"Public record checks"},{"level":2,"id":"conclusion","title":"Conclusion"}],"title_id":"editing-a-scientific-section-with-a-knowledge-toolkit-a-masked-single-section-comparison","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/STUDY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/STUDY.md","source_sha256":"59066d54a219c13214a113037b0f3257378d77c83105b755bc80251db51ae0d5","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"glossary","kind":"glossary","type":"glossary","path":"reference/glossary/GLOSSARY.md","route":"/en/reference/glossary/","title":"Knowledge Contract Glossary","titleRu":"Knowledge Contract Glossary","summary":"Shared English definitions, Russian equivalents, technical spellings and distinctions between related concepts.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1 · <strong>Maintainer:</strong> Pavel Valentov</p>\n<p>This glossary defines the preferred vocabulary shared by the scientific papers, engineering specifications and engineering studies. Human-readable terms use ordinary words. Code identifiers remain literal when an interface requires them. A Russian equivalent helps trace the earlier manuscripts; it does not create a second entity. Definitions describe an obligation or an observation; their presence in this glossary is not evidence that a particular implementation satisfies them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"knowledge-and-its-representation\">Knowledge and its representation</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Preferred term</th>\n<th>Russian equivalent</th>\n<th>Definition and boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Knowledge Contract</td>\n<td>Контракт знаний</td>\n<td>A versioned specification of knowledge, intended use, grounds, dependencies, constraints and assessment obligations. Its existence does not establish truth or authorize execution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Semantic object</td>\n<td>Смысловой объект</td>\n<td>An addressable, versioned interpretation with content, attribution, context and grounds. A retrieval chunk is a representation for search and need not have the same boundaries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Source</td>\n<td>Источник</td>\n<td>Material from which an interpretation or observation is derived. Source authorship, authenticity, fidelity and truth are separate questions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Source representation</td>\n<td>Представление источника</td>\n<td>A particular version of text, image, table, audio or other material.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Source fragment</td>\n<td>Фрагмент источника</td>\n<td>A region addressed within a specified source representation. Its locator must state the relevant selection convention.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Interpretation</td>\n<td>Интерпретация</td>\n<td>A reading of material under stated assumptions. Several readings can remain possible.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Attribution</td>\n<td>Атрибуция</td>\n<td>An association between addressed content and the person or system that stated, interpreted or assessed it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Provenance</td>\n<td>Происхождение</td>\n<td>The trace of entities, activities and responsible agents involved in producing a record. Traceability alone does not prove correctness.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ground</td>\n<td>Основание</td>\n<td>A source, observation, rule or argument that supports a specified claim under declared assumptions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Context</td>\n<td>Контекст</td>\n<td>Conditions needed to interpret or use content: referents, scope, representation, time, exceptions, recipient and other relevant circumstances.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Applicability</td>\n<td>Применимость</td>\n<td>Suitability of a particular interpretation or artifact for a named use under current or explicitly historical conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Constraint</td>\n<td>Ограничение</td>\n<td>A rule or condition restricting admissible interpretation, transformation, delivery or action. It is an explicit contract obligation, not a synonym for an acceptance criterion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Protected property</td>\n<td>Сохраняемое свойство</td>\n<td>A specified observation or relation that a transformation is required to preserve.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Projection</td>\n<td>Проекция</td>\n<td>A selected representation for a purpose, with included and omitted units, transformation rules and losses made explicit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Loss</td>\n<td>Потеря</td>\n<td>Information or distinctions not preserved by a transformation within a declared observation model.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Uncertainty field</td>\n<td>Поле неопределённости</td>\n<td>An explicit record of an unresolved reading, qualification or confidence limitation. Omitting the field from a delivered projection can remove information even when the source record retains it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Generated and unreviewed</td>\n<td>Сгенерировано, не проверено</td>\n<td>Two separate facts: a system produced content, and no qualifying review is established. A generated description is not an author statement; a later review must identify its subject and scope. Historical code may combine the two labels as <code>generated/unreviewed</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"requirements-work-and-agency\">Requirements, work and agency</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Preferred term</th>\n<th>Russian equivalent</th>\n<th>Definition and boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Work Item</td>\n<td>Единица работы</td>\n<td>One goal-bearing unit with versioned requirements, constraints, assignments, attempts and acceptance criteria. <code>WorkItem</code> is the code spelling of this same concept, not another type.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Requirement</td>\n<td>Требование</td>\n<td>A condition the requested result or process must satisfy in a specified revision and scope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Acceptance criterion</td>\n<td>Критерий приёмки</td>\n<td>A rule for deciding whether an observed result satisfies a named requirement. It must bind to the actual output and attempt.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Role</td>\n<td>Роль</td>\n<td>A named responsibility and decision scope. A role definition is distinct from an assignment to an executor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Skill</td>\n<td>Навык</td>\n<td>A reusable description of a method or procedure and the conditions for using it. A <code>SKILL.md</code> file supplies instructions, not evidence that its reader can perform them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Competency</td>\n<td>Компетенция</td>\n<td>A specified or assessed ability to achieve a class of results under stated conditions. A competency definition and evidence about a particular executor remain distinct records.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Capability</td>\n<td>Способность исполнителя</td>\n<td>The ability available to an identified executor in an actual environment, established to the degree supported by its assessment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Blueprint</td>\n<td>Схема решения</td>\n<td>A reusable process or structural arrangement for achieving a result. It connects components, steps, dependencies, choices, constraints and acceptance obligations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assignment</td>\n<td>Назначение</td>\n<td>A binding of an executor to a responsibility or Work Item under a specified scope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Authority</td>\n<td>Полномочие</td>\n<td>The legitimate scope of decisions or actions supplied by an identified authorization basis.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Grant</td>\n<td>Разрешение</td>\n<td>A concrete authorization with subject, action, resource, purpose, conditions and validity scope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Attempt</td>\n<td>Попытка исполнения</td>\n<td>One addressed execution attempt bound to a Work Item, requirements, contract, actor and inputs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Outcome</td>\n<td>Исход</td>\n<td>The result of an operation under its declared contract: success, partial, refused, failed or unresolved.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Effect observation</td>\n<td>Наблюдение эффекта</td>\n<td>What is established about an external change caused by an attempt. A timeout can leave this unknown.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ready</td>\n<td>Готово к выдаче</td>\n<td>Availability or lifecycle readiness under a named profile. It is not an operation outcome and does not replace <code>success</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>refused</code>, <code>failed</code> or <code>unresolved</code>. A ready informational result need not authorize execution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Core and profile</td>\n<td>Ядро и профиль</td>\n<td>The core supplies common obligations; a profile adds a named, versioned set of representation or use conditions. A profile must not cancel a mandatory core condition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Required profile</td>\n<td>Обязательный профиль</td>\n<td>A profile whose interpretation is necessary for the intended use, including any profile carrying a mandatory constraint. An unknown required profile blocks that use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Optional and opaque profile</td>\n<td>Необязательный и непрозрачный профиль</td>\n<td>An optional profile may be preserved without interpretation only when no mandatory obligation depends on it. Opaque means its meaning is not interpreted; byte preservation is not conformance to its semantics.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"retrieval-composition-and-reuse\">Retrieval, composition and reuse</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Preferred term</th>\n<th>Russian equivalent</th>\n<th>Definition and boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Candidate</td>\n<td>Кандидат</td>\n<td>A potentially useful retrieved item awaiting the checks needed for a particular use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Coverage</td>\n<td>Покрытие требований</td>\n<td>Established support for the required slots of a named request at a named stage. Presence of a keyword is not semantic fulfilment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Search assurance</td>\n<td>Полнота процедуры поиска</td>\n<td>A statement about the search procedure within its declared scope, separate from coverage of the selected answer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Mandatory dependency</td>\n<td>Обязательная зависимость</td>\n<td>A relation requiring another pinned unit or condition for the specified use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Dependency closure</td>\n<td>Замыкание зависимостей</td>\n<td>The least set containing selected roots and all their active mandatory dependencies within the declared graph. It cannot recover unrecorded edges.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Context package</td>\n<td>Пакет контекста</td>\n<td>Versioned selected knowledge with requirements, grounds, dependency closure, coverage, projection and resource account for a particular use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Package and task package</td>\n<td>Пакет и пакет задачи</td>\n<td>A package is a declared collection with an inventory and identity. A task package additionally binds selected content to a request and its obligations. Neither name implies a complete dependency closure or a small size.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Document passport</td>\n<td>Паспорт документа</td>\n<td>The historical package’s metadata record identifying a document, representation, source and related conditions. It is not an identity document or an independent certificate of correctness.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Operational closure</td>\n<td>Операционное замыкание</td>\n<td>The dependencies and bindings required to use a package in a named operation: for example, roles, skills, rules, criteria, source references and authority. A graph closure is sufficient only when that graph records every relevant obligation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Compiled artifact</td>\n<td>Скомпилированный артефакт</td>\n<td>A saved derivative of an extraction, selection, transformation or composition, with its producing recipe and dependencies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Build recipe</td>\n<td>Рецепт сборки</td>\n<td>The declared inputs, transformation versions, parameters and material environment used to produce an artifact.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Content identity</td>\n<td>Идентичность содержимого</td>\n<td>Identity within a declared representation and hash domain. The current <code>sha256/raw-utf8-json-v1</code> exchange profile hashes exact UTF-8 JSON bytes; it does not promise equality for differently serialized values.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Lookup identity</td>\n<td>Ключ поиска кандидата</td>\n<td>A key derived from a declared candidate descriptor. Finding the same key does not establish equality of descriptors or eligibility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Candidate descriptor</td>\n<td>Описание кандидата для повторного использования</td>\n<td>The fields that an implementation compares after lookup: subject and representation identities, producing recipe and dependencies, relevant request scope and requirements, temporal mode, recipient and permission scope. The selected profile declares required fields and comparison rules; a title or hash alone is not this comparison.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Admission</td>\n<td>Допуск к применению</td>\n<td>A decision that specified requirements and constraints permit a particular use under an identified policy and trust boundary.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Freshness</td>\n<td>Актуальность по времени</td>\n<td>Satisfaction of a specified temporal policy, distinct from semantic applicability and authorization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Retention</td>\n<td>Срок хранения</td>\n<td>A storage policy for preserving bytes, distinct from how long their use remains valid.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Residency</td>\n<td>Наличие в хранилище</td>\n<td>The observed presence or absence of identified bytes at a storage location: <code>resident</code>, <code>evicted</code>, <code>absent</code> or <code>unknown</code>. It says nothing by itself about their freshness, truth or permitted use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Artifact lifecycle</td>\n<td>Жизненный цикл артефакта</td>\n<td>The states and events of preparation, assessment, use, supersession, revocation and storage. Retention is its storage part, not a synonym for the whole lifecycle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Invalidation</td>\n<td>Пересмотр пригодности</td>\n<td>A change that requires eligibility or a derived state to be withdrawn or reconsidered. It need not delete the historical bytes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Serve boundary</td>\n<td>Момент фактической выдачи</td>\n<td>The declared consistency point at which a selected result is actually released to a recipient.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Knowledge utility</td>\n<td>Полезность знания</td>\n<td>Task-, policy- and horizon-dependent benefit relative to a stated baseline and cost model. It is not an intrinsic scalar property of a document.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"evidence-versions-and-review\">Evidence, versions and review</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Preferred term</th>\n<th>Russian equivalent</th>\n<th>Definition and boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>Свидетельство</td>\n<td>An addressed observation or record supporting a specified claim under a method and trust boundary. An assertion that a test passed is weaker than inspectable test outputs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assessment</td>\n<td>Оценка</td>\n<td>A method-bound judgment about a particular subject revision and property.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assessment result</td>\n<td>Результат оценки</td>\n<td><code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>unknown</code> or <code>not_applicable</code> for one property in a defined scope. <code>not_applicable</code> discharges a mandatory condition only with an identified rule and justified applicability witness; it is not a substitute for unavailable evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Trusted input</td>\n<td>Доверенный вход</td>\n<td>A value accepted as a premise within a declared evaluation. Structural consistency of that value does not authenticate its external origin or truth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Trust boundary</td>\n<td>Граница доверия</td>\n<td>The point at which a source, actor or process is accepted for a stated kind of evidence or authority. A test must identify which inputs it checks and which it assumes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Counterexample</td>\n<td>Контрпример</td>\n<td>A case violating the conclusion of a stated claim while satisfying its relevant premises, or exposing that a needed premise was omitted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Conformance</td>\n<td>Соответствие спецификации</td>\n<td>Satisfaction of specified structural or behavioral requirements in a declared scope. It does not establish every empirical benefit claimed for a system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Reproduction</td>\n<td>Воспроизведение</td>\n<td>Running an available procedure again on its stated inputs and comparing outputs under declared criteria. Inspecting a retained report is not a rerun.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Demonstration or illustration</td>\n<td>Демонстрация или иллюстрация</td>\n<td>An example constructed to expose a mechanism or counterexample. Re-running that example reproduces its own computation, not a historical experiment on different inputs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Replication</td>\n<td>Независимое повторение</td>\n<td>A separately conducted evaluation of a claim with its own implementation or observations. Independence must be described, not inferred from a different filename or agent role.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Qualification</td>\n<td>Квалификация процедуры</td>\n<td>Evidence that an identified implementation meets a declared finite acceptance procedure. Development and regression controls can qualify a bounded release; they do not establish performance on held-out data or general competence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Preregistration</td>\n<td>Предварительная фиксация протокола</td>\n<td>Recording intended inputs, criteria and analysis before the outcomes being evaluated are disclosed. A local timestamped plan is distinct from a publicly registered protocol; neither prevents all deviations or bias.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Masked review</td>\n<td>Рецензирование с замаскированными условиями</td>\n<td>Assessment using labels that hide the condition assignment from the assessor. The text can still reveal clues; masking neither proves blindness nor makes an agent judge independent or calibrated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Positive control</td>\n<td>Положительный контроль</td>\n<td>A case designed to meet the admitted or successful condition, so an always-reject implementation cannot pass the suite. Its representativeness must be justified separately.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Stress or boundary case</td>\n<td>Нагрузочный пример или проверка границы</td>\n<td>A deliberately adverse case probing the limits of the model or its trusted premises. An expected false-input acceptance is reported as a boundary failure, separately from ordinary conformance counts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Regression control</td>\n<td>Регрессионная проверка</td>\n<td>A previously known case rerun after a change to detect a return of the tested defect. It is not an unseen challenge or a new independent observation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Mutation check</td>\n<td>Мутационная проверка</td>\n<td>Deliberately altering an implementation and checking whether the suite distinguishes the altered behavior. The declared mutants and any equivalent mutants bound the conclusion; killing a few mutants is not proof of general correctness.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Revision</td>\n<td>Редакция объекта</td>\n<td>An immutable identified content state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Edition</td>\n<td>Издание корпуса документов</td>\n<td>A coherent version of publications and their reading manifest. It is independent of a runtime protocol or service version.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Representation profile</td>\n<td>Профиль представления</td>\n<td>A versioned encoding and interpretation convention for a kind of artifact.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Current head</td>\n<td>Текущая выбранная редакция</td>\n<td>A mutable selection within an identified namespace and policy. A historical current marker does not prove present currency.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Adoption decision</td>\n<td>Решение о принятии</td>\n<td>A governance act accepting a norm or publication within an authority’s scope. It is not a truth predicate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Internal agent review</td>\n<td>Внутренняя агентная рецензия</td>\n<td>A recorded assessment by an agent, with its actual input, method and limitations. It is not independent external peer review.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Unknown</td>\n<td>Не установлено</td>\n<td>The available observations do not establish a value or verdict. It cannot be replaced with false, absent or pass without a justified rule.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Withheld</td>\n<td>Не раскрывается</td>\n<td>A value is not disclosed under a restriction. This does not assert that the value is absent.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"scoped-mathematical-notation\">Scoped mathematical notation</h2>\n<p>Symbols are local to the model named in each document. A shared letter does not identify a shared quantity. The following crosswalk prevents the earlier cost notation from being mistaken for the scientific utility model.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Symbol</th>\n<th>Scope and meaning</th>\n<th>Cross-document boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Semantic-object and reuse papers: mandatory conditions of request <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Each condition requires a passed assessment or a justified non-applicability witness; no silent exclusion of unknown conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Roots}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Dependency-closure model: initially selected vertices</td>\n<td>Distinct from <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(q)</annotation></semantics></math> and from a monetary reuse cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Knowledge-utility paper: accounting or decision horizon</td>\n<td>The retained reuse study used <code>h</code> for hit fraction; current explanatory text uses <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho</annotation></semantics></math> for that fraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(k\\mid\\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{\\mathrm{set}}(S)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Utility paper and context-composition paper: task-conditioned or set value</td>\n<td>The retained reuse program’s <code>V</code> means validation cost, now written <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_v</annotation></semantics></math> in the study</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N,G,B</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Utility paper and reuse study: request count, cold admissible-path cost, preparation cost</td>\n<td>Comparable only under the same horizon, quality requirements and cost boundary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_{\\mathrm{hit}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Utility paper: complete cost of an admitted hit</td>\n<td>Equals <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_v+c_r</annotation></semantics></math> in the study only for the stated all-hit simplified case, with no additional rebuilding, invalidation or storage cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_v,c_r,\\rho</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Reuse study: per-request validation, additional admitted-hit cost, usable-hit fraction</td>\n<td>Historical data and code preserve their original keys <code>V</code>, <code>R</code>, <code>h</code>; new calculations state the mapping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(N)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Reuse study: overhead over the accounting horizon</td>\n<td>The closed fixed-cost break-even formula applies only when <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math> and the other relevant parameters are independent of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n<td>Constrained-transformation paper: residual information retained for reconstruction</td>\n<td>A function of an artifact; unrelated to the reuse study’s scalar hit fraction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"editorial-rules-for-terminology\">Editorial rules for terminology</h2>\n<p>Use <strong>Work Item</strong> in English prose and <code>WorkItem</code> only for a literal identifier. Use “skill,” “competency,” “capability,” “role,” and “authority” only with the distinctions above. “Validated,” “verified,” “complete,” “current” and “independent” require an explicit subject and scope. Introduce an abbreviation only if repeated use saves meaningful reading effort.</p>\n<p>Quoted historical labels such as “independent review” remain unchanged in evidence. In the studies discussed here, the label often means a separately assigned agent within the same session or agent family, not independent external peer review. Current summaries state that actual scope. “Exact” identifies the compared bytes or observation; “all” identifies its finite denominator. Retained development, regression, stress and ordinary cases remain separate even when a historical summary combined them.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/\">artifact registry</a> defines file formats. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">status specification</a> defines operational values. The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/\">source map</a> retains older names and identifiers solely for traceability.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"knowledge-and-its-representation","title":"Knowledge and its representation"},{"level":2,"id":"requirements-work-and-agency","title":"Requirements, work and agency"},{"level":2,"id":"retrieval-composition-and-reuse","title":"Retrieval, composition and reuse"},{"level":2,"id":"evidence-versions-and-review","title":"Evidence, versions and review"},{"level":2,"id":"scoped-mathematical-notation","title":"Scoped mathematical notation"},{"level":2,"id":"editorial-rules-for-terminology","title":"Editorial rules for terminology"}],"title_id":"knowledge-contract-glossary","math_count":17,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/reference/glossary/GLOSSARY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/reference/glossary/GLOSSARY.md","source_sha256":"5147262a4739b194cd1712d04f3b205ad701c77f7c214ba7cfb68094d12ddabb","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"bibliography","kind":"bibliography","type":"bibliography","path":"reference/bibliography/BIBLIOGRAPHY.md","route":"/en/reference/bibliography/","title":"Primary Sources and Research Foundations","titleRu":"Primary Sources and Research Foundations","summary":"Named primary sources and the exact conceptual or technical role each plays in the current edition.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1 · <strong>Bibliographic verification:</strong> 4 November 2026</p>\n<p>The entries below identify the primary sources actually used by the current edition. They distinguish established results and external formats from this project’s own synthesis. A cited source supplies the stated conceptual or technical foundation; it does not endorse Knowledge Contract or establish the effectiveness of its implementation. No third-party book text or source-page images are reproduced in this publication.</p>\n<h2 id=\"provenance\">Provenance</h2>\n<p>Luc Moreau and Paolo Missier, editors. <strong>PROV-DM: The PROV Data Model.</strong> W3C Recommendation, 30 April 2013. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/2013/REC-prov-dm-20130430/\">Dated specification</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for the distinction among entities, activities and agents in provenance, and for the separation of provenance from a judgment about content. The project adds task-specific applicability and admission obligations; those are not claims made by PROV-DM on its behalf.</p>\n<h2 id=\"skos\">SKOS</h2>\n<p>Alistair Miles and Sean Bechhofer, editors. <strong>SKOS Simple Knowledge Organization System Reference.</strong> W3C Recommendation, 18 August 2009. <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/2009/REC-skos-reference-20090818/\">Dated specification</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for concepts, labels and knowledge-organization relations. A shared label or concept relation is not a proof of semantic equivalence or truth.</p>\n<h2 id=\"partial-correctness\">Partial correctness</h2>\n<p>C. A. R. Hoare. <strong>An Axiomatic Basis for Computer Programming.</strong> <em>Communications of the ACM</em> 12(10), 1969, 576–580, 583. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/363235.363259\">DOI: 10.1145/363235.363259</a>. <a href=\"https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/publications/title/Tony.Hoare.html\">Author’s institutional bibliography</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for the established idea of reasoning about programs through preconditions, postconditions and inference rules. The transformation paper adapts conditional preservation reasoning to its explicitly defined relations. Its composition argument is not presented as a novel replacement for program logic or a proof of termination.</p>\n<h2 id=\"submodular-maximization\">Submodular maximization</h2>\n<p>George L. Nemhauser, Laurence A. Wolsey and Marshall L. Fisher. <strong>An Analysis of Approximations for Maximizing Submodular Set Functions—I.</strong> <em>Mathematical Programming</em> 14, 1978, 265–294. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01588971\">DOI: 10.1007/BF01588971</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for the classical greedy approximation result under normalized, monotone submodular value and a cardinality limit. The context-composition paper states those assumptions and explains why complementary knowledge units can violate submodularity. The guarantee is not transferred to arbitrary budgets, penalties or semantic quality functions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"retrieval-augmented-generation\">Retrieval-augmented generation</h2>\n<p>Patrick Lewis, Ethan Perez, Aleksandra Piktus, Fabio Petroni, Vladimir Karpukhin, Naman Goyal, Heinrich Küttler, Mike Lewis, Wen-tau Yih, Tim Rocktäschel, Sebastian Riedel and Douwe Kiela. <strong>Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks.</strong> <em>Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems</em> 33, 2020. <a href=\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/hash/6b493230205f780e1bc26945df7481e5-Abstract.html\">Conference publication</a>. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401\">Author manuscript</a>.</p>\n<p>Used as the primary reference for generation conditioned on retrieved material. The context-composition paper studies explicit coverage and preservation obligations; it does not claim to have replicated the cited experiments or surpassed their models.</p>\n<h2 id=\"computational-complexity\">Computational complexity</h2>\n<p>Richard M. Karp. <strong>Reducibility among Combinatorial Problems.</strong> In <em>Complexity of Computer Computations</em>, 1972, 85–103. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9\">DOI: 10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9</a>.</p>\n<p>Used as the primary source for the classical complexity context of set cover. The current paper gives its own explicit mapping from a finite covering problem to a restricted context-selection instance. This does not imply that every practical instance is hard or that finite exact search is impossible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"program-slicing\">Program slicing</h2>\n<p>Mark Weiser. <strong>Program Slicing.</strong> <em>IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering</em> SE-10(4), 1984, 352–357. <a href=\"https://faculty.cc.gatech.edu/~harrold/6340/cs6340_fall2009/Readings/Weiser.Slicing.TSE.pdf\">Original article</a>.</p>\n<p>Used in <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">semantic objects and context</a> to identify the established behavior-preservation problem behind a backward slice. The paper gives a restricted acyclic model; it does not introduce a new general slicing algorithm or infer semantic preservation for arbitrary text.</p>\n<h2 id=\"truth-maintenance\">Truth maintenance</h2>\n<p>Jon Doyle. <strong>A Truth Maintenance System.</strong> <em>Artificial Intelligence</em> 12(3), 1979, 231–272. <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0004370279900080\">Publisher record</a>. <a href=\"https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/5377b306-4ecc-4687-b1f5-78cbb4a0543a\">MIT report version, AI Memo 521</a>.</p>\n<p>Used in <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision and governance</a> as prior work on recording reasons and revising beliefs as assumptions change. The project’s reachability argument identifies potentially affected assessments; it is not an implementation of Doyle’s system.</p>\n<h2 id=\"build-systems\">Build systems</h2>\n<p>Andrey Mokhov, Neil Mitchell and Simon Peyton Jones. <strong>Build Systems à la Carte.</strong> <em>Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages</em> 2(ICFP), article 79, 2018, 1–29. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1145/3236774\">DOI: 10.1145/3236774</a>. <a href=\"https://simon.peytonjones.org/assets/pdfs/build-systems-original.pdf\">Author’s article</a>.</p>\n<p>Used in <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled knowledge reuse</a> for the established distinction between scheduling and rebuilding, and for correctness relative to declared dependencies. Agreement with build dependencies does not establish a source’s truth, a new task’s semantic requirements or current authority.</p>\n<h2 id=\"views-and-complements\">Views and complements</h2>\n<p>François Bancilhon and Nicolas Spyratos. <strong>Update Semantics of Relational Views.</strong> <em>ACM Transactions on Database Systems</em> 6(4), 1981, 557–575. <a href=\"https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~david/cs848s18/constant-complement.pdf\">Original article</a>.</p>\n<p>Used in <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">constrained transformations</a> as a prior construction in which a view and retained complement determine database state and constrain update translation. The analogy motivates retaining residual information; the project does not claim those view-update laws for natural-language projections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"theory-change\">Theory change</h2>\n<p>Carlos E. Alchourrón, Peter Gärdenfors and David Makinson. <strong>On the Logic of Theory Change: Partial Meet Contraction and Revision Functions.</strong> <em>The Journal of Symbolic Logic</em> 50(2), 1985, 510–530. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2307/2274239\">DOI: 10.2307/2274239</a>. <a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-symbolic-logic/article/abs/on-the-logic-of-theory-change-partial-meet-contraction-and-revision-functions/7ED837BAD5FB6D9A7C77906D73527F9C\">Publisher record</a>.</p>\n<p>Used in <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision and governance</a> to distinguish logical contraction and revision from versioned artifacts and adoption decisions. The current model neither defines an AGM revision operator nor claims to satisfy the AGM postulates.</p>\n<h2 id=\"causal-inference\">Causal inference</h2>\n<p>Judea Pearl. <strong>Causal Inference in Statistics: An Overview.</strong> <em>Statistics Surveys</em> 3, 2009, 96–146. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1214/09-SS057\">DOI: 10.1214/09-SS057</a>. <a href=\"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/1sv6k47m\">Author’s institutional copy</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for the distinction between observed association and causal conclusions under assumptions about interventions and confounding. The utility paper therefore distinguishes measured task scores from identified causal benefits and does not treat a small exploratory comparison as a population estimate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"http-caching\">HTTP caching</h2>\n<p>Roy T. Fielding, Mark Nottingham and Julian Reschke, editors. <strong>HTTP Caching.</strong> RFC 9111, Internet Standard, June 2022. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111.html\">RFC 9111</a>.</p>\n<p>Used as a reference for HTTP cache controls and freshness concepts. Knowledge applicability, semantic dependency closure and action authorization remain additional project obligations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"stale-http-responses\">Stale HTTP responses</h2>\n<p>Mark Nottingham. <strong>HTTP Cache-Control Extensions for Stale Content.</strong> RFC 5861, Informational, May 2010. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5861.html\">RFC 5861</a>.</p>\n<p>Used as a clearly limited analogy for stale-while-revalidate and stale-if-error behavior. This document is informational, and its HTTP mechanisms do not authorize reuse of revoked knowledge or actions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"requirement-language\">Requirement language</h2>\n<p>Scott Bradner. <strong>Key Words for Use in RFCs to Indicate Requirement Levels.</strong> RFC 2119, BCP 14, March 1997. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119.html\">RFC 2119</a>.</p>\n<p>Barry Leiba. <strong>Ambiguity of Uppercase vs Lowercase in RFC 2119 Key Words.</strong> RFC 8174, BCP 14, May 2017. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8174.html\">RFC 8174</a>.</p>\n<p>Engineering specifications use capitalized MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT and MAY in this sense. Their force concerns conformance to the stated project specification; it is not a claim that the specification is an IETF standard.</p>\n<h2 id=\"json-exchange-and-schemas\">JSON exchange and schemas</h2>\n<p>Tim Bray, editor. <strong>The JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Data Interchange Format.</strong> RFC 8259, Internet Standard, December 2017. <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259\">RFC 8259</a>.</p>\n<p>Austin Wright, Henry Andrews, Ben Hutton and Greg Dennis, editors. <strong>JSON Schema: A Media Type for Describing JSON Documents.</strong> Draft 2020-12. <a href=\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/json-schema-core\">Official core specification</a>.</p>\n<p>Used for the exchange syntax and JSON Schema dialect of the bounded observation, dependency-change, book-package and index-receipt profiles. A schema validates the declared data shape and constraints; it does not authenticate an observation or perform every cross-record semantic check. The exact-byte hash profile does not imply JSON value canonicalization.</p>\n<h2 id=\"agent-artifact-formats\">Agent artifact formats</h2>\n<p>Agent Skills maintainers. <strong>Agent Skills Specification.</strong> <a href=\"https://agentskills.io/specification\">Official specification</a>, consulted 4 November 2026.</p>\n<p>Used for skill-directory naming, the required <code>SKILL.md</code> file, its <code>name</code> and <code>description</code> metadata, and supported extension metadata. The project defines its own additional reading structure while preserving compatibility with that format.</p>\n<p>AGENTS.md maintainers. <strong>AGENTS.md.</strong> <a href=\"https://agents.md/\">Official format description</a>, consulted 4 November 2026.</p>\n<p>Used for repository instructions to coding agents. The format is ordinary Markdown and does not prescribe a universal schema for agent identities, competencies, roles or blueprints. The project’s formats for those artifacts are explicitly its own conventions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"evaluation-methods\">Evaluation methods</h2>\n<p>The following sources explain methodological distinctions used in the studies. They do not authenticate the historical runs, supply missing fixtures or turn same-session reviews into independent replication.</p>\n<p>Brian A. Nosek, Charles R. Ebersole, Alexander C. DeHaven and David T. Mellor. <strong>The Preregistration Revolution.</strong> <em>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</em> 115(11), 2018, 2600–2606. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1708274114\">DOI: 10.1073/pnas.1708274114</a>. <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5856500/\">Primary article</a>. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial pilot</a> distinguishes a protocol fixed before its retained outputs from an analysis composed after results were known. Its local recorded protocol was not registered in an external public registry.</p>\n<p>Lianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying Sheng, Siyuan Zhuang, Zhanghao Wu, Yonghao Zhuang, Zi Lin, Zhuohan Li, Dacheng Li, Eric P. Xing, Hao Zhang, Joseph E. Gonzalez and Ion Stoica. <strong>Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena.</strong> <em>Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 36, Datasets and Benchmarks Track</em>, 2023. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685\">Author manuscript</a>. Used for the need to examine judge biases and the limits of agreement. The editorial and <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">projection</a> studies use their own coarse rubrics and masking; they do not inherit the cited benchmark’s agreement or validation results.</p>\n<p>Richard A. DeMillo, Richard J. Lipton and Frederick G. Sayward. <strong>Hints on Test Data Selection: Help for the Practicing Programmer.</strong> <em>Computer</em> 11(4), April 1978, 34–41. <a href=\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/1646911\">Publisher record</a>. <a href=\"https://www.cerias.purdue.edu/apps/reports_and_papers/view/919\">Institutional bibliographic record</a>. This is historical background for deliberate program mutation. The project reports its declared mutants and whether they are distinguished; a finite mutation result is not general test adequacy.</p>\n<p>Alfred Tarski. <strong>A Lattice-Theoretical Fixpoint Theorem and Its Applications.</strong> <em>Pacific Journal of Mathematics</em> 5(2), 1955, 285–309. <a href=\"https://msp.org/pjm/1955/5-2/pjm-v5-n2-p11-s.pdf\">Publisher’s original article</a>. This supplies general fixed-point background. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency study</a> computes the least closure by finite monotone iteration on the power set of a declared graph; its proof and implementation checks do not establish that every semantic dependency was recorded.</p>\n<p>National Institute of Standards and Technology. <strong>SCLITE Scoring Package: Alignment and Scoring Documentation.</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/usnistgov/SCTK/blob/master/doc/sclite.htm\">Official SCTK documentation</a>, consulted 4 November 2026. Used for reference–hypothesis alignment and substitution, deletion and insertion counts underlying word-error reporting. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">transcription study</a> states its character- and word-unit definitions explicitly; it reports no measured CER or WER because the required reference and protocol are unavailable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"experimental-source-books\">Experimental source books</h2>\n<p>These books identify the historical materials used by the engineering studies. They are not sources for a new scientific theorem and their practical advice is not endorsed by this publication. The retained export identities, rather than the catalogue pages alone, identify the actual inputs that were processed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Книга о вкусной и здоровой пище</strong> (<em>The Book of Tasty and Healthy Food</em>). Ministry of Food Industry of the USSR. Moscow: Pishchepromizdat, 1952. <a href=\"https://rusneb.ru/catalog/000199_000009_005811805/\">National Electronic Library record</a>. Used as the cooking export in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">transcription study</a>. The retained export key <code>molchanova</code> and Russian label “Молчанова” identify that input in project reports; they are not a claim of sole authorship. The cited catalogue identifies the Ministry, and this edition does not infer an editor’s identity from an export key.</li>\n<li><strong>Навигация, лоция и мореходная астрономия</strong> (<em>Navigation, Pilotage, and Nautical Astronomy</em>). G. I. Fain. Moscow: Transport, 1977. <a href=\"https://rusneb.ru/catalog/000199_000009_007594442/\">National Electronic Library record</a>. Used as the navigation export; the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/corpus-audit-source.json\">retained corpus audit</a> identifies the processed representation.</li>\n<li><strong>Сто схемных задач по автоматике</strong> (<em>One Hundred Circuit Problems in Automation</em>). V. A. Timofeev. Leningrad: Sudostroenie, 1964. These are the bibliographic descriptors recorded for the automation export in the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/evidence/corpus-audit-source.json\">retained corpus audit</a>. An independently verified stable catalogue record for this exact edition was not established in this review; the project audit is input provenance, not external bibliographic confirmation. The historical engineering tasks examine representations and preservation, not modern operational certification.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"editorial-input\">Editorial input</h3>\n<p>Maxim Ilyakhov and Lyudmila Sarycheva. <strong>Пиши, сокращай: Как создавать сильный текст</strong> (<em>Write and Shorten: How to Create Strong Text</em>). 4th ed. Moscow: Alpina Publisher, 2024, 400 printed pages, <strong>as declared in the supplied OCR bibliographic line</strong>. <a href=\"https://alpinabook.ru/catalog/book-pishi-sokrashchay-2025/\">Publisher’s work listing</a>. No independently verified ISBN for the supplied edition is asserted.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">editorial pilot</a> used the project’s skills derived from a supplied OCR representation of this work. The same derived package is examined in the <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">dependency-closure study</a>, whose <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/layout-preservation-report.json\">layout preservation report</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/evidence/dependency-graph.json\">dependency graph</a> retain the source’s record names and dependency metadata.</p>\n<p>The supplied filename uses “2025,” and the digital input contains 427 physical page entries. These descriptors differ from the OCR bibliographic line and do not authenticate an edition. The publisher’s current listing identifies the work but does not authenticate the exact privately supplied file. The publication distributes the pilot’s own texts, evaluations and package metadata, not the book or the private source-derived toolkit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"project-sources-and-evidence\">Project sources and evidence</h2>\n<p>Pavel Valentov. <strong>Knowledge Contract and Meaning Management: earlier publication corpus.</strong> Repository snapshot <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/b50243b28e9ad7376e28c749a21479ef46ec9d60\">b50243b28e9ad7376e28c749a21479ef46ec9d60</a>.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/\">source map</a> records the migration from fourteen earlier paper files and ten specifications to the current reading structure. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/evidence-index.json\">engineering evidence index</a> binds individual empirical or analytical claims to their supporting records. Each study describes what can be rerun publicly, what is available only as a retained report, and what remains unmeasured.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"provenance","title":"Provenance"},{"level":2,"id":"skos","title":"SKOS"},{"level":2,"id":"partial-correctness","title":"Partial correctness"},{"level":2,"id":"submodular-maximization","title":"Submodular maximization"},{"level":2,"id":"retrieval-augmented-generation","title":"Retrieval-augmented generation"},{"level":2,"id":"computational-complexity","title":"Computational complexity"},{"level":2,"id":"program-slicing","title":"Program slicing"},{"level":2,"id":"truth-maintenance","title":"Truth maintenance"},{"level":2,"id":"build-systems","title":"Build systems"},{"level":2,"id":"views-and-complements","title":"Views and complements"},{"level":2,"id":"theory-change","title":"Theory change"},{"level":2,"id":"causal-inference","title":"Causal inference"},{"level":2,"id":"http-caching","title":"HTTP caching"},{"level":2,"id":"stale-http-responses","title":"Stale HTTP responses"},{"level":2,"id":"requirement-language","title":"Requirement language"},{"level":2,"id":"json-exchange-and-schemas","title":"JSON exchange and schemas"},{"level":2,"id":"agent-artifact-formats","title":"Agent artifact formats"},{"level":2,"id":"evaluation-methods","title":"Evaluation methods"},{"level":2,"id":"experimental-source-books","title":"Experimental source books"},{"level":3,"id":"editorial-input","title":"Editorial input"},{"level":2,"id":"project-sources-and-evidence","title":"Project sources and evidence"}],"title_id":"primary-sources-and-research-foundations","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/reference/bibliography/BIBLIOGRAPHY.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/reference/bibliography/BIBLIOGRAPHY.md","source_sha256":"bbfc162fcb52b74abefad92d0cda0f72cacc77ff1a697dfc685d5380b8cf457d","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-dialogue.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"source-map","kind":"appendix","type":"appendix","path":"appendices/source-map/APPENDIX.md","route":"/en/appendices/source-map/","title":"Source Map, Earlier Editions, and Protocol Compatibility","titleRu":"Source Map, Earlier Editions, and Protocol Compatibility","summary":"The migration from the earlier corpus, immutable history, evidence provenance and unchanged protocol versions.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1 · <strong>Source audit date:</strong> 4 November 2026</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-this-edition-changes\">1. What this edition changes</h2>\n<p>The earlier repository contained fourteen short English paper entry pages, fourteen much longer Russian manuscripts, ten English specification entries and ten Russian specification texts. The long texts combined scientific arguments, domain studies, iteration notes and references to supporting records outside the public release.</p>\n<p>This edition reorganizes those materials into eight complete English scientific papers, eleven engineering specifications, six engineering studies, a common glossary and bibliography, and two supporting appendices. Scientific results, normative requirements and observations are now separately identifiable. The prior iteration histories remain recoverable at an immutable repository revision instead of appearing as repeated handoff sections in the current reading path.</p>\n<p>The exact prior publication snapshot is <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/b50243b28e9ad7376e28c749a21479ef46ec9d60\">b50243b28e9ad7376e28c749a21479ef46ec9d60</a>. The prior site snapshot is <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/contract-place-site/tree/64ddd981296cb65390beecd802f1682618a7f080\">64ddd981296cb65390beecd802f1682618a7f080</a>. These references are historical evidence, not aliases for the mutable main branches.</p>\n<p>The independent review addressed published edition 1.0 at <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e\">ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e</a>. Corrections are made from that immutable base. Historical evidence and its origin hashes remain unchanged; corrected interpretations and new finite checks have their own records. The current documents identify the stronger premises needed by the formal results, the actual scope of the observations, and the breaking book-package profile described below.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-scientific-crosswalk\">2. Scientific crosswalk</h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Earlier manuscript</th>\n<th>Current scientific home</th>\n<th>Material separated into engineering</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Introduction</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/publication-catalog.json\">Publication inventory</a> and <a href=\"/en/appendices/api-integration/#2-documented-public-interface\">API interface boundaries</a>; no inventory establishes implementation conformance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Semantic objects and extraction</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">Source transcription and uncertainty</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Context and applicability</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects and context</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/\">Declared dependency graphs and finite delivery checks</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Transformations and transfer</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">Constrained transformations</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/\">Executable projections and revision</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Query, retrieval and coverage</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">Retrieval and context composition</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/\">Coverage and delivery requirements</a>; no finite retrieval-search performance study is claimed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Budgeted context composition</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/\">Retrieval and context composition</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/#delivered-record-counts-and-fault-controls\">Delivered record counts and fault controls</a>; these are graph records, not measured token or byte costs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Dialogue and learning</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/\">Interaction and memory</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/contract-family/#2-common-contract-record\">Operation and task bindings</a> and <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/#targeted-revision-and-a-strong-alternative\">targeted revision cases</a>; no longitudinal dialogue or learning study</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Reasoning and evidence</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/#counterexamples-and-the-comparator\">Checker trust boundaries and counterexamples</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Documents and memory</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/\">Interaction and memory</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/\">Readable projection audit</a> and <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/\">single-section editorial pilot</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Work, roles, skills and Knowledge Contract</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">Controlled knowledge reuse</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">revision and governance</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">Core</a>, <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/\">artifact registry</a> and <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/\">profile conformance</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Revision and canon</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/revision-and-governance/\">Revision and governance</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/\">Versioned-rule requirements</a> and <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/#targeted-revision-and-a-strong-alternative\">bounded addressed revision observations</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Evaluation and falsification</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">Evidence and falsification</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/evidence-index.json\">Claim–evidence index</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/README.md#counting-and-interpretation\">ordinary versus boundary counts</a>, and <a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/#counterexamples-and-the-comparator\">comparator tie</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Compilation, caching and reuse</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">Controlled knowledge reuse</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/\">Compiled-artifact requirements</a>, <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/\">invalidation</a> and <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/\">reuse policy</a>; no deployed generation or idempotence experiment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Knowledge utility and resource economics</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/science/knowledge-utility/\">Knowledge utility</a></td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/\">Synthetic admission checks and analytical costs</a>; no measured production cost benefit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The numerical filenames of the earlier edition are retained only as compatibility links. They lead to the current scientific home and to their exact earlier English and Russian texts. Consolidation is topical: it does not silently convert an untested historical proposal into a proved claim. A historical claim not supported by the present argument or evidence remains a historical proposal, accessible in the source snapshot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-engineering-crosswalk\">3. Engineering crosswalk</h2>\n<p>The ten earlier specification subjects map one-to-one to these current files:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Earlier subject</th>\n<th>Current specification</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Knowledge Contract v3</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">Core model and admission</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Contract family</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/contract-family/\">Knowledge operations</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Semantic types</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/\">Semantic types and representations</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Context package</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/\">Coverage, dependencies and delivery</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Compiled artifact contract</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/\">Compiled artifacts and derivation</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Artifact registry</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/\">Readable names and document formats</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Status model</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/\">Assessment, execution and publication states</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Cache identity and invalidation</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/\">Identity and invalidation</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Reuse policy</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/\">Time, authorization and outcomes</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Knowledge evolution contract</td>\n<td><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/\">Versioned rules</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The additional <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/\">book-package specification</a> originated with the schema, validator and synthetic fixtures added in upstream revision <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/570f76b6d9a189295c3229254821c30748383965\">570f76b6d9a189295c3229254821c30748383965</a>. Edition 1.0 preserved those upstream bytes. The current correction defines the breaking <strong><code>book-package/2</code></strong> profile and <strong><code>KCBook*/v2</code></strong> records: review results bind to their actual subject, scope and criteria; rights classification constrains permissible licensing and export; readiness follows the complete declared state rules. This is an explicit format change, not a silent reinterpretation of historical <code>v1</code> records.</p>\n<p>The last reviewed <code>v1</code> implementation remains available at <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/tree/ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e\">ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e</a>. A consumer MUST identify the profile and schema versions it accepts. Conversion to <code>v2</code> requires a new identified record and any missing assessment evidence; renaming a schema token MUST NOT turn an old review, rights assertion or status into a newly established fact. No automatic reassessment or corpus-admission change is performed by publishing these documents.</p>\n<p>Engineering studies contain the practical domain material, including the historical cooking, navigation and electrical examples. They distinguish publicly replayable procedures from inspectable retained reports and unavailable third-party source corpora. The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/evidence-index.json\">evidence index</a> records claim-level support and limits; a navigational link alone is not an empirical justification.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-protocol-compatibility\">4. Protocol compatibility</h2>\n<p>Publication edition <strong>1.0</strong> identifies the original reviewed set of documents; the current publication’s edition is stated in its catalogue and metadata. Neither is a renamed runtime protocol. The following identifiers are preserved where historical records need them. Capitalized requirements use <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Identifier</th>\n<th>Actual scope</th>\n<th>Compatibility statement</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>3.0.0-draft.1</code></td>\n<td>Earlier target Knowledge Contract architecture</td>\n<td>No stable protocol release is inferred from the editorial edition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>kc-synthesis/0.1</code></td>\n<td>Bounded interpreted-JSON core/profile implementation</td>\n<td>Its retained results do not implement all requirements of the current architecture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>mm-envelope/0.1</code></td>\n<td>Common carrier used by the historical registry and examples</td>\n<td>The carrier and its 34 artifact types are neither an exhaustive taxonomy of knowledge nor a limit on later contracts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>i16-compiled-artifact/0.1</code></td>\n<td>Bounded compiled-artifact representation</td>\n<td>Original observations retain their original profile and validator scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>i17-reuse-policy/0.1</code></td>\n<td>Bounded time and reuse-policy model</td>\n<td>Interval calculations do not constitute production authorization or distributed atomic serving</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>knowledge-evolution-metadata/0.2-proposal</code></td>\n<td>Earlier evolution metadata proposal</td>\n<td>Publication of architectural requirements does not claim a deployed migration service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Knowledge Contract admission runtime</td>\n<td>Service that decides admission under its explicitly advertised profiles and policies</td>\n<td>No stable deployed runtime version is established by this publication; an operator MUST supply its actual implementation and profile identities when claiming conformance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>kc2_admission</code></td>\n<td>Legacy book-record field name</td>\n<td>It is not a runtime or protocol version. Its presence does not establish admission, and the publication does not rename it to imply a new runtime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>KCBook*/v1</code></td>\n<td>Book records in the immutable edition-1.0 base</td>\n<td>Historical schema behavior remains auditable at <code>ed2e188c89a1a50a187f3d55bf32baab54c87f7e</code>; conversion does not retroactively validate it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>book-package/2</code>, <code>KCBook*/v2</code></td>\n<td>Current corrected book representation and validator profile</td>\n<td>Breaking fields and decisions require explicit migration and fresh evidence where absent; they do not identify the admission runtime</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>An implementation claiming this edition MUST state which requirements it satisfies and link corresponding evidence. A change of schema, field semantics or wire version requires its own migration and compatibility record. The publication process MUST NOT rewrite retained raw evidence merely to remove historical development labels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-provenance-and-rights\">5. Provenance and rights</h2>\n<p>Each study’s <code>evidence/origin.json</code> identifies the origin, exact output hash and transformation status of its evidence files. An exact recovered JSON is distinguished from a readable prose view reconstructed from retained HTML. The latter does not claim to reproduce unavailable original Markdown bytes.</p>\n<p>Third-party books, page images and OCR corpora are not redistributed here. The current arguments are self-contained; publicly runnable examples use original synthetic data or metadata-only graphs. Restricted source access limits independent reproduction of the corresponding historical experiments, and the studies state that limit at the claim it affects.</p>\n<p>Project text remains <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/LICENSE\">CC BY 4.0</a>; code remains <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/LICENSE-CODE\">Apache-2.0</a>. The current <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/SOURCES.md\">source register</a> replaces the earlier catch-all description for the new edition with named primary works and explicit document coverage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-reading-and-citation\">6. Reading and citation</h2>\n<p>Cite a paper by its human-readable title, author, publication edition and immutable revision or released DOI when one actually exists. No DOI is invented for this edition. Use the paper for its argument, the specification for a requirement, and the study or exact evidence record for an observation. The <a href=\"/en/reference/glossary/\">glossary</a> resolves shared terms without repeating the full definitions in every article.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-internal-review-and-verification\">7. Internal review and verification</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/reviews/publication-integrity/REVIEW.md\">publication review</a> records the actual scientific and evidence reviews, exact input identities, integration corrections, local checks and unperformed release checks. It distinguishes internal content acceptance from external peer review and from the separate repository, website and corpus-admission outcomes.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-what-this-edition-changes","title":"1. What this edition changes"},{"level":2,"id":"2-scientific-crosswalk","title":"2. Scientific crosswalk"},{"level":2,"id":"3-engineering-crosswalk","title":"3. Engineering crosswalk"},{"level":2,"id":"4-protocol-compatibility","title":"4. Protocol compatibility"},{"level":2,"id":"5-provenance-and-rights","title":"5. Provenance and rights"},{"level":2,"id":"6-reading-and-citation","title":"6. Reading and citation"},{"level":2,"id":"7-internal-review-and-verification","title":"7. Internal review and verification"}],"title_id":"source-map-earlier-editions-and-protocol-compatibility","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/appendices/source-map/APPENDIX.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/appendices/source-map/APPENDIX.md","source_sha256":"eca6631650eb303aa5451eb3f173ce82cd06d73c2184ed460abe65188c61f25c","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-prism.webp","category":"engineering"},{"canonical":true,"id":"api-integration","kind":"appendix","type":"appendix","path":"appendices/api-integration/APPENDIX.md","route":"/en/appendices/api-integration/","title":"API Integration and Publication Consistency","titleRu":"API Integration and Publication Consistency","summary":"Published read interfaces, task-contract delivery requirements and consistency between repository, reader and index.","language":"en","html":"<p><strong>Publication edition:</strong> 1.1 · <strong>Interface inventory date:</strong> 4 November 2026</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-purpose\">1. Purpose</h2>\n<p>The repository publishes knowledge and specifications. The website renders a selected immutable publication. The service exposes machine interfaces. These are related products with separate versions and evidence. A successful website build does not establish service deployment, a current search index, or admission of a complete task-specific Knowledge Contract.</p>\n<p>This appendix defines the connection between those boundaries. The <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/\">core specification</a> supplies the obligations for a ready contract; the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/\">context-package specification</a> supplies composition and delivery requirements.</p>\n<p>Sections 3–7 define requirements using <a href=\"/en/reference/bibliography/#requirement-language\">BCP 14</a>. Section 2 is an informative inventory of the documented read surface, not a claim that a task-contract service or the receipt profile below has been deployed. Requirement identifiers remain stable within this appendix.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R001.</strong> Conformance MUST name the implemented profile, exact implementation revision and assessed requirements; publication of these requirements does not establish that conformance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-documented-public-interface\">2. Documented public interface</h2>\n<p>The public site describes the following read surface:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Interface</th>\n<th>Documented role</th>\n<th>Boundary</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/v1/search</code></td>\n<td>Retrieve candidate passages; used by the site’s anonymous GET search</td>\n<td>Retrieval ranking does not establish correctness, completeness or task admission</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/v1/related</code></td>\n<td>Retrieve related records</td>\n<td>A relation or similarity is not an equivalence or authority grant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/v1/fetch</code></td>\n<td>Fetch an addressed record through the read interface</td>\n<td>The returned revision and representation must be inspected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/mcp</code></td>\n<td>MCP access to the documented service surface</td>\n<td>Availability of a tool does not establish every operation described by the research</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>https://api.contract.place/health</code></td>\n<td>Service health information</td>\n<td>Health is distinct from corpus freshness and scientific validity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The existing site’s <code>llms.txt</code> names the read schema <code>ContractPlaceRead/v1</code>. This appendix does not infer HTTP methods, parameter schemas or authentication behavior for routes other than the observed site search. Clients use the service’s actual API contract. A task-specific contract delivery capability requires its own implemented and tested interface; no <code>/compose</code> endpoint is established here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-contract-delivery-requirements\">3. Contract delivery requirements</h2>\n<p><strong>API-R002.</strong> <strong>Request binding.</strong> A service offering a ready Knowledge Contract MUST bind the request to the task and requirements revision, intended recipient and use, applicable constraints, required profiles, temporal mode, and resource limits.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R003.</strong> Where any required value is unknown, it MUST report the gap or obtain the missing information before asserting admission for that use.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R004.</strong> <strong>Response and outcome.</strong> The response MUST identify the exact contract and representation versions, selected knowledge revisions, sources and grounds, mandatory dependency closure, coverage, omitted or unresolved obligations, applicable constraints, assessments and the decision’s trust boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R005.</strong> Its operation outcome MUST be one of <strong><code>success</code>, <code>partial</code>, <code>refused</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>unresolved</code></strong>, with the <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/#3-canonical-dimension-vocabularies\">status model’s meanings</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R006.</strong> <code>ready</code> is an availability or lifecycle state; it MUST NOT replace an outcome token.</p>\n<p>A failure to execute the delivery operation is <code>failed</code>; an outcome or material obligation that cannot be established is <code>unresolved</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R007.</strong> <strong>Admission scope.</strong> A ready-for-use assertion MUST be backed by the mandatory checks for the declared use.</p>\n<p>A nonempty JSON object or successful search is insufficient. A response that supports reading may still lack the capability or grant needed for execution. A rendered skill description cannot supply those missing facts.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R008.</strong> The <strong>Knowledge Contract admission runtime</strong> is the service boundary responsible for such a decision; its actual implementation and profile versions MUST be identified rather than inferred from the publication edition or the legacy field <code>kc2_admission</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R009.</strong> <strong>Execution binding.</strong> If the contract is issued for execution, the system MUST subsequently bind the actual attempt, actor, environment, contract revision, outputs, effects and acceptance evidence.</p>\n<p>Knowledge delivery and execution acceptance are separate operations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-three-manifests\">4. Three manifests</h2>\n<p>Publication consistency requires three independently inspectable identities:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Manifest</th>\n<th>Required identity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Repository publication catalogue</td>\n<td>Edition and the canonical document paths/types in one source commit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Website publication manifest</td>\n<td>Exact source repository commit, edition, document hashes, renderer version and generated routes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>API index manifest</td>\n<td>Actual indexed source commits and content hashes, build identity, admitted paths and applicable decisions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Website identity.</strong> The website’s source commit is pinned in <code>publication-manifest.json</code> in the site repository. Its generated <code>publication.json</code> binds reader routes to source hashes.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R010.</strong> A deployment MUST build those pages from the declared commit and reject an uncommitted or mismatched source tree.</p>\n<p><strong>Repository-scoped comparison.</strong> The API may contain multiple repositories or releases.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R011.</strong> A client MUST establish repository identity before comparing a result’s source revision with the reader’s publication commit.</p>\n<p>The canonical repository identifier for this project is <strong><code>https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract</code></strong>; a revision comparison is scoped to this identifier.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R012.</strong> It MUST NOT be applied to an unrelated source.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R013.</strong> Unknown and different revisions MUST be distinguished from a match.</p>\n<p>An API-provided label of “current” alone is not proof of a matching publication.</p>\n<p>In the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/contract-place-site/blob/cdca604245165bbec80ce3624fdc65e9cbde6a01/src/site/search.js\">inspected site adapter</a>, the relevant response fields are <strong><code>source_revision</code></strong> and <strong><code>citation.canonical_url</code></strong>. The latter supplies repository identity through an HTTPS GitHub URL of the form <code>https://github.com/{owner}/{repository}/blob/{full-commit}/{path}</code>; the adapter compares the identified owner/repository and revision with the pinned reader. The normalized <code>repository_url</code> used by the receipt profile below is an operator-record field, not a claim that the backend already returns a field with that name. If the canonical URL is absent, malformed, points to another host or does not identify an immutable revision, a matching publication cannot be established.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R014.</strong> A client MUST NOT substitute an untrusted title, branch label or bare hash for that identity.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R015.</strong> If URL revision and <code>source_revision</code> conflict, it MUST report a mismatch.</p>\n<p>The read schema’s published field contract, once inspected, remains authoritative for the actual API representation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-observed-mismatch-and-release-gate\">5. Observed mismatch and release gate</h2>\n<p>The pre-restructure site linked the newer paper entries while a search inspection returned a Knowledge Contract snapshot at <code>39e05969728ea7a51f162cdbefac32fd8f54dbb6</code>, whose README still described publication folders as empty. That was a consistency mismatch between the reading surface and search evidence, not proof that the newer documents were absent from GitHub.</p>\n<p>The reader addresses the presentation boundary by exposing its exact source revision and showing whether a search result’s revision matches it.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R016.</strong> Refreshing or promoting the backend index is a separate operation. <strong>Admission governance.</strong> An indexing operation MUST use the repository’s existing publication-decision and allowlist process; a catalogue entry is not a substitute for that approval.</p>\n<p>Publishing this appendix does not admit paths, refresh an index or change an allowlist.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R017.</strong> <strong>Release gate.</strong> Before a release is described as consistently indexed, the operator MUST record all six checks below against the exact source revision.</p>\n<p>A health check alone cannot pass this gate. A missing observation is <code>unknown</code>, not <code>pass</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Check key</th>\n<th>Required observation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>admitted_snapshot</code></td>\n<td>Admitted path hashes and decision references agree with the intended immutable source snapshot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>index_snapshot</code></td>\n<td>The actual index build and manifest identify that exported snapshot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>reported_revision</code></td>\n<td>Returned result identities report the intended repository and revision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>query_coverage</code></td>\n<td>A declared query set retrieves the specified expected documents; selection and exclusions are recorded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>supersession</code></td>\n<td>Removed or superseded records have the declared observed status, including an explicit empty inventory when none apply</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>fetch_identity</code></td>\n<td>Fetching every retained result identity returns the same revision and content hash reported for that result</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"release-receipt-profile\">Release receipt profile</h3>\n<p><strong>API-R018.</strong> <strong>Receipt.</strong> A gate record conforming to <strong><code>KCIndexReleaseReceipt/v1</code></strong> MUST satisfy the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/appendices/api-integration/index-release-receipt.schema.json\">receipt JSON Schema</a> and the semantic checks here.</p>\n<p>It is a new operator interchange profile for this publication, not evidence that the existing service emits it. This appendix supplies no production receipt and claims no new deployment or index run.</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-50\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Required field</th>\n<th>Representation and meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>schema</code></td>\n<td>Exact token <code>KCIndexReleaseReceipt/v1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>repository_url</code>, <code>source_revision</code></td>\n<td>Canonical HTTPS repository URL and full 40-character lowercase Git commit identity; one receipt covers one repository revision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>observed_at</code>, <code>observer</code>, <code>evidence_refs</code></td>\n<td>RFC 3339 observation time, accountable observer identity and durable evidence references for the actual run</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>publication_manifest_sha256</code></td>\n<td>SHA-256 of the website/publication manifest used for comparison</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>index_build_id</code>, <code>index_manifest_sha256</code></td>\n<td>Actual backend build identity and exact manifest hash, not a desired future value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>admitted_paths</code></td>\n<td>Nonempty list of <code>{path, sha256, decision_ref}</code> for the exact admitted source files</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>queries</code></td>\n<td>Nonempty declared list of <code>{id, query, expected_paths, results}</code>; each result records <code>{record_id, path, source_revision, source_sha256, representation_sha256, canonical_url}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>fetches</code></td>\n<td>Readback list of <code>{record_id, source_revision, representation_sha256}</code> for every retained query result</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>superseded_records</code></td>\n<td>List of <code>{record_id, expected_status, observed_status, evidence_ref}</code>; <code>[]</code> explicitly means that the inspected change inventory has no such records</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>checks</code></td>\n<td>All six named gate outcomes, each <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code> or <code>unknown</code>; no omitted check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td><code>decision</code></td>\n<td><code>pass</code> only if every check passed; otherwise <code>fail</code> if any check failed, and <code>unknown</code> when none failed but at least one is unknown</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td><code>scope</code>, <code>limitations</code></td>\n<td>The query selection rationale, included/excluded paths and limits of observation; limitations may be an explicitly empty list</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>API-R019.</strong> Query IDs, admitted paths, and record IDs within each results or fetch list MUST be unique.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R020.</strong> The same record MAY be found by several different queries.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R021.</strong> Each query’s expected paths MUST be drawn from <code>admitted_paths</code>; every expected path MUST have a matching result before <code>query_coverage</code> can pass.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R022.</strong> Each retained result MUST identify the declared repository and <code>source_revision</code>, and its source path and <code>source_sha256</code> MUST match the admitted snapshot.</p>\n<p><code>representation_sha256</code> identifies the exact returned representation bytes, separately from the whole source file.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R023.</strong> A service returning passages MUST provide both bindings or leave the relevant check unknown.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R024.</strong> Every result MUST have a matching fetch identity and representation hash before <code>fetch_identity</code> can pass.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R025.</strong> Every superseded record’s observed status MUST equal its required status before <code>supersession</code> can pass.</p>\n<p>Schema validation alone does not establish these correspondences, the truth of recorded observations or authority to index.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-client-presentation\">6. Client presentation</h2>\n<p><strong>API-R026.</strong> <strong>Presentation.</strong> The default result view SHOULD show the passage, human title, source link and indexed revision.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R027.</strong> Technical evidence about admission, uncertainty and score semantics MAY be placed in an accessible details section.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R028.</strong> Material limits MUST remain visible where they change a user’s decision.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R029.</strong> A retrieval score MUST NOT be presented as a probability that a claim is true without an independently established calibration and its scope.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R030.</strong> Scientific reading SHOULD remain possible without making API calls: full pages, equations, references and evidence links are served as the static selected edition.</p>\n<p>API failure therefore need not remove the published scientific argument.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-acceptance-cases\">7. Acceptance cases</h2>\n<p><strong>API-R031.</strong> <strong>Acceptance cases.</strong> The integration MUST reject a site build from the wrong source commit, a dirty publication tree, a missing referenced document and a local link escaping the publication.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R032.</strong> Search presentation MUST distinguish a matching Knowledge Contract revision, an older revision, an unknown revision and a record from another repository.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R033.</strong> Rendering MUST preserve equations, tables, footnotes and document anchors.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R034.</strong> Receipt validation MUST reject omitted checks, a passing decision with a nonpassing check, an ambiguous repository/revision identity, duplicate identities, missing expected paths, and a mismatched fetch readback.</p>\n<p><strong>API-R035.</strong> A contract-delivery acceptance suite MUST additionally exercise missing constraints, a mandatory unknown profile, incomplete closure, insufficient budget, revoked disclosure authority, stale source evidence, and a correct informational response that does not authorize execution.</p>\n<p>No successful result is claimed here for an interface that has not undergone that suite.</p>\n<p>For the evidential interpretation of these checks, see <a href=\"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/\">evidence and falsification</a>, <a href=\"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/\">controlled reuse</a>, and the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/engineering/studies/README.md\">engineering studies</a>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"1-purpose","title":"1. Purpose"},{"level":2,"id":"2-documented-public-interface","title":"2. Documented public interface"},{"level":2,"id":"3-contract-delivery-requirements","title":"3. Contract delivery requirements"},{"level":2,"id":"4-three-manifests","title":"4. Three manifests"},{"level":2,"id":"5-observed-mismatch-and-release-gate","title":"5. Observed mismatch and release gate"},{"level":3,"id":"release-receipt-profile","title":"Release receipt profile"},{"level":2,"id":"6-client-presentation","title":"6. Client presentation"},{"level":2,"id":"7-acceptance-cases","title":"7. Acceptance cases"}],"title_id":"api-integration-and-publication-consistency","math_count":0,"source":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/appendices/api-integration/APPENDIX.md","history":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/commits/a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b/appendices/api-integration/APPENDIX.md","source_sha256":"2e1c800cf2bb1c61696e34d516b23db1f3f65fa7c58f983bf3124c17854cefe3","revision":"a0450428704fcd41b411203f2fc88c06c779610b","image":"knowledge-landscape.webp","category":"engineering"}],"articles":[{"abstract":"The available sources are heterogeneous: queries, search results, memory, instructions, tools, Policy, and model assumptions together form the available context, yet they do not automatically determine which task-specific semantic commitment has been selected, how it was issued, what realizes it, or whether a particular action is permitted.\n\nThe central contribution is Meaning Management as an architectural layer governing the lifecycle of task-specific semantic commitments. The selection is fixed by a Resolution Receipt; the Knowledge Contract is separated from issuance; post-issuance Binding is separated from the current Authorization. Variant A, the BKG, the Resolver, the identity model, Expansion/Evolution, and the execution gate formally specify the mechanisms that support this decomposition.\n\nThe surveyed purposive corpus covers prompting, retrieval, memory, orchestration, provenance, workflows, policy evaluation, and knowledge representation individually, but provides no single model of this governed transition; the finding is bounded by the corpus and asserts no historical priority. Practical usefulness is tested by nine hypotheses and nine protocols, HYP/EXP-001–009. The experiments have not been run, so error reduction, improved diagnosis, and acceptable cost are not demonstrated.","description":"A conceptual architecture that separates available context, selected semantic commitments, the immutable Knowledge Contract, runtime binding, and the authorization of a particular action.","image":"architecture-original.jpg","source":"https://arcanada.ai/en/blog/meaning-management-knowledge-contract-architecture","status":"The Status of This Publication\n\nResearch 17 — Russian Semantic Source v1 Frozen. The canonical Russian version of the conceptual architecture and the preregistration specification. Empirical validation of the architecture has not yet been performed.\n\nDOI: 10.5281/zenodo.22013923 — the English preprint is deposited in Zenodo (CERN) under CC BY 4.0. Please cite the DOI.\n\nThis status does not imply a finished implementation, a proven superiority, or production readiness. This English text is a translation of the frozen Russian source through the shared Arcanada glossary, not an independent academic rewrite.","title":"Meaning Management in Autonomous AI Systems: the Knowledge Contract Architecture","id":"knowledge-contract-architecture"},{"abstract":"Systems of cooperating LLM agents exchange state as prose. Prose handoffs lose deontic force, invert relation directions, invite readers to fill gaps with defaults, and leave a knowledge store’s internal wholeness uncheckable. We present a knowledge substrate in which the unit of exchange is a context — a finite set of typed, scoped assertions under a snapshot pin — carried on immutable, digest-pinned, append-only revisions, with every algebraic law of the transfer operations proved or refuted on this carrier rather than assumed. The substrate is implemented and mutation-tested — a refusal catalog of 27 codes at the 2026-08-30 evidence pin, each proven red and growing since only through that same door (§8.10); sixteen invariants enforced at honestly labeled tiers; the reduction ladder R0–R3 with exactness certificates — and exercised on real work: a live store grown from 14 to 134 revisions at the §8.10 recount — organic dogfooding for the first stretch, then a bulk corpus lift (14→63, §8.8) and requirements work this paper does not describe (63→134, §8.10), named as what each stretch was rather than folded under one word (an adversarial review, 2026-09-09, found “dogfooding” over-general for growth this paper’s own §8.8/§8.10 attribute to two other causes) — an admission gate 4-for-4 on real tasks, two write races decided by the append-only law, and two contour defects the run itself surfaced — a truncating renderer and an ungoverned executor over-claim. The work refutes three of this line’s own claims — two facets of the token-economy claim and one store-debt figure, the last felled by the parallel run rather than the evaluation — and narrows the delta claim; the refutations are the headline result. On a stage-snapshot-dominated corpus — session-to-session handoffs, where the pilot’s largest reductions (43.1%, 36.4%) lived, are excluded from the systematic sample and untested here — automated spec-only encoding inflates tokens 7.6% across 18 samples, while a structured-summary baseline under the same instruction reaches 14.8% losslessly on the same sources: reduction tracks the encoder, not the format, on this frame. Hand encoding lands within 0.1 percentage points of the pilot’s 38% on a 4-sample subset where the automated arm reduces only 2.2% — a single-point match against a pilot whose own per-artifact spread is about 12 points, stated as what it is rather than as reproduction. Recall of load-bearing source units — not fidelity in the full sense; this measure counts survival of what the source stated and cannot see a fabricated addition (§1.1’s second failure class), a limit an adversarial review found unstated (2026-09-09) — measured jointly: automated packets preserve 99.3% of 437 semantic units, but the hand encoding behind the 38% preserved only 82.7% — the token win and the completeness rule trade against each other, and token-matched compression across formats remains unmeasured. The delta claim narrows to within-task chains (4/4 counter-pairs); at near-lossless encoding, 144 blind downstream grades hit a 47/48 ceiling in every arm alike — no test with that little variance has the power to distinguish packet from honest prose, and none is claimed to. What the typed carrier exhibits, uncompared (an adversarial review, 2026-09-09, found “demonstrably earns” overstated — §8.7 item 4): machine-checkable recall of load-bearing source units after the fact — an observed MUST→SHOULD drift caught by the evaluation’s unit audit, checkable because modality is a field, and not a fidelity claim in the sense that would also catch a fabricated addition — admission gating, digest-decided concurrency, and projection focus. The co-evolution objection — that the corpus and its enforcing checks were built together — has two measured limits. A sensitivity replay, run in the store’s own append-only order, finds pre-enforcement source content for 10 of the 15 discriminable refusal codes; for the other 5 the objection stands, tested and unrefuted. A separate check-removal experiment finds three codes whose suppression admits a revision that subsequently crashes the unchanged Markdown renderer, with no pre-enforcement source witness for those three. This establishes necessity for that operation on those inputs, not independence of the corpus or failure of downstream LLM work. For the algebraic laws of §4 and the invariants of §5 the objection is untested — none of Theorem 1, Proposition 5, I4 or I5 is among the 26 codes the earlier 86-code sensitivity replay reached (§8.7 item 9); membership in the current replay’s 31 witnessed codes was not re-checked. What the design is not compared against is named to the same standard rather than conceded in a phrase: retrieval-augmented handoff, standard graph serializations under shape validation, and token-matched baselines are unmeasured, each carrying the result that would falsify the design. The method itself is adapted rather than invented — specification mutation and vacuity detection, credited in §2.10, where the analogy to weak-versus-strong mutation is withdrawn as wrong. A measurement instrument fabricated a number twice; only recomputation-from-source in CI held.\n\nWhat this paper is for, stated so it can be quoted against us: it reports what a typed, digest-pinned substrate for inter-agent knowledge transfer exhibits, uncompared — and the measured answer is refusal, not economy.\n\n","description":"The second version of the Knowledge Contract work: the typed substrate implemented and mutation-tested, its algebraic laws proved or refuted on the carrier, and the empirical check version 1 declared missing — with the refutations as the headline result.","image":"knowledge-v2-original.jpg","source":"https://arcanada.ai/en/blog/meaning-management-knowledge-contract-v2","status":"The Status of This Publication\n\nAuthor: Pavel Valentov — Independent Researcher. ORCID: 0009-0001-2567-4584. No co-authors, affiliations, or funding sources are claimed.\n\nResearch 17 — Version 2. English Semantic Source. The second version of the Knowledge Contract work. Version 1 (frozen 2026-08-14) declared: «Empirical validation of the architecture has not yet been performed.» This version carries that validation: the substrate is implemented and mutation-tested, and the evaluation’s headline results are refutations of this line’s own earlier claims.\n\nPredecessor: Version 1 — DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.22013923 (CC BY 4.0). Version 1 remains frozen and citable; where the two versions disagree, the disagreement is stated here and v1 stands unchanged.\n\nCanonical language: unlike v1 (frozen Russian source, derived English), v2’s source is English; a Russian rendering, if made, derives from this text through the shared Arcanada glossary. This v2 text is not yet deposited on Zenodo; versioning the deposited record is the author’s act. This status does not imply proven superiority or production readiness.","title":"Knowledge Contracts, Version 2: A Typed Substrate for Inter-Agent Knowledge Transfer, with Its Algebraic Laws Proved or Refuted","id":"knowledge-contract-v2"}],"aliases":{"research/p00":"science/semantic-objects-and-context","research/p01":"science/semantic-objects-and-context","research/p02":"science/semantic-objects-and-context","research/p03":"science/constrained-transformations","research/p04":"science/retrieval-and-context-composition","research/p05":"science/retrieval-and-context-composition","research/p06":"science/interaction-and-memory","research/p07":"science/evidence-and-falsification","research/p08":"science/interaction-and-memory","research/p09":"science/controlled-knowledge-reuse","research/p10":"science/revision-and-governance","research/p11":"science/evidence-and-falsification","research/p12":"science/controlled-knowledge-reuse","research/p13":"science/knowledge-utility","docs/artifact-registry":"engineering/specifications/artifact-registry","docs/cache-identity-and-invalidation":"engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation","docs/compiled-artifact-contract":"engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract","docs/context-package":"engineering/specifications/context-package","docs/contract-family":"engineering/specifications/contract-family","docs/knowledge-contract-v3":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core","docs/knowledge-evolution-contract":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution","docs/reuse-policy":"engineering/specifications/reuse-policy","docs/semantic-types":"engineering/specifications/semantic-types","docs/status-model":"engineering/specifications/status-model","wiki/knowledge-contract":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core","wiki/provenance":"science/semantic-objects-and-context","wiki/versioned-knowledge":"science/revision-and-governance","docs/model":"engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core"},"roadmap":{"pin":{"repository":"Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract","commit":"f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695","files":[{"path":"ROADMAP.md","role":"rendered","sha256":"c84f38452b481db776a282d512172a78ca1534f335edc51c29395ea04f004f02","url":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/ROADMAP.md"},{"path":"roadmap/TASKS.md","role":"rendered","sha256":"f5208ed8fd91fe30ffaf00b37c27e8d0ceba89f30f7f46045cac676da057ec79","url":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/TASKS.md"},{"path":"roadmap/dependencies.json","role":"rendered","sha256":"b894c7135f129ee207f684ab60f62cf3694cff87613e44475ab085648c3629dd","url":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/dependencies.json"},{"path":"roadmap/EXPERIMENTS.md","role":"linked","sha256":"f9da63b7aaf2bc0e5c3054f9b256828018e76d80a8f247180f175ad010c030b5","url":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/EXPERIMENTS.md"},{"path":"roadmap/FOUNDATIONS_RELEASE_PLAN.md","role":"linked","sha256":"368b62ada0a31eebc9504b47adcbbbe9566e9a58287792064b090e06a52120d1","url":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/FOUNDATIONS_RELEASE_PLAN.md"},{"path":"roadmap/SITE_DELIVERY.md","role":"introduction","sha256":"3247bee17344104c3cf1fae4cac5fc7468115022c46f501d8d72c4055f3114bd","url":"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/SITE_DELIVERY.md"}]},"revision":"2026-11-06.1","baseline_commit":"717c43deb514adc5b40f76837e7ee0fb2da37102","execution_authority":"Muneral","execution_bindings":"unverified; RM labels are not work-item IDs","edge_semantics":"requires_output: reviewed prerequisite for dependent acceptance/execution, not for exploratory design; this DAG is not the complete manuscript-preparation scheduling policy","introduction":"Knowledge Contract publishes bounded scientific and engineering results while maintaining a transparent research programme. This roadmap distinguishes existing evidence from planned work on human meaning, formal representations, algebra, geometry and practical verification. A planned task is not a completed result; negative or inconclusive findings will be preserved and reflected in future revisions.","tasks_intro":"# Research and engineering work packages\n\nThese are executable task definitions, not completed research. Public RM identifiers are stable roadmap references, not Muneral IDs. Execution ownership, acceptance and state remain in the existing work graph; RM-04 reconciles the bindings. No deadline or completion is invented. Proposed priorities: P0 = immediate transparency/release enablement; P1 = next substantive research/verification; P2 = subsequent expansion and integration. All tasks begin as **planned, execution unverified**.\n\nKC-Brain and Agent-Brain retain analytical authorship, examples, scientific tests, interpretation and acceptance. SWE roles perform only the necessary server, task-registry, implementation or deployment operations with prepared inputs. A task naming SWE does not delegate scientific authorship. Each dependent task needs the named reviewed output, not closure of an entire research programme. Every task also uses the common evidence rules in [EXPERIMENTS.md](EXPERIMENTS.md).\n\nThe [dependency definition](dependencies.json) is the machine-readable companion. [ROADMAP.md](../ROADMAP.md) gives release boundaries, sequence and baseline sources. Task acceptance means the promised deliverable is complete; its scientific result may be negative or inconclusive.","tasks_intro_html":"<p>These are executable task definitions, not completed research. Public RM identifiers are stable roadmap references, not Muneral IDs. Execution ownership, acceptance and state remain in the existing work graph; RM-04 reconciles the bindings. No deadline or completion is invented. Proposed priorities: P0 = immediate transparency/release enablement; P1 = next substantive research/verification; P2 = subsequent expansion and integration. All tasks begin as <strong>planned, execution unverified</strong>.</p>\n<p>KC-Brain and Agent-Brain retain analytical authorship, examples, scientific tests, interpretation and acceptance. SWE roles perform only the necessary server, task-registry, implementation or deployment operations with prepared inputs. A task naming SWE does not delegate scientific authorship. Each dependent task needs the named reviewed output, not closure of an entire research programme. Every task also uses the common evidence rules in <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/EXPERIMENTS.md\">EXPERIMENTS.md</a>.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/dependencies.json\">dependency definition</a> is the machine-readable companion. <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/ROADMAP.md\">ROADMAP.md</a> gives release boundaries, sequence and baseline sources. Task acceptance means the promised deliverable is complete; its scientific result may be negative or inconclusive.</p>\n","overview_html":"<p><strong>A bounded publication now; a transparent programme of research next.</strong></p>\n<p>This roadmap separates two commitments: finish a readable and scientifically honest edition of the existing corpus, and develop missing foundations and verification without pretending that planned work is already evidence. It defines 25 work packages, priorities, acceptance conditions and adversarial checks. None of the future research results is asserted as completed.</p>\n<p>Read the <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/TASKS.md\">task definitions</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/dependencies.json\">dependency graph</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/EXPERIMENTS.md\">experiment and falsification plan</a>, and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/SITE_DELIVERY.md\">Contract Place delivery specification</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"src-1-baseline-and-scope\">1. Baseline and scope</h3>\n<p>The baseline is Knowledge Contract commit <code>717c43deb514adc5b40f76837e7ee0fb2da37102</code>. Its <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/publication-catalog.json\">publication catalogue</a> contains 29 documents: eight papers, eleven engineering specifications, six studies, a glossary, a bibliography and two appendices. Editorial proposals already made for the first two papers are separate from this baseline; their current review and publication states need their own evidence. This roadmap does not rewrite those manuscripts or imply their deployment.</p>\n<p>We are not starting from an absence of mathematics. <a href=\"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/\">Semantic objects</a> and <a href=\"/en/science/constrained-transformations/\">constrained transformations</a> already define objects, observations, conditional preservation, equivalence, composition and reconstruction. The <a href=\"/en/appendices/source-map/\">source map</a> preserves earlier work, but its topical mapping is not a claim-by-claim completeness audit. Historical algebra/geometry drafts are research inputs, not automatically current normative authority.</p>\n<p>The missing work includes a coherent introduction from human meaning to explicit computational abstractions; a fuller family of algebraic and geometric profiles; diversified examples and attempted refutations; and verified links from claims through requirements to actual code, deployment and useful task outcomes. Mathematical notation alone, a schema pass, a small exhaustive enumeration and a production observation have different evidential roles.</p>\n<p>The scope is the Knowledge Contract scientific/engineering corpus and its agent/knowledge-base interfaces. It is not an instruction to restart a bulk conversion of third-party books. Existing source-derived skills may be used within declared applicability, rights and review limits.</p>\n<h3 id=\"src-2-two-tracks-and-a-finite-release-rule\">2. Two tracks and a finite release rule</h3>\n<p><strong>Track A: editorial completion (RM-25).</strong> Review the frozen catalogue document by document, including chapters, references and appendices. Improve research questions, contribution statements, definitions, argument order, examples, conclusions and typography. Preserve mathematical premises, normative identifiers and raw evidence; identify substantive corrections separately. Use genre-specific structures for papers, specifications and studies. Apply each accepted change to the owning repository, then verify the actual site output through the normal publishing workflow.</p>\n<p><strong>Track B: research and engineering (RM-01 to RM-24).</strong> Develop the missing materials as bounded work packages. A failed or inconclusive hypothesis is a valid research result when the method, evidence and consequence are reported honestly. It is not a reason to conceal the study or claim success.</p>\n<p>New research does not automatically block Track A. A local release block is justified only when a known defect makes an affected assertion false or materially misleading, a required evidence/source/rights condition fails, or the document/site delivery is broken. Correct, narrow or explicitly withdraw that assertion; do not wait for a universal theory. Optional richer examples, larger tests and future geometry results remain roadmap items. Completion of the existing corpus is not completion of all future research.</p>\n<p>The original roadmap-only restart checkpoint was superseded by the author’s later <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/FOUNDATIONS_RELEASE_PLAN.md\">foundations-first manuscript plan</a> (§§1, 3 and 7). The default preparation order is F01 → F02 → F03 (a substantive revision at the existing transformation-paper path) → F04 → F05 → F06, with each subsequent document started only under an explicit user-directed step. F labels are manuscript units, not Muneral IDs; this preparation order is not an additional <code>requires_output</code> graph. Roadmap delivery (RM-03), existing first-two-paper review/publication and material-defect corrections remain independently authorized. Later direct author instructions to edit particular existing papers supersede the old roadmap-only hold for those papers; they must not be retroactively rejected.</p>\n<p>For the default general editorial resumption, <strong>foundation package available</strong> means that all six units have complete substantive, inspectable manuscript candidates at their canonical paths (F03 changes the existing paper, F06 reports actual derived or executed cases rather than unperformed tests), and a version-pinned definitions/claims/sources/corpus-relations map records remaining uncertainties and conflicts. Availability does not mean that independent scientific reviews, merge, deployment, indexing, every future experiment or all 25 RM tasks have succeeded. Each scientific release still follows its own exact-revision review and publication controls; a newer explicit per-document author instruction may change the preparation order without changing these release controls.</p>\n<h3 id=\"src-3-known-gaps-and-work-packages\">3. Known gaps and work packages</h3>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n<col class=\"table-width-33\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Gap or requested extension</th>\n<th>Work packages</th>\n<th>Nature of the gap</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Historical mathematical material is not mapped at claim level</td>\n<td>RM-01, RM-22</td>\n<td>Traceability and evidence recovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Finite counts lack sufficient explanation of carrier, bounds and implementation scope</td>\n<td>RM-02, RM-11</td>\n<td>Reporting and verification design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Readers cannot see the planned programme and actual execution bindings</td>\n<td>RM-03, RM-04</td>\n<td>Public communication and task reconciliation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>No unified foundations treatment from human interpretation to bounded computable use</td>\n<td>RM-05, RM-06, RM-07</td>\n<td>Exposition, research positioning and representation contract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Algebra profiles and practically diverse transformation examples are incomplete</td>\n<td>RM-08, RM-09, RM-10</td>\n<td>Theory, examples and attempted refutation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Geometry is not yet a sufficiently explicit, validated current profile</td>\n<td>RM-12, RM-13, RM-14</td>\n<td>Definitions, evaluation and retrieval interface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Specification and reference models do not establish deployed enforcement</td>\n<td>RM-15, RM-16, RM-18, RM-19</td>\n<td>Traceability and runtime conformance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Correct extraction of material context and ambiguity is not established by record validity</td>\n<td>RM-17, RM-21</td>\n<td>Source fidelity and robustness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>General quality/cost benefit is not established</td>\n<td>RM-20</td>\n<td>Controlled empirical comparison</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Cross-language editorial effectiveness and comprehension remain bounded</td>\n<td>RM-23</td>\n<td>Applicability and reader evaluation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Results need controlled integration without an endless publication hold</td>\n<td>RM-24, RM-25</td>\n<td>Revision and finite editorial completion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is the currently identified scope, not a claim that no undiscovered gap exists. RM-01 refines the ledger without silently enlarging the frozen editorial release.</p>\n<h3 id=\"src-4-priorities-and-dependency-structure\">4. Priorities and dependency structure</h3>\n<p>P0 means immediate transparency or release enablement: RM-01, RM-02, RM-03, RM-04 and RM-25. P1 means next substantive foundations, algebra, geometry and verification work. P2 means subsequent robustness, comparisons and result integration. These are proposed ordering priorities, not dates or proof that a worker has started.</p>\n<p>The machine graph has 25 nodes and 45 <code>requires_output</code> edges. An edge means that the named reviewed output is needed for acceptance or dependent execution. Preliminary reading and design may proceed earlier. Four <code>informs_not_blocks</code> links are advisory and do not create execution cycles. The graph does not mean that a whole upstream research programme must finish before a useful incremental result can be used.</p>\n<p>The main research path is:</p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>Claim inventory -&gt; related work -&gt; human-meaning foundations -&gt; formal bridge\n    -&gt; algebra profiles -&gt; practical cases -&gt; scaled checks -&gt; conformance\n    -&gt; geometry profile -&gt; geometry tests -&gt; hybrid retrieval -&gt; agent use\nClaim inventory -&gt; falsification docket -&gt; theory/code/evidence trace\n    -&gt; runtime and revision checks -&gt; controlled comparisons</code></pre>\n<p>These lines summarize the graph; <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/dependencies.json\">dependencies.json</a> is the exact edge definition. Site publication and task reconciliation run beside the research path. Editorial work is not dependent on completion of that path.</p>\n<h3 id=\"src-5-ownership-tasks-and-status\">5. Ownership, tasks and status</h3>\n<p>KC-Brain owns foundations, mathematical interpretation, manuscript content and scientific acceptance. Agent-Brain owns integration analysis, the independent adversarial view and cross-component designs. Brains write and review the actual artifacts themselves. Engineers receive only necessary server, implementation, registry or deployment operations with prepared inputs and acceptance conditions.</p>\n<p>The public <code>RM-xx</code> labels identify these definitions. They are not invented Muneral identifiers and do not establish execution status. Muneral remains the execution authority. RM-04 reconciles existing work and creates only missing items, retaining original task history. Its private binding report maps every public definition to actual work items or a precisely stated unresolved binding; no private identifiers, credentials, server locations or mail content need be published.</p>\n<p>A public status update is a dated projection of actual task evidence. At this roadmap revision, task execution bindings are <strong>not verified here</strong> and research is <strong>planned</strong>. Do not label all items scheduled, in progress or completed merely because they are written down. Avoid unsupported completion percentages and fixed delivery dates. Public status values may include planned, active, under review, blocked, completed or deferred only with the corresponding dated source; unknown remains unknown.</p>\n<p>Completing a task means delivering and assessing its promised output. A research output can refute the proposed hypothesis. Completion never means that a theory has become universally true.</p>\n<h3 id=\"src-6-startable-work\">6. Startable work</h3>\n<p>KC-Brain can begin RM-01 and RM-02 without waiting for a server. Agent-Brain can review the gap map and prepare the RM-10 adversarial docket from identified claims. KC-SWE can immediately reconcile the task graph (RM-04) and implement/publish the prepared roadmap surface (RM-03) under the repository and deployment rules. There is no broad instruction to delegate the theoretical work to SWE.</p>\n<p>Later server runs require a finite prepared input packet: source/build pins, cases, permitted operations, expected evidence, budgets and negative controls. Do not run a new costly benchmark merely because its task exists. Reconcile the existing consumer, readiness, editorial and verification work before adding a duplicate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"src-7-baseline-sources-and-evidence-boundaries\">7. Baseline sources and evidence boundaries</h3>\n<p>The baseline references are project documents, not new experiments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/717c43deb514adc5b40f76837e7ee0fb2da37102/publication-catalog.json\">Catalogue at the audited commit</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/717c43deb514adc5b40f76837e7ee0fb2da37102/papers/semantic-objects-and-context/PAPER.md\">Semantic objects and applicability</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/717c43deb514adc5b40f76837e7ee0fb2da37102/papers/constrained-transformations/PAPER.md\">Transformations, composition, equivalence and residuals</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/717c43deb514adc5b40f76837e7ee0fb2da37102/appendices/source-map/APPENDIX.md\">Historical source map and explicit limits of consolidation</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/engineering/studies/evidence-index.json\">Engineering claim/evidence index</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/engineering/studies/historical-recovery-gaps.json\">historical recovery inventory</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/contract-family/\">Operation contracts</a> and <a href=\"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/\">semantic representations</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/PUBLISHING.md\">Publication procedure</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/CONTRIBUTING.md\">contribution rules</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/contract-place-site/blob/main/README.md\">site build contract</a>. The site’s actual build source must be pinned again during delivery; this mutable documentation link is not a deployment receipt.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The roadmap changes no theorem, wire profile, publication edition, allowlist or runtime permission. Proposed new foundations/geometry manuscripts enter the publication catalogue only after they exist and pass the relevant reviews.</p>\n","overview_toc":[{"level":3,"id":"src-1-baseline-and-scope","title":"1. Baseline and scope"},{"level":3,"id":"src-2-two-tracks-and-a-finite-release-rule","title":"2. Two tracks and a finite release rule"},{"level":3,"id":"src-3-known-gaps-and-work-packages","title":"3. Known gaps and work packages"},{"level":3,"id":"src-4-priorities-and-dependency-structure","title":"4. Priorities and dependency structure"},{"level":3,"id":"src-5-ownership-tasks-and-status","title":"5. Ownership, tasks and status"},{"level":3,"id":"src-6-startable-work","title":"6. Startable work"},{"level":3,"id":"src-7-baseline-sources-and-evidence-boundaries","title":"7. Baseline sources and evidence boundaries"}],"tasks":[{"id":"RM-01","anchor":"rm-01","title":"Recover the claim-level mathematical crosswalk","priority":"P0","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":[],"needed_by":["RM-05","RM-06","RM-08","RM-10","RM-12","RM-15","RM-22","RM-23","RM-24"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>The source map is topical, not a complete accounting of historical definitions, laws and geometry proposals.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Inventory all 29 catalogue documents by section and the historical I10/I12 leads. Map each relevant definition, proposition, counterexample and limitation to a current location or an explicit disposition. Separate absent exposition, unsupported claims, missing evidence and absent implementation; do not equate these gaps.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A version-pinned claim inventory, old-to-current crosswalk and gap ledger with source anchors.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Every inspected historical item has retained, revised, superseded, deferred or rejected disposition and rationale; unknown coverage remains explicit; a second architect checks omissions.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Try to find a historical premise, exception or result not accounted for by the map; an omitted item reopens only the affected row.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>Ready for Brain analysis now; no server or full-corpus experiment is required.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-02","anchor":"rm-02","title":"Explain and publish the scope of each verification result","priority":"P0","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":[],"needed_by":["RM-11","RM-23"],"informs":[],"informed_by":["RM-22"],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Raw case totals can conceal the carrier, duplicated behaviors, arbitrary bounds and the distinction between a model and production code.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Prepare a verification passport for each cited checker: exact program/hash, tested object, parameter space, enumeration formula, iteration bound, oracle, vacuous cases, observed verdicts and exclusions. Reconcile the graph-root 4096 cases with the different historical 16-cubed Boolean-function triples. Publish only original or cleared reproducible inputs.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>Public reproducibility packets and per-claim verification passports; preserved historical reports.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>A reviewer can derive each case count and rerun the named program; an arbitrary small bound is explained, not advertised as sufficient; all production imports or their absence are disclosed.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Remove a necessary assumption and inject an implementation defect. A checker unable to detect the intended defect cannot support that assertion; same-code reruns are not independent validation.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>Brain prepares the explanatory records now; KC-SWE later executes the exact prepared public packet on the required host.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-03","anchor":"rm-03","title":"Publish the research roadmap on Contract Place","priority":"P0","owner":"KC-SWE","status":"planned","requires":[],"needed_by":[],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Readers need a visible separation between current results, known limits and planned research.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Implement the prepared roadmap reading surface using the canonical repository files, the existing site build and its pinned source revision. Provide localized navigation, task anchors, priorities, dependencies and truthful delivery state. Follow SITE_DELIVERY.md; do not create a second independent task database.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A site PR, generated roadmap pages, navigation entry, deployment and readback receipt.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>The deployed page identifies the exact source commit, shows all roadmap items and their definitions, preserves current paper routes, works on desktop/mobile and exposes no private execution data. The report includes real URLs, build/deploy identity and substantive content checks.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Test a stale source pin, broken task anchor, missing task and blocked/no-evidence status. None may render as completed research.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>Ready for the necessary site/server work after reconciling existing tasks and the reviewed source PR; no completed theory is a prerequisite.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-04","anchor":"rm-04","title":"Bind roadmap definitions to the execution work graph","priority":"P0","owner":"KC-SWE","status":"planned","requires":[],"needed_by":[],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Public roadmap IDs describe planned work; they are not verified task identities or execution status.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Reconcile each item against existing Muneral work, including the existing editorial, validation and consumer tasks. Reuse or split existing scope without losing history; create only genuinely missing work items. Return actual IDs, owners and typed dependencies privately. Publish only a sanitized, timestamped status projection where supported.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A complete private RM-ID to real-work-item map and evidence-backed public status policy.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>All 25 definitions resolve to existing or newly created real tasks, or an exact unresolved binding is named. No invented UUIDs, duplicate engineering dispatch or status inferred from mail delivery; dependencies match the roadmap graph.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Attempt duplicate registration and conflicting status updates. Reconciliation preserves one execution authority and reports disagreement.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>Ready for the narrow registry operation now; task creation is not permission to run every research experiment.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-05","anchor":"rm-05","title":"Write the foundations: from human meaning to usable knowledge","priority":"P1","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-01","RM-06"],"needed_by":["RM-07"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>The corpus has semantic objects and conditional results but lacks a single accessible foundations treatment spanning human meaning and agent use.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Draft a standalone introduction with scope, research questions and a terminology map. Separate expression, referent, proposition, intended meaning, context, interpretation, truth, justification, applicability and authorization. Trace human abstraction to explicit representation, formal interpretation, checked operation, knowledge storage and bounded agent use. State where ambiguity or judgment cannot be made deterministic by declaration.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A proposed foundations PAPER.md, examples, glossary changes and a map to existing papers; no automatic publication-catalogue promotion.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>A new reader can follow one example end to end and locate all assumptions. Existing work and external ideas are credited. The text distinguishes definitions, conjectures, established arguments and implementation proposals.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Use ambiguous intent, metaphor, context-dependent reference and conflicting norms to test whether the proposed account overclaims a universal reduction of human meaning.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-06","anchor":"rm-06","title":"Establish related work and the boundary of novelty","priority":"P1","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-01"],"needed_by":["RM-05","RM-08","RM-12"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Standard mathematical constructions and project-specific integration need a transparent comparison with prior work.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Review primary sources in semantics/pragmatics, model theory and logic, program correctness, abstraction, database views/provenance, conceptual or vector representations and knowledge graphs. Compare assumptions, operations and guarantees rather than matching names. Treat unavailable or unpublished material as unavailable, not a reconstructed authoritative theory.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A primary-source matrix, bibliography additions and a novelty/non-novelty statement for each proposed contribution.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Every borrowed definition or result has a specific attribution; unsupported priority or novelty claims are removed; external theories are not silently identified with KC.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Seek earlier work that already supplies the claimed contribution; narrowing or abandoning a novelty claim is an acceptable result.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-07","anchor":"rm-07","title":"Specify the human-to-formal semantic bridge","priority":"P1","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-05"],"needed_by":["RM-08","RM-09","RM-12","RM-14","RM-17"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>The structure of a semantic object does not itself define a correct conversion of arbitrary language into computable content.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Define a bounded typed representation with scoped negation, quantifiers, conditions, units, time, referents, attribution, alternatives, provenance and uncertainty. Specify candidate extraction separately from deterministic validation. Define abstract/concrete mappings, supported language fragments, loss accounting, computability limits and refusal or unresolved outcomes. Connect it to professional artifacts without treating a skill file as capability.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A representation/adapter specification, conversion contract and worked human-text to formal-object to artifact examples.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Each mapping has a declared domain/codomain, assumptions, loss record and executable checks where feasible; competing readings survive until justified resolution; unsupported constructs are not coerced into false precision.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Find a text pair collapsed into the same representation despite different required actions, or a round trip that drops a mandatory qualifier.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-08","anchor":"rm-08","title":"Complete the profile algebra of meaning operations","priority":"P1","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-01","RM-06","RM-07"],"needed_by":["RM-09"],"informs":["RM-12"],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Operations exist, but a coherent family of carriers, partial domains and laws is not fully traced to all intended uses.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Specify equivalence, entailment, conjunction/merge, common consequence, projection, residual, reconstruction, composition and transfer for named profiles. State partial domains, nonempty/consistent interpretations, representative independence, supported decision procedures and resource limits. Distinguish observation equivalence from identity and similarity; list laws that do not hold.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>An algebra profile document, law register, proof obligations and bounded executable reference semantics.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Every operation has types, domain, semantics, at least one positive and adverse example, and an explicit proof/test status. A standard Boolean result is not relabeled as a new theory or a universal document operation.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Attack domain congruence, noninjective reconstruction, incompatible observation interfaces, inconsistent premises, and claims of minimal residual size.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-09","anchor":"rm-09","title":"Build practical algebra examples and adversarial controls","priority":"P1","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-07","RM-08","RM-10"],"needed_by":["RM-11","RM-13","RM-17"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Small synthetic spaces do not provide a diverse test set of practically meaningful transformations.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Instantiate the algebra families in EXPERIMENTS.md using original or licensed sources. Cover units, exceptions, roles, scoped negation, attributions, changing recipients, residuals, conflicting sources and cross-domain transfer. Preserve source anchors and both positive and minimally changed negative cases. Separate visible development cases from withheld evaluation cases.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A versioned algebra benchmark, independent annotations/oracles and a case-to-law coverage matrix.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Every required semantic phenomenon has an expected observation and a counterexample control; ambiguous cases have sets of permissible answers rather than fabricated single labels; coverage is reported by family, not just count.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Have the independent architect construct unseen cases that defeat the proposed rule; retain failures, annotation disagreement and dataset leakage findings.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-10","anchor":"rm-10","title":"Maintain proof obligations and a falsification docket","priority":"P1","owner":"Agent-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-01"],"needed_by":["RM-09","RM-11","RM-13","RM-15","RM-17","RM-19","RM-24"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>A failed premise, failed implementation and counterexample to a theorem are different diagnoses.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>For every selected claim record quantifiers, assumptions, carrier, intended observation, proof route and attempted refutation. Classify evidence as deductive argument, bounded model, implementation regression or empirical comparison. A theorem counterexample satisfies all premises and violates the conclusion; premise-breaking cases test boundaries or conformance instead.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A claim/falsification register with adversarial cases, proof gaps and disposition rules.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Each major claim has a concrete attack and criterion for supported-within-scope, refuted, inconclusive or not measured. A verified counterexample forces correction/retraction of the affected claim, not a cosmetic roadmap note.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Search for vacuous success, hidden policy vetoes, inconsistent carriers, circular oracles and quantifier changes. Report unsuccessful searches with their actual bounds.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-11","anchor":"rm-11","title":"Scale bounded checks and connect them to implementation","priority":"P1","owner":"KC-SWE","status":"planned","requires":["RM-02","RM-09","RM-10"],"needed_by":["RM-16"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Three-vertex enumeration and the fixed iteration limit do not establish large-graph or production correctness.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Run the Brain-prepared plan with parameterized graph sizes, independent closure oracle, long chains/cycles, generated and mutation cases, and explicit deterministic budgets. Start with complete n=1..4 scope when feasible and use justified generated cases beyond it. Replace size-specific loop bounds only through reviewed implementation; separate model-only runs from tests importing actual code.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>Versioned runner/results, mutation report, seed and environment manifest, time/resource measurements.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Counts are derived and regenerated; original 4096 cases remain reproducible; long-chain defects are detected; model-only, production and unexecuted scopes remain separate. Exhaustive n=5 is not an arbitrary release requirement.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Inject missing-edge, early-stop, wrong-root, inconsistent-oracle and vacuous-case mutations; surviving mutants are reported and explained.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-12","anchor":"rm-12","title":"Develop the geometry-of-meaning profile","priority":"P1","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-01","RM-06","RM-07"],"needed_by":["RM-13","RM-14","RM-21"],"informs":[],"informed_by":["RM-08"],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Historical geometry arguments exist, but a current explicit and validated geometry profile is not established.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Specify what points/regions represent, the embedding map and version, dimensions, context conditioning, similarity or distance, topology if used, and claimed invariants. State metric axioms only when proved or verified for the chosen definition; separate pseudometric, similarity and directed entailment. Define information loss and the limits of inverse interpretation.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A geometry research document with precise definitions, candidate claims, countermodels and an evaluation plan.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>No geometric term is decorative: each has a carrier, operation and intended use. Similarity never grants truth, equivalence or authority by itself; no embedding is advertised as complete human meaning.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Construct threshold nontransitivity, collisions, norm changes and context-dependent nearest-neighbor reversals; a contradiction narrows or rejects the affected claim.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-13","anchor":"rm-13","title":"Test geometry against semantic distinctions","priority":"P1","owner":"Agent-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-09","RM-10","RM-12"],"needed_by":["RM-14"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Geometric similarity has not been validated against the distinctions required by concrete KC tasks.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Prepare a frozen comparison of high-similarity contradictions and low-similarity equivalent expressions. Include negation, quantifier scope, units, numbers, actor swaps and context. Compare lexical, vector and formal/profile-aware routes. Calibrate thresholds only on development data; keep separate unseen evaluation and explicit coverage/refusal outcomes.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A geometry benchmark/protocol and a server-ready run packet; actual results supplied by the engineering executor.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Report unsafe equivalence/merge decisions, retrieval coverage, calibration and variability by phenomenon. Fix sample size, primary measures and confidence/uncertainty method before the held-out run; one favorable example is not a measured advantage.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Search for a system that looks precise only because it refuses everything, learns test labels, or uses similarity as an unexamined correctness oracle.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-14","anchor":"rm-14","title":"Specify hybrid retrieval without semantic overreach","priority":"P1","owner":"Agent-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-07","RM-12","RM-13"],"needed_by":["RM-18"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Candidate ranking and contract-based admissibility need a documented interface between the geometric and logical layers.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Specify geometry/lexical retrieval as candidate generation, followed by source, scope, dependency, policy and semantic checks. Record ranking exclusions versus mandatory rejections. Define adapters to Scrutator without inventing new grants or namespaces. Retain an uncertainty/refusal route and a useful positive path.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A consumer interface/decision record and positive/adversarial fixtures with explicit handoff conditions.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>A similar but incompatible record is not delivered as accepted knowledge; a valid record can complete the path; cost and recall losses of checks are measured separately.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Attempt metadata-hash substitution, omitted dependency, misleading similarity, wrong reader and context-budget truncation.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-15","anchor":"rm-15","title":"Trace theory to requirements, code and observations","priority":"P1","owner":"Agent-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-01","RM-10"],"needed_by":["RM-16","RM-18","RM-19"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Existing propositions, specifications, standalone checks and services are not connected by a complete verified map.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>For each selected property identify the exact definition/proposition, requirement ID, implementation function/build, tests, raw evidence and limits. Distinguish code read, component tested and live behavior observed. Mark specified-but-unimplemented operations explicitly; reuse existing consumer/readiness tasks rather than dispatch duplicates.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A versioned traceability matrix with missing links and bounded implementation requests.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Every claimed implemented guarantee has a resolvable source and appropriately scoped observation; a document next to a model does not count as enforcement.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Follow an allegedly supported guarantee to an unrelated test, stale build, stub or unsupported representation; downgrade that row instead of treating the matrix as proof.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-16","anchor":"rm-16","title":"Run production-representative conformance and fault tests","priority":"P1","owner":"Agent-SWE","status":"planned","requires":["RM-11","RM-15"],"needed_by":["RM-20"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Finite reference checks do not establish that the deployed implementation enforces the same contract.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Execute prepared fixtures against named actual builds in authorized environments. Exercise malformed data, unsupported profiles, missing dependencies, wrong subject/recipient, stale evidence and altered outputs; include positive controls and independent observable counters. Preserve secrets and unrelated environments.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>Build-pinned execution reports, raw logs, input manifests, coverage and unresolved defects.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Every result names the exercised boundary and exact implementation; failures before a boundary do not verify later checks. No global conformance verdict from a subset.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Mutate or bypass an actual gate and show the test observes the intended violation; zero downstream calls require a positive-path control.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-17","anchor":"rm-17","title":"Evaluate extraction, ambiguity and source fidelity","priority":"P1","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-07","RM-09","RM-10"],"needed_by":["RM-18","RM-21"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Well-formed records do not show that all material semantic conditions were extracted from a source.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Prepare independently annotated, rights-cleared tasks for extraction and transformation, with source coordinates, preserved alternatives and damage/uncertainty labels. Define omission, hallucination, attribution and operator-scope errors separately. Freeze the evaluation protocol before any model run and preserve human/agent reviewer roles honestly.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>Annotation guide, evaluation split, source-bound reference answers and exact run plan.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Disagreement and inaccessible source regions remain visible; no producer-only judgment is called independent ground truth; deterministic validation is not confused with semantic completeness.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Insert a crucial exception in a table, footnote, image or different chapter and test whether a structurally valid artifact silently loses it.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-18","anchor":"rm-18","title":"Demonstrate accepted knowledge in a bounded agent task","priority":"P1","owner":"Agent-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-14","RM-15","RM-17"],"needed_by":["RM-20"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Prepared artifacts, indexed entries, exact retrieval and successful task use are separate stages.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Prepare an advanced research demonstration of one accepted semantic artifact and its finite required closure, including the RM-14 hybrid-retrieval interface, then reuse a suitable existing private consumer route for exact readback, complete context delivery and bounded task use. Bind source, representation, revision, purpose and assessment. Engineer executes only the required ingestion/service operations.</p>\n"},{"key":"scope","label":"Scope separation","html":"<p>RM-18 is an advanced research extension whose own acceptance depends on RM-14, RM-15 and RM-17. The existing Live Lab exact-consumer baseline has its own approved KCLIVE prerequisites and can supply earlier evidence without satisfying RM-18. Do not alias the whole RM-18 task to that baseline or import geometry / hybrid-retrieval dependencies into an otherwise ready KCLIVE task. Link only an actually matching, reviewed output; keep both task identities and their distinct acceptance conditions.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>An exact-byte handoff manifest, task output, assessment and trace across storage/retrieval/use.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Whole-document returned bytes match accepted hashes; all mandatory dependencies are present; the actual output is evaluated. Native task-run authority is not inferred from ordinary-session use.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Change one source, request, reader grant or dependency while retaining the old artifact; stale acceptance must not silently survive.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-19","anchor":"rm-19","title":"Verify current-use authority and revision races","priority":"P1","owner":"Agent-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-10","RM-15"],"needed_by":["RM-20"],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>A recent check or TTL does not establish authorization at the protected operation or safe publication ordering.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Specify a concrete admission point, supported snapshot/lease/lock assumptions, generation identity and revocation behavior. Prepare races involving check-then-revoke, slow-old-writer, lost/reordered invalidation and changed recipients. Reuse existing authority/readiness/install work and request only missing bounded observations.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A concurrency/failure model, test fixtures and a requirements-to-observation review.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>The coordination mechanism is named and tested at its actual boundary; no native success from CONTRACT_MISSING; event deduplication is not presented as recovery of lost events.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Force the interleaving that violates the claimed current-state property and check whether the implementation detects or prevents it.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-20","anchor":"rm-20","title":"Measure practical benefit against strong alternatives","priority":"P2","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-16","RM-18","RM-19"],"needed_by":[],"informs":["RM-24"],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>The corpus does not establish universal quality, latency or economic superiority.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Design task-matched comparisons among no reuse, simple strong reuse, lexical/vector retrieval and contract-aware routes where applicable. Equalize required quality and authority. Account for preparation, storage, validation, retries, review and coordination without double counting. Choose sample size and uncertainty reporting before evaluation.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A preregistered comparison protocol and, after execution, complete results including ties and adverse outcomes.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Separate causal claims from observations, retain all declared outcomes and report when overhead exceeds benefit. No selection of only successful cached tasks.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Test conditions where a simple baseline ties or wins and where safe rejection creates apparent speed improvements at the expense of usefulness.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-21","anchor":"rm-21","title":"Test multilingual, multimodal and shifted-context limits","priority":"P2","owner":"Agent-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-12","RM-17"],"needed_by":[],"informs":[],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Language/genre and modality transfer cannot be inferred from one textual profile.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Prepare paired cross-language and multimodal cases with independently checked references, including numeric notation, image legends, OCR damage, missing assets and changed professional context. Keep interface translation separate from manuscript translation. Bound intended use instead of promising universal transfer.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A robustness suite, profile applicability table and per-family report.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Failures and unresolved readings remain distinct; correlations across translated cases do not inflate independent sample counts; model/data versions and rights are recorded.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Find a translation or visual normalization that leaves topical similarity unchanged but reverses a scoped condition or numerical interpretation.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-22","anchor":"rm-22","title":"Recover historical evidence without rewriting history","priority":"P1","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-01"],"needed_by":[],"informs":["RM-02"],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Some historical programs/fixtures remain unavailable and source-rights constraints limit reproducibility.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Audit the existing recovery inventory and cited reports; request only exact missing exports/logs from their owners. Preserve originals and hashes; distinguish recovered original, reconstructed view and new synthetic reproduction. Prepare public substitutes only when clearly labeled and appropriately licensed.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>An updated recovery inventory, evidence provenance and permitted reproduction bundles.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Every gap has recovered, unavailable or superseded-by-new-study disposition; no invented past execution or retrospective alteration of raw data.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Try to reproduce a historical conclusion from only available inputs; an inability is reported, not repaired by substituting a new result without disclosure.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-23","anchor":"rm-23","title":"Validate editorial skills and reader comprehension","priority":"P2","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-01","RM-02"],"needed_by":[],"informs":["RM-25"],"informed_by":[],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Russian applied-writing skills and a single-section tie do not establish English scientific-editing effectiveness.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Define a bounded genre/language adaptation and protected-claim contract. Evaluate complete documents with separate scientific review and comprehension questions, and compare with a well-specified ordinary editing baseline where a benefit claim is intended. Preserve measured ties and unknown reader results.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>An adaptation profile, edit trace, independent review and appropriately scoped comprehension study.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Critical conditions, negations, equations and citations are retained or explicitly corrected; readable does not mean shorter; author self-review is not called external peer review.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Seek a clearer-sounding edit that turns sufficient conditions into necessary ones, deletes a qualifier or increases confidence without evidence.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-24","anchor":"rm-24","title":"Integrate new findings through versioned research updates","priority":"P2","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":["RM-01","RM-10"],"needed_by":[],"informs":[],"informed_by":["RM-20"],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>New theory and negative results need a controlled route back into papers, specifications, studies and appendices.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>For each completed work package prepare its claim-level impact set, corrected manuscript/specification/study, evidence links and historical compatibility mapping. Add new foundations or geometry papers to the catalogue only when actual reviewed documents exist. Preserve task outcome separately from scientific support.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>Reviewed versioned changes, impact/review records and repository/site/index readback where applicable.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>A positive, negative or inconclusive research outcome is accurately reflected in every affected claim; planned work is never cited as completed evidence. Integrate incrementally, not after the entire backlog.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Check for stale downstream theorems, unsupported catalogue entries, rewritten old evidence or a site serving a different source commit.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>After the named dependency outputs are reviewed; design can start earlier without claiming them complete.</p>\n"}]},{"id":"RM-25","anchor":"rm-25","title":"Finish the bounded editorial publication pass","priority":"P0","owner":"KC-Brain","status":"planned","requires":[],"needed_by":[],"informs":[],"informed_by":["RM-23"],"fields":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap","html":"<p>Unlimited research expansion must not prevent a readable, scientifically honest release of the existing corpus.</p>\n"},{"key":"work","label":"Work","html":"<p>Freeze the current editorial inventory at the 29 catalogue documents, then review sections one document at a time with protected scientific content, genre-specific structure and exact repository changes. Include specifications, studies, glossary, bibliography and appendices. Repair material false claims locally; defer enrichment to this roadmap. Existing approved first/second-document publication work is preserved.</p>\n"},{"key":"deliverables","label":"Deliverables","html":"<p>A finite per-document editorial checklist, reviewed changes and verified publication handoffs.</p>\n"},{"key":"acceptance","label":"Acceptance","html":"<p>Each document has readable structure, correct attribution, intact or explicitly corrected claims, visible limitations, working references and actual site confirmation after release. No requirement to solve all meaning research first.</p>\n"},{"key":"adversarial","label":"Adversarial check","html":"<p>Reject a pass that only looks scholarly, hides an unsupported result, lacks source traceability or treats successful typography/CI as scientific validation.</p>\n"},{"key":"start","label":"Start boundary","html":"<p>The earlier roadmap-only checkpoint is superseded by <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/roadmap/FOUNDATIONS_RELEASE_PLAN.md\">FOUNDATIONS_RELEASE_PLAN.md</a> (§§1, 3, 7). For otherwise unassigned general editorial work, use that plan’s F01–F06 preparation order and the precise “foundation package available” threshold defined in <a href=\"https://github.com/Arcanada-one/knowledge-contract/blob/f6d42ca1d1d1e09d678332d4fec2311aa847d695/ROADMAP.md\">ROADMAP.md</a> §2. This is a preparation priority, not a prerequisite that all research experiments, independent publications or the 25 RM tasks finish. Preserve the already authorized first-two-paper reviews and delivery, roadmap-site publication and material-defect fixes. Later explicit author instructions for particular existing papers take precedence over this default order; they do not waive scientific review, DCO, CI or site verification.</p>\n"}]}],"advisory":[{"from":"RM-23","to":"RM-25","kind":"informs_not_blocks"},{"from":"RM-20","to":"RM-24","kind":"informs_not_blocks"},{"from":"RM-22","to":"RM-02","kind":"informs_not_blocks"},{"from":"RM-08","to":"RM-12","kind":"informs_not_blocks"}],"required_edges":45},"source":"https://contract.place/","license":"CC-BY-4.0","author":"Pavel Valentov","retrieved":"2026-11-04","reader_aliases":{"science":"/en/science/","engineering":"/en/engineering/","engineering/specifications":"/en/engineering/specifications/","engineering/studies":"/en/engineering/studies/","reference":"/en/reference/","appendices":"/en/appendices/","search":"/en/search/","science/human-meaning-and-computable-knowledge":"/en/science/human-meaning-and-computable-knowledge/","science/semantic-objects-and-context":"/en/science/semantic-objects-and-context/","science/constrained-transformations":"/en/science/constrained-transformations/","science/retrieval-and-context-composition":"/en/science/retrieval-and-context-composition/","science/interaction-and-memory":"/en/science/interaction-and-memory/","science/evidence-and-falsification":"/en/science/evidence-and-falsification/","science/revision-and-governance":"/en/science/revision-and-governance/","science/controlled-knowledge-reuse":"/en/science/controlled-knowledge-reuse/","science/knowledge-utility":"/en/science/knowledge-utility/","engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core":"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-contract-core/","engineering/specifications/contract-family":"/en/engineering/specifications/contract-family/","engineering/specifications/semantic-types":"/en/engineering/specifications/semantic-types/","engineering/specifications/context-package":"/en/engineering/specifications/context-package/","engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract":"/en/engineering/specifications/compiled-artifact-contract/","engineering/specifications/artifact-registry":"/en/engineering/specifications/artifact-registry/","engineering/specifications/status-model":"/en/engineering/specifications/status-model/","engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation":"/en/engineering/specifications/cache-identity-and-invalidation/","engineering/specifications/reuse-policy":"/en/engineering/specifications/reuse-policy/","engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution":"/en/engineering/specifications/knowledge-evolution/","engineering/specifications/book-package":"/en/engineering/specifications/book-package/","engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance":"/en/engineering/studies/contract-profile-conformance/","engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery":"/en/engineering/studies/dependency-closure-and-package-delivery/","engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty":"/en/engineering/studies/source-transcription-and-uncertainty/","engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision":"/en/engineering/studies/executable-projections-and-revision/","engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls":"/en/engineering/studies/reuse-admission-and-cost-controls/","engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot":"/en/engineering/studies/editorial-skill-pilot/","reference/glossary":"/en/reference/glossary/","reference/bibliography":"/en/reference/bibliography/","appendices/source-map":"/en/appendices/source-map/","appendices/api-integration":"/en/appendices/api-integration/"}};
